\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 House approves legislation to protect  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  access across US\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Gambino, Chris Stein in Washington and Adam Gabbatt in New York \par \uc2 942 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 July 2022\par 20:18 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Vote was largely symbolic as two bills stand all but no chance of overcoming Republican opposition in the evenly-divided Senate\par \par \uc2 The House of Representatives on Friday approved legislation that would protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access nationwide, the first action by Democrats in Congress to respond to the supreme court decision in late June {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturning Roe v Wade}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The vote was largely symbolic \u8211\'20\'13 the bills stand all but no chance of overcoming Republican opposition in the evenly divided Senate, where 60 votes are needed to move legislation forward.\par \par \uc2 But the action, the first in the post-Roe era, begins what Democrats promise will be an all-out, potentially years-long, political campaign to restore \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in all 50 states.\par \par \uc2 Already, Republican-led legislatures in states across wide swaths of the south and midwest are moving quickly to enact restrictions or bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that were once unlawful under the precedent set by the landmark 1973 Roe v Wade ruling by the supreme court, while Democratic-led states have acted to expand access and protections for women seeking the procedure.\par \par \uc2 The June ruling was expected to lead to bans in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2022/jun/28/tracking-where-abortion-laws-stand-in-every-state"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nearly half of US states}}}\uc2 , though lawsuits and legislative delays vary when they would take effect.\par \par \uc2 US president \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and party leaders are under mounting pressure from their supporters, who are furious over the court\u8217\'20\'19s decision to invalidate a half-century constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and frustrated that their party leaders appeared to lack a cogent plan of action.\par \par \uc2 Before the vote, the House speaker, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , and a coalition of Democratic women, all wearing green, a color that has come to symbolize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, stood on the steps of the Capitol chanting: \u8220\'20\'1CWe are not going back.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 During her remarks on the floor, Pelosi warned that Republicans would seek a \u8220\'20\'1Cbarbaric\u8221\'20\'1D national ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if they win control of the chamber.\par \par \uc2 Many Democrats highlighted {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jul/15/doctor-10-year-old-rape-victim-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the case}}}\uc2  of a 10-year-old Ohio girl who was raped and then had to cross state lines into Indiana in order to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of tighter restrictions in her own state, as an example of the tragic consequences of the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling. Many conservatives cast doubt on the veracity of the story, which was confirmed when a man was arraigned in the rape.\par \par \uc2 House Democrats approved two measures on Friday. One would protect the right to travel across state lines for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, a new flashpoint in the debate as anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups push legislation that would block women from traveling out-of-state. That passed the House by 223 votes to 205 no votes.\par \par \uc2 The measure would also shield healthcare providers who perform abortions on out-of-state patients from legal repercussions.\par \par \uc2 The other bill, a version of which already passed the House last year, would establish \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in federal law, effectively overturning a flurry of state restrictions and bans and giving a national legislative underpinning to a federal right that had been dictated by the court.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.congress.gov/bill/117th-congress/house-bill/8296"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 measure}}}\uc2  would guarantee \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access until fetal viability, the point at which a human fetus is widely deemed able to survive outsidethe uterus, roughly considered to be around 24 weeks, or after that point if the mother\u8217\'20\'19s health or life are at risk. It passed by 219 yes votes to 210 no votes.\par \par \uc2 It would also prohibit what its authors say are medically unnecessary restrictions designed to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access under the guise of protecting women\u8217\'20\'19s health.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou should not have more rights if you get pregnant in California than if you get pregnant in Texas,\u8221\'20\'1D said Congresswoman Judy Chu, a Democrat from California and the author of the Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Protection Act.\par \par \uc2 Friday\u8217\'20\'19s action was also an attempt to put Republicans on the record on an issue Democrats believe will galvanize their ranks in November\u8217\'20\'19s midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 Only a narrow sliver of Americans believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be banned, and consistent majorities oppose the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision to overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 Republicans uniformly opposed the bills.\par \par \uc2 A number of Republican lawmakers have embraced a nationwide ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , promising a flurry of new federal restrictions if they win control of Congress in the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 There are two Republican senators in the House who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2  of Alaska and \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  of Maine. They do not support the House\u8217\'20\'19s bill codifying Roe, saying it goes further than the supreme court precedent. They have introduced alternative legislation, but even so, it is unlikely to persuade enough Republican senators to overcome the 60-vote threshold.\par \par \uc2 Asked whether Democrats should work with these Republican senators on a compromise plan, Pelosi told reporters: \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re not going to negotiate a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to choose.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has faced widespread criticism from Democrats disappointed with his response to the overturning of Roe, which they viewed as belated and overly cautious. The president has since displayed a more aggressive tone on the issue and directed his administration to take additional steps to protect access.\par \par \uc2 But ultimately he said the only way to \u8220\'20\'1Ctruly\u8221\'20\'1D protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights was for Congress to act, and to do that voters needed to elect at least two more Democratic senators in November.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe must ensure that the American people remember in November,\u8221\'20\'1D Pelosi said, \u8220\'20\'1Cbecause with two more Democratic senators we will be able to eliminate the filibuster when it comes to a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to choose and to make reproductive freedom the law of the land.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220715ei7f00335\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
