\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18An inequality story\u8217\'20\'19: Utah  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban will drive women further into poverty\b0\par\par\uc2 Mackenzie Ryan in Salt Lake City \par \uc2 1,527 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 July 2022\par 19:22 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Women already make 30% less than men in the state, with new restrictions affecting women of color and single mothers\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI was horrified.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Utah State Representative Angela Romero had long known that if the supreme court ruled to overturn Roe v Wade, her state\u8217\'20\'19s trigger law would come into effect and elective abortions would quickly be banned. But, even armed with that knowledge she still couldn\u8217\'20\'19t believe that scenario had become reality.\par \par \uc2 The district Romero represents sits on the west side of Salt Lake City, an area that stands in stark economic and cultural contrast to the predominantly white, wealthy suburbs on the city\u8217\'20\'19s east side.\par \par \uc2 With access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  newly restricted, Romero was \u8220\'20\'1Cespecially concerned about marginalized communities, communities of color, and this forcing them to stay in poverty\u8221\'20\'1D, she said at her office in Salt Lake City on Tuesday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re always cutting social services as a state,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CHow are people supposed to get out of poverty if they don\u8217\'20\'19t get the support they need? We don\u8217\'20\'19t do that as a state already.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Utah already put up hurdles for people to get abortions, with an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://informedconsent.health.utah.gov/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 informed consent law}}}\uc2  that required people wanting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to go through a 72-hour waiting period and complete an education module.\par \par \uc2 The state was one of several with so-called trigger laws, which banned elective abortions shortly after the supreme court ruled to overturned Roe v Wade on 24 June. The implementation of Utah\u8217\'20\'19s law, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://le.utah.gov/~2020/bills/static/sb0174.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 SB 174}}}\uc2 , is temporarily blocked, after a judge granted a 14-day restraining order following a lawsuit filed by Planned Parenthood of Utah. On Monday, a judge will rule if the trigger law violates the state\u8217\'20\'19s constitution. In the meantime a different state law, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://le.utah.gov/~2019/bills/static/HB0136.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 HB 136}}}\uc2 , went into effect, banning abortions after 18 weeks.\par \par \uc2 The new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions will widen Utah\u8217\'20\'19s already dire economic gender and racial disparities, argue legislators, economists and women in the state.\par \par \uc2 Utah women live in poverty at a lower rate than the national average, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.usu.edu/uwlp/research/snapshots"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Utah Women and Leadership Project (UWLP) research}}}\uc2 . But a more granular look reveals women of color are having a very different experience in the Beehive State economy. Nearly 34% of Black women, 27.5% of Native American women, 18.5% of Latina women, 14.1% of Asian women, and 11.8% of Pacific Islander women in the state live in poverty.\par \par \uc2 Single mothers of all demographic backgrounds have it the hardest, according to UWLP data, with more than 35% of single mothers with children under five living in poverty.\par \par \uc2 Utah women make about 30% less than men, one of the biggest gender wage gaps in the country.\par \par \uc2 When bans go into effect, women with means are still going to find a way and they\u8217\'20\'19re going to travel to other states\par \par \uc2 Caitlyn M K Myers\par \par \uc2 Making ends meet is a struggle Romero knows all too well. Before she was a legislator, Romero became a single mother at 23. She had a strong support system that many people don\u8217\'20\'19t, she said. She took out loans, lived in public housing and earned a master\u8217\'20\'19s degree. It took her until four or five years ago to pay off the debt she incurred.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWill individuals [who are now denied abortions] have access to daycare for children? Healthcare for children? Will they be able to pay rent? What kind of resources are we going to have available once the child is born? What are we doing to prevent families from becoming homeless?\u8221\'20\'1D she asked.\par \par \uc2 Poverty booms and credit dives\par \par \uc2 The National Bureau of Economic Research\uc2 , a non-profit research organization, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nber.org/papers/w26662"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 calculated}}}\uc2  in 2020 that women who were turned away and unable to get abortions had 80% increase in negative credit events. The same women experienced unpaid debts, lingering and growing in size, evictions, and bankruptcies with negative events up to four years after their child was born.\par \par \uc2 Whether or not to have a child is the \u8220\'20\'1Cmost economically consequential decision\u8221\'20\'1D a woman makes in her lifetime, said Caitlyn M K Myers, an economist at \uc2 Middlebury College\uc2 , who has spent the past 15 years studying the causal effects of reproductive policy.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, Myers argued, open up a \u8220\'20\'1Cfinancial gulf\u8221\'20\'1D between women who are able to obtain abortions and those who are denied them.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen bans go into effect, women with means are still going to find a way and they\u8217\'20\'19re going to travel to other states,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s the women who are the poorest and most vulnerable who can\u8217\'20\'19t get out, who can\u8217\'20\'19t get away to travel hundreds of miles to find a provider. They\u8217\'20\'19re the ones whose lives are going to be economically impacted for the long-term.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 About a quarter of women in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans won\u8217\'20\'19t be able to travel to other states, Myers said, adding that that group of women is disproportionately young, disproportionally poor, and of disproportionally women of color.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s an inequality story,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Chloe Sokol, 19, learned she was pregnant while on birth control shortly before Roe v Wade was overturned, she said. She saved up $450, drove to Salt Lake, and got an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I would hate to have a child and not have money, especially with inflation and the baby formula shortage, especially at my stage of life\par \par \uc2 Chloe Sokol\par \par \uc2 Sokol lives paycheck to paycheck and dreams of becoming a tattoo artist, which requires a long apprenticeship. Her boyfriend already works long hours.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt would have definitely been a really grueling process,\u8221\'20\'1D she said about having to continue with an unplanned, unwanted pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI know that if we have kids, we want to give our kids the best life. I would hate to have a child and not have money, especially with inflation and the baby formula shortage, especially at my stage of life.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Worries over unpaid debt\par \par \uc2 Candida Duran Tavera, 30, borrowed money to have her own \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2014. She was in a relationship at the time. She and her partner used condoms, but it wasn\u8217\'20\'19t 100% effective. She could barely afford her $200 a month rent, let alone the costs of raising a child.\par \par \uc2 Three years later, married and wanting a child, she gave birth to a daughter who was born with complications. Her daughter is now four, and Taveras says she\u8217\'20\'19s still paying off the medical debt.\par \par \uc2 Now a doula, Tavera provides emotional support to women going through childbirth and abortions and also works for Planned Parenthood, though she is not speaking on behalf of the organization. Women who are being forced to give birth might not have the financial capacity to pay their medical bills, she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re going to get into more debt,\u8221\'20\'1D she said, adding that would hurt their credit, their ability to rent a house or buy a car, she added.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe people who I am working with are people who are heavily policed and heavily discriminated against,\u8221\'20\'1D she continued, adding that the healthcare system was not set up for Black and brown bodies even before the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s June ruling.\par \par \uc2 She pointed to the sharp disparities in maternal health outcomes in the US, and the rise in maternal mortality rates for Black women in the country. Data by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/data/hestat/maternal-mortality/2020/maternal-mortality-rates-2020.htm#:~:text=In%202020%2C%20861%20women%20were,20.1%20in%202019%20(Table)."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Center for Disease Control and Prevention}}}\uc2  (CDC) showed the maternal mortality rate for Black women was 55.3 deaths per 100,000 live births in 2020, a significant increase from 2019. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s safer for women of color to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  than go through childbirth,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey really fucked us over intentionally. It was malicious,\u8221\'20\'1D she said about Utah\u8217\'20\'19s trigger law.\par \par \uc2 This will have a chilling effect on everything that\u8217\'20\'19s reproductive health. Beyond just \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Luz Escamilla\par \par \uc2 Utah State Senator Luz Escamilla is equally blunt about the outcome of the end of elective abortions in Utah. \u8220\'20\'1CWomen are going to die,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Escamilla argues the new laws will result in less medical care for the poor, the working poor and underinsured. She thinks there will be more aggressive, intrusive policies introduced in the upcoming legislative session.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis will have a chilling effect on everything that\u8217\'20\'19s reproductive health. Beyond just \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D she added. \u8220\'20\'1CAs we are heading into a recession, how scary that is!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Escamilla sits on the state\u8217\'20\'19s Women in the Economy Commission. For the past couple of years, the commission has been studying why so many Utah women entered higher education and then left, entered the workforce and then left. The main reason, the commission found, was a lack of access to affordable childcare.\par \par \uc2 A lot of employment decisions Utah women make involve healthcare considerations, Escamilla said. The state\u8217\'20\'19s attempt to ban all elective abortions after the supreme court decision has made many women in Utah, especially young women, feel like second-class citizens, she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not going to change until you get more women elected,\u8221\'20\'1D she argued. \u8220\'20\'1CSuddenly now this is real.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220715ei7e002uv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
