\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18At death\u8217\'20\'19s door\u8217\'20\'19:  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  bans endanger lives of high-risk patients, Texas study shows\b0\par\par\uc2 Mary Tuma \par \uc2 1,212 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 July 2022\par 19:33 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 In a preview of what\u8217\'20\'19s to come in half the country, a near-total ban has led some providers to deny care until mothers\u8217\'20\'19 health deteriorated\par \par \uc2 Facing a rupture of membranes before fetal viability \u8211\'20\'13 a condition in which water breaks too early \u8211\'20\'13 a pregnant patient in Texas desperately needed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . She risked infection, sepsis, excessive bleeding and even death.\par \par \uc2 But her healthcare provider\u8217\'20\'19s hands were tied by Senate Bill 8, a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/may/19/texas-abortion-ban-law-greg-abbott"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 near-total ban}}}\uc2  in effect since September 2021, preventing her from accessing that potentially life-saving care in her home state. Despite the risk associated with air travel, she boarded a plane to obtain the procedure out of state. Her obstetrician cautioned that she could go into labor in-flight and give birth to a stillborn 19-week fetus. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you labor on the plane, leave the placenta inside of you,\u8221\'20\'1D the doctor warned.\par \par \uc2 Even before Roe v Wade was overturned last month, paving the way for a pre-Roe ban to take effect in Texas, a different {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/01/texas-abortion-law-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  state \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban}}}\uc2  dramatically limited care after the detection of embryonic cardiac activity, typically around six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Despite a carve-out for medical emergencies, the law endangered the lives of high-risk pregnant patients, according to Texas researchers documenting its consequences in a recent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMp2207423?query=featured_home"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  New England Journal of Medicine study}}}\uc2 . Some patients needed to be \u8220\'20\'1Cat death\u8217\'20\'19s door\u8221\'20\'1D to receive pregnancy termination under the law, the paper found, underscoring how \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans create dangerous repercussions for complicated pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 The law, SB 8, offers no exception for rape, incest or severe fetal abnormality and has barred in-state care for the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sites.utexas.edu/txpep/files/2021/07/TxPEP-research-brief-senate-bill-8.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  overwhelming majority}}}\uc2  of Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients. For months after it was enacted, it was widely considered the strictest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in the US. Now, as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2022/jun/28/tracking-where-abortion-laws-stand-in-every-state"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  nearly half of US states}}}\uc2  have either banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or are about to, Texas offers an unsettling harbinger of what is to come.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat we have seen in Texas could very well be a preview of what we will see elsewhere,\u8221\'20\'1D Dr Whitney Arey, lead author of the paper and researcher with the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sites.utexas.edu/txpep/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas Policy Evaluation Project}}}\uc2 , a group that tracks the impact of state reproductive health policies, told the Guardian. \u8220\'20\'1CThese narrow exceptions in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans create a climate of fear for providers, which will be really dangerous for some of the most vulnerable patients and put many lives at risk.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In an effort to address the problem, the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  (\uc2 HHS\uc2 ) on Monday issued {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/emergency-medical-care-letter-to-health-care-providers.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  guidance}}}\uc2  making clear that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  must be available in emergencies, regardless of state restrictions.\par \par \uc2 But {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2022/07/11/biden-abortion-roevwade-00045166%20https://19thnews.org/2022/07/abortion-guidance-pregnant-people-hhs-biden-administration/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 experts stress}}}\uc2  the rule will be only symbolic without strong government enforcement and public awareness. The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://capitol.texas.gov/BillLookup/History.aspx?LegSess=87R&Bill=SB8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  Texas law}}}\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s exception for \u8220\'20\'1Cmedical emergencies\u8221\'20\'1D is not well-defined, leaving hospitals and clinicians often erring on the side of caution, and it is unclear whether the \uc2 HHS\uc2  rule will change that. The law empowers anyone to sue a provider or someone who \u8220\'20\'1Caids or abets\u8221\'20\'1D an illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for damages of at least $10,000, creating a \u8220\'20\'1Cchilling effect\u8221\'20\'1D on health care professionals.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEven if the guidance is pretty clear, I think some doctors in these situations will still find it a really challenging decision to make,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mary Ziegler, a professor at the \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2  who specializes in the legal history of reproductive health. \u8220\'20\'1CThey may continue to be uncomfortable \u8211\'20\'13 especially considering some of the harsh penalties in state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, including life in prison.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18They are waiting until heart failure\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 In Texas, patients with medically complex pregnancies or preexisting medical conditions are being forced to delay \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care until their situations become life-threatening or until fetal cardiac activity is no longer detectable, according to the study, which was conducted by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, researchers and maternal-fetal medicine specialists, who interviewed 25 clinicians and 20 patients with high-risk pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 One ob-gyn recalled only a single patient who was able to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at their hospital under the law\u8217\'20\'19s exemption, after her severe cardiac condition had worsened to the point that she was admitted to the intensive care unit. In \u8220\'20\'1Cmultiple cases\u8221\'20\'1D, treating physicians sent patients home, only to see them return with signs of sepsis, the paper notes.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSome doctors are no longer offering termination when there is risk of death for the pregnant patient,\u8221\'20\'1D Dr Lorie Harper, chief of maternal-fetal medicine at the University of Texas at Austin Dell Medical School, said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey are waiting until heart failure, waiting until hemorrhaging, waiting until a patient needs to be intubated, or is [having organ failure]. Patients have to be a lot sicker before they receive life-saving care \u8211\'20\'13 and not every patient who becomes that critically ill will recover.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The researchers worry about a rise in cases of maternal mortality as a result of pregnant people being denied care. Texas already holds a maternal death rate {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/maternal-mortality/MMR-2018-State-Data-508.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  higher}}}\uc2  than the US average, with Black women disproportionately affected. Overall, the US has the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.commonwealthfund.org/publications/issue-briefs/2020/nov/maternal-mortality-maternity-care-us-compared-10-countries"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  highest}}}\uc2  maternal death rate among similarly developed countries and is the only industrialized nation where such deaths are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2017/05/12/528098789/u-s-has-the-worst-rate-of-maternal-deaths-in-the-developed-world"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  rising}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Fearing lawsuits and potential loss of medical license, some Texas clinicians believe they are not allowed even to inform patients about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in cases of increased maternal risks or poor fetal health, provide any information about out-of-state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics or transfer patient records to those clinics.\par \par \uc2 The study recounts the story of a 39-year-old woman who, after receiving a fetal diagnosis of spina bifida and a chromosomal disorder, was \u8220\'20\'1Cshocked\u8221\'20\'1D that her doctor would not even inform her about termination options. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen you already have received news like that and can barely function, the thought of then having to do your own investigating to determine where to get this medical care and to arrange going out of state feels additionally overwhelming,\u8221\'20\'1D she told them.\par \par \uc2 In an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/19/19-1392/193074/20210920174518042_19-1392%20bsacACOGetal.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  amicus brief}}}\uc2  to the supreme court, two dozen health care organizations, including the \uc2 American Medical Association\uc2  and the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2 , underscore that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans with narrow exceptions force clinicians to make an \u8220\'20\'1Cimpossible choice\u8221\'20\'1D between upholding their ethical obligations and following the law. \u8220\'20\'1CIn forcing physicians to wait until a patient is close enough to death that they will risk their license to practice medicine to save her life by providing needed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, the state indefensibly jeopardizes patients\u8217\'20\'19 health,\u8221\'20\'1D they write.\par \par \uc2 A 1925 pre-Roe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban is now in effect in Texas after it was upheld by the state supreme court. It bars all abortions with rare exceptions for the life of the mother. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers are challenging that century-old ban in court, but they are also up against a \u8220\'20\'1Ctrigger\u8221\'20\'1D law set to take effect {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2022/06/24/texas-abortion-law-supreme-court-ruling/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 at some point this summer}}}\uc2 , which would have a similar impact.\par \par \uc2 Texas researchers hope their work provides a cautionary tale to other states navigating the post-Roe landscape.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is really frustrating to not be able to offer patients the full range of healthcare information and services,\u8221\'20\'1D said Harper. \u8220\'20\'1CI fear that this all could get much worse under these trigger laws. I really fear for patients\u8217\'20\'19 lives.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220713ei7d001bc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
