\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 January 6 hearings: Trump tried to contact witness, Cheney says \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Maanvi Singh (now) and Joan E Greve in Washington \par \uc2 6,721 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 July 2022\par 23:32 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Witness declined phone call, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  says, and panel \u8216\'20\'18will take any efforts to influence witness testimony very seriously\u8217\'20\'19Sign up to receive First Thing \u8211\'20\'13 our daily briefing by email\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.13am BST\par \par \uc2 January 6 committee hearing summary\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s it from us today. Here\u8217\'20\'19s how the seventh public hearing held by the January 6 committee unfolded:\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  said \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  attempted to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jul/12/jan-6-hearing-trump-tried-to-contact-witness"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 contact one of the witnesses}}}\uc2  in the investigation after the committee\u8217\'20\'19s last hearing. According to Cheney, the Republican vice-chair of the committee, the unnamed witness did not pick up Trump\u8217\'20\'19s call. Instead, they contacted their lawyer, who in turn informed the committee about the call. The committee passed the information along to the justice department. \u8220\'20\'1CLet me say one more time: we will take any efforts to influence witness testimony very seriously,\u8221\'20\'1D Cheney said.\par \par \uc2 * The committee shared clips from \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s closed-door testimony with investigators. Cipollone, Trump\u8217\'20\'19s former White House counsel, met with the committee for more than eight hours on Friday. He told the committee that he believed Trump should have conceded the election after the electoral college certified \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s victory on 14 December. \u8220\'20\'1CIf your question is, did I believe he should concede the election at a point in time? Yes, I did,\u8221\'20\'1D Cipollone said. The committee plans to show more of Cipollone\u8217\'20\'19s testimony at its hearing next week.\par \par \uc2 * The committee argued Trump made a \u8220\'20\'1Cdeliberate\u8221\'20\'1D plan to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jul/11/jan-6-hearings-far-right-groups-capitol-attack"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 call for a march}}}\uc2  to the Capitol on 6 January. Committee member \uc2 Stephanie Murphy\uc2  showed a draft tweet from Trump\u8217\'20\'19s account reading, \u8220\'20\'1CI will be making a Big Speech at 10AM on January 6th at the Ellipse (South of the White House). Please arrive early, massive crowds expected. March to the Capitol after. Stop the Steal!!\u8221\'20\'1D The unsent and undated tweet, obtained from the National Archives, is stamped with the words \u8220\'20\'1Cpresident has seen\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 * The committee detailed an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jul/12/january-6-hearings-testimony-cipollone-meeting"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cunhinged\u8221\'20\'1D meeting}}}\uc2  at the White House on 18 December. According to multiple witnesses, the meeting devolved into insults and shouting after some of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s White House advisers attacked suggestions from \uc2 Sidney Powell\uc2 , \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  and \uc2 Michael Flynn\uc2  to seize voting machines in battleground states. The next day, Trump sent his tweet encouraging supporters to come to Washington on 6 January for a \u8220\'20\'1Cwild\u8221\'20\'1D event.\par \par \uc2 * The committee showed how Trump\u8217\'20\'19s 19 December tweet led far-right groups to zero in on 6 January to protest the election. Committee member Jamie Raskin displayed a \uc2 Facebook\uc2  post written by Oath Keepers leader Kelly Meggs on 19 December, saying he he had organized an \u8220\'20\'1Calliance\u8221\'20\'1D between the Oath Keepers and two other far-right militia groups, the Three Percenters and the Proud Boys. \u8220\'20\'1CWe have decided to work together and shut this shit down,\u8221\'20\'1D Meggs said in the post.\par \par \uc2 * One of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s former campaign advisers said his {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jul/12/january-6-hearings-trump-brad-parscale-texts"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rhetoric was directly responsible}}}\uc2  for the deaths on 6 January. \uc2 Brad Parscale\uc2  told fellow campaign adviser Katrina Pierson he felt \u8220\'20\'1Cguilty\u8221\'20\'1D about helping Trump win election in the days after the Capitol insurrection. \u8220\'20\'1CIf I was Trump and I knew my rhetoric killed someone,\u8221\'20\'1D Parscale told Pierson in a text message. Pierson replied, \u8220\'20\'1CIt wasn\u8217\'20\'19t the rhetoric.\u8221\'20\'1D \u8220\'20\'1CKatrina,\u8221\'20\'1D Parscale said. \u8220\'20\'1CYes it was.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The blog will be back tomorrow with more analysis of the hearing and updates from Washington. See you then.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.28am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.23pm BST\par \par \uc2 Analysis: January 6 testimony tells chilling tale of democracy hanging by a thread\par \par \uc2 Viewers learned of an \u8220\'20\'1Cunhinged\u8221\'20\'1D White House meeting and rioters ready for war \u8211\'20\'13 but will it close the case against Trump?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe settle our differences at the ballot box.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2 , chairman of the congressional committee investigating the January 6 attack on the US Capitol, emphasised this article of faith in his opening remarks on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Trump allies \u8220\'20\'1Cscreamed\u8221\'20\'1D at aides who resisted seizing voting machines, January 6 panel hearsRead more\par \par \uc2 But what followed was a three-hour story about how American democracy, like a rickety old house, creaked and bent and struggled to hold itself together during a thunderstorm of political violence.\par \par \uc2 There was the tale of an Oval Office meeting that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jul/12/january-6-hearings-testimony-cipollone-meeting"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 almost ended in fisticuffs}}}\uc2 . There was testimony from a former true believer in the \u8220\'20\'1Cbig lie\u8221\'20\'1D who joined the rampage at the Capitol. There were predictions that if Trump runs again, no one will be safe.\par \par \uc2 It was a chilling reminder that in a nation that has the genocide of Indigenous Americans, slavery, civil war and relentless gun violence in its cultural DNA, bloodshed is never far from the surface. Since white supremacists marched in Charlottesville, Virginia, in August 2017, extremist groups such as the Proud Boys and Oath Keepers have been ascendent.\par \par \uc2 Jamie Raskin, another member of the panel, observed: \u8220\'20\'1CThe problem of politicians whipping up mob violence to destroy fair elections is the oldest domestic enemy of constitutional democracy in America.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He quoted Abraham Lincoln: \u8220\'20\'1CMobs and demagogues will put us on a path to political tyranny.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Read more:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jul/12/january-6-hearings-testimony-cipollone-meeting"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump allies \u8216\'20\'18screamed\u8217\'20\'19 at aides who resisted seizing voting machines, January 6 panel hears}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.47pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.02pm BST\par \par \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  is reporting that \uc2 Patrick Byrne\uc2 , the former CEO of Overstock.com, will speak with the January 6 committee this week.\par \par \uc2 Byrne was referenced today in testimony from \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 , Trump\u8217\'20\'19s second White House counsel.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFirst of all, I saw the Overstock person,\u8221\'20\'1D Cipollone said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe first thing I did, I walked in, I looked at him, I said, \u8216\'20\'18Who are you?\u8217\'20\'19 And he told me.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think any of these people were providing the president with good advice. So I didn\u8217\'20\'19t understand how they had gotten in.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Byrne was at a meeting in December with \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , former security adviser \uc2 Michael Flynn\uc2  and lawyer \uc2 Sidney Powell\uc2 , during which the president was asked to name Powell a special counsel so she could work to overturn the election and have the federal government seize voting machines.\par \par \uc2 The committee confirmed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/feb/04/trump-draft-order-voting-machines-white-house-meeting"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Guardian exclusive}}}\uc2  reporting that Trump actually agreed to make Powell special counsel with oversight for seizing voting machines.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.23pm BST\par \par \uc2 Hi there, it\u8217\'20\'19s Maanvi Singh \u8211\'20\'13 taking over the blog for the next few hours.\par \par \uc2 John Bolton\uc2 , the former national security advisor, had an interesting reaction to today\u8217\'20\'19s revelations. In response to CNN anchor Jake Tapper\u8217\'20\'19s reflection that \u8220\'20\'1Cone doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have to be brilliant to attempt a coup\u8221\'20\'1D, Bolton responded that he disagrees, \u8220\'20\'1Cas somebody who has helped plan\u8221\'20\'1D coups.\par \par \uc2 enltrJake Tapper: "One doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have to be brilliant to attempt a coup."\par \par \uc2 John Bolton\uc2 : "I disagree with that. As somebody who has helped plan coup d\u8217\'20\'19etat, not here, but other places, it takes a lot of work." {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/REyqh3KtHi"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/REyqh3KtHi}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Justin Baragona (@justinbaragona) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/justinbaragona/status/1546955197045415936?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 12, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.01pm BST\par \par \uc2 After the hearing concluded, Capitol insurrectionist Stephen Ayres approached some of the law enforcement officers who defended the building on January 6 and were present for today\u8217\'20\'19s proceedings.\par \par \uc2 Ayres was seen shaking hands with Aquilino Gonell, a \uc2 US Capitol Police\uc2  sergeant who was beaten during the insurrection and can no longer work in law enforcement because of his injuries.\par \par \uc2 But one of the law enforcement officers who spoke to Ayres, former Metropolitan police department officer Michael Fanone, said he was unmoved by the man\u8217\'20\'19s remorse.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat apology doesn\u8217\'20\'19t do shit for me. I hope it does shit for him,\u8221\'20\'1D Fanone told the AP.\par \par \uc2 enltrI asked MPD office Fanone if he accepts Ayers apology and he said: \u8220\'20\'1CThat apology doesn\u8217\'20\'19t do shit for me, I hope it does shit for him.\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/iEvjkYotDa"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/iEvjkYotDa}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Farnoush Amiri (@FarnoushAmiri) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/FarnoushAmiri/status/1546951321374302208?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 12, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.16pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.45pm BST\par \par \uc2 In a bizarre, angry and \u8220\'20\'1Cunhinged\u8221\'20\'1D White House meeting on 18 December 2020, outside advisers to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  screamed insults at presidential aides who were resisting their plan to seize voting machines and name a special counsel in pursuit of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s attempt to overturn the election.\par \par \uc2 The meeting \u8211\'20\'13 which the House January 6 committee in its public hearing on Tuesday described as a \u8220\'20\'1Cheated and profane clash\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 was held between those who believed the president should admit he lost the election to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and a group of outsiders referred to by some Trump advisers as \u8220\'20\'1CTeam Crazy\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 They included Trump\u8217\'20\'19s personal lawyer, \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  ; the retired lieutenant general \uc2 Michael Flynn\uc2 , Trump\u8217\'20\'19s former national security adviser; and a lawyer for his campaign team, \uc2 Sidney Powell\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In testimony to the House January 6 committee played at the hearing, Giuliani said that at the meeting he had called the White House lawyers and aides who disagreed with that plan \u8220\'20\'1Ca bunch of pussies\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Eric Herschmann, a White House lawyer, said that Flynn \u8220\'20\'1Cscreamed at me that I was a quitter and kept standing up and turning around and screaming at me. I\u8217\'20\'19d sort of had it with him so I yelled back, \u8216\'20\'18Either come over or sit your effing ass back down.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jul/12/january-6-hearings-testimony-cipollone-meeting"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump allies \u8216\'20\'18screamed\u8217\'20\'19 at aides who resisted seizing voting machines, January 6 panel hears}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.31pm BST\par \par \uc2 Committee member Jamie Raskin, who co-led today\u8217\'20\'19s hearing with \uc2 Stephanie Murphy\uc2 , condemned \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s actions on January 6 in his closing statement.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAmerican carnage: that\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s true legacy. His desire to overthrow the people\u8217\'20\'19s election and seize the presidency, interrupting the counting of electoral college votes for the first time in American history, nearly toppled the constitutional order and brutalized hundreds and hundreds of people,\u8221\'20\'1D Raskin said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Watergate break-in was like a cub scout meeting compared to this assault on our people and our institutions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Raskin argued that the most important element of the January 6 hearings is determining what actions can be taken now to prevent similar violence in the future.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe crucial thing is the next step -- what this committee, what all of us will do to fortify our democracy against coups, political violence and campaigns to steal elections away from the people,\u8221\'20\'1D Raskin said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe need to defend both our democracy and our freedom with everything we have to declare that this American carnage ends here and now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.16pm BST\par \par \uc2 In her closing statement, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  also shared additional footage from \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s interview with the committee behind closed doors on Friday.\par \par \uc2 In the clip, Cipollone said that he and a number of other senior White House officials were urging \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to call off the insurrection on January 6.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI felt it was my obligation to continue to push for that. And others felt it was their obligation as well,\u8221\'20\'1D Cipollone said.\par \par \uc2 Asked whether it would have been possible for Trump to make some kind of public statement shortly after the insurrection started to call off the violence, Cipollone said yes, it would have been possible. Trump refused to do so for hours.\par \par \uc2 Cheney noted that Cipollone\u8217\'20\'19s testimony will feature prominently in the committee\u8217\'20\'19s hearing next week, which is expected to focus on Trump\u8217\'20\'19s actions and words as the insurrection unfolded.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.00pm BST\par \par \uc2 Trump tried to call January 6 witness, Cheney says\par \par \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , the Republican vice-chair of the January 6 committee, said that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  himself tried to contact one of the witnesses in the investigation.\par \par \uc2 According to Cheney, the witness, who has not yet been publicly revealed as a participant in the committee\u8217\'20\'19s investigation, declined the call.\par \par \uc2 Instead, the witness informed their lawyer about Trump\u8217\'20\'19s attempted call. The lawyer then informed the January 6 committee, who passed the information along to the justice department.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLet me say one more time: we will take any efforts to influence witness testimony very seriously,\u8221\'20\'1D Cheney said.\par \par \uc2 Cheney warned at the last hearing that at least two witnesses had been contacted by Trump allies urging them to stay loyal to the former president in their testimony to the committee.\par \par \uc2 Those efforts raise questions about potential witness tampering, which could open Trump and his allies up to criminal charges.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.53pm BST\par \par \uc2 Former Oath Keepers spokesperson says January 6 could have 'started a new civil war'\par \par \uc2 Jason Van Tatenhove, a former spokesperson for the far-right extremist group Oath Keepers, said the Capitol insurrectionists had planned \u8220\'20\'1Can armed revolution\u8221\'20\'1D on January 6.\par \par \uc2 He noted that the insurrectionists set up a gallows for \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , as the vice-president oversaw the congressional certification of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s victory in the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI mean, people died that day,\u8221\'20\'1D Van Tatenhove said. \u8220\'20\'1CThis could have been the spark that started a new civil war, and no one would have won there.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.50pm BST\par \par \uc2 Capitol insurrectionist Stephen Ayres said his life has changed significantly since January 6. He lost his job and had to sell his house, in addition to pleading guilty to a federal charge.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt changed my life -- not for the good. Definitely not for the better,\u8221\'20\'1D Ayres said.\par \par \uc2 Asked how he feels when he sees \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  continuing to peddle lies about widespread fraud in the 2020 election, Ayres said, \u8220\'20\'1CIt makes me mad because I was hanging on every word.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.44pm BST\par \par \uc2 Stephen Ayres, who participated in the Capitol insurrection and has pleaded guilty to one federal charge of disorderly conduct inside a restricted building, said he closely followed \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s lies about the 2020 election over social media.\par \par \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , the Republican vice-chair of the January 6 committee, asked Ayres whether it would have made a difference to him if he knew that Trump had no evidence of widespread fraud in the election.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COh, definitely,\u8221\'20\'1D Ayres said. \u8220\'20\'1CWho knows? I may not have come down here then.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Ayres said Trump had gotten \u8220\'20\'1Ceverybody riled up\u8221\'20\'1D by telling his supporters to come to Washington on January 6, as Congress certified \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s victory in the election.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe basically just followed what he said,\u8221\'20\'1D Ayres said.\par \par \uc2 Asked when he decided to leave the Capitol on January 6, Ayres said he departed after seeing Trump\u8217\'20\'19s tweet asking his supporters to leave the building.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBasically, when President Trump put his tweet out, we literally left right after that come out,\u8221\'20\'1D Ayres said. He added that he might have left before then if Trump had sent his tweet earlier.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 Jason Van Tatenhove, a former spokesperson for the far-right extremist group Oath Keepers, said he decided to leave the organization after he heard members suggest that the Holocaust wasn\u8217\'20\'19t real. (That is, of course, a baseless lie.)\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI can tell you that they may not like to call themselves a militia, but they are. They\u8217\'20\'19re a violent militia,\u8221\'20\'1D Van Tatenhove told the January 6 committee.\par \par \uc2 The Oath Keepers were one of several violent militia groups that helped orchestrate the violence on January 6, alongside the Proud Boys and the Three Percenters.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.20pm BST\par \par \uc2 Trump ally blames president's rhetoric for January 6 death\par \par \uc2 Brad Parscale\uc2 , a former senior campaign adviser to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , said he felt \u8220\'20\'1Cguilty\u8221\'20\'1D about helping him win election in the days after the Capitol insurrection.\par \par \uc2 Parscale described Trump as \u8220\'20\'1Ca sitting president asking for civil war,\u8221\'20\'1D in reference to his efforts to disrupt the congressional certification of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s victory.\par \par \uc2 Responding to Parscale\u8217\'20\'19s text message, fellow Trump adviser Katrina Pierson said, \u8220\'20\'1CYou did what you felt right at the time and therefore it was right.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Parscale responded, \u8220\'20\'1CYeah, but a woman is dead.\u8221\'20\'1D He later added, \u8220\'20\'1CIf I was Trump and I knew my rhetoric killed someone.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Pierson replied, \u8220\'20\'1CIt wasn\u8217\'20\'19t the rhetoric.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CKatrina,\u8221\'20\'1D Parscale said. \u8220\'20\'1CYes it was.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.33pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.15pm BST\par \par \uc2 The committee identified 10 Republican House members who attended a White House meeting on December 21 to discuss options for overturning the results of the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 According to the committee, those members were:\par \par \uc2 * Brian Babin\par \par \uc2 * Andy Biggs\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Matt Gaetz\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Louie Gohmert\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Paul Gosar\par \par \uc2 * Andy Harris\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Jody Hice\par \par \uc2 * Jim Jordan\par \par \uc2 * Scott Perry\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  (then a congresswoman-elect)\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.08pm BST\par \par \uc2 In his closed-door testimony before the January 6 committee, \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 , \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s former White House counsel, applauded the actions of Vice-President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  on that violent day.\par \par \uc2 Despite intense pressure from Trump and some of his allies, Pence refused to go along with the then-president\u8217\'20\'19s plans to interfere with the congressional certification of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s victory.\par \par \uc2 After the Capitol attack, Pence returned to the Senate chamber on January 6 to finish the certification process, clearing the way for Biden to take the oath of office.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think the vice-president did the right thing. I think he did the courageous thing,\u8221\'20\'1D Cipollone told investigators on Friday.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think he did a great service to this country. And I think I suggested to somebody that he should be given the Presidential Medal of Freedom for his actions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.57pm BST\par \par \uc2 Trump made 'deliberate' plan to call for march to the Capitol, Murphy says\par \par \uc2 Committee member \uc2 Stephanie Murphy\uc2  shared a draft tweet written by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  encouraging his supporters to march to the Capitol on January 6.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI will be making a Big Speech at 10AM on January 6th at the Ellipse (South of the White House),\u8221\'20\'1D the draft tweet says. \u8220\'20\'1CPlease arrive early, massive crowds expected. March to the Capitol after. Stop the Steal!!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The draft tweet, obtained by the committee from the National Archives, was undated, but it was stamped with the words \u8220\'20\'1Cpresident has seen\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 enltr"PRESIDENT HAS SEEN" {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/January6thCmte?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @January6thCmte}}}\uc2  obtained drafted, unsent tweet. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/yYg3sKFv96"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/yYg3sKFv96}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 CSPAN (@cspan) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/cspan/status/1546931780766294019?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 12, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Murphy said, \u8220\'20\'1CThe evidence confirms that this was not a spontaneous call to action, but rather a deliberate strategy decided upon in advance by the president.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The committee also showed messages from some of the January 6 rally organizers indicating that they knew of the plans to march to the Capitol but kept them quiet.\par \par \uc2 Rally organizer Kylie Kremer said in one message that Trump was just going to call for the march to the Capitol \u8220\'20\'1Cunexpectedly\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.58pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.45pm BST\par \par \uc2 The January 6 hearing resumed after a short break, and committee member Jamie Raskin shared additional information about collaboration between far-right extremist groups in the weeks leading up to the Capitol attack.\par \par \uc2 Raskin displayed a \uc2 Facebook\uc2  post written by Oath Keepers leader Kelly Meggs on 19 December, the same day that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  sent a tweet encouraging his supporters to come to Washington on January 6 for a \u8220\'20\'1Cwild\u8221\'20\'1D event.\par \par \uc2 In the post, Meggs said he had organized an \u8220\'20\'1Calliance\u8221\'20\'1D between the Oath Keepers and two other far-right militia groups, the Three Percenters and the Proud Boys.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have decided to work together and shut this shit down,\u8221\'20\'1D Meggs said in the post.\par \par \uc2 Raskin said the committee had obtained phone records showing that Meggs spoke with Proud Boys leader \uc2 Enrique Tarrio\uc2  for several minutes later that afternoon.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe very next day, the Proud Boys got to work,\u8221\'20\'1D Raskin said.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.29pm BST\par \par \uc2 The two witnesses for today\u8217\'20\'19s hearing, Stephen Ayres and Jason Van Tatenhove, have now taken their seats in the hearing room.\par \par \uc2 Ayres participated in the Capitol insurrection and recently pleaded guilty to one federal charge of disorderly conduct inside a restricted building.\par \par \uc2 Name placards for Stephen Ayres and Jason Van Tatenhove are prepared before they testify at the January 6 hearing. Photograph: J Scott Applewhite/AP\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abcnews.go.com/US/accused-jan-rioter-warned-civil-war-expected-testify/story?id=86598396"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Court documents show}}}\uc2  that Ayres admitted to traveling to Washington on January 5 to protest against the congressional certification of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s victory in the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 Two days before leaving, Ayres posted on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  saying, \u8220\'20\'1CMainstream media, social media, Democrat party, FISA courts, Chief Justice John Roberts, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , etc....all have committed TREASON against a sitting U.S. president!!! All are now put on notice by \u8216\'20\'18We The People!\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Van Tatenhove previously served as a spokesperson for the Oath Keepers, one of the far-right groups that helped carry out the violence on January 6, although he left that role before the insurrection took place.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.14pm BST\par \par \uc2 The select committee shared evidence indicating that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s 19 December tweet urging his supporters to come to Washington on January 6 led to increased attention on that particular day.\par \par \uc2 Committee member Jamie Raskin showed a request from the pro-Trump group Women for America First to change its Washington rally permit to January 6 after the then-president sent his tweet about the event.\par \par \uc2 An anonymous \uc2 Twitter\uc2  employee told the committee that Trump\u8217\'20\'19s tweet coincided with an increase in violent rhetoric among his supporters online.\par \par \uc2 One Trump supporter promised there would be \u8220\'20\'1Ca red wedding going down January 6\u8221\'20\'1D. That is a Game of Thrones reference to a wedding where many of the attendees are murdered.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.07pm BST\par \par \uc2 The January 6 hearing included detailed descriptions of an alarming, tense meeting at the White House on 18 December, as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  continued to peddle lies about widespread fraud in the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 During the meeting, Trump campaign lawyers \uc2 Sidney Powell\uc2  and \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  and former national security adviser \uc2 Michael Flynn\uc2  urged the then-president to seize voting machines in battleground states.\par \par \uc2 That idea was sharply criticized by senior administration officials, including Trump adviser Eric Herschmann, who said he considered the suggestions to be \u8220\'20\'1Cnuts\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The meeting then devolved into insults and shouting, as Powell\u8217\'20\'19s group implied that critics like Herschmann did not have the courage to overturn the election.\par \par \uc2 Cassidy Hutchinson, an aide to White House chief of staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , described the atmosphere in the West Wing on 18 December as \u8220\'20\'1Cunhinged\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Later that evening, Hutchinson took a photo of Meadows escorting Giuliani off White House grounds to ensure he did not \u8220\'20\'1Cwander back to the mansion,\u8221\'20\'1D as she said.\par \par \uc2 The day after this explosive meeting, Trump sent a tweet encouraging his supporters to come to Washington on January 6. \u8220\'20\'1CBe there.. will be wild,\u8221\'20\'1D Trump said.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.55pm BST\par \par \uc2 Cipollone denounced 'terrible idea' to seize voting machines\par \par \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  denounced an idea from \uc2 Sidney Powell\uc2 , one of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s campaign lawyers, to seize voting machines in battleground states after the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 Cipollone, Trump\u8217\'20\'19s former White House counsel, said he believed the suggestion was a \u8220\'20\'1Cterrible idea\u8221\'20\'1D that could sow distrust in American elections.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTo have the federal government seize voting machines, it\u8217\'20\'19s a terrible idea,\u8221\'20\'1D Cipollone told the January 6 committee. \u8220\'20\'1CThat is not how we do things in the United States. There\u8217\'20\'19s no legal authority.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.49pm BST\par \par \uc2 Cipollone says he believed Trump should have conceded election\par \par \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  said he believed that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  should have conceded the election after members of the electoral college met to certify \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s victory on December 14.\par \par \uc2 Cipollone, who previously served as Trump\u8217\'20\'19s White House counsel, noted that Senate Republican leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  expressed that belief as well after the electoral college meeting.\par \par \uc2 Cipollone told investigators, \u8220\'20\'1CIf your question is, did I believe he should concede the election at a point in time? Yes, I did.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.44pm BST\par \par \uc2 The committee played a clip from its interview with \uc2 Eugene Scalia\uc2 , \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s former labor secretary and son of the late supreme court justice \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Scalia told investigators that he called Trump in mid-December and advised him to concede the election, but the then-president refused to do so.\par \par \uc2 Several of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s senior White House advisers told the committee that they concluded the election was over on December 14, when members of the electoral college gathered to certify \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s victory.\par \par \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2 , Trump\u8217\'20\'19s eldest daughter and senior adviser, said she considered the election to be over by that point.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think it was my sentiment. Probably prior as well,\u8221\'20\'1D Trump said.\par \par \uc2 Kayleigh McEnany\uc2 , Trump\u8217\'20\'19s former press secretary, told investigators, \u8220\'20\'1CIn my view, on the conclusion of litigation was when I began to plan for life after the administration.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Former White House spokesperson Judd Deere said he also tried to convince Trump that the election was settled after the electoral college met.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe disagreed,\u8221\'20\'1D Deere said of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s response.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.45pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.33pm BST\par \par \uc2 Cipollone makes first (virtual) appearance at hearing\par \par \uc2 The committee showed the first clip from \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s interview with investigators, which took place behind closed doors on Friday.\par \par \uc2 In the clip, Cipollone, who served as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s White House counsel, confirmed that he had seen no evidence of widespread fraud in the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 According to Cipollone, \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , Trump\u8217\'20\'19s former chief of staff, assured him and \uc2 William Barr\uc2 , the former attorney general, that the then president would concede the election after the electoral college met to finalize \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s victory.\par \par \uc2 Instead, Trump continued to peddle lies about election fraud, and those lies culminated in the deadly attack on the Capitol on January 6.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.35pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.28pm BST\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman \uc2 Stephanie Murphy\uc2 , who is co-leading today\u8217\'20\'19s hearing alongside Jamie Raskin, outlined how \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  rejected his advisers\u8217\'20\'19 conclusion that he had fairly lost the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 Instead, Trump sent a tweet on 19 December urging his supporters to come to Washington on 6 January for a \u8220\'20\'1Cwild\u8221\'20\'1D event, which would be held as Congress convened to certify \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s victory.\par \par \uc2 That tweet \u8220\'20\'1Cserved as a call to action and in some cases as a call to arms for many of President Trump\u8217\'20\'19s most loyal supporters,\u8221\'20\'1D Murphy said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe president\u8217\'20\'19s goal was to stay in power for a second term despite losing the election,\u8221\'20\'1D Murphy said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe assembled crowd was one of the tools to achieve that goal.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.21pm BST\par \par \uc2 Cheney: 'Trump cannot escape responsibility by being willfully blind'\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , the Republican vice-chair of the January 6 committee, said the panel\u8217\'20\'19s first six hearings have shown how \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s campaign advisers and senior administration officials repeatedly told him there was no evidence of widespread fraud in the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 So, Cheney said, the strategy for defending Trump\u8217\'20\'19s actions has now shifted to arguing that the former president was manipulated by his campaign lawyers into embracing lies about the election.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPresident Trump is a 76-year-old man. He is not an impressionable child. Just like everyone else in our country, he is responsible for his own actions and his own choices,\u8221\'20\'1D Cheney said.\par \par \uc2 Cheney reiterated that Trump was told \u8220\'20\'1Cover and over again\u8221\'20\'1D in the weeks leading up to January 6 that he had fairly lost the presidential election to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNo rational or sane man in his position could disregard that information and reach the opposite conclusion,\u8221\'20\'1D Cheney said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  cannot escape responsibility by being willfully blind.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.11pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.13pm BST\par \par \uc2 Cheney confirms committee will share clips of Cipollone's testimony\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , the Republican vice-chair of the January 6 committee, confirmed that the panel will share clips from \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s interview with investigators during today\u8217\'20\'19s hearing.\par \par \uc2 Cipollone, who previously served as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s White House counsel, spoke to the committee behind closed doors for more than eight hours on Friday.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you\u8217\'20\'19ve watched these hearings, you\u8217\'20\'19ve heard us call for Mr Cipollone to come forward to testify,\u8221\'20\'1D Cheney said. \u8220\'20\'1CHe did. And Mr Cipollone\u8217\'20\'19s testimony met our expectations.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Jamie Raskin, who will co-lead today\u8217\'20\'19s hearing alongside \uc2 Stephanie Murphy\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/jul/12/jan-6-hearing-trump-extremist-groups-house-committee-us-politics-latest?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-62cd859c8f0852fc5f6bb263#block-62cd859c8f0852fc5f6bb263"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told NBC News today}}}\uc2  that Cipollone corroborated key elements of the testimony that the committee has already heard about the attack on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.05pm BST\par \par \uc2 January 6 hearing gets underway\par \par \uc2 The House select committee investigating the January 6 insurrection has now kicked off its seventh public hearing.\par \par \uc2 The hearing is expected to focus on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s potential ties to far-right extremist groups who helped carry out the violence at the Capitol on January 6.\par \par \uc2 The blog will have more updates and analysis as the hearing unfolds, so stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.58pm BST\par \par \uc2 Khiara M Bridges, professor at UC Berkeley School of Law, sharply criticized the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s legal reasoning for overturning Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 Testifying before the Senate judiciary committee, Bridges explained how the conservative justices who overturned Roe in Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health argued that the 14th amendment only protects rights that were deeply rooted in history in 1868, when the amendment was ratified.\par \par \uc2 Bridges said, \u8220\'20\'1CThe majority concludes that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are not part of the nation\u8217\'20\'19s history and tradition.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 That reasoning, Bridges said, could imperil other rights that were not established in 1868 \u8211\'20\'13 including the right to access contraception, the right for same-sex couples to marry and the right to participate in consensual intimate relationships without fear of prosecution.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe method of constitutional interpretation that the majority uses in the Dobbs decision should frighten everyone who holds dear these rights that are necessary for people to live fully human lives with dignity in this country,\u8221\'20\'1D she told the committee.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.26pm BST\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s falling poll ratings among Democrats will likely not be helped by a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.huffpost.com/entry/chad-meredith-judicial-nominee-abortion-biden_n_62cc82d9e4b0451684664530?mt8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  that he may be planning to go ahead with a controversial nomination of an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  attorney to a lifetime federal judgeship in Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 According to an unnamed source with knowledge of the White House\u8217\'20\'19s plans, Biden is minded to forge ahead with the appointment despite strong opposition from his party and from reproductive rights groups who are still reeling from last month\u8217\'20\'19s supreme court ruling overturning the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The White House is still refusing to comment on the potential appointment of Chad Meredith, a member of the conservative anti-choice \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2 , despite a tranche of leaked emails published by the Louisville Courier-Journal {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.courier-journal.com/story/news/politics/mitch-mcconnell/2022/06/29/biden-backs-anti-abortion-judge-candidate-apparent-mcconnell-deal/7767539001/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 last month}}}\uc2  that he is the president\u8217\'20\'19s pick for the soon-to-be vacant district court seat in eastern Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 Speculation is mounting that appointing Meredith could be part of a deal with the senate minority leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  (of Kentucky) to get Biden\u8217\'20\'19s last slate of judicial nominees approved ahead of the November midterms, when Democrats fear they could lose control of both chambers.\par \par \uc2 Eight national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.plannedparenthood.org/about-us/newsroom/press-releases/joint-statement-from-reproductive-health-rights-and-justice-groups-on-chad-meredith"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 issued a rare joint statement}}}\uc2  calling Meredith \u8220\'20\'1Cunacceptable at any time, but especially on the heels of six Supreme Court justices taking away a fundamental right from millions of people\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.57pm BST\par \par \uc2 Republican Senator Tom Cotton asked the witnesses at the Senate judiciary committee whether they condemn recent violence against anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups.\par \par \uc2 The lieutenant governor of Illinois, Juliana Stratton, replied that she condemns all violence, including that committed against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and clinics.\par \par \uc2 Stratton noted that incidents of arson, bombings and assaults carried out against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics and providers have increased exponentially in the past two years.\par \par \uc2 Responding to Cotton\u8217\'20\'19s question, Denise Harle, the director of the Center For Life at the \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2 , said, \u8220\'20\'1CI do condemn all violence, and I would just note that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is an act of violence against the most innocent and vulnerable human beings.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Khiara M Bridges, professor at UC Berkeley School of Law, then said, \u8220\'20\'1CI condemn violence, and I would like to note that forced birth is an act of violence.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.35pm BST\par \par \uc2 Senate judiciary committee holds hearing on end of Roe\par \par \uc2 The Senate judiciary committee is holding a hearing on the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision to overturn Roe v Wade, ending nearly 50 years of federal protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 Dr Colleen P McNicholas, the chief medical officer for Planned Parenthood of the St Louis Region and Southwest Missouri, was one of the five witnesses testifying before the committee.\par \par \uc2 McNicholas told the committee that the reversal of Roe had created \u8220\'20\'1Ctwo nations,\u8221\'20\'1D one where Americans still have reproductive rights and another where politicians \u8220\'20\'1Chave effectively appointed themselves as decisionmakers over our bodies, our lives and our futures\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 She noted that Planned Parenthood\u8217\'20\'19s clinic in Illinois had seen appointments triple overnight in the wake of the Roe reversal. That is in addition to the clinic\u8217\'20\'19s already double-booked schedule, caused by the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in Oklahoma and Texas.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToday, I\u8217\'20\'19m here to speak for patients -- the patients I serve every day, people from across the Midwest and South who navigate a ridiculous obstacle course just to access basic care,\u8221\'20\'1D McNicholas said.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.55pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.14pm BST\par \par \uc2 The January 6 committee was originally expected to hold another hearing on Thursday detailing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s response to the insurrection as it unfolded.\par \par \uc2 But a committee aide said yesterday that the panel would hold only one hearing this week, and members are instead expected to reconvene next week.\par \par \uc2 The aide said the delay was meant to give committee members an opportunity to review \u8220\'20\'1Cnew and important information\u8221\'20\'1D that has been received \u8220\'20\'1Con a daily basis\u8221\'20\'1D as the hearings unfold.\par \par \uc2 But the committee has not provided any further details about the next hearing, which could be the panel\u8217\'20\'19s last hearing for the time being.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.57pm BST\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s former top strategist, \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 , suffered heavy setbacks in his contempt of Congress case on Monday after a federal judge dismissed his motion to delay his trial, scheduled for next week, and ruled he could not make two of his principal defences to a jury.\par \par \uc2 The flurry of adverse rulings from District of Columbia district judge Carl Nichols \u8211\'20\'13 a Trump appointee \u8211\'20\'13 marked a significant knock back for Bannon, who was charged with criminal contempt after he ignored a subpoena last year from the House January 6 select committee investigating the attack on the US Capitol by extremist Trump supporters in 2021.\par \par \uc2 Nichols refused in federal court in Washington DC, to delay Bannon\u8217\'20\'19s trial date set for next Monday, saying that he saw no reason to push back proceedings after he severely limited the defences that the former Trump aide\u8217\'20\'19s lawyers could present to a jury.\par \par \uc2 The defeats for Bannon stunned his lead lawyer, David Schoen, who asked, aghast: \u8220\'20\'1CWhat\u8217\'20\'19s the point of going to trial if we don\u8217\'20\'19t have any defences?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Read the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s full report:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jul/11/steve-bannon-contempt-of-congress-trial"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bannon suffers setback as judge rejects delaying contempt of Congress trial}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.42pm BST\par \par \uc2 Raskin says Cipollone corroborated Hutchinson's testimony\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s January 6 hearing is expected to feature clips from the select committee\u8217\'20\'19s interview last week with \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 , who served as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s White House counsel.\par \par \uc2 Cipollone met with investigators behind closed doors for more than eight hours on Friday, after he was subpoenaed by the committee last month.\par \par \uc2 Jamie Raskin, who will co-lead today\u8217\'20\'19s hearing with \uc2 Stephanie Murphy\uc2 , said Cipollone corroborated key elements of the testimony already heard by the committee. That includes the testimony from Cassidy Hutchinson, a former aide to Trump\u8217\'20\'19s White House chief of staff, \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CCipollone has corroborated almost everything that we\u8217\'20\'19ve learned from the prior hearings,\u8221\'20\'1D Raskin {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/congress/cipollone-corroborated-virtually-everything-hutchinson-jan-6-panel-mem-rcna37742"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told NBC News today}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CI certainly did not hear him contradict Cassidy Hutchinson. \u8230\'20\'26 He had the opportunity to say whatever he wanted to say, so I didn\u8217\'20\'19t see any contradiction there.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Hutchinson\u8217\'20\'19s explosive testimony at a committee hearing last month included detailed descriptions of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s outrage on January 6 and in the weeks leading up to the Capitol attack, as he peddled lies about widespread fraud in the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 According to Hutchinson, Trump was informed that some of his supporters were carrying weapons on January 6 and still told them to march to the Capitol, as lawmakers met to certify \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s victory in the election.\par \par \uc2 Hutchinson said that Trump planned to go to the Capitol with his supporters and tried to grab for the steering wheel of his car when his team told him that he would instead return to the White House after his speech on January 6.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/28/january-6-committee-session-new-evidence"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ex-White House aide delivers explosive public testimony to January 6 panel}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.27pm BST\par \par \uc2 An aide to the January 6 committee said the members would focus on a meeting held on 18 December 2020, with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and members of his legal team, including \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  and \uc2 Sidney Powell\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At that point, there was a growing schism within Trump\u8217\'20\'19s inner circle between those who believed it was time for the president to accept his electoral defeat and those who pushed even more radical actions such as seizing voting machines or appointing a special counsel to investigate the election.\par \par \uc2 Hours after the meeting, Trump sent a tweet that Murphy perceived as a \u8220\'20\'1Csiren call\u8221\'20\'1D to militia groups that 6 January 2021 would be the \u8220\'20\'1Clast stand\u8221\'20\'1D in a sprawling effort to overturn the results of an election he lost.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBig protest in DC on January 6th,\u8221\'20\'1D Trump wrote in that December tweet. \u8220\'20\'1CBe there, will be wild!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The tweet was a \u8220\'20\'1Cpivotal moment that spurred a change of events including a pre-planning by the Proud Boys\u8221\'20\'1D, the aide said.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jul/11/jan-6-hearings-far-right-groups-capitol-attack"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Capitol attack panel to examine role of far-right groups in January 6 violence}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.15pm BST\par \par \uc2 January 6 hearing to examine Trump's potential ties to extremist groups\par \par \uc2 Greetings from Washington, live blog readers.\par \par \uc2 The House select committee investigating the January 6 attack on the Capitol will hold its next public hearing this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 The panel will examine \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s potential links to far-right extremist groups like the Proud Boys and the Oath Keepers, whose members participated in the January 6 insurrection.\par \par \uc2 Committee members have said the hearing will particularly focus on Trump\u8217\'20\'19s 19 December tweet urging his supporters to come to Washington for a \u8220\'20\'1Cwild\u8221\'20\'1D event on 6 January, the day that Congress was scheduled to certify \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s victory in the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 Committee member \uc2 Stephanie Murphy\uc2 , who will lead today\u8217\'20\'19s hearing alongside Jamie Raskin, said Sunday that Trump\u8217\'20\'19s tweet served as a \u8220\'20\'1Csiren call\u8221\'20\'1D to far-right extremists.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople will hear the story of that tweet and then the explosive effects it had in Trump world and specifically among the domestic violence extremist groups, the most dangerous political extremists in the country at that point,\u8221\'20\'1D Raskin said on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 The hearing will get under way at 1pm ET, so stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what else is happening today:\par \par \uc2 * The Senate judiciary committee is holding a hearing on the end of Roe. The lieutenant governor of Illinois, Juliana Stratton, will testify alongside four other witnesses.\par \par \uc2 * Biden is meeting with the Mexican president, \uc2 Andr\u233\'00\'E9s Manuel L\u243\'00\'F3pez Obrador\uc2 . The two leaders will discuss \u8220\'20\'1Ctheir visions for North America and their efforts to address global challenges such as food security, continued cooperation on migration, and joint development efforts\u8221\'20\'1D, per the White House.\par \par \uc2 * The White House will host the Congressional Picnic this afternoon. After the picnic, Biden will fly from Washington to Jerusalem.\par \par \uc2 The blog will have more updates and analysis coming up.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.00pm BST\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220712ei7c0025u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
