\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Thank the lord, I have been relieved\u8217\'20\'19: the truth about the history of  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  in America\b0\par\par\uc2 Tamara Dean \par \uc2 3,950 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 July 2022\par 13:09 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the 19th-century US was widely accepted as a means of avoiding the risks of pregnancy. The idea of banning or punishing it came later\par \par \uc2 At our rural county\u8217\'20\'19s historical society, the past lives loosely in bulletins, news clippings, maps and handwritten index cards. It\u8217\'20\'19s pieced together by pale, grey-haired women who sit at oak tables and pore over old photos. Western sun filters in, half-lighting the women as they name who\u8217\'20\'19s pictured, who has passed on. Other volunteers gossip and cut obituaries from local newspapers.\par \par \uc2 I was sent here by hearsay. For years, my neighbour has claimed that the old cemetery in the low-lying field on my Wisconsin property contains more bodies than the scant number of tombstones indicates. The epic flood of 1978 washed away the markers of the nameless \u8211\'20\'13 civil war soldiers, he says. I want to know who the dead were in life. After many walks through the cemetery, I\u8217\'20\'19m familiar with the markers that remain. One narrow footstone reads simply: \u8220\'20\'1CMAS\u8221\'20\'1D. Three marble headstones rest at odd angles among the box elder trees. Stained, eroded and lichen-crusted, the stones belong to a boy and two baby girls who died in the 1850s and 60s. On the boy\u8217\'20\'19s is a relief of a weeping willow; on the sisters\u8217\'20\'19 are rosebuds. Signs of young lives cut short.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m sitting at one of the oak tables when Carol, the historical society\u8217\'20\'19s assistant curator, hands me a binder of cemetery records. A stranger has just sat beside me, her husband opposite us. I study the list of those buried on my land. I recognise the children\u8217\'20\'19s names. I don\u8217\'20\'19t see any men\u8217\'20\'19s names. But there\u8217\'20\'19s the name of a woman I\u8217\'20\'19ve never heard of. I read it aloud: \u8220\'20\'1CNancy Ann Harris.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The stranger says: \u8220\'20\'1CShe was married to Benjamin Franklin Harris, who\u8217\'20\'19s my husband\u8217\'20\'19s great-great-great uncle.\u8221\'20\'1D She nods to her husband, who nods in confirmation.\par \par \uc2 Astonished, I turn to face her. \u8220\'20\'1CHow do you know that?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CShe died of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D the stranger adds. \u8220\'20\'1CApparently, she\u8217\'20\'19d had a lot of them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHow do you know that??\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHer death record.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhy would they put that in the public record?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Carol, standing nearby, says: \u8220\'20\'1CIt was a different time.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 I want to ask her what she means, but another woman, who has been eavesdropping, leans toward me and frowns. \u8220\'20\'1CThat Nancy must have been something else,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m startled by her dismissive tone, her vague implication. I don\u8217\'20\'19t think quickly enough to challenge her. Instead, the rest of us \u8211\'20\'13 Carol and I, and the stranger and her husband, whose great-great-great-aunt lies in my field \u8211\'20\'13 turn silent. I look down again at the five names of the buried. Long after I leave the table, I keep wishing I\u8217\'20\'19d spoken up.\par \par \uc2 * * *\par \par \uc2 On my land, the past exerts itself. I slop through the channels left by long-dead farmers. I eat apples from trees planted by settlers and groundnuts cultivated by Native Americans. I laze in the shade of oaks left unlogged for that purpose, and let a field that was burned intentionally for centuries burn its way back into prairie again. I\u8217\'20\'19ve wondered about those who ploughed, planted trees and set fires. Now Nancy reaches forward and casts me to conjure her.\par \par \uc2 The life of her husband, Benjamin Franklin \u8220\'20\'1CFrank\u8221\'20\'1D Harris, is well documented. He was a civil war soldier who belonged to a famous brigade. But my research yields no clues about Nancy\u8217\'20\'19s origins or biography.\par \par \uc2 On another visit to the historical society, I ask Carol: \u8220\'20\'1CDo you have any files about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in history? Or women\u8217\'20\'19s reproductive health in the 1800s?\u8221\'20\'1D No and no. In our rural county, women\u8217\'20\'19s history is depicted in sepia-toned images of farm wives, teacher-training school graduates and early suffragists. Private pains and joys remain private, lost to us.\par \par \uc2 Across town, I stop at the office of the Register of Deeds to see Nancy\u8217\'20\'19s death record. The registrar, a cheerful, talkative woman, shows me just-snapped photos of her granddaughter on her smartphone. She asks me to sign in and state my purpose before she\u8217\'20\'19ll allow me into the records room. I hesitate.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CGenealogical research?\u8221\'20\'1D she prompts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYes,\u8221\'20\'1D I say. Let Nancy Ann Harris be family to me.\par \par \uc2 * * *\par \par \uc2 Nancy\u8217\'20\'19s death, on 16 December 1876, at the age of 35, is among the first to be recorded in the yellowed, leather-bound volumes. The cause, \u8220\'20\'1Cpuerperal peritonitis\u8221\'20\'1D, is annotated by a triple-dagger symbol that directs the reader to a line of script rising up the margin like a pointed finger: \u8220\'20\'1CDisease caused by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 3 months this was perhaps the 10th \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  all except this followed by smart hemorrhage.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s the longest description of a death in all of those volumes. Maybe Dr RH Delap, who signed her death certificate, instructed the county clerk to copy his notes without omission. Or maybe the clerk felt he couldn\u8217\'20\'19t abbreviate the doctor\u8217\'20\'19s notes in this case. Only one other record mentions \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But on every page of those early volumes, women aged 19 to 47 are listed as dying from puerperal peritonitis, puerperal fever, puerperal septicemia, \u8220\'20\'1Cinflammation after confinement\u8221\'20\'1D, eclampsia and \u8220\'20\'1Cgiving birth to a child\u8221\'20\'1D. I find more dead fertile women than old men.\par \par \uc2 In Nancy\u8217\'20\'19s time, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a common means of birth control, a way for women of every race and social class to limit family size, manage resources and protect their health. The procedure was often a safer alternative to childbirth, which some women considered \u8220\'20\'1Cpathological and frightening\u8221\'20\'1D, and many others approached with foreboding, according to James Mohr\u8217\'20\'19s 1978 book, \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in America. Every woman, including Nancy, would have known friends, sisters or cousins who died or were debilitated while giving birth. They would have known those who took pains to avoid it.\par \par \uc2 In the 1700s and early 1800s, conception was considered a disturbance of a woman\u8217\'20\'19s natural balance. Methods of \u8220\'20\'1Cremoving a blockage\u8221\'20\'1D or \u8220\'20\'1Crestoring the menses\u8221\'20\'1D were sometimes necessary to reestablish the body\u8217\'20\'19s balance, even if they induced a miscarriage. Abortions that occurred before quickening, which was understood as the time when a pregnant woman could feel the foetus move (usually around the fourth month), were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.plannedparenthoodaction.org/issues/abortion/abortion-central-history-reproductive-health-care-america"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 legally and morally acceptable}}}\uc2 . Quickening was an indicator based on women\u8217\'20\'19s perceptions, not a medical diagnosis. But in the absence of a scientific pregnancy test, it was recognised as the threshold of human development. Before quickening, no one, not even the Catholic church, believed that a human life existed, notes Leslie J Reagan in her 1996 book, When \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Was a Crime.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was so frequent, according to one doctor, that \u8220\'20\'1Cit [was] rare to find a married woman who passes through the childbearing period who has not had one or more\u8221\'20\'1D. Women spoke of it casually. They might decide to be \u8220\'20\'1Cput straight\u8221\'20\'1D, \u8220\'20\'1Copened up\u8221\'20\'1D, or \u8220\'20\'1Cfixed\u8221\'20\'1D. (They wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have said \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D, as that term belonged to the medical lexicon, not the vocabularies of ordinary people.) One physician reported that women \u8220\'20\'1Ctalk about such matters commonly and impart information unsparingly\u8221\'20\'1D. Although some doctors would perform the procedure, many women, especially in rural areas, induced abortions on their own using drugs or herbs, and wisdom passed from one generation to another.\par \par \uc2 When Nancy was younger, she might have said to a favourite aunt, or even Amanda Martin, who had already buried two daughters in my field, \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve missed twice and need to bring my course on.\u8221\'20\'1D The older women would have understood. After all, they had probably done the same. A 19th-century euphemism for missing a menstrual period was \u8220\'20\'1Ctaking the cold\u8221\'20\'1D, implying that pregnancy was a common ailment that came and went. Once asked, they would have helped Nancy.\par \par \uc2 Years later, she might have felt confident in \u8220\'20\'1Cremoving female irregularity\u8221\'20\'1D on her own. I picture her lanky, dark-haired, with deep-set eyes, my portrait patterned after photos of settlers who lived on tracts around mine and whose descendants are my neighbours. I picture her wise. Strong-willed. If she found an hour\u8217\'20\'19s respite from chores at her cabin less than a mile from my place, where a tavern now stands, she might have asked her older stepdaughters to take care of her three younger children. She might have left the cabin, walked north over marshy ground, and found what she needed in the hills behind my place: juniper, bloodroot, mayapple, Queen Anne\u8217\'20\'19s lace seeds (in large quantities, taken early enough), or black cohosh, a plant Native American women relied on.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s also possible that Nancy sent away for a cure. In the 19th century, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  became commercialised. Ingredients from herbal folk remedies (aloe, cotton root, tansy, black hellebore, ergot of rye, pennyroyal, motherwort, slippery elm, rue) were made into pills or tonics, which were mass-produced and advertised widely in popular newspapers and magazines. Women could buy them through the mail or from local druggists. Even in rural Wisconsin, Nancy would have seen them advertised. By 1840, sales of abortifacients had surged, and profiteers who publicly promoted services to induce miscarriages flourished. One doctor called it \u8220\'20\'1Ca regularly established moneymaking trade\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Experienced women were savvy about controlling their reproduction. They had advocated for women\u8217\'20\'19s education, made themselves literate and sought out materials that described feminine organs and ailments, however sketchy that information sometimes was. It\u8217\'20\'19s possible that Nancy tucked The Married Woman\u8217\'20\'19s Private Medical Companion, published in 1847, under a tablecloth in the storage chest. She might have noted the causes of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  listed in the still-popular 18th-century text Domestic Medicine, or, A Treatise on the Prevention and Cure of Diseases By Regimen and Simple Medicines, which included violent exercise, raising great weights, jumping from a great height, punching the belly and bloodletting. Maybe, fearful and determined in her first attempt, she opted for violent exercise \u8211\'20\'13 perhaps sprinting up the same road I jog down \u8211\'20\'13 before trying something else.\par \par \uc2 According to Reagan, one woman in 1918, after her menses were restored, said to her nurse: \u8220\'20\'1CThank the Lord, I have been relieved.\u8221\'20\'1D This is how I envision Nancy on the day after each of her 10 or so abortions \u8211\'20\'13 relieved, if tired, and hopeful about her future. Maybe she took a day to rest in bed, asked her son to stoke the fire and one of her stepdaughters to make supper. She might have initially held off her husband, Frank, but then drew him closer, confident that she could deal with any consequences.\par \par \uc2 * * *\par \par \uc2 Just when Nancy entered married life and was bearing children or choosing not to, the nation\u8217\'20\'19s policies on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were changing. Mail order pills and tinctures were unregulated and sometimes lethal. In the 1820s and 30s, the risk of poisoning led to the first laws affecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which penalised those who supplied abortifacients rather than the women who bought or used them, as long as the women did so before quickening. These restrictions didn\u8217\'20\'19t stop women like Nancy from gathering herbs or sharing home remedies, nor did they eliminate quickening as a legal marker of a viable life.\par \par \uc2 Punishment for terminating a pregnancy at any stage would come only decades later. In 1857, the recently established \uc2 American Medical Association\uc2  (AMA) initiated a campaign, led by Dr Horatio R Storer, to end \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The organisation\u8217\'20\'19s reasons were many, but women\u8217\'20\'19s health was not chief among them. Reagan writes that the AMA and its members were attempting to \u8220\'20\'1Cwin professional power, control medical practice, and restrict their competitors, particularly homeopaths and midwives\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Class, racial and gender anxieties were factored into the doctors\u8217\'20\'19 fear of losing power. As immigrants poured into the US, the birthrate for white, Protestant, middle-class married women plummeted to historic lows that couldn\u8217\'20\'19t be attributed to miscarriage, celibacy or barrenness. Improved contraception was part of the reason, but so was an increase in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate. Storer and his peers became preoccupied with the latter \u8211\'20\'13 and for reasons that had less to do with protecting the right to life than with maintaining the prevailing social hierarchy. As Reagan writes, \u8220\'20\'1CAnti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists pointed out that immigrant families, many of them Catholic, were larger and would soon outpopulate native-born white \uc2 Yankees\uc2  and threaten their political power.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 At the close of the civil war, as the nation was tallying its dead and claiming more land for European-American settlement, Storer wrote a book called Why Not? to persuade white, middle-class women to choose childbearing over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He asked whether the regions west and south of New England should be \u8220\'20\'1Cfilled by our own children or by those of aliens?\u8221\'20\'1D He admonished his female readers that \u8220\'20\'1Cupon their loins depends the future destiny of the nation\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, Storer and his male colleagues were heralding medical professionalism, a quality that they felt set them apart from quacks. The AMA anointed men (and only men) who had medical education and practised orthodox medicine as \u8220\'20\'1Cregular\u8221\'20\'1D doctors, as opposed to \u8220\'20\'1Cirregular\u8221\'20\'1D homeopaths and midwives. (The organisation didn\u8217\'20\'19t admit its first female physician until 1876, the year Nancy died.) Regular doctors took the Hippocratic oath and vowed not to induce miscarriages. In the 1870s, when Nancy was dying of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at three months, AMA-affiliated physicians, who often doubled as politicians, were zealously promoting and pushing new anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s likely that the doctor who signed Nancy\u8217\'20\'19s death certificate considered himself a regular. Delap had taken medical courses and, for a time, led his county\u8217\'20\'19s chapter of the \uc2 Wisconsin Medical Society\uc2 . Like many regular doctors, he also served in the state legislature. Maybe, along with his peers, he lamented how common \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was. How churches and the press refused to take a stand against it despite being pressured to do so. How women\u8217\'20\'19s education and increasingly public information about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had caused a sharp decline in the nation\u8217\'20\'19s birthrate. And how women of his station weren\u8217\'20\'19t reproducing as quickly as immigrant Catholic women.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhere one living child is born into the world,\u8221\'20\'1D states an 1879 report by the \uc2 Wisconsin Medical Society\uc2 , \u8220\'20\'1Ctwo are done away with by means of criminal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D This was probably an exaggeration. Surveys from around the nation suggested that about one-third of pregnancies during this period were terminated intentionally. But if women kept it to themselves, who knew for sure? After all, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a way to limit fertility privately. It was, writes Mohr in \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in America, \u8220\'20\'1Ca uniquely female practice, which men could neither control nor prevent\u8221\'20\'1D. Some men called it \u8220\'20\'1Caggressively self-indulgent\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 As women sought to advance their status, regular doctors and politicians attacked \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as \u8220\'20\'1Cimmoral, unwomanly, and unpatriotic\u8221\'20\'1D, writes Reagan. They typically refused to provide information about it or other birth-control methods. For regular physicians and legislators, denying women\u8217\'20\'19s right to continue practising reproductive autonomy was an existential battle. If they couldn\u8217\'20\'19t control white, Protestant, middle-class women, they would be outnumbered in the New World.\par \par \uc2 * * *\par \par \uc2 Nancy Ann Harris died on the 10th anniversary of her marriage to Frank. I\u8217\'20\'19d like to think Frank was out of his mind with grief. When Delap arrived from the village three miles east to record Nancy\u8217\'20\'19s death, the men probably said little. Yet I wonder how Delap knew of Nancy\u8217\'20\'19s previous abortions. Maybe she didn\u8217\'20\'19t conceal them from Frank, and in his grief and confusion, he told Delap.\par \par \uc2 Then again, it\u8217\'20\'19s possible that Delap was the sort of country doctor who would, at the behest of favoured clients, induce miscarriages. One regular from Milwaukee reported on such a rural colleague in a letter to the Wisconsin Medical Journal. The country doctor had confided that \u8220\'20\'1Cwe all do that kind of work when it is in a nice family and a girl has to be protected\u8221\'20\'1D. It wasn\u8217\'20\'19t unusual for regular doctors to decry contraception and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the public even as they provided the services covertly, at their discretion. If Delap had helped Nancy, maybe he felt compelled to represent the AMA\u8217\'20\'19s stance in the footnote to her death.\par \par \uc2 Nancy\u8217\'20\'19s death record is also stored in the state historical society\u8217\'20\'19s digital archives, but she doesn\u8217\'20\'19t appear in a search for \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D. Only 12 hits are returned for that term. Most link to photographs from pro-choice marches in the 1970s and 90s. None mention Dr William H Brisbane, a scowling, heavy-browed Baptist preacher who migrated north from South Carolina and founded a village 60 miles east of where Nancy is buried. Like Frank and Delap, Brisbane was a civil war veteran and is lauded elsewhere in the state archives for his outspoken opposition to slavery. He was also a doctor, politician and anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  crusader. Years before Nancy married Frank, Brisbane wrote and lobbied for a bill to make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a crime in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 In 1858, Wisconsin passed Brisbane\u8217\'20\'19s bill and became the third state to impose criminal sanctions on women who underwent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before quickening. The sentence was up to three months in jail and a $300 fine (about $10,000 today). Neither the public nor the legislature had asked for the bill.\par \par \uc2 Brisbane acknowledged that his law was unlikely to be enforced \u8211\'20\'13 and for a while, it wasn\u8217\'20\'19t. Still, as he wrote to an AMA colleague, even if making \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a crime didn\u8217\'20\'19t result in convictions, it would surely \u8220\'20\'1Chave a moral influence to prevent it to some extent\u8221\'20\'1D. But it didn\u8217\'20\'19t. Virtually no one outside the medical establishment \u8211\'20\'13 certainly not Nancy, her sisters, cousins or friends \u8211\'20\'13 thought of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a crime. No 19th-century American woman went to jail for having one. After all, the only way authorities could be certain that a woman had had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was if she died as a result.\par \par \uc2 Even so, AMA physicians and politicians kept pushing. At the federal level, the 1873 Comstock Act prohibited, among other things, advertisements for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services and abortifacients. This resulted in an embargo on publicly printed information related to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and a growing perception of the practice as immoral. By 1880, every state had {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.plannedparenthoodaction.org/issues/abortion/abortion-central-history-reproductive-health-care-america/historical-abortion-law-timeline-1850-today"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passed laws}}}\uc2  to prohibit terminating a pregnancy intentionally at any stage. Nonetheless, women kept seeking abortions in large numbers. In Woman\u8217\'20\'19s Body, Woman\u8217\'20\'19s Right (1976), Linda Gordon cites a judge\u8217\'20\'19s estimate that, in the state of New York alone, 100,000 abortions were performed annually throughout the 1890s. Soon after the turn of the century, other contraceptive practices became more widely publicised and available, with birth-control clinics opening in the 1920s and 30s. The US\u8217\'20\'19s birthrate continued to drop until after the second world war, when a resurgence in nationalism and a return to traditional gender roles produced a short-lived domestic revival and a baby boom.\par \par \uc2 * * *\par \par \uc2 On the Sunday morning after Nancy\u8217\'20\'19s death, Frank would have left the tasks of bathing and dressing her to a female relative or friend. But he probably lifted her body into the coffin himself. Say the day was sunny, the ground lightly covered in snow when he hitched the horses. His two little girls rode with him in the wagon while his older daughters and son walked behind. They ferried the casket to the cemetery by the same route that Nancy might have taken to collect herbs. A bonfire had softened the newly frozen ground. A hole had been dug. A headstone was waiting. I would like to believe that Frank spent $5 he couldn\u8217\'20\'19t really spare to buy the headstone and have it engraved with Nancy\u8217\'20\'19s name, birth and death dates, plus a tree of many branches to symbolise the reach of her life through eternity.\par \par \uc2 Soon after Nancy\u8217\'20\'19s death, Frank, who had moved to Wisconsin from Ohio, moved farther west, to Nebraska. Abandoning the home that he and Nancy had made together might have been a measure of his sorrow. He, the civil war veteran, didn\u8217\'20\'19t die in my township. If any tombstones from my cemetery washed downstream in the flood of 1978, one was more likely Nancy\u8217\'20\'19s than a soldier\u8217\'20\'19s.\par \par \uc2 On the day I read aloud \u8220\'20\'1CNancy Ann Harris\u8221\'20\'1D at our local historical society, I also learned of the fifth person listed as buried in the cemetery in my field: Florence Palmer. Nancy lived just south of my property; Florence lived just north. Palmer was Nancy\u8217\'20\'19s original name. When Nancy died, Florence was 10 \u8211\'20\'13 the right age to be Nancy\u8217\'20\'19s niece by marriage. I wonder what the adults told her about Nancy\u8217\'20\'19s death.\par \par \uc2 By the time Florence died in 1892, at age 26, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws that had swept the nation were finally being enforced. Midwives and irregular doctors who induced miscarriages were prosecuted. Inquests after women\u8217\'20\'19s deaths from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were exposing them. Women\u8217\'20\'19s conversations went deeper underground. Churches had joined the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement. Prominent feminists joined it, too, because they believed that those who sought abortions had been victimised by men, and they felt women shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be forced into sex with its dangerous consequences. Nancy\u8217\'20\'19s generation would have known far more about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  than women in the three generations that followed. Perhaps they talked about it even more freely than women do today.\par \par \uc2 Florence\u8217\'20\'19s death record lists the same cause as Nancy\u8217\'20\'19s: puerperal peritonitis. But no triple-daggered footnote indicates the circumstance. Given that Florence already had four children and three stepchildren, it wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t surprise me if she died from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related complications. In rural Wisconsin in 1892, access to reliable information about \u8220\'20\'1Crestoring the menses\u8221\'20\'1D was scarce. Regular doctors wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t provide it, obscenity laws prevented its public dissemination, and the experienced women of the community who might have counselled Florence had taken their wisdom to the grave.\par \par \uc2 * * *\par \par \uc2 As I walk through the cemetery in summer, my boots make impressions in the soil. The ground is lumpy, damp and thick with daylilies that never bloom because of the shade. I wonder how many bones are buried beneath me. I wonder where Nancy lies. And I wonder what she\u8217\'20\'19d think of my portrayal of her, this marker I\u8217\'20\'19ve created in place of a headstone.\par \par \uc2 I remember the eavesdropping woman\u8217\'20\'19s comment: \u8220\'20\'1CThat Nancy must have been something else.\u8221\'20\'1D If we crossed paths at the historical society now, after all I\u8217\'20\'19ve learned, I wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t hesitate to speak up. I\u8217\'20\'19d point out that her judgment is rooted in 21st-century misapprehensions. Nancy\u8217\'20\'19s family and neighbours must have found her death tragic. The women who stood by as her casket was lowered might have thought, Poor dear. Maybe even, It could have been any of us. But they wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have thought the reason for her death shameful, any more than modern women would disparage a friend for being on the pill. Nancy Ann Harris wasn\u8217\'20\'19t \u8220\'20\'1Csomething else\u8221\'20\'1D. Not for her time. Although the doctor\u8217\'20\'19s footnote to her death was exceptional, she was simply an ordinary woman, if perhaps more forthcoming than the other women whose deaths he\u8217\'20\'19d attended.\par \par \uc2 This article originally appeared in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://theamericanscholar.org/safer-than-childbirth/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The American Scholar}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 Follow the Long Read on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/@gdnlongread"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @gdnlongread}}}\uc2 , listen to our podcasts {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/news/series/the-long-read"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  and sign up to the long read weekly email {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/info/ng-interactive/2017/may/05/sign-up-for-the-long-read-email"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220712ei7c001e1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
