\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 US employers\u8217\'20\'19 support for workers\u8217\'20\'19  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  care leaves serious gaps\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 970 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 July 2022\par 09:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Millions of Americans rely on their employers for health insurance and while some firms have stepped up, others have not, with those on low incomes most likely to suffer\par \par \uc2 As America faces the prospect of losing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in several dozen states some major companies have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/26/us-companies-pay-travel-costs-for-employees-seeking-abortions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 publicly announced}}}\uc2  plans to cover expenses for employees to get abortions in other states.\par \par \uc2 But many other US employers have not made such offers and workers and unions are also pointing to serious gaps in those that have made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services commitments, saying they will not cover all workers. This means that the impact of the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  decision to overturn Roe v Wade and end federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights will probably be even more pronounced.\par \par \uc2 In the US more than {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://hbr.org/2021/05/are-employer-sponsored-health-plans-on-their-way-out#:~:text=A%20lot%20has%20changed%20in,covered%20through%20employer%2Dsponsored%20programs."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  156 million Americans}}}\uc2  rely on health insurance coverage sponsored by their employers. Among the corporations to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/article/abortion-companies-travel-expenses.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announce }}}\uc2  help with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  service for employees are \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 , \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 , \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2 , Chobani, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , \uc2 Yelp\uc2 , Levi\u8217\'20\'19s, \uc2 Disney\uc2 , \uc2 Macy\u8217\'20\'19s\uc2 , \uc2 H&M\uc2 , \uc2 Nordstrom\uc2 , Nike, \uc2 Dick\u8217\'20\'19s Sporting Goods\uc2 , Reddit, \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , Uber, \uc2 Google\uc2 , \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 Apple\uc2  and others.\par \par \uc2 But there are often serious limitations in these benefits, such as \uc2 Starbucks{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.seattletimes.com/subscribe/signup-offers/?pw=redirect&subsource=paywall&return=https://www.seattletimes.com/business/starbucks/starbucks-says-it-will-cover-abortion-travel-and-gender-affirming-care/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 suggesting }}}\uc2  they will not provide this benefit to unionized employees and only for employees who can afford or are eligible to be enrolled in their healthcare plan. Many of the corporations to announce these benefits have also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://popular.info/p/the-hypocrisy-of-abortion-as-a-corporate?r=4v4dm&s=r&utm_campaign=post&utm_medium=email"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 donated }}}\uc2  to Republican elected officials or groups who are legislating \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Some of the largest employers in the US, particularly ones that pay low wages, including fast-food corporations such as McDonald\u8217\'20\'19s, and the largest employer in the US, \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , have not announced these benefits. The majority of US employers, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2022-06-24/jpmorgan-cvs-and-other-employers-will-cover-abortions-after-roe-overturned"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 60% }}}\uc2  according to a May 2022 survey conducted by the consultancy group \uc2 Gartner\uc2 , do not plan to make any changes in the wake of the Dobbs decision.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is not a direct solution to having these rights being codified at the state or federal level. At the end of the day, someone needing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should not have to go to their employer, who they just have to hope is well-meaning, and it\u8217\'20\'19s very likely that workers in low-wage industries with few workplace protections or benefits, like paid leave, will not have access to this new benefit,\u8221\'20\'1D said Asha Banerjee, an economic policy analyst at the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Banerjee also noted the concern of corporations using the benefit as leverage against unionization or worker organizing, as workers at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.yahoo.com/lifestyle/union-workers-claim-starbucks-using-135600547.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 alleged}}}\uc2 , and emphasized that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights need to be acknowledged as an economic issue rather than a cultural one, a narrative {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2022/05/09/abortion-campaign-republicans/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 elected officials}}}\uc2  have tried to argue.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAccess to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is so interconnected with these larger economic issues of financial security, independence, upward mobility, and this will affect millions, not just women and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients but their households, their families, their other children,\u8221\'20\'1D added Banerjee. \u8220\'20\'1CYou\u8217\'20\'19re absolutely going to disproportionately see low- and middle-income and Black, Hispanic and other women of color hit the hardest by this decision because many of these women are already the most economically vulnerable.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Low-wage workers are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/blog/two-thirds-of-low-wage-workers-still-lack-access-to-paid-sick-days-during-an-ongoing-pandemic/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most likely}}}\uc2  to not have any paid time off that would be required to travel to obtain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care and the 26 states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned or likely to be banned, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/blog/abortion-rights/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 average minimum wage}}}\uc2  is $8.39 an hour compared with $11.48 an hour in the states not banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 States where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned or likely to be banned also have much higher incarceration rates, are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/blog/abortion-rights/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 less likely}}}\uc2  to have expanded Medicaid, have fewer worker protections and typically have severely underfunded family social public service programs.\par \par \uc2 A 2020 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://michiganross.umich.edu/news/women-denied-abortion-may-face-years-financial-difficulties-new-study-finds"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 study }}}\uc2  by researchers at the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2 , UCLA and \uc2 University of California San Francisco\uc2 , demonstrated women who are denied \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access tend to experience large and persistent financial distress, including higher amounts of debt, a greater likelihood of a low credit score, and an 81% increase in negative public records such as bankruptcy filings, evictions and tax liens.\par \par \uc2 Sarah Miller, the lead author of the study and assistant professor of business economics and public policy at the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2 , explained the researchers followed about 1,000 women at 30 clinics in 21 states, comparing 10 years of credit report information on women who were able to receive abortions and those who were denied abortions.\par \par \uc2 Before the pregnancy, both groups of women had similar financial trends, but afterward there was a drastic spike in financial problems for those women who were denied abortions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt stayed elevated over the six years that we could observe,\u8221\'20\'1D said Miller. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s going to probably be more disadvantaged women that really can\u8217\'20\'19t make it work. They can\u8217\'20\'19t hop on a plane to California, or they don\u8217\'20\'19t work at a white-collar, professional job where their employer is going to pay for them to fly to another state to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Even when Roe v Wade was in effect there were a lot of women that already had trouble accessing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I think that that group is just going to grow.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Before Roe v Wade was overturned, the majority of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients in the US, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/infographic/2016/us-abortion-patients"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 75% }}}\uc2  according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , were poor or low-income.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe economic arguments are about millions of women and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients losing their autonomy, dignity and right to make their own economic decisions, their own choices in the economy, about whether and when to have children,\u8221\'20\'1D said Banerjee. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is about power and control and subjugation. I think policymakers and advocates need to see it for what it is in order to advocate, strategize and organize together to win these rights back.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220712ei7c0015t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
