\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Biden calls again for US assault rifles ban: \u8216\'20\'18We are living in a country awash in weapons of war\u8217\'20\'19 \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Joanna Walters \par \uc2 7,220 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 July 2022\par 20:16 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Biden speaks at event to mark passing of Safer Community ActJanuary 6 panel to focus on Trump\u8217\'20\'19s tweet at extremist group hearingSign up to receive First Thing \u8211\'20\'13 our daily briefing by email\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.06pm BST\par \par \uc2 Closing summary\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re closing the US politics live blog for today, but we\u8217\'20\'19ll be back tomorrow and will bring you all the most important news as it happens, including covering the next public hearing of the House committee investigating the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/us-capitol-breach"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US Capitol attack}}}\uc2  on 6 January 2021.\par \par \uc2 For those wishing to follow the news relating to Russia\u8217\'20\'19s invasion of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/ukraine"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ukraine}}}\uc2  , our blog on the war can be found {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2022/jul/11/russia-ukraine-war-live-news-dozens-remain-trapped-after-russian-strike-zelenskiy-says-donetsk-attack-purposely-targeted-civilians"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s where things stand:\par \par \uc2 * A federal judge{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/jul/11/majority-us-democrats-dont-want-biden-2024-house-january-6-capitol-attack-latest-updates?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-62cc78de8f08038ec3b7f993#block-62cc78de8f08038ec3b7f993"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has declined}}}\uc2  to delay the upcoming trial of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jul/10/steve-bannon-discussions-january-6-committee-capitol-attack"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Steve Bannon}}}\uc2 , an adviser to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who faces contempt charges after refusing to cooperate with the House committee investigating the January 6 insurrection.\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is once again appealing to Congress to go further with gun safety legislation and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/jul/11/majority-us-democrats-dont-want-biden-2024-house-january-6-capitol-attack-latest-updates?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-62cc4aae8f08038ec3b7f79a#block-62cc4aae8f08038ec3b7f79a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ban assault weapons}}}\uc2  for the general public in the US. At an event at the White House he said: \u8220\'20\'1CWe are living in a country awash in weapons of war.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 * The US president{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/jul/11/majority-us-democrats-dont-want-biden-2024-house-january-6-capitol-attack-latest-updates?page=with:block-62cc403e8f08038ec3b7f6f3#block-62cc403e8f08038ec3b7f6f3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hailed}}}\uc2  the long-awaited gun safety legislation recently passed with bipartisan support but also said that the act was a call to action to do more, saying the slowness of the government to act had left \u8220\'20\'1Ctoo much of a trail of bloodshed and carnage\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 * A strong majority of Democratic voters - 64% - want the party to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/jul/11/majority-us-democrats-dont-want-biden-2024-house-january-6-capitol-attack-latest-updates?page=with:block-62cc27868f08a131cadc3d38#block-62cc27868f08a131cadc3d38"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nominate someone other than Joe Biden}}}\uc2  to stand for president in the 2024 election, according to a new opinion poll by the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  and Siena College. The survey added, however, that as a result of questioning a sample of people across the US they found that in another match-up between Biden and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Biden would once again triumph.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.16pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.54pm BST\par \par \uc2 Cristina Tzintz\u250\'00\'FAn Ramirez, the president of NextGen America, traveled from Texas to participate in the group\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights protest outside the supreme court today.\par \par \uc2 Ramirez noted that her mother once obtained an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because her doctor warned of severe health risks if she carried her pregnancy to term.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI am so grateful that she was able to have that because I would have likely grown up without a mother,\u8221\'20\'1D Ramirez said.\par \par \uc2 Gesturing toward the flowers representing the lives that will be lost because of the Roe reversal, Ramirez added, \u8220\'20\'1CToday my mom might be one of these flowers and one of these lives that will be lost in the coming years.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 enltrNextGen President {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/cristinanextgen?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @cristinanextgen}}}\uc2  noted carrying a pregnancy to term is 33 times more dangerous than getting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 She mentions that her mother once chose to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of serious health concerns: \u8220\'20\'1CToday my mom might be one of these flowers.\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/xfHxDFhmZT"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/xfHxDFhmZT}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Joan Greve (@joanegreve) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/joanegreve/status/1546562377730203650?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 11, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Ramirez said \uc2 NextGen\uc2  members are now organizing in battleground states like Arizona and Michigan to ensure that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters are elected to office in November.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYoung people are pissed off, and they have every right to be, and we\u8217\'20\'19re channeling their anger into power, into action,\u8221\'20\'1D Ramirez said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe need to take every single step necessary, and everything should be on the table to look at how we protect access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and also make sure that the supreme court doesn\u8217\'20\'19t take away the right to gay marriage, that it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t take away our right to contraception. Because this wasn\u8217\'20\'19t the end; this was the opening salvo.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.04pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.52pm BST\par \par \uc2 Members of the youth voter group NextGen America gathered outside the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  today to protest against the decision to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturn Roe v Wade}}}\uc2 , which ended nearly 50 years of federal protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the US.\par \par \uc2 The group laid flowers in front of the court and wore black to mourn those who are expected to lose their lives because of the end of Roe.\par \par \uc2 enltrI was at the Supreme Court this afternoon, where the youth voter group NextGen America was laying flowers in honor of the people expected to lose their lives because of the reversal of Roe. Maternal mortality is expected to increase by 20%, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/cristinanextgen?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @cristinanextgen}}}\uc2  said. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/lneytLw1UL"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/lneytLw1UL}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Joan Greve (@joanegreve) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/joanegreve/status/1546562371283570690?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 11, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Health experts {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/2022/07/05/maternal-mortality-death-abortion-ban-roe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have said}}}\uc2  they expect maternal mortality to rise in the wake of the Roe reversal, as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will probably soon be outlawed in 26 states.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NextGen\uc2  protesters carried signs criticizing the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision. One of them read, \u8220\'20\'1CPro-life is a lie. They don\u8217\'20\'19t care if people die.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 After laying the flowers, one of the protesters cried as a trumpet player played solemn music outside the court.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.03pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.34pm BST\par \par \uc2 Judge won't delay trial for Trump ex-adviser \uc2 Steve Bannon\par \par \uc2 A federal judge has declined to delay the upcoming trial of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jul/10/steve-bannon-discussions-january-6-committee-capitol-attack"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Steve Bannon}}}\uc2  , an ex-adviser to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who faces contempt charges after refusing for months to cooperate with the House committee investigating the January 6 insurrection, the Associated Press writes.\par \par \uc2 Bannon is still scheduled to go on trial next week despite telling the House committee late Saturday that he is now prepared to testify. It\u8217\'20\'19s unclear whether Bannon will again refuse to appear before the committee with the trial pending.\par \par \uc2 Former White House strategist \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  outside federal court on Nov. 15, 2021. Photograph: Alex Brandon/AP\par \par \uc2 US District Judge Carl Nichols also ruled against several requests by Bannon\u8217\'20\'19s attorneys to seek the testimony of House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  or the committee chairman, Democratic representative \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2  of Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 And Nichols barred Bannon\u8217\'20\'19s attorneys from arguing before a jury that the committee violated House rules in demanding Bannon\u8217\'20\'19s appearance, or that Bannon had defied the subpoena on the advice of his defense counsel or at Trump\u8217\'20\'19s order.\par \par \uc2 Nichols also said he could address during jury selection any concerns about pretrial publicity due to the committee\u8217\'20\'19s ongoing hearings. If it proved impossible to pick an unbiased jury, the judge said, he would reconsider a delay.\par \par \uc2 The rulings led one of Bannon\u8217\'20\'19s attorneys, David Schoen, to speak out in frustration as he sought clarification from the judge, the Associated Press reported.\par \par \uc2 What\u8217\'20\'19s the point of going to trial here if there are no defenses?\u8221\'20\'1D Schoen asked.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAgreed,\u8221\'20\'1D Nichols responded.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to reporters outside court, Schoen said he questioned whether Bannon could effectively defend himself given Nichols\u8217\'20\'19 rulings and hinted he would appeal.\par \par \uc2 He\u8217\'20\'19s the judge. That\u8217\'20\'19s why they have a court of appeals,\u8221\'20\'1D Schoen said of Nichols.\par \par \uc2 The judge said earlier in the hearing that Bannon could argue he thought the deadline to respond to the subpoena may not have been \u8220\'20\'1Coperative\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Bannon had been one of the highest-profile Trump-allied holdouts in refusing to testify before the committee, leading to two criminal counts of contempt of Congress last year for resisting the committee\u8217\'20\'19s subpoena.\par \par \uc2 A man holds a sign saying \u8216\'20\'18Indict \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 in front of Trump Tower in New York City at an \u8220\'20\'1CIndict Trump\u8221\'20\'1D rally to advocate the indictment of former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and others, on 6 July. Photograph: Michael Brochstein/SOPA Images/REX/\uc2 Shutterstock\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.02pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.09pm BST\par \par \uc2 The House select committee investigating the January 6 insurrection at the US Capitol by extremist supporters of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has, it appears, decided to reschedule what had been expected to be a primetime hearing on Thursday evening.\par \par \uc2 There had been an earlier expectation that a hearing this Thursday would be the \u8220\'20\'1Cgrand finale\u8221\'20\'1D of the committee\u8217\'20\'19s public sessions. But first it became clear that the session wasn\u8217\'20\'19t necessarily going to be the last and now it\u8217\'20\'19s reportedly been canceled/postponed to a date to be determined - without it ever being officially publicly announced, NBC and MSNBC {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.msnbc.com/chris-jansing-reports/watch/jan-6-committee-rescheduling-hearing-planned-for-thursday-143819845520"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 enltrSCOOP w/ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/haleytalbotnbc?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @haleytalbotnbc}}}\uc2  : The January 6th Committee is rescheduling its planned, but never officially announced, Thursday prime time hearing, two sources familiar tell NBC News.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Ali Vitali (@alivitali) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/alivitali/status/1546528021972230149?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 11, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Speculation suggests new evidence is expected, not least from the potential cooperation of former Trump White House counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 , who\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://edition.cnn.com/2022/07/09/politics/pat-cipollone-january-6-executive-privilege/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 been stonewalling}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow\u8217\'20\'19s hearing was originally going to be at 10am ET, but is now due to begin at 1pm ET.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.54pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.18pm BST\par \par \uc2 From the Associated Press:\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will confront a kaleidoscope of challenges when he travels to the Middle East this week, his first trip there since taking office. With the American wars in Iraq and Afghanistan in the rearview mirror, the United States is reassessing its role in the region at a time when its focus has shifted to Europe and Asia.\par \par \uc2 A look at some of the major issues that will be at play during Biden\u8217\'20\'19s travels:\par \par \uc2 ISRAELI-ARAB COOPERATION\par \par \uc2 Biden will become the first US president to travel directly from Israel, his first destination, to Saudi Arabia, his last stop before returning to Washington. The itinerary is a reflection of friendlier relationships between Israel and its Arab neighbors, a tectonic shift that is reshaping the region\u8217\'20\'19s politics.\par \par \uc2 Under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Israel normalized relations with countries such as the United Arab Emirates through the Abraham Accords. Although no one expects Israel and Saudi Arabia to announce formal diplomatic ties during Biden\u8217\'20\'19s trip, more incremental steps could be taken, such as allowing Israeli commercial flights to cross over the kingdom en route to other countries nearby.\par \par \uc2 In addition, there\u8217\'20\'19s already a surge in security cooperation being presided over by the US military\u8217\'20\'19s central command, which oversees operations in the region. John Kirby, a national security spokesman for the White House, said the nascent military partnership is intended to foster a regional air defense system that could protect against Iranian ballistic missiles and drones.\par \par \uc2 IRAN NUCLEAR DEAL\par \par \uc2 The threat of Iran is one of the primary incentives for Israel and Arab countries to work more closely together, and the issue will likely be a top focus for Biden\u8217\'20\'19s meetings. Israel views Iran as its greatest threat, and Sunni Arab countries consider Shiite Iran as a dangerous competitor for regional power.\par \par \uc2 A key question is finding the best way to prevent Iran from developing a nuclear weapon, which it\u8217\'20\'19s believed to be closer than ever to achieving. Biden wants to rejuvenate the nuclear deal that was reached by \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  in 2015 and abandoned by Trump in 2018, but negotiations appear to have stalled.\par \par \uc2 Israel, which is widely believed to be the only nuclear-armed state in the region but does not acknowledge having such weapons, was opposed to the deal. It didn\u8217\'20\'19t like that the agreement limited Iran\u8217\'20\'19s nuclear enrichment for only a set period of time, nor did it address Iran\u8217\'20\'19s ballistic missile program or other military activities in the region. Now Israel is calling for increasing sanctions to pressure Tehran into agreeing to a more sweeping accord.\par \par \uc2 Biden is expected to visit one of Israel\u8217\'20\'19s missile defense installations as he tries to reassure Israelis that the US is committed to the country\u8217\'20\'19s protection.\par \par \uc2 ISRAELI-PALESTINIAN CONFLICT\par \par \uc2 Even though Israel is building closer ties to Arab countries, there\u8217\'20\'19s been no progress toward resolving its decades-long conflict with the Palestinians.\par \par \uc2 In fact, some Palestinians feel abandoned by Arab leaders who have reached their own deals with Israel through the Abraham Accords. That came without securing progress toward the Palestinians\u8217\'20\'19 goal of an independent state in Israeli-annexed east Jerusalem, the occupied West Bank and Gaza, lands Israel seized in the 1967 Middle East war.\par \par \uc2 And there are increasing doubts that a two-state solution is even possible at this point because Israel has spent decades expanding settlements that are now home to hundreds of thousands of Jewish settlers. Israel blames the continuing conflict on Palestinian violence and the refusal of Palestinian leaders to accept past proposals that it says would have given them a state.\par \par \uc2 Biden plans to visit with \uc2 Mahmoud Abbas\uc2 , the head of the \uc2 Palestinian Authority\uc2 , in Bethlehem during his trip. But it\u8217\'20\'19s unlikely that there will be an opportunity to prod either him or the Israeli prime minister, \uc2 Yair Lapid\uc2 , to reopen talks. \uc2 The Palestinian Authority\uc2 , which administers parts of the occupied West Bank, has grown increasingly unpopular and autocratic in recent years. Lapid is a caretaker prime minister serving while Israel braces for another round of elections later this year.\par \par \uc2 HUMAN RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 Biden will probably be confronted with more fallout over the death of Palestinian-American journalist Shireen Abu Akleh, who was killed two months ago. An analysis overseen by the United States suggested that she was shot by Israeli soldiers who were conducting a raid nearby, but it stopped short of drawing a definitive conclusion. The murky outcome led to more anger than clarity.\par \par \uc2 The treatment of journalists will also be a focal point when Biden visits Saudi Arabia. US intelligence believes that the kingdom\u8217\'20\'19s crown prince, \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2 , likely approved the killing of Jamal Khashoggi, a US-based writer for the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  who was critical of the regime. The murder was carried out by agents who worked for the crown prince, and it took place inside the Saudi consulate in Istanbul in 2018.\par \par \uc2 Dozens of activists, writers, moderate clerics and economists remain imprisoned for their criticism of \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2 . The few who\u8217\'20\'19ve been released, like blogger Raif Badawi and women\u8217\'20\'19s rights advocate Loujain al-Hathloul, face years-long travel bans and cannot speak freely. Some senior members of the royal family have been arrested or had their assets seized, and others were forced into exile.\par \par \uc2 Despite the crackdown, the crown prince has also been credited with reforms. Saudi Arabia looks and feels starkly different than just five years ago, when religious police still roamed the streets chastising women for wearing bright nail polish in malls, enforcing gender segregation in public places and ordering restaurants to turn off background music. Women can now drive, travel abroad without the permission of a male relative and attend sporting events in stadiums once reserved solely for men. Movie theaters and concerts, including one with pop star Justin Bieber, have government backing, a major change after decades of ultraconservative Wahhabi influence.\par \par \uc2 OIL\uc2  PRODUCTION\par \par \uc2 Biden will likely face pressure to temper his criticism of Saudi Arabia\u8217\'20\'19s human rights record to persuade the kingdom and its neighbors to pump more oil and alleviate months of sky-high prices at the gas pump.\par \par \uc2 Energy analysts say drivers shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t get their hopes up. \u8220\'20\'1CIf the public is looking for lower gasoline prices after this trip, I think they\u8217\'20\'19re bound to be disappointed,\u8221\'20\'1D said Samantha Gross, director of the energy security and climate initiative at the \uc2 Brookings Institution\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Saudi Arabia, among the biggest energy producers in the world, are already producing near their full capacity of 11 million barrels of oil per day. And members of Opec+ nations, including Saudi Arabia, are likely to be cautious when it comes to demands from the US.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, as the coronavirus pandemic severely scaled back travel, Trump urged Opec+ to scale back production as the US oil industry wobbled. Now, as the Russian invasion of Ukraine has driven up prices, Biden wants Opec+ to produce more even though there are fears of a global recession around the corner.\par \par \uc2 Elevated oil prices are simply good business for Saudi Arabia, the de facto leader of OPEC+. The kingdom reported that the value of its crude exports were about a $1bn per day in March and April, a 123% increase compared with the same period in 2021.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 7.58pm BST\par \par \uc2 6.39pm BST\par \par \uc2 The US-Mexico relationship, a straightforward tradeoff during the Trump administration, with Mexico tamping down on migration and the US not pressing on other issues, has become a wide range of disagreements over trade, foreign policy, energy and climate change, the Associated Press writes.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Andr\u233\'00\'E9s ManuelL\u243\'00\'F3pez Obrador\uc2  is to visit Washington tomorrow to meet with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , a month after \uc2 L\u243\'00\'F3pez Obrador\uc2  snubbed Biden\u8217\'20\'19s invitation to the Summit of the Americas in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 Mexico\u8217\'20\'19s leader had demanded that Biden invite to the summit the leaders of Cuba, Nicaragua and Venezuela, all countries with anti-democratic regimes, and he has also called US support for Ukraine \u8220\'20\'1Ca crass error.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 On that and other issues, it\u8217\'20\'19s clear \uc2 L\u243\'00\'F3pez Obrador\uc2  is getting along much worse with Biden than with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who threatened Mexico but wanted only one thing from his southern neighbor: stop migrants from reaching the border.\par \par \uc2 I think it is more that the Biden administration has tried hard to re-institutionalize the relationship and restore the relationship that\u8217\'20\'19s not centered solely on immigration and trade. And I think as a result that leads to issues coming up that AMLO is less comfortable talking about,\u8221\'20\'1D Andrew Rudman, director of the Mexico Institute at the Wilson Center, said, using the Spanish acronym by which Mexicans refer to the president.\par \par \uc2 US officials want \uc2 L\u243\'00\'F3pez Obrador\uc2  to retreat on his reliance on fossil fuels and his campaign to favor Mexico\u8217\'20\'19s state-owned electricity utility at the expense of foreign-built plants powered by gas and renewable energy. Washington has filed several complaints under the US-Mexico-Canada free trade agreement pushing Mexico to enforce environmental laws and rules guaranteeing trade union rights.\par \par \uc2 L\u243\'00\'F3pez Obrador\uc2  also has angrily rejected any US criticism of the killings of journalists in Mexico or his own efforts to weaken checks and balances in Mexico\u8217\'20\'19s government. He is also angered by US funding of civic and non-governmental groups in Mexico that he claims are part of the opposition.\par \par \uc2 It all adds up to a witches\u8217\'20\'19 brew in bilateral relations.\par \par \uc2 At the end of the day, the problem is that you have the complete mismatch in this relationship,\u8221\'20\'1D said Arturo Sarukhan, who served as Mexico\u8217\'20\'19s ambassador to the U.S. from 2006 to 2013.\par \par \uc2 The United States \u8220\'20\'1Cneeds Mexico as a key partner on everything from \u8216\'20\'18near shoring\u8217\'20\'19 (manufacturing for the U.S. market). in terms of competitiveness, in terms of North American energy security, energy independence, energy efficiency,\u8221\'20\'1D Sarukhan said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe problem is you have a Mexican president who doesn\u8217\'20\'19t care about any of these things.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Updated at 6.54pm BST\par \par \uc2 6.02pm BST\par \par \uc2 Garnell Whitfield spoke at the White House as he was introducing \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  at the gun safety event moments ago. He and relatives and community are mourning the killing of his mother, 86-year-old Ruth Whitfield, who was the oldest victim of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/buffalo-shooting"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mass shooting at a supermarket in Buffalo}}}\uc2 , upstate New York, on May 14, just days before the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/texas-school-shooting"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 school shooting in Uvalde}}}\uc2 , south Texas.\par \par \uc2 An 18-year-old white man was apprehended after 10 Black people died in a racist attack on a store, as Garnell Whitfield said: \u8220\'20\'1Cthe only supermarket in their community\u8221\'20\'1D where his mother and others went to pick up some groceries \u8220\'20\'1Cbelieving they were safe, but they were not\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Whitfield decried the \u8220\'20\'1Cweapon of war\u8221\'20\'1D the alleged gunman in the attack carried as he \u8220\'20\'1Cwalked in, camera rolling\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The perpetrator has been charged with state and federal crimes, including murder, federal hate crimes and firearms offenses.\par \par \uc2 An affidavit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/15/buffalo-shooting-suspect-charged-federal-hate-crimes"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 submitted with a criminal complaint last month}}}\uc2  said his \u8220\'20\'1Cmotive for the mass shooting was to prevent Black people from replacing white people and eliminating the white race, and to inspire others to commit similar attacks\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The gunman wore a \u8220\'20\'1Ctactical-style helmet, camouflage clothing, body armor and a \uc2 GoPro\uc2  video camera\u8221\'20\'1D, was armed with a Bushmaster XM-15 caliber rifle and carried \u8220\'20\'1Cmultiple loaded magazines\u8221\'20\'1D, the court documents said. The rifle is an AR-15 assault-style rifle, a type used in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2022/05/26/1101274322/uvalde-ar-15-style-rifle-history-shooter-mass-shooting"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 numerous mass shootings}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The gunman exited his car, killed three people in the parking lot and continued his spree inside the store. He said \u8220\'20\'1Csorry\u8221\'20\'1D to a white Tops employee who he shot in the leg, authorities said.\par \par \uc2 On the south lawn of the White House a little earlier today, Garnell Whitfield said that in the United States \u8220\'20\'1Cwe must address white supremacy\u8221\'20\'1D and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/08/post-911-domestic-terror"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 domestic terrorism}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey are a leading threat to our homeland and way of life,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Following \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to the podium, Whitfield thanked the vice president for attending his mother\u8217\'20\'19s memorial service in Buffalo, adding she was a big fan and was \u8220\'20\'1Cdancing in heaven\u8221\'20\'1D knowing Harris was in attendance.\par \par \uc2 5.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 Biden calls afresh for US assault rifles ban: 'We are living in a country awash in weapons of war'\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is once again appealing to Congress to go further with gun safety legislation and ban assault weapons in the US.\par \par \uc2 In remarks at the White House moments ago he included a vivid indictment of the proliferation of military-style assault weapons among the general public in recent years and how they repeatedly feature in mass shootings.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are living in a country awash in weapons of war,\u8221\'20\'1D the US president said. \u8220\'20\'1CWeapons that are designed to hunt are not being used [in massacres], weapons they are purchasing are designed as weapons of war, to take out an enemy. What is the rationale for these weapons outside war zones? Some people claim it\u8217\'20\'19s for sport or to hunt,\u8221\'20\'1D he continued in front of an estimated audience of about 300 people on the South Lawn of the White House.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut let\u8217\'20\'19s look at the facts,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. Biden spoke of bullets fired from an assault rifle moving twice as fast as bullets fired from handguns and \u8220\'20\'1Cmaximize the damage done\u8221\'20\'1D to people.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHuman flesh and bone is just torn apart and as difficult as it is to say, that\u8217\'20\'19s why so many people and so many in this audience \u8211\'20\'13 and I apologize for having to say it \u8211\'20\'13 need to provide DNA samples to identify the remains of their children, think of that,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYet we continue to let these weapons be sold to people with no training or expertise,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 He notes that such lethal weapons provided to soldiers require them to have extensive training and background checks and mental health assessments, and required responsible storage.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe don\u8217\'20\'19t require the same common sense measures for a stranger walking into a gun store to purchase an AR15 or some weapon like that. It makes no sense.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAssault weapons need to be banned,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey were banned, I led the fight in 1994,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, noting that the successful but temporary federal ban on assault rifles being on sale to the general public in the US ended in 2004.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn that 10 years, it was long, mass shootings went down, and when the law expired in 2004 and those weapons were allowed to be sold again, mass shootings tripled, they\u8217\'20\'19re the facts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m determined to ban these weapons again,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, to applause from the audience at the White House.\par \par \uc2 He said he also wanted to get high capacity magazines banned \u8220\'20\'1Cand I\u8217\'20\'19m not going to stop until we do it\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Biden noted that \u8220\'20\'1Cguns are the number one killer of children in the United States, more than car accidents, more than cancer\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He said that in the last 20 years, \u8220\'20\'1Cmore US high school children have died from gunshots than on-duty police officers and active-duty military combined, think of that. We can\u8217\'20\'19t just stand by. With rights come responsibilities. Yes there is a right to bear arms but we also have a right to live freely, without fear for our lives in a grocery store, in a playground...\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Updated at 5.44pm BST\par \par \uc2 5.05pm BST\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said he hopes that the legislation just passed to improve gun safety in the US is a call to achieve more on this issue, as he decried the \u8220\'20\'1Cevery day places\u8221\'20\'1D such as supermarkets, schools, nightclubs, places of worship, workplaces \u8220\'20\'1Cturned into killing fields\u8221\'20\'1D by mass shootings, and also neighborhoods blighted by gun violence, much of which is now so common the tragedies often barely make news headlines.\par \par \uc2 He called the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/25/joe-biden-signs-gun-control-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 new legislation}}}\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Can important start\u8221\'20\'1D. The legislation will toughen background checks for the youngest gun buyers, keep firearms from more domestic violence offenders and help states put in place red flag laws that make it easier for authorities to take weapons from people adjudged to be dangerous, as well as cracking down on gun trafficking.\par \par \uc2 Most of its $13bn cost will help bolster mental health programs and aid schools, which have been targeted in Newtown, Connecticut, and Parkland, Florida, and elsewhere in mass shootings.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHow many more mass shootings do we have to see where shooters 17, 18 years old is able to get his hands on a weapon and go on a killing spree?\u8221\'20\'1D Biden asked in his address.\par \par \uc2 And he noted that the legislation promised some progress so that \u8220\'20\'1Cif we can keep guns out of the hands of domestic abusers we can save the lives of their partners and we can also stop mass shootings\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Biden said the legislation \u8220\'20\'1Cis not enough and we all know that\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 4.31pm BST\par \par \uc2 'Too much of a trail of bloodshed and carnage' \u8211\'20\'13 Biden decries gun violence, slow progress\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has hailed the long-awaited gun safety legislation recently passed with bipartisan support but also said that the act was a call to action to do more.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNothing can bring back your loved ones,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden told survivors and bereaved families gathered at the White House for an event to mark the passing last month of the Safer Community Act that, among {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/25/joe-biden-signs-gun-control-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 various measures}}}\uc2 , strengthens background checks before guns can be purchased.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis has taken too long,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said moments ago, and has left \u8220\'20\'1Ctoo much of a trail of bloodshed and carnage\u8221\'20\'1D as a result of gun violence across the US.\par \par \uc2 He hailed lawmakers, families and activists present who had been instrumental in passing the first federal gun control legislation in 30 years last month, shortly after two mass shootings in Buffalo, New York, and Uvalde, Texas, in May.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBecause of your work, your advocacy, your courage, lives will be saved today and tomorrow because of this... despite the naysayers, we can make meaningful progress on dealing with gun violence,\u8221\'20\'1D the president said.\par \par \uc2 At this point he was briefly interrupted by what appeared to be some heckling, details unclear so far.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 4.53pm BST\par \par \uc2 4.14pm BST\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is hailing the recent bipartisan {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/25/joe-biden-signs-gun-control-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 gun reform legislation}}}\uc2 , even though it only enacts a fraction of what gun control advocates want in the US, with the vice president noting that \u8220\'20\'1Cfor 30 years our nation failed to pass meaningful legislation\u8221\'20\'1D addressing what she noted have been repeated calls for \u8220\'20\'1Ccommon sense action to protect our communities\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The legislation will toughen background checks for the youngest gun buyers, keep firearms from more domestic violence offenders and help states put in place red flag laws that make it easier for authorities to take weapons from people adjudged to be dangerous.\par \par \uc2 Most of its $13bn cost will help bolster mental health programs and aid schools, which have been targeted in Newtown, Connecticut, and Parkland, Florida, and elsewhere in mass shootings.\par \par \uc2 At the time of the bill\u8217\'20\'19s signing last month, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said the compromise hammered out by a bipartisan group of senators \u8220\'20\'1Cdoesn\u8217\'20\'19t do everything I want\u8221\'20\'1D but \u8220\'20\'1Cit does include actions I\u8217\'20\'19ve long called for that are going to save lives\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI know there\u8217\'20\'19s much more work to do, and I\u8217\'20\'19m never going to give up, but this is a monumental day,\u8221\'20\'1D said the president, who was joined by his wife, Jill, a teacher, for the signing.\par \par \uc2 4.05pm BST\par \par \uc2 US president \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and vice president \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  are now approaching the podium in the garden of the White House at an event to mark the bipartisan gun reform legislation {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/25/joe-biden-signs-gun-control-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passed last month}}}\uc2 , called the Safer Community Act.\par \par \uc2 The first speaker is Uvalde pediatrician Roy Guerrero, who speaks of \u8220\'20\'1Ca hollow feeling in our gut\u8221\'20\'1D in the south Texas community where a teenage shooter gunned down 19 children and two teachers in the tiny city in May.\par \par \uc2 Guerrero said he hopes that the legislation just passed is just \u8220\'20\'1Cthe start of the movement to ban assault weapons\u8221\'20\'1D in the US.\par \par \uc2 Guerrero said: \u8220\'20\'1CI spend half my days convincing kids that no one is coming for them and that they are safe\u8212\'20\'14but how do I say that knowing that the very weapons used in the attack are still freely available?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Uvalde pediatrician Roy Guerrero at White House event: "I spend half my days convincing kids that no one is coming for them and that they are safe\u8212\'20\'14but how do I say that knowing that the very weapons used in the attack are still freely available?" {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/GmgmvSw9oQ"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/GmgmvSw9oQ}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/qv6ArIZWgF"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/qv6ArIZWgF}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 ABC News (@ABC) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ABC/status/1546512640473763843?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 11, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Harris is speaking now.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 4.17pm BST\par \par \uc2 4.00pm BST\par \par \uc2 House January 6 panel member and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/05/us-capitol-attack-televised-hearing-january-6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 senior Democrat Zoe Lofgren}}}\uc2  has explained that the committee intends to present evidence \u8220\'20\'1Cconnecting the dots\u8221\'20\'1D about how different extremist groups rallied to the US Capitol on January 6, 2021, to form a violent mob that perpetrated the deadly insurrection as they sought in vain to overturn \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s 2020 election defeat by Democrat \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The panel is holding its next hearing tomorrow afternoon and the subsequent one is expected on Thursday evening.\par \par \uc2 We are going to be connecting the dots during these hearings between these groups and those who were trying - in government circles - to overturn the [2020]election. So, we do think that this story is unfolding in a way that is very serious and quite credible,\u8221\'20\'1D Lofgren of California {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://edition.cnn.com/2022/07/11/politics/january-6-committee-public-hearings-what-matters/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told CNN}}}\uc2  yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Jason Van Tatenhove, a former spokesman for the right-wing group the Oath Keepers will reportedly testify tomorrow, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://kdvr.com/news/local/jan-6-hearing-oath-keepers-jason-van-tatenhove/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 KDVR}}}\uc2  of Colorado and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://edition.cnn.com/2022/07/11/politics/january-6-committee-public-hearings-what-matters/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 CNN}}}\uc2  have said.\par \par \uc2 Panel member and Florida Democrat Stephanie Murphy{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/meet-the-press/meet-press-july-10-2022-n1296939"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told NBC yesterday}}}\uc2  about a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jul/11/house-january-6-committee-to-focus-on-trumps-tweet-at-extremist-group-hearing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 vital tweet by Donald Trump}}}\uc2  in late 2020 and far-right groups such as the Proud Boys and Oath Keepers that:\par \par \uc2 Without spoiling anything that comes this week and encouraging folks to tune in to the specifics, what I will say is that we will lay out the body of evidence that we have that talks about how the president\u8217\'20\'19s tweet on the wee hours of December 19th of \u8216\'20\'18Be there, be wild,\u8217\'20\'19 was a siren call to these folks. And we\u8217\'20\'19ll talk in detail about what that caused them to do, how that caused them to organize, as well as who else was amplifying that message.\par \par \uc2 3.38pm BST\par \par \uc2 The House January 6 select committee is expected to make the case at its seventh hearing Tuesday that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2  gave the signal to the extremist groups that stormed the Capitol to target and obstruct the congressional certification of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s electoral college win.\par \par \uc2 The panel will zero in on a pivotal tweet sent by the former president in the early hours of the morning on 19 December 2020, according to sources close to the inquiry who spoke on the condition of anonymity to discuss the forthcoming hearing.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBig protest in D.C. on January 6th,\u8221\'20\'1D Trump said in the tweet. \u8220\'20\'1CBe there, will be wild!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The select committee will say at the hearing \u8211\'20\'13 led by congressmen \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  and \uc2 Stephanie Murphy\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 that Trump\u8217\'20\'19s tweet was the catalyst that triggered the Proud Boys and Oath Keepers groups, as well as Stop the Steal activists, to target the certification.\par \par \uc2 And Trump sent the tweet knowing that for those groups, it amounted to a confirmation that they should put into motion their plans for January 6, the select committee will say, and encouraged thousands of other supporters to also march on the Capitol for a protest.\par \par \uc2 The tweet was the pivotal moment in the timeline leading up to the Capitol attack, the select committee will say, since it was from that point that the Proud Boys and Oath Keepers seriously started preparations, and Stop the Steal started applying for permits.\par \par \uc2 The select committee also currently plans to play video clips from former White House counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jul/06/january-6-trump-white-house-counsel-pat-cipollone"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recent testimony to House investigators}}}\uc2  at Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s hearing.\par \par \uc2 Raskin is expected to first touch on the immediate events before the tweet: a contentious White House meeting on 18 December 2020 where Trump weighed seizing voting machines and appointing conspiracy theorist \uc2 Sidney Powell\uc2  as special counsel to investigate election fraud.\par \par \uc2 The meeting involved Trump and four informal advisers, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/feb/04/trump-draft-order-voting-machines-white-house-meeting"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Guardian has reported}}}\uc2 , including Trump\u8217\'20\'19s ex-national security adviser, \uc2 Michael Flynn\uc2 , ex-Trump campaign lawyer \uc2 Sidney Powell\uc2 , ex-Overstock CEO Patrick Byrne and ex-Trump aide Emily Newman.\par \par \uc2 Once in the Oval Office, they implored Trump to invoke executive order 13848, which granted him emergency powers in the event of foreign interference in the election \u8211\'20\'13 though that had not happened \u8211\'20\'13 to seize voting machines and install Powell as special counsel.\par \par \uc2 The former president ultimately demurred on both of the proposals. But after the Flynn-Powell-Byrne-Newman plan for him to overturn the election fell apart, the select committee will say, he turned his attention to January 6 as his final chance and sent his tweet.\par \par \uc2 Read the full report {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jul/11/house-january-6-committee-to-focus-on-trumps-tweet-at-extremist-group-hearing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 3.21pm BST\par \par \uc2 In the quirky world of opinion polls, there is a \u8220\'20\'1Cglimmer of good news\u8221\'20\'1D for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  notes, in its survey conducted in conjunction with Siena College.\par \par \uc2 Even though almost two thirds of US Democratic voters don\u8217\'20\'19t want him to be the nominee in 2024, if Biden does fight the election and his Republican opponent is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  again, the Democrat will win, according to this morning\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/07/11/us/politics/biden-approval-polling-2024.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 newly-published poll}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Biden would beat Trump in that hypothetical match-up by 44% to 41% if those questioned in the survey had their way.\par \par \uc2 The Times notes that \u8220\'20\'1Cthe result is a reminder of one of Mr. Biden\u8217\'20\'19s favorite aphorisms: \u8216\'20\'18Don\u8217\'20\'19t compare me to the Almighty, compare me to the alternative.\u8217\'20\'19 The poll showed that Democratic misgivings about Mr. Biden seemed to mostly melt away when presented with a choice between him and Mr. Trump: 92 percent of Democrats said they would stick with Mr. Biden.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Its report details the discontent with Biden\u8217\'20\'19s presidency and outlook, however, adding:\par \par \uc2 Jobs and the economy were the most important problem facing the country according to 20 percent of voters, with inflation and the cost of living (15 percent) close behind as prices are rising at the fastest rate in a generation. One in 10 voters named the state of American democracy and political division as the most pressing issue, about the same share who named gun policies, after several high-profile mass shootings.\par \par \uc2 More than 75 percent of voters in the poll said the economy was \u8220\'20\'1Cextremely important\u8221\'20\'1D to them. And yet only 1 percent rated economic conditions as excellent. Among those who are typically working age \u8212\'20\'14 voters 18 to 64 years old \u8212\'20\'14 only 6 percent said the economy was good or excellent, while 93 percent rated it poor or only fair.\par \par \uc2 The White House has tried to trumpet strong job growth, including on Friday when Mr. Biden declared that he had overseen \u8220\'20\'1Cthe fastest and strongest jobs recovery in American history.\u8221\'20\'1D But the Times/Siena poll showed a vast disconnect between those boasts, and the strength of some economic indicators, and the financial reality that most Americans feel they are confronting....\par \par \uc2 On the whole, voters appeared to like Mr. Biden more than they like his performance as president, with 39 percent saying they have a favorable impression of him \u8212\'20\'14 six percentage points higher than his job approval.\par \par \uc2 In saying they wanted a different nominee in 2024, Democrats cited a variety of reasons, with the most in an open-ended question citing his age (33 percent), followed closely by unhappiness with how he is doing the job. About one in eight Democrats just said that they wanted someone new, and one in 10 said he was not progressive enough. Smaller fractions expressed doubts about his ability to win and his mental acuity.\par \par \uc2 2.54pm BST\par \par \uc2 Strong majority of Democratic voters want party to nominate someone other than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  for president next time - poll\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s approval rating has been struggling mightily for a year and the US president\u8217\'20\'19s popularity is now shockingly low even among his own supporters across America, with 64% of Democratic voters saying they want someone else to be the party\u8217\'20\'19s presidential nominee in the 2024 election, according to a new opinion poll carried out by the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  and Siena College and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/07/11/us/politics/biden-approval-polling-2024.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 published by the newspaper}}}\uc2  this morning.\par \par \uc2 It describes Biden \u8220\'20\'1Chemorrhaging support\u8221\'20\'1D amid a bleak national outlook on life and politics, and only 26% of Democratic US voters telling pollsters that they want the party to renominate the current president to run for a second term.\par \par \uc2 The results make shocking and grim reading for the White House this morning.\par \par \uc2 The report laments a \u8220\'20\'1Ccountry gripped by a pervasive sense of pessimism\u8221\'20\'1D and notes that voters across the nation gave the president a dismal 33% approval rating amid, overwhelmingly, concern about the economy.\par \par \uc2 More than 75% of registered voters think the US is \u8220\'20\'1Cmoving in the wrong direction\u8221\'20\'1D with a pessimism that \u8220\'20\'1Cspans every corner of the country, every age range and racial group, cities, suburbs and rural areas, as well as both political parties,\u8221\'20\'1D the NYT reports.\par \par \uc2 Only 13% of American voters said the nation was on the right track \u8212\'20\'14 the lowest point in Times polling since the depths of the financial crisis more than a decade ago.\par \par \uc2 Biden had earlier as the presidential nominee signaled that he regarded himself as preparing the way for a new guard of Democratic leaders, but since he became president and has been pressed on whether he would seek a second term he has repeatedly said he would.\par \par \uc2 At 79 he is the oldest US president in history and, alarmingly, the Times reports that among Democratic voters under the age of 30, a staggering 94% would prefer a different presidential nominee for their party going into the 2024 presidential election.\par \par \uc2 Three quarters of voters surveyed said the economy was \u8220\'20\'1Cextremely important\u8221\'20\'1D to them but only one percent think that current economic conditions are excellent.\par \par \uc2 2.32pm BST\par \par \uc2 Democratic voters don't want Biden to lead them into 2024 presidential election \u8211\'20\'13 new poll\par \par \uc2 Good morning, US politics blog readers, it\u8217\'20\'19s summer time but the living isn\u8217\'20\'19t easy in Washington whether you\u8217\'20\'19re a Democrat or a Republican. It\u8217\'20\'19s going to be a busy day at the start of a busy week, so let\u8217\'20\'19s get going.\par \par \uc2 * A new opinion poll in the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  this morning makes {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/07/11/us/politics/biden-approval-polling-2024.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 stomach-dropping reading}}}\uc2  for the US president, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , reporting that 64% of Democratic voters don\u8217\'20\'19t want Biden to be their presidential candidate in the 2024 election. The newspaper says: \u8220\'20\'1CWith the country gripped by a pervasive sense of pessimism, the president is hemorrhaging support \u8230\'20\'26 [the majority of Democratic party voters would] prefer a new standard-bearer in the 2024 campaign,\u8221\'20\'1D according to a NYT/Siena College poll, \u8220\'20\'1Cas voters nationwide have soured on his leadership, giving him a meager 33% job-approval rating.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 * The House January 6 committee investigating the insurrection by extremist Trump supporters at the US Capitol in 2021 is due to hold two hearings this week, tomorrow and Thursday. It will spell out tomorrow afternoon {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jul/11/house-january-6-committee-to-focus-on-trumps-tweet-at-extremist-group-hearing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the connections}}}\uc2  between the leading rightwing domestic extremist groups in the US as they planned to descend on Washington to try to overturn \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s defeat in the 2020 election and, ultimately, will set about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://edition.cnn.com/2022/07/11/politics/january-6-committee-public-hearings-what-matters/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cconnecting the dots\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  between those groups and the then Republican president himself and his role in inciting their actions.\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and US vice president \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  will speak at the White House this morning at an event to mark the passing, against the odds on Capitol Hill these days, of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/25/joe-biden-signs-gun-control-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the gun reform bill}}}\uc2  that followed the mass shootings in New York and Texas but before the Fourth of July massacre in Illinois.\par \par \uc2 * The January 6 panel is expected to hold a primetime hearing on Thursday evening as its grand finale after setting out vivid and potent testimony and evidence about the attack on the US Capitol in the dying days of the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 * A court filing this morning has revealed that Justin Clark, an attorney to former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , was interviewed by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  late last month. The interview is ostensibly linked to the criminal contempt case against \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  for refusing congressional demands for his testimony in relation to the Capitol attack. But details are sparse so far and we\u8217\'20\'19ll keep you abreast of developments.\par \par \uc2 2022-07-11T13:32:17Z2022-07-11T20:17:38Z{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://media.guim.co.uk/8258d9706fc5e124d196352f0d27bb2d5cf4f6df/0_96_3500_2102/500.jpgJoe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://media.guim.co.uk/8258d9706fc5e124d196352f0d27bb2d5cf4f6df/0_96_3500_2102/500.jpgJoe}}}\uc2  Biden holds event to mark the passage of the Safer Communities Act at the White House in Washington DC Monday.Former White House strategist \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  outside federal court on Nov. 15, 2021.A man holds a sign saying \u8216\'20\'18Indict \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 in front of Trump Tower in New York City at an \u8220\'20\'1CIndict Trump\u8221\'20\'1D rally to advocate the indictment of former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and others, on 6 July.Garnell Whitfield, Jr., speaks as President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and Uvalde, Texas pediatrician Roy Guerrero look on, during an event at the White House today to celebrate the passage of the \u8220\'20\'1CBipartisan Safer Communities Act\u8221\'20\'1D.Don\u8217\'20\'19t mess. Rep. \uc2 Zoe Lofgren\uc2 , D-Calif., left, and Chairman \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2 , D-Miss., listen as the House select committee investigating the Jan. 6 attack on the U.S. Capitol holds a hearing at the Capitol in Washington, Tuesday, June 28, 2022.Members of the Oath Keepers on the East Front of the U.S. Capitol on Jan. 6, 2021, in Washington.Mad Max. A man hold his hand to his heart as a Proud Boys organizer recites the US pledge of allegiance during a Proud Boys rally in Portland, Oregon on September 26, 2020, as some of the far-right group\u8217\'20\'19s members gather to show support to then-US president Donald Trump.Rep. \uc2 Stephanie Murphy\uc2 , D-Fla., joined from left by Rep. \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2 , D-Md., and Speaker of the House \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , D-Calif., speaks as she is named to a new select committee to investigate the violent Jan. 6 insurrection at the Capitol, on Capitol Hill in Washington, Thursday, July 1, 2021.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220711ei7b0020c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
