\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 FDA\uc2  could approve over-the-counter purchase of first birth control pill\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza \par \uc2 710 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 July 2022\par 19:59 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The agency is considering the application by HRA Pharma to make Opill available without a prescription\par \par \uc2 The Food and Drug Administration will consider an application for the first birth control pill to be sold without a prescription.\par \par \uc2 The application from HRA Pharma would seek to make Opill \u8211\'20\'13 an every day, prescription-only hormonal contraception first approved in 1973 \u8211\'20\'13 available over-the-counter. Such an approval from the \uc2 FDA\uc2  would allow people to purchase \u8220\'20\'1Cthe pill\u8221\'20\'1D without a prescription for the first time since oral contraceptives became widely available in the 1960s.\par \par \uc2 The application will also cast oral contraceptives into a fraught political moment in the US. The \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  ended federal protection for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights late last month, throwing into question the future of birth control.\par \par \uc2 The drugmaker said the timing is unrelated. A decision on the application could come as soon as 2023.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis historic application marks a groundbreaking moment in contraceptive access and reproductive equity in the US,\u8221\'20\'1D said HRA Pharma\u8217\'20\'19s chief strategic operations and innovation officer, Fre\u180\'00\'B4de\u180\'00\'B4rique Welgryn. \u8220\'20\'1CMore than 60 years ago, prescription birth control pills in the US empowered women to plan if and when they want to get pregnant.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Making birth control available without a prescription will \u8220\'20\'1Chelp even more women and people access contraception without facing unnecessary barriers\u8221\'20\'1D, said Welgryn, whose company has already submitted the application.\par \par \uc2 Most oral contraceptives are exceedingly safe, using a combination of estrogen and progestin to prevent pregnancy. Opill uses only progestin, which may make it a better candidate for over-the-counter marketing, since it can be used even by people with a history of blood clotting or uncontrolled high blood pressure, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/progestin-only-hormonal-birth-control-pill-and-injection"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists}}}\uc2  (ACOG).\par \par \uc2 However, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/progestin-only-hormonal-birth-control-pill-and-injection"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cmini-pill,\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  as some progestin-only contraceptives are called, also has side effects. The most common is breakthrough bleeding between periods, which can be unpredictable, according to \uc2 ACOG\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Presently, all hormonal daily birth control pills require a prescription in the US, and many are covered by health insurance. However, obtaining such a prescription has been difficult for many US women. Roughly one-in-four women who had ever attempted to get a hormonal birth control prescription reported difficulty doing so, often because of language barriers, lack of insurance or cost, according to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26666711/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2015 study}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 If approved, over-the-counter birth control would be a victory years in the making. The Free the Pill Coalition has worked to make birth control available without a prescription for nearly two decades in the US, saying it is now available without a prescription in more than 100 countries. The \uc2 American Medical Association\uc2 , which is the nation\u8217\'20\'19s largest professional association of doctors, said in June it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ama-assn.org/press-center/press-releases/ama-urges-fda-make-oral-contraceptive-available-over-counter"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 supports over-the-counter birth control}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Over-the-counter birth control would also provide an alternative form of family planning in the chaotic aftermath of the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision to overturn its landmark 1973 ruling in Roe v Wade, which granted {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jul/08/abortion-roe-v-wade-maternal-care-deserts-louisiana"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 federal \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  protections}}}\uc2 .\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2022/jun/28/tracking-where-abortion-laws-stand-in-every-state"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Several states}}}\uc2  have already begun to enforce near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, and that number is expected to grow to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/05/abortion-deserts-clinics-access-closed-map"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 at least 26}}}\uc2  in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 The debate about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights could spill over into the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s work on contraception. Already, contraception is among the most politicized aspects of the agency\u8217\'20\'19s work. Emergency contraception, sold under the brand name {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2022/jun/28/emergency-contraception-pills-pharmacies"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPlan B\u8221\'20\'1D in the US}}}\uc2 , is already available over-the-counter. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  recently said medication to terminate a pregnancy, often called the \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill\u8221\'20\'1D or \u8220\'20\'1Cmedication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D can be prescribed via telemedicine.\par \par \uc2 However, states hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups have already signaled that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/apr/07/us-mail-order-abortions-oklahoma"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 medication \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  is the next battleground}}}\uc2  for clamping down on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. That could put the \uc2 FDA\uc2  and states into direct conflict as states seek to regulate a medication that was federally approved.\par \par \uc2 That conflict is likely to be sorted out in court. Should it reach the supreme court, the battle between the \uc2 FDA\uc2  and states could come before a conservative supermajority, some members of which have already expressed a willingness to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/clarence-thomas-roe-gay-marriage-contraception-lgbtq"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturn federal rights to contraceptives}}}\uc2  and to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2022/jun/30/supreme-court-ruling-epa-west-virginia-climate-experts-activists-lawyers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 re-evaluate the power of federal agencies to regulate states}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220711ei7b0035z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
