\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18It\u8217\'20\'19s incredibly far-reaching\u8217\'20\'19: medical students on the Roe reversal\b0\par\par\uc2 Gray Chapman \par \uc2 1,358 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 July 2022\par 17:53 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 For students pursuing their medical education in states banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the ruling leaves them grappling with the challenges of a healthcare procedure becoming criminalized\par \par \uc2 Fourth-year medical student Mackenzie Bennett was on a conference call when news broke that Roe v Wade had been overturned. The topic was telehealth and medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe stopped the meeting, we just had to log off and sit in those feelings for a minute. It was honestly really devastating,\u8221\'20\'1D says Bennett, who is pursuing dual medical and public health degrees specializing in OB-GYN at Emory School of Medicine in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 In coming weeks, Georgia will {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsbtv.com/news/local/decision-georgias-heartbeat-law-could-come-early-mid-july/MUUG35UI6VH5BDO3E3YNZZTYJE/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 probably ban most abortions}}}\uc2  after six weeks. For students like Bennett pursuing their medical education in states poised to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, the ruling impacts not just the training they will receive, it leaves them grappling with the personal, moral and practical challenges of a common (and sometimes, life-saving) healthcare procedure becoming criminalized. Clinical training opportunities for providing abortions are already limited in the US \u8211\'20\'13 typically, students who want that training have to seek it out. In the wake of Roe\u8217\'20\'19s overturning, those opportunities will become even more limited, forcing some students to travel out of state to seek out full-spectrum training, potentially prompting an exodus of medical students from the states banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services.\par \par \uc2 The medical institutions they attend are left wondering how their OB-GYN programs will be able to give students the required clinical training to maintain their accreditation. That clinical experience involves observation of, and hands-on training in, uterine evacuation procedures, including medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , first-trimester aspiration \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and dilation and evacuation (D&E) \u8211\'20\'13 procedures used not just for induced abortions, but also for miscarriage management and other aspects of reproductive healthcare.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf we can\u8217\'20\'19t show that we\u8217\'20\'19re providing enough of an experience for them to gain competency in that area, then that threatens the accreditation of any program that\u8217\'20\'19s meeting that challenge,\u8221\'20\'1D says Dr Carrie Cwiak, an Emory professor of obstetrics and gynecology, and director of the medical school\u8217\'20\'19s family planning division. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s what we\u8217\'20\'19re potentially concerned about.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Some of Bennett\u8217\'20\'19s classmates have raised concerns about the what-ifs: what happens if an ectopic pregnancy rolls into the emergency department? What does this mean for cancer treatment? \u8220\'20\'1CIt has implications for everyone\u8217\'20\'19s practice and everyone\u8217\'20\'19s personal life,\u8221\'20\'1D says Bennett, who is a member of Medical Students for Choice. \u8220\'20\'1CI think people are realizing that this impacts them no matter what specialty they\u8217\'20\'19re pursuing. It\u8217\'20\'19s incredibly far-reaching.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 I don\u8217\'20\'19t think I let myself believe that this future was so close and so present\par \par \uc2 Sachi Shastri, second-year student\par \par \uc2 Medical students are weighing implications of Roe\u8217\'20\'19s reversal on both professional and personal levels. \u8220\'20\'1CAs someone who has a uterus and can get pregnant, this will affect me personally,\u8221\'20\'1D says Laura Rush, a second-year student at a different medical school in Georgia (the school did not want students giving its name). \u8220\'20\'1CBut also as future physicians, potentially OBs, who want to treat our patients with empathy and using evidence-based medicine, it feels like it\u8217\'20\'19s disregarding a lot of that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Sachi Shastri, a second-year student at the same school, is considering a career in either OB-GYN or psychiatry. She says that the news, while not a surprise, was still shocking \u8211\'20\'13 as is the sense that her future career could be in jeopardy. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think I let myself believe that this future was so close and so present.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She says peers at her school who previously never expressed opinions about abortions are now speaking up. Rush adds that in the wake of the supreme court ruling, there was an increase in signups for her campus\u8217\'20\'19s chapter of Medical Students for Choice.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 There is only one accrediting body for OB-GYN residency training in the US, The Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education (\uc2 ACGME\uc2 ). In response to the supreme court decision, \uc2 ACGME\uc2  is proposing possible changes to its rules for OB-GYN programs: in states where laws prevent students from receiving clinical experience, programs will have to provide students access to training in a state that does. (Students with religious or moral objections can opt out.) While some medical students \u8211\'20\'13 OB-track or otherwise \u8211\'20\'13 seek out elective travel rotations already as a way to broaden their experience, Cwiak points out that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans could make these travel rotations mandatory \u8211\'20\'13 a financial and logistical burden for some students.\par \par \uc2 Cwiak says when it comes to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  education lectures won\u8217\'20\'19t change, and educators might increasingly incorporate simulations with models. \u8220\'20\'1CIn medical education, we need to ensure that people have direct observation of the care we provide, and participation in that care, under supervision,\u8221\'20\'1D says Cwiak. \u8220\'20\'1CEspecially when you\u8217\'20\'19re talking about procedures: you have to develop a skill, and confidence in that skill, and make sure you\u8217\'20\'19ve done enough cases to learn that skill adequately.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 To learn those skills, some students are limiting their residency options to states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legally protected. \u8220\'20\'1CI think that\u8217\'20\'19s a large undertone of the conversations I\u8217\'20\'19m having with other medical students, especially ones that are in their last year like me, as we\u8217\'20\'19re applying for residency,\u8221\'20\'1D Bennett says. \u8220\'20\'1CThis has a major impact on where you can get trained, and what kind of training you can get.\u8221\'20\'1D And, by extension, where medical trainees choose to eventually practice.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m worried that I won\u8217\'20\'19t be able to get the training I want\par \par \uc2 Ben Haseen, student at Atlanta\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Morehouse School of Medicine\par \par \uc2 Others are concerned that the training they have already lined up in restricted states will no longer be available to them. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve got a rotation lined up in reproductive health [in Georgia] where I would get that training on doing medical abortions, and I\u8217\'20\'19m worried that I won\u8217\'20\'19t be able to get the training I want,\u8221\'20\'1D says Ben Haseen, a medical student at Atlanta\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Morehouse School of Medicine\uc2 . Haseen adds that, as a transgender man, access to reproductive care is a personal issue, not just a professional one. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a big deal for me, because medical access is my biggest passion,\u8221\'20\'1D he says.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWith the restrictive laws, you likely will see a behavioral change, that people will make decisions about where they choose to train, where they choose to practice, depending on the legislative landscape [in that state],\u8221\'20\'1D explains Cwiak. \u8220\'20\'1CIf states and their legislations are interfering with your ability to practice safe, effective, ethical healthcare, like \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, you could understand that people would be reticent about training and practicing there.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s an ethical dilemma for some trainees: leave their state to seek out the training they want, or stay and try to train and practice within the legislative confines of their state. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m very conflicted because I do not want to leave the south,\u8221\'20\'1D says Haseen. \u8220\'20\'1CI love being in the south because I love the patients here. I\u8217\'20\'19m at this crossroads where I could get training up north and then come back, but I also don\u8217\'20\'19t want to leave my patients here and abandon them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 With over half the country\u8217\'20\'19s states likely to ban or severely restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , students and educators alike are also concerned that an exodus of providers from these states, will further compound healthcare disparities and worsen existing public health crises. (Data show that states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sph.tulane.edu/news/study-finds-higher-maternal-mortality-rates-states-more-abortion-restrictions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 higher maternal mortality rates}}}\uc2 . In Georgia, half of the state\u8217\'20\'19s counties lack a single OB-GYN provider, and maternal mortality rates are among the worst in the country.)\par \par \uc2 The people who live in these states \u8220\'20\'1Cstill deserve an excellent quality of care and excellent doctors with world-class training, just like everywhere else in the country\u8221\'20\'1D, says Bennett. \u8220\'20\'1CBut if people feel like they can\u8217\'20\'19t get a full education here, they\u8217\'20\'19re not going to want to come here, and that just makes everything worse down the line.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220711ei7b002s3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
