\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Biden\u8217\'20\'19s executive order on  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  is better than nothing. But not much better\b0\par\par\uc2 Moira Donegan \par \uc2 1,036 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 July 2022\par 09:47 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The president boasted his administration would use \u8216\'20\'18every tool available\u8217\'20\'19 to secure \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. So why is his order so lacking?\par \par \uc2 Probably the most enthusiastic assessment that an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocate can make for President Biden\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.gov/briefing-room/statements-releases/2022/07/08/fact-sheet-president-biden-to-sign-executive-order-protecting-access-to-reproductive-health-care-services/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 executive order}}}\uc2  that aims to \u8220\'20\'1Cprotect access to reproductive health services\u8221\'20\'1D is that it\u8217\'20\'19s better than nothing. That\u8217\'20\'19s because the order, signed by Biden in a brief ceremony at the White House on Friday as vice-president Harris and secretary of Health and Human Services \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2  looked on, has been spoken of by the White House in only the vaguest terms. The order consists of a series of directives aimed at \uc2 HHS\uc2  and the justice department, but these directives are imprecisely worded. They create few obligations for these agencies; they appear designed not to ruffle any feathers. It\u8217\'20\'19s unclear what, precisely, the order will mean for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, and specifically what actions those agencies will now be required to take.\par \par \uc2 The executive order calls for expanded access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not been outlawed; it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t say whether this will include eliminating the current, medically unnecessary restrictions on the drugs or making them available over the counter, as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates have called for. It asks \uc2 HHS\uc2  to make \u8220\'20\'1Cupdates to current guidelines\u8221\'20\'1D, for emergency medical care, in an effort to reduce deaths in pregnant women whose doctors refuse to intervene in medical crises for fear of harming a fetus and incurring liability; it does not call for \uc2 HHS\uc2  to solidify these guidelines into a rule that would more forcefully protect women\u8217\'20\'19s lives.\par \par \uc2 It asks the Department of Justice to convene volunteer lawyers to represent people trying to get or provide legal abortions and gestures vaguely at providing women defense for things like crossing state lines or obtaining care in one state that is illegal in the one where they live. But it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t say whether the administration will work to support the attorneys already doing this work, like those at the Texas-based {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://janesdueprocess.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Jane\u8217\'20\'19s Due Process}}}\uc2  or the legal non-profit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ifwhenhow.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 If/When/How}}}\uc2 , and it does not say how it will make sure that this supply of volunteer, pro bono legal assistance doesn\u8217\'20\'19t dry up. The order talks about protecting privacy and combatting disinformation, but it makes no mention of crisis pregnancy centers, the fake clinics that deceive patients, disseminate false information about abortions, and suck up large amount of information about the women they lure through their doors. The order calls for \uc2 HHS\uc2  to expand access to contraception, but doesn\u8217\'20\'19t say how.\par \par \uc2 The short version seems to be, that the Biden administration will make no effort to reverse the sadistic and draconian attacks on women\u8217\'20\'19s rights in red states. But it will make some kind of vague, still-undefined effort to stop them from spreading to blue ones.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s not much of a payoff, considering the massive amount of pressure from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates that it took to elicit this response from the Biden administration. Despite having a six-week heads-up on the coming overturn of Roe after a draft of the majority opinion was leaked in early May, and in spite of having more than a year since the case, whose outcome was never in doubt, was granted cert by the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 , the Biden administration seemed flat-footed and caught off guard by the end of national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Reporting from CNN claims that the White House was unique among court watchers in being surprised when the Dobbs decision was released on 24 June. In a sign of how seriously the administration is taking women\u8217\'20\'19s rights, an aide assigned to respond to the Dobbs decision was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2022/07/05/politics/democrats-frustrated-biden-lack-of-urgency-supreme-court-setbacks/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 out getting coffee}}}\uc2  when she heard about the opinion\u8217\'20\'19s release from a push notification on her phone. For his part, Biden himself is so enthusiastic about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights that he was planning to nominate an anti-choice Republican judge to a lifetime appointment on a federal district court in Kentucky that very same day.\par \par \uc2 Overall, the administration has seemed unwilling to move towards a more robust defense of women\u8217\'20\'19s freedoms, and unwilling to treat the reversal of Roe as the disaster for equal rights and civil liberties that their base sees it as. They issue capacious statements \u8211\'20\'13 and, now, a capacious executive order \u8211\'20\'13 that are light on specifics, and tend to conspicuously avoid the word \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D When \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  began his signing ceremony for his executive order, he did not immediately turn to the reason why he was there \u8211\'20\'13 the rollback of a fundamental civil and human right for half of his constituents. Instead, he took a moment to boast about some promising jobs numbers. When the signing ceremony concluded, the first question the president took was from a male reporter, who asked about the assassination of former Japanese prime minister Shinzo Abe.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps what\u8217\'20\'19s most noticeable about Biden\u8217\'20\'19s executive order is what it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t say. It does not say that the administration will make federal lands in red states available for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, as some legal experts have urged. It does not say that the DoJ will bring lawsuits against states that ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication, on the theory that such bans violate the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s supremacy. It does not pledge a repeal of the Hyde amendment.\par \par \uc2 The somewhat lukewarm reception of Biden\u8217\'20\'19s EO from the reproductive rights community may have been tempered by reporting from \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  on Friday that the administration had dismissed the idea of declaring a national health emergency in response to the supreme court ruling, a move that would have empowered the administration to respond proportionately to the massive and ongoing threat to women\u8217\'20\'19s safety and liberty. According to Bloomberg, Biden and his advisers ditched the idea because they didn\u8217\'20\'19t want to get sued over it. In his statements before signing the executive order, Biden said that his administration would \u8220\'20\'1Cuse every tool available\u8221\'20\'1D to secure \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. Well, apparently not every tool.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Moira Donegan is a Guardian US columnist\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220710ei7a000uo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
