\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Biden in crisis mode as specter of one-term Carter haunts White House\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Gambino in Washington \par \uc2 1,488 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 July 2022\par 08:46 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 President urged to act more forcefully to deal with rising inflation, gun violence and dire supreme court rulings\par \par \uc2 At an Independence Day barbecue, crises cascading around him, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  declared that he had \u8220\'20\'1Cnever been more optimistic about America than I am today\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Of course there were challenges, grave ones, the US president told the military families assembled on the south lawn of the White House. And the nation had a troubling history of taking \u8220\'20\'1Cgiant steps forward\u8221\'20\'1D and then a \u8220\'20\'1Cfew steps backwards\u8221\'20\'1D, he acknowledged.\par \par \uc2 But Biden gave a hopeful speech that reflected his often unshakable faith in the American experiment on the 246th anniversary of its founding.\par \par \uc2 Yet many Americans, even his own supporters, no longer share the president\u8217\'20\'19s confidence. To many observers Biden appears to be at a moment of profound crisis in his presidency: and one he is struggling to address. The specter of \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 a one-term Democrat whose failure to win the 1980 election ushered in the \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  era \u8211\'20\'13 is starting to haunt the Biden White House.\par \par \uc2 With decades-high inflation, near-weekly mass shootings, a drumbeat of alarming disclosures about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s attempts to overturn his election defeat, and successive supreme court rulings that shifted the country\u8217\'20\'19s political landscape sharply rightward, Biden\u8217\'20\'19s rosy speech-making struck even his fellow Democrats as ill-suited for what they view as a moment of existential peril for the country.\par \par \uc2 A new {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://go.politicoemail.com/?qs=205780f806abb659f0cc24b6d07e890e0e1d488d5247dc8f7d36c5f98e544a4b696e23cc1382ee155b7456ad69c611dd"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Monmouth poll}}}\uc2  captured the depth of America\u8217\'20\'19s pessimism: at present just 10% of Americans believe the country is on the right track, compared with 88% who say it is on the wrong track. Confidence in the country\u8217\'20\'19s institutions fell to record lows this year, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://go.politicoemail.com/?qs=205780f806abb659a8c3cc675e04bde052f7bfac9a064c4c0b11f9b6d1e2316ac4abf6a568b3b55321153e90d6f4c36f"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 latest Gallup survey}}}\uc2 . The presidency and the supreme court suffered the most precipitous declines, while Congress drew the lowest levels of confidence of any institution at just 7%.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf that sunny optimism were paired with actual steps to secure the future that the president claims to be excited about, it would ring less hollow,\u8221\'20\'1D said Tr\u233\'00\'E9 Easton, a progressive Democratic strategist. \u8220\'20\'1CBut right now it seems disconnected from the reality that many people, especially people who worked very hard to get President Biden and Vice-President [Kamala] Harris elected, are experiencing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Last month, a conservative super-majority on the supreme court ended the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , paving the way for new restrictions and bans in Republican-controlled states across the country. Meanwhile, democracy experts are sounding the alarm as Republican candidates who embraced conspiracy theories about the 2020 election win primary elections for key positions of power.\par \par \uc2 With control of Congress, governorships and statehouses at stake this November, many supporters and allies are pleading with Biden to lead with the urgency and force they believe this moment demands.\par \par \uc2 Under mounting pressure from supporters and allies to deliver a more assertive response, Biden on Friday signed an executive order that the White House said would protect women seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In his most impassioned remarks to date, Biden said the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision was \u8220\'20\'1Can exercise in raw political power\u8221\'20\'1D and warned that Republicans would seek a national ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in they win control of Congress in November.\par \par \uc2 Democrats broadly welcomed the order and the passion. Still others hoped it was just a \u8220\'20\'1Cfirst step,\u8221\'20\'1D noting that the action did not include some of the more novel actions Democrats have called for, such as opening \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics on federal lands in states where the procedure is banned or declaring a national emergency.\par \par \uc2 Before the signing ceremony on Friday, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.bloomberglaw.com/health-law-and-business/biden-team-rejected-emergency-declaration-over-roe-decision?context=search&index=0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bloomberg}}}\uc2  reported that the White House considered declaring a national public health emergency as a number of Democratic lawmakers and activists have urged him to do, but ultimately decided against it.\par \par \uc2 That caution, a hallmark of Biden\u8217\'20\'19s decades-long political career, has frustrated many Democrats who fear democracy itself is under an assault.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEverything\u8217\'20\'19s on the line right now. It\u8217\'20\'19s truly existential,\u8221\'20\'1D Easton said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt just doesn\u8217\'20\'19t seem like he understands that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 New reports of a White House struggling to respond to mounting challenges have even fueled a discussion among Democrats over whether Biden should seek re-election in 2024.\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, speculation has mounted over potential alternatives. Among them are California governor, \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , has positioned himself as a pugnacious leader in the fight to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and Illinois governor, JB Pritzker, offered a guttural response to the Independence Day shooting in his state that drew contrast with Biden\u8217\'20\'19s more restrained approach.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you are angry today, I\u8217\'20\'19m here to tell you to be angry,\u8221\'20\'1D Pritzker said. In a statement, Biden condemned the attack as yet another \u8220\'20\'1Csenseless act of violence\u8221\'20\'1D and held a moment of silence for the victims at the White House.\par \par \uc2 The White House has rejected that criticism, arguing that Biden has responded \u8211\'20\'13 quickly and forcefully \u8211\'20\'13 to the mounting crises facing the nation. Asked about Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 criticism of Biden, White House press secretary \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  said the president has been quick to tackle the nation\u8217\'20\'19s crises.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe president showed urgency. He showed fury. He showed frustration,\u8221\'20\'1D she said of Biden\u8217\'20\'19s response to the recent mass shootings, and that his leadership paved the way for a bipartisan gun safety compromise, breaking decades of gridlock in Washington over how to address gun violence.\par \par \uc2 Democrats fears\u8217\'20\'19 come as the party faces a historically challenging electoral landscape, with prognosticators anticipating a Republican takeover of Congress in November.\par \par \uc2 Cristina Tzintz\u250\'00\'FAn Ramirez, the president and executive director of NextGen America, a youth-vote mobilization organization in the country, said the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling on Roe clarified the stakes for many young people. But she said they\u8217\'20\'19re looking for bold leadership in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Democrats must put \u8220\'20\'1Ceverything on the table\u8221\'20\'1D to keep an \u8220\'20\'1Cultra-rightwing and extremist minority from overtaking every major institution in our country,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s what\u8217\'20\'19s on the ballot in 2022.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Biden on Friday said his executive powers were limited and Democrats lacked the numbers in Congress to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights at the nation level.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CVote, vote, vote vote,\u8221\'20\'1D he implored Americans angry over the ruling. \u8220\'20\'1CWe need two additional pro-choice senators and a pro-choice House to codify Roe. Your vote can make that a reality.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 For months, the White House has careened from crisis to crisis. Inflation, war in Europe, record gas prices, an irrepressible pandemic and a baby formula shortage have all contributed to the national malaise and Biden\u8217\'20\'19s low approval rating.\par \par \uc2 Sarah Longwell, a moderate Republican strategist who holds focus groups with suburban women, said voters constantly tell her that they wish they heard from Biden more.\par \par \uc2 Facing a difficult political landscape, she said voters want to see that Biden is willing to take on the \u8220\'20\'1Cmost extreme elements of the Republican party\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEven if he can\u8217\'20\'19t do anything about it, the bully pulpit is a powerful thing,\u8221\'20\'1D she said adding: \u8220\'20\'1CPeople think this is madness. They want to be able to take their kids to a July Fourth parade and not worry about somebody getting shot. And they want their leader to reflect that back to them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 On Friday, Biden sought to do just that. He hammered Republicans for pursuing bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without exceptions for rape or incest and highlighted the case of a 10-year-old rape victim who was forced to travel out of state for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He previously endorsed an exception to the Senate filibuster rule in order to pass \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections, but he\u8217\'20\'19s so far declined to embrace calls for court reform like term limits or court expansion. And in response to the extraordinary revelations about the 6 January attack on the Capitol, Biden has mostly declined to comment, deferring to the congressional committee investigating the attack and the justice department, which is weighing whether to prosecute \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for his role in the violent assault on American democracy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn this hour, if you want to commit to democracy, the thing to do is to not laud the institutions that we have as they\u8217\'20\'19re currently constituted, but to set to work on amending these institutions to meet present exigencies,\u8221\'20\'1D said William Howell, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://harris.uchicago.edu/directory/william-howell"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a political scientist at the University of Chicago}}}\uc2  and the author of Presidents, Populism, and the Crisis of Democracy.\par \par \uc2 He said Biden\u8217\'20\'19s commitments to democratic norms and traditions are critical, particularly after the Trump years, but that should not impede him from addressing the \u8220\'20\'1Cacute need for us to revisit our institutions\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18The ante-status quo was dysfunctional \u8211\'20\'13 it was unacceptable in the face of the pressing challenges that our country faces,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CWhile there\u8217\'20\'19s a need for a reset, there is a greater need for leadership in terms of institutional reform.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220709ei79000ul\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
