\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Pregnant women face increasingly dangerous risks as doctors flee punitive US states\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza \par \uc2 1,289 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 July 2022\par 15:24 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 With doctors facing threats of criminal penalties for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , many states will become \u8216\'20\'18maternity care deserts\u8217\'20\'19 where there is no hospital, birthing center or obstetrician\par \par \uc2 Louisiana is fighting to become a leader in the race to criminalize doctors who allegedly provide abortions, since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  ended federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections.\par \par \uc2 In doing so, the state may also become an example of how \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans could worsen maternal health in America, as criminal penalties across the US redefine where and how doctors are willing to practice.\par \par \uc2 In turn, that is likely to worsen a leading reason some states are more dangerous places to give birth \u8211\'20\'13 lack of hospitals, birthing centers and obstetricians.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt should be no surprise that in a lot of the states where there\u8217\'20\'19s a [trigger ban], there\u8217\'20\'19s a strong correlation [with maternity care deserts],\u8221\'20\'1D said Stacey Stewart, president and chief executive of the March of Dimes, an organization that advocates for maternal and infant health and is strictly neutral on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Many of the same states hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have also pursued {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://videocast.nih.gov/watch=31715"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 intersecting policies}}}\uc2  that can worsen health overall for residents, such as refusal to expand a public health health insurance program for the poor, called Medicaid.\par \par \uc2 Now, the severe criminal penalties and extraordinary civil liability doctors are exposed to under such anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  statutes could become fundamental to how and where healthcare providers decide to practice.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s been an unprecedented encroachment of the legal world into these medical decisions,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr \uc2 Rebekah Gee\uc2 , an obstetrician and gynecologist in New Orleans, chief executive of the family medicine practice Nest Health, and former secretary for the Louisiana department of health. Louisiana\u8217\'20\'19s trigger law allows for fines of up to $250,000 and 15 years in prison for wrongly inducing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThese are penalties people have for rape and armed robbery,\u8221\'20\'1D said Gee.\par \par \uc2 About half of US states are expected to try to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from conception or very early in pregnancy, the result of a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cataclysmic supreme court decision}}}\uc2  that ended {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/24/scotus-roe-wade-decision-what-happens-next"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nearly 50 years of federal protection for \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CForget the fines \u8211\'20\'13 people are afraid to go to prison,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lisa M Wayne, executive director of the National \uc2 Association of Criminal Defense Lawyers (NACDL)\uc2 . NACDL has fielded calls from reproductive rights groups, medical associations and even other attorneys in recent days, trying to sort out the kind of legal exposure they may face state-to-state.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNobody wants to sign up thinking their freedom or liberty is going to be interfered with,\u8221\'20\'1D said Wayne.\par \par \uc2 Louisiana provides an especially salient example of how the geography of medicine could be affected. If the trigger bans in Louisiana and neighboring states go into effect, as expected, the nearest state without an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban would be hundreds of miles away in Illinois.\par \par \uc2 Louisiana\u8217\'20\'19s would also be especially punitive, allowing prosecutors to seek lengthy prison sentences and felony charges. The state law also provides no exemption for victims of rape or incest.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans will probably cause \u8220\'20\'1Ceven greater workforce challenges in certain areas, particularly if obstetrician-gynecologists are unable to live and work in states with restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws\u8221\'20\'1D, said Dr Jen Villavicencio, lead for equity transformation at the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For now, the state\u8217\'20\'19s trigger law has been blocked by a state court, but state officials such as the Republican attorney general, \uc2 Jeff Landry\uc2 , have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wwno.org/2022-07-05/louisiana-ag-asks-state-supreme-court-to-reinstate-abortion-ban-before-july-8-hearing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 argued in favor}}}\uc2  of the ban. Nevertheless, the court case to stop the law has created a record of how doctors say a climate of fear and confusion has settled on the state and is certain to affect care.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFrankly, I am worried I could go to prison just for handling a miscarriage as I always have,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr Nina Breakstone, an emergency doctor in Terrytown, Louisiana, in an affidavit in opposition to the state\u8217\'20\'19s trigger ban.\par \par \uc2 Frankly, I am worried I could go to prison just for handling a miscarriage\par \par \uc2 Nina Breakstone\par \par \uc2 In another affidavit, an emergency medicine doctor said colleagues were scared of reporting miscarriages on patients\u8217\'20\'19 charts because, medically, a miscarriage is described as a \u8220\'20\'1Cspontaneous \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CProviders like myself are left with absolutely no direction on how to resume their practice or what, if any of their actions may subject them to prison time and enormous fines,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr Rebecca Perret, an ob-gyn in Orleans parish, in another affidavit.\par \par \uc2 By the admission of the state\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ldh.la.gov/assets/docs/LegisReports/SR240HR294RS201992019.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 own health department}}}\uc2 , Louisiana has among the worst maternal mortality rates in the nation, in which Black patients are four times more likely to die than their white counterparts. The state also struggles to attract doctors to care for pregnant patients. About one-third of counties, called parishes, lack an obstetrician, according to court documents.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, for every 100,000 births, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.americashealthrankings.org/explore/health-of-women-and-children/measure/maternal_mortality_b/state/LA"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 33 women died}}}\uc2 . That is well above the US national average of 20 deaths for every 100,000 live births. Louisiana\u8217\'20\'19s rate is more than five times the rate of maternal mortality in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.commonwealthfund.org/publications/issue-briefs/2020/nov/maternal-mortality-maternity-care-us-compared-10-countries"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 United Kingdom}}}\uc2 , and according to another report in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32682858/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 American Journal of Obstetrics and Gynecology}}}\uc2 , on par with those of Kyrgyzstan.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhere you live matters,\u8221\'20\'1D Dr Juanita Chinn, a demographer and expert in population health at the National Institute of Child and Human Health Development, told the Guardian. \u8220\'20\'1CA geographic barrier in the ability to access care will likely only heighten the risk of experiencing a severe maternal morbidity event or a maternal mortality.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Many parishes are \u8220\'20\'1Cmaternity care deserts\u8221\'20\'1D, or places where there is no hospital, birthing center or obstetrician, according to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.marchofdimes.org/materials/2020-Maternity-Care-Report.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2020 report from the March of Dimes}}}\uc2 . That lack of doctors has already become a driving factor in the risks pregnant patients face in Louisiana, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S104938672030102X"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 research shows}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In court documents, doctors said Louisiana\u8217\'20\'19s decision to attempt to enact severe penalties for alleged abortions has already dissuaded some from practicing in the state, has hospital administrators worried about staffing shortages and medical school professors concerned about the future workforce.\par \par \uc2 Other doctors said in affidavits that such a climate is likely to have enormous knock-on effects for patients in Louisiana, as medical residents choose other states in which to practice and doctors look elsewhere for work.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI am concerned maternal mortality rates will only get worse if the trigger ban [goes] into effect,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr Jennifer Avegno, an emergency medicine doctor and director of the New Orleans health department, in a court document. In part, she said, maternal mortality would get worse because patients, especially in rural area, would need to travel further for care.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COne of the main reasons women are more likely to die in Louisiana is because they are forced to travel long distances for care in many areas of the state due to lack of healthcare providers,\u8221\'20\'1D said Avegno.\par \par \uc2 Dr Clarissa Jo Beutler, a family medicine doctor, wrote to the court that she worried about ripple effects on general medicine, as doctors often prescribe medications that can cause a miscarriage or pregnancy complications for depression, migraines and autoimmune disorders.\par \par \uc2 Dr Valerie Williams, an ob-gyn in Orleans parish and an assistant professor at Louisiana State University Healthcare Network, said in an affidavit she worried the trigger bans would impact the \u8220\'20\'1Cnumber and quality\u8221\'20\'1D of obstetric residents who choose to train in the state, thereby impacting the state\u8217\'20\'19s future workforce. Already, the ban has dissuaded one provider from coming to Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen helping find a replacement for my former position, an amazing candidate applied who was very motivated to practice in Louisiana,\u8221\'20\'1D said Williams. \u8220\'20\'1COnce she heard Louisiana has trigger bans with severe penalties for physicians, however, she backed out.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220708ei78000um\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
