\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The brutal US  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ruling is a potential death sentence for all pregnant women\b0\par\par\uc2 Emma Brockes \par \uc2 833 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 July 2022\par 12:16 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Doctors in many states are now barred from intervening unless they\u8217\'20\'19re sure someone is about to die. Inevitably, some will\par \par \uc2 To mitigate the shock, perhaps, or because people believed it, one thing said in the immediate wake of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/jul/03/abortion-roe-v-wade-womens-rights-rebecca-solnit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturning of Roe v Wade}}}\uc2  was that for much of the US, not a great deal would change. Symbolically, of course, it was horrific, potentially prefiguring a larger swing by the supreme court against civil rights. Practically, however, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  limits across large swaths of the country were already so severe, and the availability of clinics so reduced, that it raised the question of how much difference would this make anyway.\par \par \uc2 In the two weeks since the justices made their decision, the answer to that question has been rising to the surface. Doctor by doctor, activist by activist, the implications of a total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in the eight states in which it was instantly triggered, and the further nine expected to follow suit within weeks, have begun to be outlined. The threat posed to women\u8217\'20\'19s health is so staggering, so nonsensical, as to seem barely possible.\par \par \uc2 The danger of illegal abortions; the mental and financial disaster of forcing women to have babies they don\u8217\'20\'19t want; the outrage of the state controlling what you do with your body, and the implications of forced pregnancy when it\u8217\'20\'19s a result of rape \u8211\'20\'13 all these arguments have been staples of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics for decades. Now that the worst has happened, however, they are joined by a bunch of considerations that are so wildly beyond reason, so fanatically punitive, that they represent a potential death sentence for any pregnant woman.\par \par \uc2 The most startling of these is the failure in those states implementing a total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban to make an exception for women who discover their foetus won\u8217\'20\'19t live. The vast majority of abortions in the US happen inside the first 12 weeks. So called \u8220\'20\'1Clate stage\u8221\'20\'1D abortions, occurring later than 16 weeks and, in even fewer cases, after 21 weeks, represent less than 5% of the total and, according to doctors, are in most cases a response to the discovery of catastrophic foetal abnormalities. In these instances, the baby will die within hours or days of birth. What an extraordinary decision by those pious members of the supreme court to force a woman to carry a baby to term so she can watch it die.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s not rusty coat-hangers or gin and bath tubs this time. It\u8217\'20\'19s doctors, on pain of prosecution, being barred from intervening when a woman is miscarrying. When the only \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  loophole exists around a threat to the mother\u8217\'20\'19s life, you\u8217\'20\'19d better be sure she\u8217\'20\'19s about to die before you jump in to help. These are margin calls that won\u8217\'20\'19t always go the woman\u8217\'20\'19s way and that make every miscarriage a potentially fatal event.\par \par \uc2 The same goes for every ectopic pregnancy. A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/07/04/opinion/ectopic-pregnancy-roe-abortion.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 deranged piece}}}\uc2  in the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  this week argued that ectopic pregnancies \u8211\'20\'13 a bunch of cells attaching to the wall of the fallopian tubes which, if not excised, will rupture and kill the woman \u8211\'20\'13 might be viewed in the same way as a premature baby, and the ending of the pregnancy referred to as \u8220\'20\'1Cdelivering\u8221\'20\'1D that baby. You can\u8217\'20\'19t incubate a baby in your fallopian tube, nor can you \u8220\'20\'1Cdeliver\u8221\'20\'1D a baby from an ectopic pregnancy, which can be detected at five or six weeks. Misinterpreting this lethal malfunction is mad.\par \par \uc2 There has been a flippant, self-soothing reflex for those of us in liberal states to say, awful as this is, they can\u8217\'20\'19t do anything to women in New York or California. This may be true, although \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers across the country will come under much greater pressure once women are travelling farther to use them. What it has taken a moment to understand is that pregnant women in the US, irrespective of home state, may have to consider avoiding large areas of their own country in case they are denied emergency treatment while there.\par \par \uc2 This puts the US on a footing with Malta, where an American woman on holiday recently suffered a partial miscarriage and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/woman-who-had-miscarriage-in-malta-taken-to-spain-to-abort/2022/06/24/c05ed13c-f3be-11ec-ac16-8fbf7194cd78_story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 had to be airlifted}}}\uc2  to Spain for her D&C (dilation and curettage). \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned in Malta. A woman who has miscarried and needs foetal tissue removed to forestall infection will be monitored but not treated. For that to happen in Malta, as in larger and larger areas of the US in the coming weeks, doctors must stand back and wait for women to start dying.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Emma Brockes is a Guardian columnist\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Do you have an opinion on the issues raised in this article? If you would like to submit a letter of up to 300 words to be considered for publication, email it to us at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:guardian.letters@theguardian.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 guardian.letters@theguardian.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220708ei7800131\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
