\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  removing posts with mentions of  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  pills\b0\par\par\uc2 Kari Paul and agencies \par \uc2 697 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 July 2022\par 12:38 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 In light of the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s verdict, memes and status updates on social media on how to access the medicines have exploded\par \par \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  have begun removing posts related to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, as posts about such medication spiked following the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling stripping away constitutional protections for abortions.\par \par \uc2 Memes and status updates explaining how people can obtain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills in the mail have exploded across social platforms in recent days.\par \par \uc2 General mentions of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, as well as posts mentioning specific versions such as mifepristone and misoprostol, suddenly spiked on Friday morning across \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , Reddit and TV broadcasts, according to an analysis by the media intelligence firm \uc2 Zignal Labs\uc2 . By Sunday, Zignal had counted more than 250,000 such mentions.\par \par \uc2 Some of the posts demonstrated how people can legally obtain medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by mail across the US, through \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  telehealth services like {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.heyjane.co/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Hey Jane}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.justthepill.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Just the Pill}}}\uc2 , and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mychoix.co/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Choix}}}\uc2  in states where such healthcare is legal.\par \par \uc2 In other posts individual users offered to mail prescriptions to women living in states that criminalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  following Friday\u8217\'20\'19s supreme court decision.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, underground \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill networks have begun to pop up, said Eric Feinberg, a researcher at the Coalition for a Safer Web. Screenshots provided to the Guardian showed Mifepristone for sale in private \uc2 Facebook\uc2  groups with names like \u8220\'20\'1CMTP Kit and Other Pills\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1CCleaning and \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Pills\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Almost immediately, \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  began removing some of the posts directly offering pills to people, \uc2 Vice Media{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vice.com/en/article/m7gav3/facebook-is-banning-people-who-say-they-will-mail-abortion-pills?mc_cid=2136b7ea6d&mc_eid=403f38fef4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 first reported}}}\uc2  on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The Associated Press obtained a screenshot on Friday of one \uc2 Instagram\uc2  post from a woman who offered to purchase or forward \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills through the mail, minutes after the court ruled to overturn the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDM me if you want to order \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, but want them sent to my address instead of yours,\u8221\'20\'1D the post on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  read. \uc2 Instagram\uc2  took it down within moments.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, an AP reporter tested how \uc2 Meta\uc2 , \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s parent company, would respond to a similar post on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , writing: \u8220\'20\'1CIf you send me your address, I will mail you \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills.\u8221\'20\'1D The post was removed within one minute.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Facebook\uc2  account was immediately put on a \u8220\'20\'1Cwarning\u8221\'20\'1D status for the post, which \uc2 Facebook\uc2  said violated its standards on \u8220\'20\'1Cguns, animals and other regulated goods\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Yet, when the AP reporter made the same exact post but swapped out the words \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills\u8221\'20\'1D for \u8220\'20\'1Ca gun\u8221\'20\'1D, the post remained untouched. A post with the same exact offer to mail \u8220\'20\'1Cweed\u8221\'20\'1D was also left up and not considered a violation.\par \par \uc2 In an email, a \uc2 Meta\uc2  spokesperson pointed to company policies that prohibit the sale of certain items, including guns, alcohol, drugs and pharmaceuticals. The company did not explain the apparent discrepancies in its enforcement of that policy, including the ongoing issue of guns {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2022/jun/15/meta-firearms-sales-facebook-instagram-ghost-guns"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 being sold openly}}}\uc2  on its platform.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Meta\uc2  spokesperson, \uc2 Andy Stone\uc2 , confirmed in a tweet on Monday that the company will not allow individuals to gift or sell pharmaceuticals on its platform, but will allow content that shares information on how to access pills.\par \par \uc2 Stone acknowledged some problems with enforcing that policy across its platforms, which include \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve discovered some instances of incorrect enforcement and are correcting these,\u8221\'20\'1D Stone said in the tweet.\par \par \uc2 Most states in the US allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to be administered via mail, while 19 prohibit such medication from being taken at home without a medical provider present. More than half of all abortions in the US are medication abortions, according to pro-choice research group the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The US attorney general, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , said on Friday that states should not ban mifepristone, the medication used to induce an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CStates may not ban mifepristone based on disagreement with the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s expert judgment about its safety and efficacy,\u8221\'20\'1D Garland said in a Friday statement.\par \par \uc2 But some Republicans have already tried to stop their residents from obtaining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills through the mail, with some states like West Virginia and Tennessee prohibiting providers from prescribing the medication through telemedicine consultation.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220706ei76002qo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
