\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 After Roe, are Republicans willing to expand the social safety net?\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Stein in Washington \par \uc2 1,437 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 July 2022\par 15:51 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The party has shown little enthusiasm to help those affected by unplanned pregnancies \u8211\'20\'13 is anything likely to change?\par \par \uc2 Republicans across the United States cast the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision last month that allowed states to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a victory for \u8220\'20\'1Clife\u8221\'20\'1D. Left unsaid was the quality of life that families and mothers set to be left dealing with unplanned pregnancies might have.\par \par \uc2 For years, the Republican party has pushed to ban a procedure that is mostly sought out {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/infographic/2016/us-abortion-patients"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 by people who are poor}}}\uc2 , while showing much less enthusiasm for efforts to permanently expand the country\u8217\'20\'19s social safety net. Critics have labeled the party\u8217\'20\'19s stance as caring a lot politically about unborn fetuses, but losing interest in them when they are born as American citizens.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/article/2021/10/26-states-are-certain-or-likely-ban-abortion-without-roe-heres-which-ones-and-why"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Twenty-six states}}}\uc2  are now expected to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  entirely following the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling in Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization, and following November\u8217\'20\'19s midterm elections, Republicans could gain control of one or both houses of Congress, and make gains in state legislatures.\par \par \uc2 That dynamic could now give many more Americans a closeup look at what the party\u8217\'20\'19s policies mean for women and families dealing with any wave of unplanned pregnancies, and there are signs Republicans are worried about what they will see.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COver the years, we have written on federal policies that we consider \u8216\'20\'18pro-life\u8217\'20\'19 that support pregnant women, not just policies that restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This line of thinking is no longer a luxury of thought for pro-lifers like us. It is an obligation of pro-life advocacy in the future as we enter what will be a dynamic, uncertain, and uneven state landscape for years to come,\u8221\'20\'1D Republican operatives Mark Rodgers and Kiki Bradley {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nationalreview.com/2022/06/a-marshall-plan-for-the-pro-life-cause/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote in the National Review}}}\uc2  last month, in an essay calling for the party to get behind policies like paid family leave and tax credits for families with children.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA number of leaders understand that our expressions of concern for life would ring hollow without our movement\u8217\'20\'19s advocacy of a support system for pregnant women and their babies.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Already, there are signs of some Republican lawmakers moving to address these concerns. Republicans senators have proposed two bills expanding aspects of the government social safety net, and lawmakers may end up considering them before the year is out and the new Congress convenes in 2023.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s certainly, I think, at least at the intellectual level, a recognition that we need to be approaching these issues in a fresh way,\u8221\'20\'1D said Brad Wilcox, a \uc2 University of Virginia\uc2  professor affiliated with the Institute for Family Studies and the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 , both right-leaning thinktanks.\par \par \uc2 Yet opponents of the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision in Dobbs can\u8217\'20\'19t help but contrast the fervency within the party for outlawing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with their relative historic coolness towards programs that could help people affected by the bans.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRepublicans claim to be for small government \u8211\'20\'13 but where it comes to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  they are for big government. Traditional views about women \u8211\'20\'13 and disrespect for poor women \u8211\'20\'13 may blind Republicans to these contradictions,\u8221\'20\'1D said Reva Siegel, a Yale Law School professor who wrote a brief unsuccessfully urging the supreme court to overturn the Mississippi law at issue in the Dobbs case.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Republican party now has its chance to show the nation what pro-life means. In many states now banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , its policies are more concerned with control than care. I hope the party can change course, but I have yet to see a Republican jurisdiction that has developed a philosophy of social services even remotely appropriate to accompany laws requiring pregnant women to give birth.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The downfall of national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in America comes as the US remains an outlier among its wealthy peers in terms of social services. It famously has no national health insurance program, and is the only wealthy country not to offer paid family leave, according to the \uc2 Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Data shows that women who seek abortions tend to be the ones who could stand to benefit the most from social services. According to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , 75% of American \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients are poor and 60% already have a child.\par \par \uc2 State bans already on the books could {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Caitlin_K_Myers/status/1522540905881903104"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lead to an additional 75,000 births a year}}}\uc2 , Caitlin Knowles Myers, an economics professor at \uc2 Middlebury College\uc2 , predicted, and the costs of an unplanned pregnancy have been shown to be substantial. A \uc2 University of California, San Francisco{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ansirh.org/sites/default/files/publications/files/the_harms_of_denying_a_woman_a_wanted_abortion_4-16-2020.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 study}}}\uc2  found that women who wanted an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but could not get one saw their household poverty rates increase for at least four years as compared with women who were able to access the procedure, and also struggled to pay for necessities like food and transportation for years after.\par \par \uc2 The US is also a uniquely deadly place to give birth. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.commonwealthfund.org/publications/issue-briefs/2020/nov/maternal-mortality-maternity-care-us-compared-10-countries"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Commonwealth Fund found}}}\uc2  that in 2018, the United States\u8217\'20\'19s ratio of deaths to live births was more than twice that of most other wealthy countries.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kff.org/medicaid/issue-brief/status-of-state-medicaid-expansion-decisions-interactive-map/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 12 states still have not expanded Medicaid health insurance coverage for poor people}}}\uc2  offered under the Affordable Care Act, and of these, only Kansas and North Carolina are among those not immediately banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At the state level, there have been moves by lawmakers to address these disparities. The Republican-led legislatures in Georgia, Tennessee and Texas, for instance, have pushed to expand Medicaid coverage to women after they gave birth, even as they have declined to take part in the program\u8217\'20\'19s expansion. South Carolina and Georgia recently enacted legislation giving state employees paid parental leave.\par \par \uc2 But much of the most powerful social welfare legislation comes from Congress, where progress has been uneven. In 2021, Democrats pushed through a huge spending package that included a provision sending monthly checks to almost all families with children, which was credited {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://static1.squarespace.com/static/610831a16c95260dbd68934a/t/61f946b1cb0bb75fd2ca03ad/1643726515657/Child-Tax-Credit-Research-Roundup-CPSP-2021.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 with slashing child poverty}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  proposed continuing it in his big Build Back Better proposal to revamp social services and fight climate change, but the package won no Republican support and died amid infighting among Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Samuel Hammond, director of social policy at the Niskanen Center, said Republicans will now be under pressure to pass legislation to aid families and children by the same groups that wanted them to overturn the 49-year-old Roe v Wade ruling that allowed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide: social conservatives.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe kind of bargain they had was we will pass our tax cuts and deregulations and you will get conservative court justices,\u8221\'20\'1D Hammond said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd now that Roe has been repealed, you can\u8217\'20\'19t put Amy Coney Barrett on the court twice.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In the past weeks, Senate Republicans have announced legislation to expand aid to families, casting their proposals as \u8220\'20\'1Cpro-life\u8221\'20\'1D responses to the end of Roe. Florida\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Marco Rubio{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.rubio.senate.gov/public/index.cfm/press-releases?id=EE5F77FA-68D0-4B2D-B27A-D23B710161B4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has published a plan}}}\uc2  that would allow families to pull from their social security benefits to fund paid family leave, expand a tax credit meant to help families with children, while also allowing religious groups to play a greater role in federal social service programs.\par \par \uc2 In a press release filled with endorsements from anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.romney.senate.gov/romney-family-security-act-2-0-one-of-the-most-important-efforts-to-support-the-family-in-nearly-thirty-years/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 three senators announced}}}\uc2  a bill that would give families $350 each month for a young child and $250 for a child attending school. The proposal is similar to the child tax credit that Biden unsuccessfully tried to extend, but requires families to earn a certain amount to be eligible and, Hammond said, would be less effective at slashing poverty.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou want to make sure you\u8217\'20\'19re supporting families that are making some effort to support themselves,\u8221\'20\'1D Wilcox said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou don\u8217\'20\'19t want to be promoting policies that lock in support for a model of family life that is detached from work and from marriage, which are of course two of the key avenues for economic progress even in the 21st century.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 While the Democratic-led Congress has been deadlocked over social policy for months, Hammond predicted the proposal from the three Republican lawmakers could be worked into a larger tax bill that has to be passed by the year end, giving Republicans an opportunity to show that they want to help families.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhere they\u8217\'20\'19ve always pulled their punches is on these more proactive social policies,\u8221\'20\'1D Hammond said. \u8220\'20\'1CI think this is now the window for the Republicans to put their stamp on a major extension to the child credit in a way that\u8217\'20\'19s more generous to parents and make good on this outreach to parents and this Christian conservative minority.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220705ei75000rv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
