\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Tech companies in spotlight as US  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ruling sparks privacy threat\b0\par\par\uc2 Edward Helmore \par \uc2 948 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 July 2022\par 19:04 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  has acted amid fears police could use location and other data to prosecute those seeking care\par \par \uc2 In the wake of the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s decision to end women\u8217\'20\'19s constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , some tech companies are moving to close loopholes that allow personal data brokers to monitor and sell information amid fears that mobile apps could be used by US states to police \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  said on Friday it would {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2022/jul/01/google-delete-location-data-abortion-clinic"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 automatically delete records}}}\uc2  of user visits to sensitive locations, including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics. Privacy researchers as well as women\u8217\'20\'19s rights groups welcomed the move, having warned that apps used for period tracking, pregnancy and family planning could be used to prosecute those seeking reproductive care.\par \par \uc2 The \u8220\'20\'1Clocation history\u8221\'20\'1D feature on \uc2 Google\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Android services is typically turned off, the company said. But even if it is active, the company will now delete history of visits to places that many people would prefer to keep private.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSome of the places people visit \u8211\'20\'13 including medical facilities like counseling centers, domestic violence shelters, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, fertility centers, addiction treatment facilities, weight loss clinics, cosmetic surgery clinics, and others \u8211\'20\'13 can be particularly personal,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Google\uc2  said in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://blog.google/technology/safety-security/protecting-peoples-privacy-on-health-topics/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 post}}}\uc2  on its website.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf our systems identify that someone has visited one of these places, we will delete these entries from Location History soon after they visit.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Among other changes is an update to \uc2 Fitbit\uc2  software on wearable devices that allows users to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/28/why-us-woman-are-deleting-their-period-tracking-apps"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 track their periods}}}\uc2 . Users will now have an ability to delete those logs.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  did not say the policy change was in response to the decision to reverse the supreme court landmark Roe v Wade ruling, which in 1973 established nationwide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. But privacy experts have warned that should some states seek to criminalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedures, it could lead to patients and providers having their search, location, email and cloud data used against them in prosecutions.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Chin, a fellow with the Strategic Technologies Program at the Center for Strategic and International Studies in Washington DC, said there was an implicit challenge to privacy in the overturning of Roe, highlighted by the absence of a comprehensive federal data privacy law in the US.\par \par \uc2 The data ecosystem system is so leaky that companies aren\u8217\'20\'19t prepared to cut back on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  metadata without changing their entire models\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Chin\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBecause law enforcement officers often access data from the private sector, the massive scope and scale of data collection by internet platforms and consumer devices introduce unique questions over the appropriate limitations of government surveillance to enforce state laws,\u8221\'20\'1D Chin {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.csis.org/analysis/what-privacy-united-states-could-look-without-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote in a recent paper}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In an interview with the Guardian on Saturday, Chin pointed out that it is not just period or fitness apps that could be targeted by law enforcement. Many popular devices and mobile apps {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.markey.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/letters-_apple_google_play_store_data_policies.pdf?utm_campaign=wp_the_technology_202&utm_medium=email&utm_source=newsletter&wpisrc=nl_technology202"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 collect}}}\uc2  personal information, including geolocation, browsing activity, search history, private communications, social media posts, photos, videos and financial transactions, which are then used by developers, data aggregators, advertisers and other third parties.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a really huge problem,\u8221\'20\'1D Chin said. \u8220\'20\'1CCompanies have made it their business model to collect and share data and the data ecosystem system is so leaky that they aren\u8217\'20\'19t prepared essentially to cut back on collecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  metadata without changing their entire models.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Law enforcement agencies frequently access data held by private companies through a variety of mechanisms, including legal requests. From January to June 2020 alone, \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2 , and \uc2 Microsoft{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/how-big-tech-created-data-treasure-trove-for-police-e8a664c7814cc6dd560ba0e0c435bf90"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 received more than}}}\uc2  112,000 legal requests to access data from federal, state and local law enforcement agencies. They fulfilled approximately 85% of those requests.\par \par \uc2 From January to June 2021, \uc2 Google\uc2  received more than 149,000 requests, of which about 78% were fulfilled, according to its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://transparencyreport.google.com/user-data/overview?hl=en_US"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 transparency report.}}}\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  said on Friday that it would continue to resist improper or overly broad demands for data by the government. In another update, it said it would designate US advertisers as providing abortions even if they dispense pills by mail after a virtual consultation, but lack their own facilities.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe remain committed to protecting our users against improper government demands for data, and we will continue to oppose demands that are overly broad or otherwise legally objectionable,\u8221\'20\'1D the company said.\par \par \uc2 In statement after the Roe decision, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.eff.org/deeplinks/2022/05/what-companies-can-do-now-protect-digital-rights-post-roe-world"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  Electronic Frontier Foundation}}}\uc2  said: \u8220\'20\'1CService providers can expect a raft of subpoenas and warrants seeking user data that could be employed to prosecute \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  seekers, providers and helpers. They can also expect pressure to aggressively police the use of their services to provide information that may be classified in many states as facilitating a crime.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhatever your position on reproductive rights, this is a frightening prospect for data privacy and online expression,\u8221\'20\'1D the foundation added.\par \par \uc2 One prior case that worries data privacy experts is that of Latice Fisher, who was charged with second degree murder in 2017 after she experienced a pregnancy loss at home. Fisher was ultimately released from jail, and the charges against her were dropped \u8211\'20\'13 but not before law enforcement had looked up her search history that included a search for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills.\par \par \uc2 But while \uc2 Google\uc2  has acted over its location history software, if prosecutions begin to flow from the states enforcing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, the question over data brokers may only become more pressing.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are so many entities that collect location data,\u8221\'20\'1D Chin said. \u8220\'20\'1CWhile it\u8217\'20\'19s good that \uc2 Google\uc2  is stepping to say it will limit and delete data for certain locations, that is just one company. There are so many other entities collecting location data that could be a potential vessel for law enforcement.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Reuters contributed reporting\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220702ei720025t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
