\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 The week the supreme court reshaped America: \u8216\'20\'18We\u8217\'20\'19re being hurled back decades\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Victoria Bekiempis in New York \par \uc2 1,143 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 July 2022\par 19:12 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Momentous decisions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , guns, the environment and religion have left many wondering: what next?\par \par \uc2 Last week the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/us-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US Supreme Court}}}\uc2  started its summer break, but it left behind an America that many believe has been fundamentally reshaped after a momentous series of decisions by the conservative majority on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , guns, the power of government agencies, and the role of religion in public life.\par \par \uc2 The series of decisions have spurred {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/26/elizabeth-warren-democrats-question-supreme-court-legitimacy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 extensive condemnation}}}\uc2  outside conservative America and many are left wondering what, if anything, can be done.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re absolutely in a constitutional crisis,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lawrence Gostin, a law professor at \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2  and director of the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s center on Global Health Law. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd our democracy is now one of the most fragile democracies among our peer nations.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe haven\u8217\'20\'19t fallen over the cliff \u8211\'20\'13 we still abide by the rule of law, more or less, and still have elections, more or less \u8211\'20\'13 but the terms of our democracy have really been eviscerated by the supreme court.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The most consequential ruling of this term came on 24 June: on that day, a conservative super majority of justices struck down {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Roe v. Wade}}}\uc2 . The 1973 Supreme Court decision had held that US women have the Constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 By stripping women, girls, and other pregnant people of this longstanding constitutional right, approximately 26 states are poised to ban, or severely limit, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; some already have. Some of these states\u8217\'20\'19 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans make no exception for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis decision not only goes against the will of the people \u8211\'20\'13 the majority of people support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 it goes against modern progress, the progress of history,\u8221\'20\'1D said Coco Das, an organizer in Texas with Rise Up 4 \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Rights. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s based on biblical literalism, a fundamentalist Christian fanatical movement.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re being hurled back decades, if not centuries, to be honest,\u8221\'20\'1D Das said, later adding: \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re really trying to transform society to one that\u8217\'20\'19s dominated on the basis of white supremacy, male supremacy, Christian supremacy. It\u8217\'20\'19s very dangerous.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWithout the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , women can\u8217\'20\'19t be free, and if women aren\u8217\'20\'19t free, nobody\u8217\'20\'19s free.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 On 23 June, the supreme court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/23/us-supreme-court-new-york-law-gun-control"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 struck down}}}\uc2  a 1911 New York state gun law that imposed strict restrictions on carrying firearms outside the home. The decision, in New York State Rifle & Pistol Association v Bruen, came after mass shootings in Uvalde and Buffalo that left more than two dozen dead.\par \par \uc2 Conservative justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  said that the state law \u8211\'20\'13 which had stipulated that anyone desiring to carry a handgun in public needed \u8220\'20\'1Cproper cause\u8221\'20\'1D to do so \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/23/us-supreme-court-new-york-law-gun-control"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 violated}}}\uc2  the second amendment right to bear arms.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe decision ignores this shocking crisis of gun violence every day, engulfing not only New York, but our entire country,\u8221\'20\'1D said New York City mayor Eric Adams. \u8220\'20\'1CThe opinion claims to be based on [the nation\u8217\'20\'19s] historical past, but does not account for the reality of today. It ignores the present, and it endangers our future.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Several decisions handed down have intensified concerns that conservative justices no longer respect the separation of church and state. They {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2022/jun/27/supreme-court-coach-prayers-joseph-kennedy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voted}}}\uc2  in favor of an ex-public high school football coach who was suspended for praying with athletes on the field after games.\par \par \uc2 The justices also rejected a Maine law that prohibited religious schools from getting tuition aid from public funds, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thehill.com/homenews/house/3540071-boebert-says-she-is-tired-of-separation-between-church-and-state-the-church-is-supposed-to-direct-the-government/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to the Hill}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThis court continues to dismantle the wall of separation between church and state that the framers fought to build,\u8221\'20\'1D liberal justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  said in her dissent.\par \par \uc2 The court\u8217\'20\'19s recent decisions involving tribal lands and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/30/us-supreme-court-ruling-restricts-federal-power-greenhouse-gas-emissions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 environmental protections}}}\uc2  have also sparked criticism. The justices {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/30/scotus-decision-native-tribal-lands-oklahoma"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 decided}}}\uc2  Wednesday that state prosecutors may pursue criminal cases for crimes perpetrated by non-Native persons against Native persons on tribal land.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWith today\u8217\'20\'19s decision, the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  ruled against legal precedent and the basic principles of congressional authority and Indian law,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://anadisgoi.com/index.php/government-stories/983-statement-from-cherokee-nation-principal-chief-on-supreme-court-decision-in-oklahoma-v-castro-huerta"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 commented}}}\uc2  Chuck Hoskin Jr, Cherokee nation principal chief.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2022/feb/28/supreme-court-epa-biden-climate-crisis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 supported litigation }}}\uc2  brought by West Virginia that insisted the \uc2 US Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) \uc2 be restricted in its regulation of planet-heating gasses from the energy industry.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe decision to side with polluters over the public will cost American lives and cause an enormous amount of preventable suffering, with the biggest burden falling on low-income communities and communities of color,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/MikeBloomberg/status/1542510844931502080?ref_src=twsrc%5Egoogle%7Ctwcamp%5Eserp%7Ctwgr%5Etweet"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2 Michael Bloomberg\uc2 , a \uc2 UN\uc2  special envoy and former New York City mayor.\par \par \uc2 Legal experts said that steps can be taken to prevent the Supreme Court from exerting limitless power. Several have suggested that lawmakers could enact term limits, for example.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe don\u8217\'20\'19t have to just accede to this notion that the supreme court has the last word on every consequential issue in the country,\u8221\'20\'1D said Gabe Roth, executive director of the judicial watchdog non-profit Fix the Court. \u8220\'20\'1CCongress can step in and pass laws that protect certain rights.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are people who are really focusing on what folks might want to do in November, but there are plenty of legislative days between now and then to get some bills passed,\u8221\'20\'1D Roth continued. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re in new territory here, and elected officials need to be up to the task of reining in a runaway court\u8217\'20\'19s power.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The constitution grants lifetime tenure to supreme court justices. Roth believes, however, that there are legislative ways that could move them to senior status \u8211\'20\'13 meaning they would keep their lifetime appointment as required, but not wield decades-long decision making power.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDoing so would send a signal to the justices that they don\u8217\'20\'19t have the final say\u8211\'20\'13ensuring that these \u8216\'20\'18philosopher kings\u8217\'20\'19 aren\u8217\'20\'19t ruling over us for 30 or 40 years is an important step in that direction.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Gostin suggested term limits on the court, and/or an \u8220\'20\'1Cindependent rigorous evaluation of potential justices by an independent panel\u8221\'20\'1D. That panel, in turn, would provide a list of judicial candidates to the president.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThose two are very much established in other countries around the world. That would make a lot of sense,\u8221\'20\'1D Gostin said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe problem is: There\u8217\'20\'19s no political appetite for it, and you need to get Congress to buy into it and the Republicans surely do not want that, because they see the supreme court appointees as a war.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey are winning the war, so why would they change the terms of engagement?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Gostin also invoked a martial analogy when discussing recent court decisions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt seems like the supreme court is at war with the American people,\u8221\'20\'1D Gostin said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s certainly serving the desires and passions of one third of the country, but it\u8217\'20\'19s completely ignoring the consequences for the vast majority of the country.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220702ei72000rt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
