\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Biden calls court\u8217\'20\'19s Roe ruling \u8216\'20\'18tragic reversal\u8217\'20\'19 during meeting with Democratic governors \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Gambino in Washington \par \uc2 2,833 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 July 2022\par 20:26 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 President: \u8216\'20\'18I share the public outrage of this extremist court\u8217\'20\'19Thanks to state courts, abortions can resume in four statesAnti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement achieved its goal \u8211\'20\'13 but it is from doneSign up to receive First Thing \u8211\'20\'13 our daily briefing by email\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.18pm BST\par \par \uc2 Evening summary\par \par \uc2 This afternoon \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  met with a group of Democratic governors to highlight their efforts to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . During the meeting, Biden called the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling a \u8220\'20\'1Ctragic reversal\u8221\'20\'1D and again vowed that the federal government was exploring more actions it could take to help women access reproductive care.\par \par \uc2 * Speaking from the White House, Biden said the administration had already taken steps to protect women. He said the Justice Department would defend anyone who travels to another state to have \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and said the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  was working to make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medications more available. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is not over,\u8221\'20\'1D he promised.\par \par \uc2 * Biden acknowledged that Democrats do not have enough votes in the Senate to change the filibuster rules to pass a bill protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other privacy rates. He urged Americans to vote for pro-choice candidates, noting that two more Democratic senators would likely be enough to carve out an exception in the filibuster to pass \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 * The governors of New York and New Mexico urged Biden to consider using federal lands in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned or severely restricted to provide reproductive care. The White House has so far dismissed the suggestion as \u8220\'20\'1Cwell intentioned\u8221\'20\'1D but impractically and potentially risky.\par \par \uc2 * Biden also warned that if Republicans win control of Congress they will seek to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide.\par \par \uc2 * Biden also announced that he will award the presidential medal of freedom to 17 people, including actor Denzel Washington, gymnast Simone Biles and the late Arizona senator, \uc2 John McCain\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s all from us this week. But for more, we invite you to listen to the latest episode of Politics Weekly America. This week, columnists Jonathan Freedland and Jill Filipovic discuss \u8220\'20\'1Cwhether it\u8217\'20\'19s still possible for a deeply divided court of nine judges, a group that now has a 6-3 conservative majority, to keep the promise to the American people of \u8216\'20\'18equal protection\u8217\'20\'19, and what happens if it can\u8217\'20\'19t.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.18pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.44pm BST\par \par \uc2 Biden concluded the public portion of the meeting, but asked the governors to stick around so they could discuss ways in which the federal government might act to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 During a press conference yesterday, Biden suggested that he might unveil a series of new actions but there was no such announcement.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.35pm BST\par \par \uc2 Speaking first, New York governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , said her state is acting quickly to shore up women\u8217\'20\'19s reproductive rights in its constitution and protect access to contraception and other rights.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is frightening time for women all across our nation, a lot of fear and anxiety out there,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Hochul also pushed Biden to use federal lands for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services \u8211\'20\'13 a suggestion that the White House has so far dismissed as \u8220\'20\'1Cwell-intentioned\u8221\'20\'1D but potentially risky.\par \par \uc2 Next we\u8217\'20\'19re hearing from North Carolina governor, \uc2 Roy Cooper\uc2 , a Democratic in a Republican-leaning state.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis democratic governor is going to hold the line to protect women\u8217\'20\'19s reproductive freedom in our state,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. But he said he needs more Democrats in the state legislature to help sustain his vetos of Republican bills that seek to ban or severely restrict abortions.\par \par \uc2 Already he said North Carolina is seeing an influx of patients from other states with bans and tighter restrictions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are in fact that brick wall against this horrific supreme court decision,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Michelle Lujan Grisham\uc2 , the governor of New Mexico said. She outlined the ways New Mexico was preparing to be a haven for women coming from neighboring states that have already outlawed abortions.\par \par \uc2 She also pressed Biden to do more at the federal level to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, such as setting up \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics on tribal lands, should a tribe want to open private clinics for non-Native Americans to receive care.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.56pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.26pm BST\par \par \uc2 Biden calls Roe ruling a 'tragic reversal'\par \par \uc2 Opening the meeting with Democratic governors, Biden called the court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a \u8220\'20\'1Ctragic reversal\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI share the public outrage of this extremist court that is committed to moving America backwards,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said. He vowed to fight to protect women\u8217\'20\'19s rights: \u8220\'20\'1CThis is not over.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He pointed to two steps the administration has taken to increase the availability of medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and protect women who travel out-of-state for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He also warned that if Republicans won control of Congress they would try to pass a nationwide ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Per the White House, the Democratic governors participating in Friday\u8217\'20\'19s meeting are:\par \par \uc2 Ned Lamont\uc2 , Governor of Connecticut\par \par \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , Governor of New York\par \par \uc2 Michelle Lujan Grisham\uc2 , Governor of New Mexico\par \par \uc2 JB Pritzker, Governor of Illinois\par \par \uc2 Jay Inslee\uc2 , Governor of Washington\par \par \uc2 Kate Brown, Governor or Oregon\par \par \uc2 Roy Cooper\uc2 , Governor of North Carolina\par \par \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2 , Governor of Colorado\par \par \uc2 Dan McKee\uc2 , Governor of Rhode Island\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.56pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.57pm BST\par \par \uc2 As we await Biden\u8217\'20\'19s appearance with Democratic governors, the White House announced that the president will travel to Cleveland, Ohio next week.\par \par \uc2 There he will speak about his \u8220\'20\'1Ceconomic agenda and building the economy from the bottom up and the middle out,\u8221\'20\'1D the White House said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.59pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.31pm BST\par \par \uc2 In what has become something of a pattern for Republicans, an Utah lawmaker has apologized for a bizarre comment that suggested women could do more to prevent pregnancies resulting from rape. (See: Todd Akin.)\par \par \uc2 According to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sltrib.com/news/politics/2022/06/24/utah-republicans-take/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Salt Lake Tribune}}}\uc2 , Utah state representative, Karianne Lisonbee, said during a press conference that she had received messages urging lawmakers should also hold men accountable for unwanted pregnancies in the wake of the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling on Roe.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI got a text message today saying I should seek to control men\u8217\'20\'19s ejaculations and not women\u8217\'20\'19s pregnancies,\u8221\'20\'1D Lisonbee reportedly said. She added: \u8220\'20\'1CI do trust women enough to control when they allow a man to ejaculate inside of them and to control that intake of semen.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In a statement to the paper, she clarified her remarks and pointed to her efforts to expand protections for victims of sexual assault.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWomen do not have a choice when they are raped and have protections under Utah\u8217\'20\'19s trigger law,\u8221\'20\'1D she told the \uc2 Tribune\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThe political and social divide in America seems to be expanding at an ever-faster pace. I am committed to ongoing respectful and civil engagement. I can always do better and will continue to try.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jul/01/utah-republican-women-control-intake-of-semen-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Utah Republican apologises for saying women can control \u8216\'20\'18intake of semen\u8217\'20\'19}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.20pm BST\par \par \uc2 Here are the other names of individuals who will receive the presidential medal of freedom next week.\par \par \uc2 Julieta Garc\u237\'00\'EDa, t he former president of The University of Texas at Brownsville and the first Hispanic woman to serve as a college president\par \par \uc2 Father Alexander Karloutsos, the former Vicar General of the Greek Orthodox Archdiocese of America.\par \par \uc2 Sandra Lindsay, a New York critical care nurse who was the first American to receive a COVID-19 vaccine outside of clinical trial.\par \par \uc2 Alan Simpson, a former Republican senator from Wyoming who advocated for campaign finance reform, responsible governance, and marriage equality.\par \par \uc2 Wilma Vaught, one of the most decorated women in the history of the US military.\par \par \uc2 Ra\u250\'00\'FAl Yzaguirre, a civil rights advocate who served as CEO and president of \uc2 National Council of La Raza\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.03pm BST\par \par \uc2 Gymnast Simon Biles, actor Denzel Washington, the late \uc2 Apple\uc2  founder, \uc2 Steve Jobs\uc2 , soccer player Megan Rapinoe, the late Arizona senator \uc2 John McCain\uc2 , and former Congresswoman Gabby Giffords are among the 17 people who will receive the Presidential Medal of Freedom this month.\par \par \uc2 It is the nation\u8217\'20\'19s highest civil honor, presented by the president to individuals who have \u8220\'20\'1Cdemonstrate[d] the power of possibilities and embody the soul of the nation \u8211\'20\'13 hard work, perseverance, and faith,\u8221\'20\'1D the White House said in a press release.\par \par \uc2 Biden will present the awards during a ceremony at the White House on 7 July.\par \par \uc2 Recipients also include barrier-breaking activists and lawmakers such as Sister Simone Campbell, a Catholic social justice advocate, Fred Gray, one of the first black members of the Alabama State legislature, Diane Nash, a founding member of the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee (SNCC), \uc2 Richard Trumka\uc2 , the late leader of the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 , and Khizr Khan, a Gold Star father who rose to prominence when he challenged Trump\u8217\'20\'19s commitment to the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.04pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.10pm BST\par \par \uc2 Republican congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  is in the fight of her political life as she tries to keep her seat while leading the charge against her party\u8217\'20\'19s most popular figure, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Last night she participated in a debate against her opponent, the one-time Trump critic turned loyalist Harriet Hageman. Here\u8217\'20\'19s Martin Pengelly\u8217\'20\'19s write up of the event.\par \par \uc2 Down in the polls and facing losing her seat in Congress over her opposition to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and membership of the House January 6 committee, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  came out swinging in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/cheney-to-debate-wyoming-gop-foes-after-jan-6-hearings/2022/06/30/6fb0a134-f837-11ec-81db-ac07a394a86b_story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a Republican debate in Wyoming}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe truth matters,\u8221\'20\'1D she {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://oilcity.news/community/2022/06/30/watch-rep-liz-cheney-debates-gop-primary-opponents/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  in Sheridan on Thursday night, targeting Harriet Hageman, the candidate endorsed by Trump, and challenging her to say the 2020 election was not stolen.\par \par \uc2 Hageman did not do so.\par \par \uc2 Cheney, Hageman and three other candidates will contest the primary on 16 August. Hageman {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/politics/story/2022-06-27/liz-cheney-january-6-wyoming-voters"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 leads}}}\uc2  by about 30 points in polls.\par \par \uc2 Cheney, a strict conservative and the daughter of the former congressman, defense secretary and vice-president \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2 , has been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/06/23/us/politics/liz-cheney-wyoming-democrats.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 seeking}}}\uc2  to convince Democrats to switch registration and back her.\par \par \uc2 On the debate stage, Cheney said: \u8220\'20\'1C I\u8217\'20\'19m frankly stunned that one of my opponents on the stage who is a member of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/wyoming"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Wyoming}}}\uc2  bar, who has sworn an oath as many of us on this stage have to the constitution, would be in a position where she is suggesting that somehow what happened on January 6 was justified or that somehow \u8230\'20\'26 the people have the right to ignore the rulings of the courts.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jul/01/liz-cheney-harriet-hageman-wyoming-republican-primary-debate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Truth matters\u8217\'20\'19: Liz Cheney lambasts Trump-backed rival in Wyoming debate}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.35pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.48pm BST\par \par \uc2 Congress has a very short window to act before the August recess. And in a campaign year, these few weeks are perhaps lawmakers last best chance to pass meaningful legislation before the November elections.\par \par \uc2 One of the biggest ticket items on their very long to-do list is a bill aimed at boosting research and domestic manufacturing to be more competitive with China. The legislation has gone through many iterations \u8211\'20\'13 and names \u8211\'20\'13 in the months since a bipartisan group of lawmakers first introduced the proposal but it recently it appeared to be on track. Then on Thursday, Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , threw an unexpected wrench in the plan.\par \par \uc2 McConnell warned that if Democrats pursue a reconciliation bill, which is shaping up to be a dramatically scaled back version of Biden\u8217\'20\'19s climate and social policy agenda, Republicans would block the China competitiveness bill.\par \par \uc2 enltrLet me be perfectly clear: there will be no bipartisan USICA as long as Democrats are pursuing a partisan reconciliation bill.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Leader McConnell (@LeaderMcConnell) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/LeaderMcConnell/status/1542600738823618564?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 30, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 It is only the latest example of how the Republican leader wields his power - even from the minority.\par \par \uc2 How this plays out is anyone\u8217\'20\'19s guess. There is a lot of bipartisan and industry support for the competitiveness bill, blandly referred to as the United States Innovation and Competition Act or USICA for short.\par \par \uc2 Lining up 50 votes to pass the Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 reconciliation package was always going to be a heavy lift for Senate majority leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 . You\u8217\'20\'19ll remember Biden\u8217\'20\'19s sweeping agenda collapsed last year amid opposition from Democrats Joe Manchin and \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2 . But talks have begun anew with the aim of crafting a potentially $1tn compromise package. Pressuring is building on Democrats to use their majorities to pass a climate bill in light of the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision on Thursday {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/30/us-supreme-court-ruling-restricts-federal-power-greenhouse-gas-emissions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 limiting the Environmental Protection Agency\u8217\'20\'19s authority}}}\uc2  to regulate the greenhouse gas emissions.\par \par \uc2 But many of the sticking points remain, particularly around reforms to the tax code.\par \par \uc2 Manchin wants to repeal the 2017 Trump tax cuts, but a group of House Democrats, whose votes are almost certainly needed to pass the bill in the lower chamber, won\u8217\'20\'19t back and plan to change the tax code without also repealing a cap on the amount of state and local taxes that homeowners can deduct.\par \par \uc2 There are a lot of details to be ironed out and very few calendar days left to do it.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.04pm BST\par \par \uc2 At the end of this week\u8217\'20\'19s explosive hearing by the House committee investigating the January 6 attack, vice chair \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  made a startling announcement: members had evidence that allies of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had attempted to \u8220\'20\'1Cinfluence\u8221\'20\'1D witness testimony.\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Hugo Lowell has confirmed at least one of the witnesses was former White House aide Cassidy Hutchinson, who testified this week that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/28/january-6-committee-session-new-evidence"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  Trump knowingly directed armed supporters to march to the Capitol }}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 and tried to join them.\par \par \uc2 Former Trump White House aide Cassidy Hutchinson received at least one message tacitly warning her not to cooperate with the House January 6 select committee from an associate of former White House chief of staff {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/mark-meadows"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mark Meadows}}}\uc2 , according to two sources familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 Ex-White House aide delivers explosive public testimony to January 6 panelRead more\par \par \uc2 The message in question was the second of the two warnings that the select committee disclosed at the end of its special hearing when Hutchinson testified about how {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2  directed a crowd he knew was armed to march on the Capitol, the sources said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C[A person] let me know you have your deposition tomorrow. He wants me to let you know that he\u8217\'20\'19s thinking about you. He knows you\u8217\'20\'19re loyal, and you\u8217\'20\'19re going to do the right thing when you go in for your deposition,\u8221\'20\'1D read the message. The redaction was Meadows, the sources said.\par \par \uc2 The message was presented during closing remarks {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/28/january-6-committee-session-new-evidence"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 at the special hearing with Hutchinson}}}\uc2  by the panel\u8217\'20\'19s vice-chair, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , who characterized the missive as improper pressure on a crucial witness that could extend to illegal witness tampering or intimidation.\par \par \uc2 The exact identity of the person who sent Hutchinson the message \u8211\'20\'13 beyond the fact that they were an associate of Meadows \u8211\'20\'13 could not be confirmed on Thursday, but that may be in part because the select committee may wish to interview that person, the sources said.\par \par \uc2 That appears to indicate that the person who sent the message was a close associate of the former White House chief of staff who may themselves be a fact witness to what Trump and Meadows were doing and thinking ahead of the Capitol attack.\par \par \uc2 Neither a spokesman for Meadows nor Hutchinson responded to a request for comment on Thursday evening.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/30/jan-6-hearings-trump-mark-meadows-cassidy-hutchinson-threatened"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mark Meadows\u8217\'20\'19 associate threatened ex-White House aide before her testimony}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.38pm BST\par \par \uc2 Good morning and welcome to our coverage of all things politics. It\u8217\'20\'19s Friday in Washington DC, which means most lawmakers have fled the capital for the holiday weekend, leaving behind an ever-growing list of urgent and unfinished business.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will meet today with six Democratic governors to discuss protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the wake of the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision to overturn Roe v Wade. At a press conference in Madrid on Thursday, Biden said he would announce additional actions the White House would take to secure reproductive care.\par \par \uc2 He has been under pressure from a number of Democratic lawmakers and progressives alarmed by what they view as the administration\u8217\'20\'19s belated and overly cautious response to this moment of crisis. So far, the White House has pushed back against calls to use federal lands for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, and other proposals it worries could have unintended legal risks for patients and providers.\par \par \uc2 Some states that halted abortions in the immediate, chaotic aftermath of the high court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling have resumed offering the procedure while lower courts consider challenges to the new restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Read more here.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jul/01/abortion-us-states-supreme-court-the-19th"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Thanks to state courts, abortions can resume in four US states. For now }}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220701ei71001xh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
