\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Thanks to state courts, abortions can resume in four US states. For now\b0\par\par\uc2 Shefali Luthra and Chabeli Carrazana for the 19th \par \uc2 1,903 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 July 2022\par 12:25 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Some clinics have resumed services while courts weigh the legality of state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans \u8211\'20\'13 and they\u8217\'20\'19re trying to provide as much care as they canOriginally published by the 19th\par \par \uc2 Patients burst into tears on the phone. For once, it was the good kind.\par \par \uc2 Starting Tuesday, at least for short time, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be legal again in Texas, and Andrea Gallegos had no time to lose. Her clinic, Alamo Women\u8217\'20\'19s Reproductive Services in San Antonio, had been closed since Friday. They had been forced to send patients home when Roe v Wade was overturned, canceling 25 scheduled abortions. Patients originally scheduled to come in Monday and Tuesday lost their appointments as well.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, her main thought was, how many patients could she see? How many could come back today?\par \par \uc2 She started dialing, first calling the people she had sent home on Friday, then moving on to the Monday patients, then the ones originally slated for Tuesday. It was the first time in a while she felt she\u8217\'20\'19d been the bearer of good news.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey were incredibly grateful that we called and reached out,\u8221\'20\'1D Gallegos said. \u8220\'20\'1CTheir first reaction was, \u8216\'20\'18How soon do you need me to be there?\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The days since Roe v Wade was overturned have been a morass of unprecedented legal chaos. States have started to enforce trigger laws, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans written specifically to take effect if and when federal protections were overturned, as well as statutes like the one in Texas, pre-Roe laws that have never been repealed. But in Louisiana, Utah, Texas and Kentucky \u8211\'20\'13 four states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was banned just days ago \u8211\'20\'13 the procedure can resume. For now.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics are now challenging these same \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans \u8211\'20\'13 arguing in lawsuits before state courts that the laws don\u8217\'20\'19t comply with state constitutions, or that they are otherwise fundamentally flawed. So far, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://19thnews.org/2022/06/louisiana-utah-judges-block-trigger-laws/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have been temporarily blocked}}}\uc2  in four states: Texas, Louisiana, Utah and Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 Now, clinics in those states are figuring out next steps \u8212\'20\'14 or if they can feasibly offer services in an indeterminate window of time.\par \par \uc2 When the supreme court overturned Roe v Wade on Friday, Texas officials began enforcing a criminal statute banning the procedure \u8211\'20\'13 one that predated the landmark Roe decision. Clinics across the state ceased providing abortions.\par \par \uc2 Almost right away, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups challenged the Texas ban. On Tuesday, a state judge announced that she would hear arguments on 12 July, in two weeks\u8217\'20\'19 time. Until then \u8211\'20\'13 assuming the state doesn\u8217\'20\'19t appeal to a higher court faster \u8211\'20\'13 abortions would be able to resume under the state\u8217\'20\'19s six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 That Tuesday, 10 of the 25 Friday patients came in to Gallegos\u8217\'20\'19 San Antonio clinic to receive an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A handful didn\u8217\'20\'19t pick up the phone. Some told Gallegos they had found appointments in New Mexico \u8211\'20\'13 they would rather wait a few weeks and go out of state for care than take an appointment that might be canceled yet again.\par \par \uc2 And still others who were eligible for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday were now beyond six weeks of pregnancy. The two days without legal access had put them past the state limit.\par \par \uc2 Not all clinics in Louisiana, Utah and Texas have resumed providing abortions, and some of the ones that have reopened are doing it with limited staffing as they try to call providers back in. In Texas, multiple Planned Parenthood sites are still not scheduling abortions. But staffers at the clinics that are say this window feels like a last chance to provide care to people who are often desperate for help. All believe the window is temporary \u8211\'20\'13 and probably short-lived.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEven though it was a small feat of just seeing 10 patients, that was 10 more people that go to access healthcare,\u8221\'20\'1D Gallegos said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s still a victory.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Whole Woman\u8217\'20\'19s Health, which operates four clinics in Texas, told the 19th as of Wednesday that it was booking patients. Whole Woman\u8217\'20\'19s has specialized in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care for two decades, and has served as an access point across five states. They plan to continue offering abortions until they are forced to close, said Marva Sadler, the senior director for clinical services.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have always been \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  forward \u8211\'20\'13 that\u8217\'20\'19s what we do,\u8221\'20\'1D Sadler said. \u8220\'20\'1COur work with Texas women is not over.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The states all have other restrictions still on their books. In Texas, Louisiana and Kentucky, patients are required to wait 24 hours after their first appointment before they can have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In Utah, the waiting period is even longer at 72 hours.\par \par \uc2 The patients who came on Tuesday to Gallegos\u8217\'20\'19s clinic had already had their first of two appointments, so they could get their abortions. But on Wednesday, her staff wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be able to perform any. At 9am, they started seeing patients for their \u8220\'20\'1Cpre-admits.\u8221\'20\'1D On Thursday \u8211\'20\'13 assuming people were still under six weeks of pregnancy and that another court had not reversed the decision \u8211\'20\'13 those patients could all come back for their actual abortions.\par \par \uc2 But those all feel like big assumptions. Already, Gallegos is warning every patient she schedules that at a moment\u8217\'20\'19s notice, their appointments could be canceled yet again.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI personally don\u8217\'20\'19t feel like we have very long,\u8221\'20\'1D Gallegos said. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ll be really surprised if we get through this week.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In Louisiana, clinics are also racing to see as many patients as they can seen before 8 July, when the first hearing will take place in the case that granted a temporary restraining order that allowed abortions to resume on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Amy Irvin, the spokesperson for Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Care Center in New Orleans and Delta Clinic of Baton Rouge, two of the three \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in the entire state, said the past week had felt like emotional whiplash.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the New Orleans clinic called 65 patients who were scheduled for the mandatory counseling appointments ahead of their abortions to let them know they could not be seen that day. Irvin said one patient was so distraught by the news that she threatened to kill herself. Clinic staff worked to get her an appointment at the nearest clinic in Colorado, more than 1,000 miles away.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, when the temporary restraining order was put in place allowing abortions to resume, that woman was already many miles away, while numerous others were making plans to travel to neighboring states, including New Mexico and Illinois, spending {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://19thnews.org/2022/06/abortion-cost-inflation-travel-gas-airfare-medical-supplies/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hundreds more dollars}}}\uc2  to access care that was now suddenly legal again in Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 By Monday morning, calls went out to those who were turned away first, and new appointments scheduled while the procedure was temporarily legal again.\par \par \uc2 The challenge facing clinics in Louisiana, Texas and Utah now is availability.\par \par \uc2 Both Louisiana clinics were only able to open on a limited schedule this week because some of their staff had already made other arrangements for work. Already, it\u8217\'20\'19s difficult to staff \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics because of the specialized training needed \u8211\'20\'13 doctors are typically flown in for procedures. Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Care Center and Delta Clinic opened just one day this week: the Baton Rouge clinic on Tuesday and the New Orleans clinic on Thursday. The third clinic in the state, Hope Medical Group for Women in Shreveport, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://lailluminator.com/2022/06/27/louisiana-abortion-clinics-resume-services-after-judge-temporarily-blocks-ban/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 resumed abortions on Tuesday}}}\uc2  and will continue to offer appointments through the week.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn less than 72 hours, physicians and staff thought they were out of a job, that they were not going to be able to see patients, and then on Monday to be notified the clinic is reopening,\u8221\'20\'1D Irvin said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe physicians and staff are being called back on very short notice.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Care Center and Delta Clinic are hoping they can open more days next week.\par \par \uc2 Already, the confusion and uncertainty will drive patients deeper into their pregnancies before they can make a decision about where it will be even legal for them to access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, clinic staff told the 19th.\par \par \uc2 More than a thousand miles away, that\u8217\'20\'19s what worries Karrie Galloway, who heads the Planned Parenthood Association of Utah, which operates three of the state\u8217\'20\'19s four clinics.\par \par \uc2 The state government certified its trigger ban on Friday night, allowing a total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban to go into effect immediately. But by then, the clinic was already closed, with 13 patients on the schedule for Saturday. That morning, her staff was forced to cancel all of those procedures. Some patients had already shown up at the clinic before being told abortions were no longer legal in Utah. Others found out over the phone, in calls Galloway described as \u8220\'20\'1Cheart-wrenching\u8221\'20\'1D. Patients were angry, she said. And she could hardly blame them.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re telling them that a faceless political person has commandeered the authority over their own body,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 That day, her organization filed suit in state court. On Monday, they hoped for the best: patients filling the waiting room in the off chance that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban would be lifted that day.\par \par \uc2 At roughly 3.40 that afternoon, Galloway marched down the stairs from her office, going straight into the clinic. Their lawsuit had succeeded. The state judge was going to sign an order temporarily blocking Utah\u8217\'20\'19s trigger law. For two more weeks, at least, they could keep seeing patients.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt just felt so good after a bunch of bad news after bad news to be able to say to people, \u8216\'20\'18It\u8217\'20\'19s OK, we\u8217\'20\'19re going to work it out,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Staff at Planned Parenthood\u8217\'20\'19s three Utah clinics moved swiftly, signing in for their shifts right away and getting to work seeing patients. That afternoon, they provided five abortions. Across the three clinics, they put 33 more patients on the schedule for the next day.\par \par \uc2 Galloway\u8217\'20\'19s staff plans to work full-tilt over the next two weeks, providing care up to the state\u8217\'20\'19s legal limit of 22 weeks. There are limits to what they can do. All of the doctors who work at her clinics have other full-time jobs. Still, she said, they plan to do all they can to see as many patients as they can. And in two weeks, they\u8217\'20\'19ll go make their case yet again. She\u8217\'20\'19s still hopeful they can convince the court that, even without Roe, Utah\u8217\'20\'19s own state constitution guarantees the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 that Utah\u8217\'20\'19s trigger law cannot stand.\par \par \uc2 Otherwise, she said, she can\u8217\'20\'19t imagine what will happen to her patients. Some might travel out of state to clinics in Colorado, which is seven hours away from Salt Lake City, or to Las Vegas, a similar distance. Others could drive west, to California.\par \par \uc2 But she knows many of the people she serves won\u8217\'20\'19t be able to make that journey. It\u8217\'20\'19s too far. It\u8217\'20\'19s too expensive.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat is only available to a certain percentage of the population,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CPeople who have financial resources, emotional resources and support that allow them to make a seven-hour drive each way, and who have money for a plane or bus ticket.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://membership.theguardian.com/event/what-will-the-end-of-abortion-rights-in-america-mean-for-the-world-358372139717"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 What would the end of \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights in America mean for the world}}}\uc2  ? Join our live discussion on Wednesday, 6 July, 1pm-2pm ET. Book tickets {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://membership.theguardian.com/event/what-will-the-end-of-abortion-rights-in-america-mean-for-the-world-358372139717"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220701ei71001e3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
