\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  sworn in to supreme court after ruling deals blow to climate crisis \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Gambino \par \uc2 4,850 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 June 2022\par 20:19 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Jackson sworn in as first Black female supreme court justiceSupreme court hobbles government power to limit emissionsBiden can end Trump\u8217\'20\'19s \u8216\'20\'18Remain in Mexico\u8217\'20\'19, supreme court rulesBiden supports changing filibuster rules to codify RoeJanuary 6 panel subpoenas former White House counselSign up to receive First Thing \u8211\'20\'13 our daily briefing by email\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.09pm BST\par \par \uc2 Closing summary\par \par \uc2 Today marked the end an extraordinary term for the supreme court, the aftershocks of which will be felt for years, decades and perhaps even generations to come. From \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to climate, prayer in school to guns, American life looks differently today than it did just a few weeks ago. The court itself also looks differently. For the first time in its more than 200 year history, a Black women will sit on the court.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what else happened today.\par \par \uc2 * The supreme court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/30/us-supreme-court-ruling-restricts-federal-power-greenhouse-gas-emissions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sided with conservative states}}}\uc2  in a ruling with profound implications for the global effort to tackle the climate crisis. In a statement, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  vowed to find new ways to limit greenhouse gas emissions and transition to renewable energy.\par \par \uc2 * In its final decision of the term, a majority of justices agreed that Biden could end his predecessor\u8217\'20\'19s controversial immigration policy.\par \par \uc2 * A judge in Florida said he would temporarily block a law banning abortions after 15-weeks from taking effect.\par \par \uc2 * New polling by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnorc.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Associated Press-NORC Center for Public Affairs Research}}}\uc2  suggests that half of all Americans believe \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  should be charged over his actions on January 6.\par \par \uc2 * The Justice Department on Thursday announced it was opening an investigation into the \uc2 New York Police Department\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s special victims division after concluding that there was \u8220\'20\'1Csignificant justification\u8221\'20\'1D to examine its handling of sex-abuse cases.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.54pm BST\par \par \uc2 In a new piece for the Guardian, climate scientist Peter Kalmus warns that the Supreme Court\u8217\'20\'19s decision will have far-reaching and devastating consequences for the planet \u8211\'20\'13 and humanity.\par \par \uc2 In an era of crises, global heating increasingly stands out as the single greatest emergency humanity faces,\u8221\'20\'1D Kalmus writes. \u8220\'20\'1CGlobal heating is driving {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/15/us-weather-heat-warnings-alerts-record-temperatures"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 extreme heat}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/science/environment/lake-mead-nears-dead-pool-status-water-levels-hit-another-historic-low-rcna34733"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 drought}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/06/24/world/asia/sylhet-bangladesh-floods.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 flooding}}}\uc2  in the US and around the world. It\u8217\'20\'19s driving {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thehill.com/policy/equilibrium-sustainability/3535264-climate-change-fueling-worsening-droughts-wildfires/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wildfire}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1029/2021EF002608"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ecosystem collapse}}}\uc2 , and may already be contributing to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/6/6/famine-risk-rises-in-somalia-as-rains-fail-food-prices-soar-un"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 famine}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/03/18/climate/climate-armed-conflict-water.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 warfare}}}\uc2 . Crucially, this is all worsening day by day, and it will continue to worsen until we end the fossil fuel industry.\par \par \uc2 ...\par \par \uc2 Without a livable planet, nothing else matters. As the Earth\u8217\'20\'19s capacity to support life continues to degrade, millions, eventually billions of people will be displaced and die, fascism will rise, climate wars will intensify and the rule of law will break down. The myth of American exceptionalism will offer no protection from deadly heat and climate famine.\par \par \uc2 In the US we now live under the sway of robed, superstitious fools hellbent on rolling back basic civil liberties and rejecting scientific facts. \uc2 Carl Sagan\uc2 , warning against this sort of anti-science, wrote: \u8220\'20\'1CThe candle flame gutters. Its little pool of light trembles. Darkness gathers. The demons begin to stir.\u8221\'20\'1D The consequences of ignoring scientists for too long are coming home to roost.\par \par \uc2 We desperately need a government {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.carbonbrief.org/guest-post-how-the-us-can-cut-its-emissions-in-half-by-2030/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 working to stop Earth\u8217\'20\'19s breakdown}}}\uc2  rather than accelerate it, but petitions or pleas to \u8220\'20\'1Cvote harder\u8221\'20\'1D will not make this happen. Due to capture by the ultra-rich, our only option is to fight. To shift society into {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2021/sep/10/net-zero-2050-deadly-procrastination-fossil-fuels"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 emergency mode}}}\uc2  and end the fossil fuel industry, we must join together and do all we can to wake people up to the grave danger we are in. We must {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://insideclimatenews.org/todaysclimate/in-scientist-rebellion-researchers-face-arrest-for-climate-action/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 engage in climate disobedience}}}\uc2 . I believe that the tides could still turn, that power could shift suddenly. But this can only happen when enough people join the fight.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/jun/30/us-supreme-court-epa-decision-devastating-humanity"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The US supreme court just made yet another devastating decision for humanity | Peter Kalmus}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.56pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.49pm BST\par \par \uc2 As Democrats search for ways to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, a group of liberal senators are calling on the Pentagon to ensure military servicemembers will have access to the procedure regardless of where they are stationed.\par \par \uc2 In a letter, Senate Democrats on the Armed Services Committee, led by Hawaii senator Mazie Hirono, asked Secretary of Defense Lloyd Austin to act to \u8220\'20\'1Cpreserve the health and welfare of our Soldiers, Sailors, Airmen, Marines, and Guardians.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It asks the Department of Defense to provide a plan that ensures women seeking reproductive care in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is severely restricted or banned are allowed to travel out of state to seek care, as well as protects their privacy\par \par \uc2 CNN {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2022/06/30/politics/democratic-letter-abortion-access-military-pentagon/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 first}}}\uc2  reported the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hirono.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/6.30.2022_%20Joint%20Letter%20to%20DOD%20on%20Dobbs%20decision.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 letter}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEntrusted to your care are hundreds of thousands of troops, dependents, and Department of Defense civilians who have lost access to safe abortions and now face threats of criminal prosecution for seeking out those services,\u8221\'20\'1D the Democratic senators wrote.\par \par \uc2 It concludes : \u8220\'20\'1CWe owe it to these service members to look after them and ensure they have the ability to continue accessing safe reproductive health care no matter where their military service sends them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.50pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 In a dissenting opinion on Thursday, supreme court justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  incorrectly suggested that Covid-19 vaccines were developed using the cells of \u8220\'20\'1Caborted children\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Politico {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2022/06/30/clarence-thomas-claims-covid-vaccines-are-derived-from-the-cells-of-aborted-children-00043483"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 spotted}}}\uc2  the claim from the conservative justice in a dissenting opinion in response to a decision by the court not to hear a challenge to New York\u8217\'20\'19s vaccine mandate.\par \par \uc2 Over the objection of Thomas and two other conservative justices, the supreme court on Thursday allowed New York to require all healthcare works show proof of vaccination.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey object on religious grounds to all available COVID\u8211\'20\'1319 vaccines because they were developed using cell lines derived from aborted children,\u8221\'20\'1D Thomas said of the 16 healthcare workers who brought the challenge.\par \par \uc2 Rumors and conspiracy theories fueled vaccine hesitancy and undermined public faith in public health institutions in the United States, where more than 1 million Americans have died from covid-19.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s Politico correcting the record.\par \par \uc2 None of the Covid-19 vaccines in the United States contain the cells of aborted fetuses. Cells obtained from elective abortions decades ago were used in testing during the Covid vaccine development process, a practice that is common in vaccine testing \u8212\'20\'14 including for the rubella and chickenpox vaccinations.\par \par \uc2 A group of doctors, nurses and other health care workers brought the case, suing the U.S. District Court for the Northern District of New York in an objection to the state\u8217\'20\'19s vaccine mandate on religious grounds. The district court issued a preliminary injunction, but the Court of Appeals reversed it and the Supreme Court ultimately declined to hear the challenge on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Conservative Justices \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  and \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  joined Thomas in his dissenting opinion. And some Thomas defenders noted that he was simply reciting the allegations made by those refusing to get the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 Read the full story {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2022/06/30/clarence-thomas-claims-covid-vaccines-are-derived-from-the-cells-of-aborted-children-00043483"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.35pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.39pm BST\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department on Thursday {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-announces-investigation-new-york-city-police-department-s-special-victims"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced}}}\uc2  that it had opened a civil rights investigation into the New York City police department\u8217\'20\'19s special victims division after concluding there was \u8220\'20\'1Csignificant justification\u8221\'20\'1D to examine its handling of sex-abuse cases.\par \par \uc2 In a press release, federal prosecutors said the department had received reports of deficiencies dating back more than a decade.\par \par \uc2 The investigation will look at whether the division has engaged in a pattern of gender-biased policing, examining allegations that include \u8220\'20\'1Cfailing to conduct basic investigative steps and instead shaming and abusing survivors and re-traumatizing them during investigations,\u8221\'20\'1D the department said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CVictims of sex crimes deserve the same rigorous and unbiased investigations of their cases that the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  affords to other categories of crime,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Damian Williams\uc2 , the US attorney for the Southern District of New York, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLikewise, relentless and effective pursuit of perpetrators of sexual violence, unburdened by gender stereotypes or differential treatment, is essential to public safety. We look forward to working with our partners in EDNY and the Civil Rights Division to assess the \uc2 NYPD\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s practices in this area.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.18pm BST\par \par \uc2 As \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics shutter around the country and providers navigate a fast-changing legal environment, a judge in Florida said he would temporarily block a 15-week ban from taking effect in the state.\par \par \uc2 The decision comes in response to a court challenge by reproductive healthcare providers who argued that the Florida state constitution guarantees a right to the procedure.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-health-florida-ron-desantis-legislature-7d8d912cf00f13dd1026848ec2041dc3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 According to the Associated Press}}}\uc2 , the judge, John Cooper, issued the ruling from the bench, but it does not take effect until he signs a written order. The law, passed earlier this year by the state\u8217\'20\'19s Republican-controlled legislature and signed into law by Republican governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , goes into effect Friday.\par \par \uc2 Cooper said Florida\u8217\'20\'19s ban was \u8220\'20\'1Cunconstitutional in that it violates the privacy provision of the Florida Constitution.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 DeSantis\u8217\'20\'19 office said it would appeal the ruling.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.26pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.49pm BST\par \par \uc2 In a new statement, Biden vowed to press forward with executive actions to combat climate change despite what he called the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cdevastating\u8221\'20\'1D ruling on Friday that significantly hobbles the government\u8217\'20\'19s ability to limit carbon gas emissions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile this decision risks damaging our nation\u8217\'20\'19s ability to keep our air clean and combat climate change, I will not relent in using my lawful authorities to protect public health and tackle the climate crisis,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said in the statement.\par \par \uc2 Biden said he has directed federal agencies to review the decision in search of ways the administration might still be able to limit pollution.\par \par \uc2 We cannot and will not ignore the danger to public health and existential threat the climate crisis poses. The science confirms what we all see with our own eyes \u8211\'20\'13 the wildfires, droughts, extreme heat, and intense storms are endangering our lives and livelihoods.\par \par \uc2 I will take action. My Administration will continue using lawful executive authority, including the \uc2 EPA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s legally-upheld authorities, to keep our air clean, protect public health, and tackle the climate crisis. We will work with states and cities to pass and uphold laws that protect their citizens. And we will keep pushing for additional Congressional action, so that Americans can fully seize the economic opportunities, cost-saving benefits, and security of a clean energy future. Together, we will tackle environmental injustice, create good-paying jobs, and lower costs for families building the clean energy economy.\par \par \uc2 Our fight against climate change must carry forward, and it will.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.10pm BST\par \par \uc2 A new survey from the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnorc.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Associated Press-NORC Center for Public Affairs Research}}}\uc2  found that nearly half of US adults believe \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  should be charged with a crime for his role in the January 6 attack on the Capitol, compared with 31% who say he should not be.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 6 in 10 US adults say he \u8220\'20\'1Cbears a great deal or quite a bit of responsibility\u8221\'20\'1D for the violence that unfolded at the Capitol, it found.\par \par \uc2 The survey was conducted after the first five public hearings held by the House committee investigating the attack but before Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s hearing, which featured explosive testimony from Cassidy Hutchinson, a top aide to Trump\u8217\'20\'19s final chief of staff, \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Unsurprisingly, views of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s culpability varied widely along party lines. Nevertheless, it is perhaps a sobering data point for the former president as he toys with a second bid for the White House.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.12pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.54pm BST\par \par \uc2 Today so far\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s been a busy morning in Washington. Here\u8217\'20\'19s where things stand.\par \par \uc2 * The supreme court ended a monumental session with another pair of consequential decisions. In a 6-3 decision, the court\u8217\'20\'19s conservative majority sided with Republican officials and fossil fuel companies in a ruling that curbs the administration\u8217\'20\'19s ability to combat global warming.\par \par \uc2 * In a second ruling, the court agreed 5-4 that Biden had the authority to end a controversial immigration policy enacted by his predecessor, known informally as the \u8220\'20\'1CRemain in Mexico\u8221\'20\'1D program.\par \par \uc2 * During a press conference in Madrid, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said he supported changing the Senate rules to pass \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and privacy protections. But Democrats do not have enough votes to alter, much less eliminate, the filibuster.And as long as the filibuster remains in place, they lack the Republican support to pass legislation that would codify Roe into law.\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  was sworn in as the 116th supreme court justice. She is the first Black woman to serve on the court.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.34pm BST\par \par \uc2 For this history books. \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  is sworn in as the 116th supreme court justice and the first Black woman to serve on the court.\par \par \uc2 Judge \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  is sworn in as the newest associate justice of the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 . Photograph: Reuters\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.36pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.15pm BST\par \par \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  sworn in as first Black woman Supreme Court justice\par \par \uc2 History made.\par \par \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  was sworn in as the newest associate justice of the supreme court on Thursday, becoming the first Black woman in history to ascend to the nation\u8217\'20\'19s highest bench.\par \par \uc2 enltrWATCH: Judge \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  is officially sworn in as first Black female Justice of the Supreme Court. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/sHdcaCS1Y2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/sHdcaCS1Y2}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/95Oz59jW3z"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/95Oz59jW3z}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 CBS News (@CBSNews) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/CBSNews/status/1542542458210353153?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 30, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 In a brief ceremony at the supreme court, Chief Justice Roberts administered the Constitutional oath. Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 , who retired at noon, delivered the judicial oath. She is the court\u8217\'20\'19s 116th justice.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAre you prepared to take the oath,\u8221\'20\'1D Roberts asked. \u8220\'20\'1CI am,\u8221\'20\'1D Jackson said, raising her right hand.\par \par \uc2 The 51-year-old Jackson joins the court at an extraordinary moment, after one of the most consequential terms in modern memory. The court\u8217\'20\'19s 6-3 conservative supermajority handed down a slew of decisions that expanded gun rights, eliminated the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and, just today, curtailed the government\u8217\'20\'19s ability to fight climate change.\par \par \uc2 Her confirmation was the fulfillment of a promise \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  made to supporters during the 2020 presidential campaign, when he vowed to nominate a Black woman justice if a vacancy arose. Earlier this year, Breyer announced he would retire at the end of the term, paving the way for her elevation to the court.\par \par \uc2 A former public defender, she brings a unique background. Her arrival is expected to do little to change the court\u8217\'20\'19s ideological composition as she views herself in the mold of her predecessor, one of just three liberals on the court.\par \par \uc2 Roberts said there would be a formal investiture in the fall.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.22pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.43pm BST\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Patrick Leahy\uc2 , the 82-year-old Democrat from Vermont, will undergo hip surgery today after falling in his Virginia home, his office said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The statement notes that Leahy, a skilled photographer, was born blind in one eye and has had a \u8220\'20\'1Clifelong struggle\u8221\'20\'1D with depth perception. \u8220\'20\'1CHe has taken some remarkable dingers over the years but this one finally caught up with him,\u8221\'20\'1D it said.\par \par \uc2 The statement said Leahy is expected to make a full recovery but did not offer any timeline for his return. In a Senate divided 50-50, his absence could delay Democrats plans to confirm a host of judicial nominations and a new director to lead the \uc2 Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives\uc2 . It may also imperil negotiations over a reconciliation bill, that may be the vehicle for Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 scaled-back climate proposals, all the more urgent in light of the Supreme Court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling today.\par \par \uc2 enltrNow at risk: timely confirmation of ATF and judicial noms, including a DC Circuit judge, and possible reconciliation votes. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/nMsrox8pdj"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/nMsrox8pdj}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Mike DeBonis (@mikedebonis) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/mikedebonis/status/1542489139072962561?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 30, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.17pm BST\par \par \uc2 Biden reiterates his support for changing the filibuster rules to pass \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections.\par \par \uc2 enltrWe have to codify Roe v. Wade into law.\par \par \uc2 And as I said this morning: If the filibuster gets in the way, then we need to make an exception to get it done.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 President Biden (@POTUS) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/POTUS/status/1542525374235643904?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 30, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.59pm BST\par \par \uc2 Democrats and environmental groups are fuming over the Supreme Court\u8217\'20\'19s decision in West Virginia v. \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2 , which has dramatic consequences for the administration\u8217\'20\'19s climate ambitions but also for the world\u8217\'20\'19s ability to cut emissions at the speed and scale scientists say are needed to avert catastrophic climate change.\par \par \uc2 A sampling:\par \par \uc2 enltrDecisions like WV vs. \uc2 EPA\uc2  make it clear just how much the system is rigged against us.\par \par \uc2 A Supreme Court that sides with the fossil fuel industry over the health and safety of its people is anti-life and illegitimate.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Sunrise Movement ?? (@sunrisemvmt) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/sunrisemvmt/status/1542513302516793346?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 30, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrOur planet is on fire, and this extremist Supreme Court has destroyed the federal government\u8217\'20\'19s ability to fight back.\par \par \uc2 This radical Supreme Court is increasingly facing a legitimacy crisis, and we can't let them have the last word.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Elizabeth Warren (@SenWarren) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SenWarren/status/1542516572631142401?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 30, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 From the liberal justices\u8217\'20\'19 dissent:\par \par \uc2 enltrJustice Kagan: "The Court appoints itself\u8212\'20\'14instead of Congress or the expert agency\u8212\'20\'14the decision- maker on climate policy. I cannot think of many things more frightening. Respectfully, I dissent." {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/L3sGnqB8Zg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/L3sGnqB8Zg}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Kyle Griffin (@kylegriffin1) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/kylegriffin1/status/1542513317893140481?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 30, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.12pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.49pm BST\par \par \uc2 The session may have ended, but that\u8217\'20\'19s not all from the Supreme Court today. Our Sam Levine notes that the Supreme Court has announced that it will take up a \u8220\'20\'1Chugely consequential\u8221\'20\'1D case related to redistricting and other election law disputes next term.\par \par \uc2 enltrBreaking news: The supreme court agreed to hear a hugely consequential case challenging the right of state courts to weigh in on redistricting and other election law disputes\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Sam Levine (@srl) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/srl/status/1542520173814394881?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 30, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrJUST IN: Supreme Court to take up a North Carolina case on whether state courts or legislatures have the final word on elections. Will be a testing of the \u8220\'20\'1Cindependent state legislature\u8221\'20\'1D theory that underpinned much of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s effort to overturn the 2020 election\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Allan Smith (@akarl_smith) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/akarl_smith/status/1542521535897882625?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 30, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.02pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.41pm BST\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court decided 5-4 that Biden had the authority to terminate the controversial \u8220\'20\'1CRemain in Mexico\u8221\'20\'1D policy that forced asylum seekers mostly from Central America arriving at the US southern border to await approval in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 The decision was written by chief justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  and joined by justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and the court\u8217\'20\'19s three liberals. The program, known formally as the Migrant Protection Protocols, was established by the Trump administration as a means to stem immigration at the southwestern border. Under the policy, tens of thousands of migrants have been returned to Mexico, where they await consideration of their cases in squalid and dangerous encampments along the border.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s more from yours truly on what the decision means.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2022/jun/30/supreme-court-immigration-biden-trump-remain-in-mexico-decision"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Biden can end Trump\u8217\'20\'19s \u8216\'20\'18Remain in Mexico\u8217\'20\'19 program, supreme court rules}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.20pm BST\par \par \uc2 Biden can end Trump-era 'Remain in Mexico' policy, supreme court rules\par \par \uc2 In its second and final decision of the day, the Supreme Court on Thursday said Biden can terminate a controversial Trump-era immigration policy, known as Remain in Mexico. The ruling affirms a president\u8217\'20\'19s broad power to set the nation\u8217\'20\'19s immigration policy.\par \par \uc2 The ruling concludes the most consequential supreme court term in recent memory.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI am authorized to announce that the Court has acted upon all cases submitted to the Court for decision this term,\u8221\'20\'1D Roberts {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/publicinfo/press/pressreleases/pr_06-30-22"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 writes}}}\uc2  in his end of term statement. The court will now recess from \u8220\'20\'1Ctoday until the first Monday in October 2022.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.11pm BST\par \par \uc2 Supreme Court limits federal power to regulate carbon emissions\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court on Thursday sharply curbed the federal government\u8217\'20\'19s authority to limit carbon emissions from existing power plants, dealing a major blow to the administration\u8217\'20\'19s climate goals.\par \par \uc2 In a 6-3 ruling, the court\u8217\'20\'19s conservative supermajority sided with conservative states and fossil fuel companies in a move that will hobble efforts to move the US away from coal-burning power plants and toward sources of renewable energy.\par \par \uc2 The decision will have \u8220\'20\'1Cprofound implications for the government\u8217\'20\'19s overall regulatory power\u8221\'20\'1D and will \u8220\'20\'1C seriously hinder America\u8217\'20\'19s ability to stave off disastrous {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/climate-crisis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 global heating}}}\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D writes Oliver Milman, the \uc2 Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s environment reporter.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s more from Oliver:\par \par \uc2 The case, which was backed by a host of other Republican-led states including Texas and Kentucky, was highly unusual in that it was based upon the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2015/aug/03/obamas-clean-power-plan-hailed-as-strongest-ever-climate-action-by-a-us-president"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Clean Power Plan}}}\uc2 , an Obama-era strategy to cut emissions from coal-fired power plants that never came into effect. The Biden administration sought to have the case dismissed as baseless given the plan was dropped and has not been resurrected.\par \par \uc2 Not only was this case about a regulation that does not exist, that never took effect, and which would have imposed obligations on the energy sector that it would have met regardless. It also involves two legal doctrines that are not mentioned in the constitution, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/feb/28/us-supreme-court-rightwing-climate-crisis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 and that most scholars agree have no basis in any federal statute.}}}\par \par \uc2 However, the supreme court has sided with West Virginia, a major coal mining state, which argued that \u8220\'20\'1Cunelected bureaucrats\u8221\'20\'1D at the \uc2 EPA\uc2  should not be allowed to reshape its economy by limiting pollution \u8211\'20\'13 even though emissions from coal are helping {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2017/dec/28/climate-change-2017-warmest-year-extreme-weather"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cause worsening}}}\uc2  flooding, heatwaves and droughts around the world, as well as killing millions of people through toxic air.\par \par \uc2 It is the most important climate change case to come before the supreme court in more than a decade.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/30/us-supreme-court-ruling-restricts-federal-power-greenhouse-gas-emissions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US supreme court hobbles government power to limit harmful emissions}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.14pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.57pm BST\par \par \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , the Republican congresswoman from Wyoming, said her party had a choice to make: it could be loyal to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or the Constitution, but not both.\par \par \uc2 In scathing speech at the Ronald Reagan Presidential Library in Simi Valley, she assailed Republicans who have ignored or downplayed revelations about Trump\u8217\'20\'19s attempts to overturning the 2020 election, accusing them of being \u8220\'20\'1Cwilling hostages to this dangerous and irrational man.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have to choose, because Republicans cannot be loyal to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and to the Constitution,\u8221\'20\'1D she said, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/politics/story/2022-06-29/liz-cheney-reagan-library"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to the LA Times}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The daughter of Republican vice president \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2 , her name was once synonymous with the small-government conservatism that defined the party. But now she finds herself ostracized, a lonely Trump critic determined to break his grip on her party, even if it costs her her political career.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.00pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.32pm BST\par \par \uc2 Biden supports changing filibuster rules to codify Roe\par \par \uc2 Biden said he would support changing the Senate filibuster rules to codify \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights nationally.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have to codify Roe v Wade in the law and the wya to do that is to make sure Congress votes to do that. And if the filibuster gets in the way, it\u8217\'20\'19s like voting rights,... we should require an exception to the filibuster for this action,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 The filibuster requires 60 votes to advance most legislation in the evenly-divided Senate. All 50 Democrats and two Republicans say they would support, in theory, legislation codifying \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which leaves them 8 votes short of the 60-vote threshold. However, Democrats lack the necessary 50 votes to eliminate or change the filibuster rules.\par \par \uc2 Under pressure from pro-choice advocates for not doing enough, Biden said he would meet with a group of governors on Friday to discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. He told reporters he would announce long-promised actions by the federal government to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access at the event.\par \par \uc2 Given his evolution on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Biden is asked if he is the party\u8217\'20\'19s best messenger on the issue.\par \par \uc2 He let out a chuckle: \u8220\'20\'1CYeah, I am.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m the president of the United States of America. That makes me the best messenger,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m the only president they got. And I feel extremely strongly that I\u8217\'20\'19m going to everything in my power which I legally can do in terms of executive orders as well as push the Congress and the public.\par \par \uc2 The bottom line here is, if you care, if the polling data is correct, and you think this decision by the court was an outrage or a significant mistake: VOTE. Show up and vote. Vote in the off-year and vote vote vote. That\u8217\'20\'19s how we\u8217\'20\'19ll change it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.58pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.24pm BST\par \par \uc2 Biden insisted that not one single world leader believes the US is going \u8220\'20\'1Cbackward\u8221\'20\'1D amid decision to end the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , expanding weapons, soaring inflation and near-daily revelations about the shocking efforts by his predecessor to cling to power.\par \par \uc2 In Madrid, he said he\u8217\'20\'19s only heard world leaders thank him for the US leadership on Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe one thing that has been destabilizing is the outrageous behavior of the Supreme Court of the United in overruling not only Roe v Wade but essentially challenging the right to privacy,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Of its decision on Roe, Biden said: \u8220\'20\'1CIt is a mistake in my view for the Supreme Court to do what it did.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Asked whether there will come a day when the US will no longer be able to support Ukraine, he said no. He said he doesn\u8217\'20\'19t know how or when the war will end but vowed that it would \u8220\'20\'1Cnot end with a Russian defeat of Ukraine in Ukraine.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe reason why gas prices are up is because of Russia,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden says. \u8220\'20\'1CRussia, Russia, Russia.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Russian leader \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , he said, \u8220\'20\'1Cis getting exactly what he did not want. He wanted the Finlandization of \uc2 NATO\uc2 . He got the \uc2 NATO\uc2 -ization of Finland\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.01pm BST\par \par \uc2 Biden is holding a press conference in Madrid.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis summit was about strengthening our alliance, meeting the challenges of our world as it is today, and the threats we\u8217\'20\'19re going to face in the future,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said. He noted that the world has changed since Nato last drafted its mission statement, which considered Russia an ally and made no mention of China.\par \par \uc2 He also applauded the decision to welcome Finland and Sweden joining the Nato alliance.\par \par \uc2 He accidentally said \u8220\'20\'1CSwitzerland\u8221\'20\'1D instead of Sweden and, catching himself, joked: \u8220\'20\'1CSwitzerland. My goodness.I\u8217\'20\'19m getting really anxious here about expanding Nato. Sweden!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He again declared that the US was prepared to defend \u8220\'20\'1Cevery inch\u8221\'20\'1D of Nato territory. \u8220\'20\'1CThe United States is rallying the world to stand with Ukraine.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.15pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.49pm BST\par \par \uc2 Supreme court to issue final decisions of blockbuster term\par \par \uc2 Good morning and welcome to our live coverage of the day in politics.\par \par \uc2 Today Americans are bracing for two major decisions that will conclude what has been among the most consequential supreme court terms in recent decades. From \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to guns, the rulings by the 6-3 conservative supermajority have dramatically reshaped American life. Now we await for their opinion on two cases that could have significant consequences for the executive branch\u8217\'20\'19s ability to make policy.\par \par \uc2 * Following the end of this blockbuster session, Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  will officially retire at noon and \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  will be sworn in as the newest associate justice in a small ceremony. Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  will administer the constitutional oath and Breyer will administer the judicial oath.\par \par \uc2 * Meanwhile, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is concluding his trip to Europe, where he met with European and Nato allies. He will deliver remarks and take questions from reporters at a press conference in Madrid at some point this morning.\par \par \uc2 * Last night, the House committee investigating the January 6 attack on the Capitol has issued a subpoena for former White House counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 . Cassidy Hutchinson, a former top aide to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s final chief of staff, \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , testified Tuesday that Cipollone insisted Trump not go to the Capitol with his supporters as Congress certified Biden\u8217\'20\'19s electoral victory on January 6. He also sought to have Trump urge his supporters to leave the Capitol after the riot began, according to Hutchinson.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/29/january-6-committee-subpoena-pat-cipollone"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 January 6 committee subpoenas former White House counsel Pat Cipollone}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220630ei6u001rz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
