\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Why were Democrats caught flat-footed by the end of Roe v Wade?\b0\par\par\uc2 Jill Filipovic \par \uc2 1,425 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 June 2022\par 15:28 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Democrats can\u8217\'20\'19t fix the past. But the least they can do is learn from it \u8211\'20\'13 and change course accordingly\par \par \uc2 With Roe v Wade overturned by the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  and American women now living in a nation where our most fundamental rights are dependent on the state in which we reside, a lot of us are looking around and asking, \u8220\'20\'1Chow did we get here?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 There is much blame to go around, and the bulk of it rests on the shoulders of the right-wing anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/magazine/story/2014/05/religious-right-real-origins-107133/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sprung out of the white supremacist movement}}}\uc2  that fought to maintain Jim Crow and school segregation. The racist, misogynist Religious Right gained tremendous power within the Republican party; the Republican party proved itself willing to lie, cheat, and steal to get their way; and as a result, Americans are now living in an undemocratic nation of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/jun/25/us-supreme-court-illegitimate-institution"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reactionary minority rule}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But the Democratic party hasn\u8217\'20\'19t done enough to help itself, its supporters, and women more broadly.\par \par \uc2 There are a great many points where Democrats could have kept the country on the rails. Chief among them is in the aftermath of the 2000 election, when \uc2 Al Gore\uc2  won the popular vote, but the supreme court, along partisan lines, installed \uc2 George W Bush\uc2  as president. If the reverse had happened \u8211\'20\'13 if our arcane Electoral College system had put a Democratic loser in office over a Republican who won more votes \u8211\'20\'13 rest assured that the Republican party would have gotten rid of that undemocratic institution as soon as it had the chance.\par \par \uc2 Democrats, though, did nothing \u8211\'20\'13 even though \u8220\'20\'1Cone person, one vote\u8221\'20\'1D is likely how most Americans believe our system works, and is an easy advocacy line. When Obama took office, Democrats controlled both houses of Congress. In the states, Democrats controlled more legislatures than Republicans did; more states had a Democratic trifecta (Democratic governors plus both state legislative bodies) than a Republican one. There was a brief moment here to get a lot done in the name of both democracy and women\u8217\'20\'19s rights: get rid of the undemocratic Electoral College; codify Roe; rescind the Hyde Amendment, which bars federal Medicaid dollars from funding abortions for poor women, and the Helms Amendment, which bars US funding from paying for abortions for women overseas. Advocates asked the Obama administration to do all of that; they did none.\par \par \uc2 If there is one moment that portended all of what we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing today, it was Bush v. Gore in 2001. Democrats had a chance to correct it. They had a base that was livid about what had happened, and a country primed to accept a \u8220\'20\'1Cone person, one vote\u8221\'20\'1D rule for elections. And despite a huge win in 2008, they did absolutely nothing to prevent such an undemocratic result from happening again.\par \par \uc2 Two years after Obama\u8217\'20\'19s big win, Republicans {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2022/06/25/us/how-roe-ended.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 swept the midterms}}}\uc2  in what remains one of the most significant shifts in American political power in the last century. It wasn\u8217\'20\'19t just standard Republican candidates who won \u8211\'20\'13 it was Tea Party enthusiasts, right-wing extremists, conspiracy nuts, hardcore misogynists and unrepentant racists, all of whom set the state for Trump\u8217\'20\'19s rise and his eventual party takeover. Once in power, they focused on restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, passing hundreds of laws and imposing a smorgasbord of new restrictions.\par \par \uc2 They have controlled both chambers of the legislature in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2022/06/25/us/how-roe-ended.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than half of US states}}}\uc2  ever since.\par \par \uc2 Once in power, Republicans focused on keeping themselves there, democracy be damned. They scaled up efforts to restrict voting rights, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/research-reports/impact-voter-suppression-communities-color"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 carefully calibrating}}}\uc2  their laws to decrease Democratic turnout \u8211\'20\'13 that is, to make it harder for people of color to vote. They used whatever power they had to deliver for their constituents \u8211\'20\'13 not stuff like healthcare or poverty alleviation that people might actually need, but the culture war stuff that satisfied a punitive desire to screw over perceived enemies.\par \par \uc2 Democrats, on the other hand, made endless compromises.\par \par \uc2 When supreme court justice \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2  died in 2016, \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  was still the president, and he had the right to appoint a judge to fill Scalia\u8217\'20\'19s seat. The Republican Party, though, had control of the Senate and blocked him, claiming that, because it was an election year, the American people had the right to pick the president who would pick the next Supreme Court judge. They did not apply this same rule to themselves just four years later, when Trump was in the same position \u8211\'20\'13 he appointed Amy Coney Barrett to replace \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 . And while some Democrats complained, they certainly did not play hardball; Sen. Dianne Feinstein even applauded Barrett\u8217\'20\'19s speedy and illegitimate confirmation hearings.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , of course, lost the popular vote; millions more American voted for \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 . But, thanks to an Electoral College system kept in place despite its long-apparent flaws, the majority lost in 2016. We all know what happened next.\par \par \uc2 In early May, a draft of the supreme court opinion to overturn Roe leaked to the press. It was a shock, but not a total surprise \u8211\'20\'13 supreme court watchers and reproductive rights advocates had been warning that this particular court was ready and willing to overturn Roe, and that they might use the Dobbs case to do it.\par \par \uc2 But even with that heads up, the day the Dobbs opinion was published, Democrats seemed to be caught flat-footed. Democrats offered {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vulture.com/2022/06/nancy-pelosi-poem-roe-v-wade-overturn.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 poems}}}\uc2  and made fundraising pleas. They asked us to vote \u8211\'20\'13 even though we did, in huge numbers, in 2016 and 2018 and 2020; even though three million more Americans voted for the Democratic nominee in 2016 than the Republican one.\par \par \uc2 Only a small handful of Democrats, led most notably by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ewarren/status/1540412306294816768"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Elizabeth Warren}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AOC/status/1541111717140234241"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez}}}\uc2 , suggested anything even remotely resembling an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/CAKitchener/status/1541499684790870019"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 innovative}}}\uc2  response. And even they seemed to be coming up with it on the fly.\par \par \uc2 The unfortunate reality is that there is no immediate perfect solution for the problem at hand. The supreme court has struck down basic rights for women, giving conservative states enormous control over women\u8217\'20\'19s bodies. Even a federal law codifying Roe is vulnerable to Republican takeover, and tricky to pass given the current make-up of Congress and the fact that the slim Democratic majority in the senate includes conservative Democrats Joe Manchin and Krysten Sinema.\par \par \uc2 But that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t mean there is nothing Democrats can do. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , for example, could make securing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights for as many women as possible his number-one priority; he could rescind {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kff.org/womens-health-policy/issue-brief/the-hyde-amendment-and-coverage-for-abortion-services/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 federal}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kff.org/global-health-policy/issue-brief/the-helms-amendment-and-abortion-laws-in-countries-receiving-u-s-global-health-assistance/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 amendments}}}\uc2  that limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access; he could give permission for clinics to open on federal lands; he could go to the mat for medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  availability; he could be clear that he will expand the court and end the filibuster.\par \par \uc2 Instead, he\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2022/06/27/politics/biden-admin-us-travel-inflation/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 setting off on a national tour}}}\uc2  to remind Americans that they think he\u8217\'20\'19s to blame for inflation.\par \par \uc2 Voting for Democrats matters. One problem that Democrats are currently facing is that they simply don\u8217\'20\'19t have enough of a majority to get done what their base wants, and they have two feckless narcissists with Ds next their names who are hampering the party\u8217\'20\'19s agenda. The midterms matter; more Democrats in office means more opportunities to pass laws that protect women and human rights more generally.\par \par \uc2 But that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t mean Democrats powerless now, or that they have any right to pin the blame on voters. At the very least, Democrats should take a look at what has happened since 2001, and recognize the situation for the emergency that it is. Most of the conservative judges on the court \u8211\'20\'13 most of the judges who just overturned Roe v. Wade \u8211\'20\'13 were appointed by presidents who initially lost the popular vote but took office anyway. This has happened twice in just 20 years.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and democracy go hand in a hand. A nation is not democratic if half of its population do not have basic rights, let alone equal rights. As the US faces a series of crises of democracy, from an attempted coup to a hostile takeover by a reactionary minority to an unprecedented rollback of civil rights, there is a straight line that runs from Bush v Gore to Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health.\par \par \uc2 Democrats can\u8217\'20\'19t fix the past. But the least they can do is learn from it \u8211\'20\'13 and change course accordingly.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Jill Filipovic is the author of the The H-Spot: The Feminist Pursuit of Happiness\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220630ei6u00261\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
