\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Spotlight back on  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  in New Zealand after US Roe v Wade ruling\b0\par\par\uc2 Tess McClure in Auckland \par \uc2 596 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 June 2022\par 01:37 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 MPs spar over their stances, with opposition leader Christopher Luxon forced to manage the gulf between his personal views and National party policy\par \par \uc2 As the repeal of Roe v Wade rolls back women\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the US, New Zealand politicians are facing fresh scrutiny of their own anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stances.\par \par \uc2 New Zealanders broadly support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, with \uc2 Ipsos\uc2  polling indicating 77% of the population supports a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to terminate a pregnancy in some or any circumstances. In 2020, the country formally decriminalised \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , allowing terminations at up to 20 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Some local activists, however, are concerned that with high-profile anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politicians in parliament, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/26/abortion-rights-protests-across-us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturn of Roe v Wade}}}\uc2  could galvanise similar movements closer to home.\par \par \uc2 After the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  decision on Sunday, National MP Simon O\u8217\'20\'19Connor stoked controversy by posting \u8220\'20\'1Cthis is a good day\u8221\'20\'1D on social media. O\u8217\'20\'19Connor, who describes himself as \u8220\'20\'1Cstrongly pro-life\u8221\'20\'1D, had spoken against New Zealand\u8217\'20\'19s previous law reform \u8211\'20\'13 and acknowledged the bill\u8217\'20\'19s passing by quoting the book of Romans: \u8220\'20\'1CVengeance is mine; I will repay, saith the Lord.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This forced opposition leader Christopher Luxon into an awkward position in trying to reconcile his own personal views \u8211\'20\'13 the evangelical Christian has previously agreed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is tantamount to murder \u8211\'20\'13 and those of his party.\par \par \uc2 Luxon requested O\u8217\'20\'19Connor remove the post, saying it did not reflect the party\u8217\'20\'19s position, and issued a statement that Roe v Wade was \u8220\'20\'1Cnot an NZ issue\u8221\'20\'1D. He said the overturn of the law was \u8220\'20\'1Cdistressing for many women everywhere and I empathise with them\u8221\'20\'1D. Luxon said would not re-litigate New Zealand\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws and that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services would keep funding under a National government.\par \par \uc2 Deputy prime minister Grant Robertson accused Luxon on Monday of using \u8220\'20\'1Cpolitical spin\u8221\'20\'1D to disguise his stance. \u8220\'20\'1CI think New Zealanders need to ask themselves what Christopher Luxon\u8217\'20\'19s stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  actually is,\u8221\'20\'1D Robertson said in a radio interview. \u8220\'20\'1CWhile Mr Luxon might be saying convenient political things now, I think people know where he really stands.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Luxon, in turn, has called Robertson\u8217\'20\'19s comments \u8220\'20\'1Ca distraction\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CIf anyone is spinning, it is Grant Robertson, who would rather not be talking about the economy or the cost of living.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Debate over MPs\u8217\'20\'19 views have not been restricted to New Zealand\u8217\'20\'19s right wing. Foreign minister \uc2 Nanaia Mahuta\uc2  said on social media on Monday that \u8220\'20\'1Cthe \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s overturning of Roe v Wade is draconian\u8221\'20\'1D. But Mahuta faced blowback of her own for voting against the most recent liberalisation of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws.\par \par \uc2 Advocacy group ALRANZ \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Rights Aotearoa said in a statement it was \u8220\'20\'1Cconcerned about global repercussions [of Roe v Wade], as anti-choice groups elsewhere are emboldened to reverse hard-won gains\u8221\'20\'1D. The group pointed to \u8220\'20\'1Cworrying remarks made by members of the opposition party\u8221\'20\'1D and said it \u8220\'20\'1Curges Kiwis to remain vigilant regarding our new-won freedoms\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 ALRANZ executive committee member Terry Bellamak said Roe v Wade \u8220\'20\'1Chas implications for countries everywhere\u8221\'20\'1D and she was concerned that Luxon \u8220\'20\'1Cis on record as saying he believes that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is tantamount to murder.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe\u8217\'20\'19s also on record as saying things like \u8230\'20\'26 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law reform is settled law. Unfortunately, for Mr Luxon, we\u8217\'20\'19ve heard that before \u8211\'20\'13 fairly recently, from supreme court justices,\u8221\'20\'1D Bellamak said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s hard to have confidence in Mr Luxon\u8217\'20\'19s assurances when that example stands before us front and centre.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220629ei6t0008d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
