\par\par \uc2 Australia news\par \b \uc2 Explainer: Is  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  legal in Australia and what are the laws in each state?\b0\par\par\uc2 Tory Shepherd \par \uc2 1,320 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2022\par 16:05 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 There are different rules for when and how women can access terminations depending on where you liveFollow our Australia news live blog for the latest updatesGet our free news app ; get our morning email briefing\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in Australia is \u8220\'20\'1Ctracking well\u8221\'20\'1D despite attacks from anti-choice groups, experts say, as the world reels from the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s decision to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/jun/27/devastating-australian-politicians-respond-to-us-supreme-courts-decision-on-abortion-rights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturn Roe v Wade}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But they said that while \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been mostly decriminalised, non-legal barriers remain for women across the country.\par \par \uc2 Cost, geography and \u8220\'20\'1Cresidual stigma\u8221\'20\'1D can make it harder for those living outside cities, people on temporary work visas and other vulnerable groups.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s the state of play for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in this country.\par \par \uc2 Is \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal across Australia?\par \par \uc2 The states and territories all have different rules about when and how women can access terminations. Western Australia is the only state or territory where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains under the criminal code. With South Australia as the most recent to decriminalise abortions, with the changes {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://indaily.com.au/news/2022/06/24/abortion-to-finally-be-decriminalised-in-sa-from-july/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 starting on 7 July}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Across Australia, medical abortions are available until nine weeks\u8217\'20\'19 gestation (with SA restrictions to lift on 7 July). A medical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  uses mifepristone to end the pregnancy and misoprostol to expel it. These must be prescribed by a doctor and are taken orally in the form of pills.\par \par \uc2 The limits on surgical abortions vary, from 16 weeks in the ACT to 24 weeks in Victoria, before further approvals are needed.\par \par \uc2 The head of policy at \uc2 Marie Stopes International\uc2  (MSI) Australia, Bonney Corbin, said Australia was on track to have universal care by 2030 as outlined in the bipartisan {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.health.gov.au/sites/default/files/documents/2021/05/national-women-s-health-strategy-2020-2030.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 National Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Strategy}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CComparatively we\u8217\'20\'19re tracking very well,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m confident that commitment will stick. We are in such a good place to keep moving forward with reproductive rights in Australia. We could be leading the world in 2030 if we stick to that trajectory.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Corbin said while \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has largely been decriminalised, some aspects of it remain in criminal law. \u8220\'20\'1C[Another] part of the problem is that we have different laws in different states and territories,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 What are the other barriers to accessing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 It can cost hundreds of dollars for a medical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and thousands for a surgical termination.\par \par \uc2 Underfunding in the health system is another issue, Corbin said, along with geography.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CGeography can be a barrier to access \u8211\'20\'13 but that can also be a myth,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s all about having a good GP.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTelehealth is now helping, because it\u8217\'20\'19s accessible across Australia for the first time, but it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t suit everyone\u8217\'20\'19s personal circumstances. For some of those women in remote areas, travelling to a city might be a preferable option.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s still a \u8220\'20\'1Cpostcode lottery\u8221\'20\'1D that determines what access women will get, she said, and those on temporary visas don\u8217\'20\'19t have access to Medicare. Most private health insurers have a year-long waiting period before they\u8217\'20\'19ll provide abortions, and even then not all of them do.\par \par \uc2 Some doctors refuse to perform abortions and don\u8217\'20\'19t refer patients on, despite the obligation to, while pharmacists might refuse to supply medical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs, she said.\par \par \uc2 Dr Tania Penovic, a research group leader in gender and sexuality for the Castan Centre for Human Rights Law at \uc2 Monash University\uc2 , said doctors in a rural town, for example, might also be reliant on work in a Catholic hospital and worry they will be \u8220\'20\'1Cblacklisted\u8221\'20\'1D if they provide or help people get abortions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe stigma is a real deterrent to working in this area,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhy would you subject yourself to the possible personal attacks, the threats?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Penovic\u8217\'20\'19s 2021 study published in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277539521000340"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Women\u8217\'20\'19s Studies International Forum}}}\uc2  found the three main non-legal barriers to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access were financial, geographic, and \u8220\'20\'1Cdeficiencies in practitioner attitudes, education and training\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The barriers have been amplified during the pandemic, she found, generating a \u8220\'20\'1Cdisproportionate and intersectional impact on the most marginalised and disempowered women in society\u8221\'20\'1D including women with disabilities, refugees and First Nations women.\par \par \uc2 Abusive partners can also control reproductive and sexual choices, through rape, sabotaging contraception, refusing to use contraception and then controlling women\u8217\'20\'19s access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What is the likelihood of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights being rolled back?\par \par \uc2 Corbin said the anti-choice movement was \u8220\'20\'1Cnoisy\u8221\'20\'1D and could contribute to a stigma around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but was unlikely to influence policy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn Australia, the anti-choice movement is funded by the US, which can give them a large platform and a lot of noise, but that noise doesn\u8217\'20\'19t equate to power,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile they may seem active, they\u8217\'20\'19re not going to influence change.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C[But] the rhetoric contributes to stigma, which spreads shame and doom around reproductive rights. It drains energy, but if we can focus on the fact that Australia is progressing towards reproductive justice, we can celebrate the fact that we are groundbreaking right now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Penovic said women\u8217\'20\'19s rights are \u8220\'20\'1Chard-fought\u8221\'20\'1D and can be \u8220\'20\'1Ceasily lost\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 For example in 1996, independent senator Brian Harradine (who held the balance of power) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/commentisfree/2020/jul/15/abortion-drugs-remain-inaccessible-unsafe-and-unaffordable-for-many-australian-women"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 struck a deal}}}\uc2  with the Howard government to support a partial privatisation of \uc2 Telstra\uc2  in return for giving then health minister Tony Abbott the power to veto the import, manufacture or use of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug RU486.\par \par \uc2 It took more than a decade to overturn that power, and there are still cost and access problems stopping women getting medical abortions, as well as surgical ones.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAustralia is tracking relatively well, but I think what this shows us is that women\u8217\'20\'19s rights, gender equality is hard-fought and needs to be carefully protected because it\u8217\'20\'19s vulnerable,\u8221\'20\'1D Penovic said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt can very easily be lost.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She said while the pandemic and the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.abc.net.au/news/2021-08-30/access-to-abortion-really-difficult-in-regional-qld/100414012"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  closure of some Marie Stopes clinics}}}\uc2  made access more complicated for some, telehealth made it easier for others. And she said that US-inspired anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements were active in Australia.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile it\u8217\'20\'19s right to celebrate our victories, the politicisation of healthcare has real implications for access and what we see in the US is a perfect example of that,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe other thing we\u8217\'20\'19ve found is that, because of the residual stigma because of decades of criminalisation, we do have a problem.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/guardian-australia-morning-mail/2014/jun/24/-sp-guardian-australias-morning-mail-subscribe-by-email?CMP=copyembed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sign up to receive an email with the top stories from Guardian Australia every morning}}}\par \par \uc2 Penovic said while the situation in Australia was \u8220\'20\'1Cvery different\u8221\'20\'1D to that in the US, \u8220\'20\'1Cwe can\u8217\'20\'19t rest on our laurels\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe politicisation of the issue, and how people can be brainwashed \u8211\'20\'13 it\u8217\'20\'19s heartbreaking.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 What are the rules in each state and territory?\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 In the ACT, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal up to 16 weeks\u8217\'20\'19 gestation and must be performed by a doctor or a nurse. After 16 weeks, the patient will need to travel interstate.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 In New South Wales, abortions can be performed at up to 22 weeks. After that, two doctors must approve the procedure.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 In the Northern Territory, one doctor can approve and perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at up to 24 weeks. After 24 weeks, two doctors need to approve an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 In Queensland, abortions can be performed at up to 22 weeks. After 22 weeks, two doctors must approve the procedure.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 In SA, from 7 July, abortions are allowed until 22 weeks and six days, after which two doctors have to agree that it is necessary.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 In Tasmania, abortions can be performed at up to 16 weeks. After 16 weeks, two doctors must approve the procedure.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 In Victoria, abortions can be performed at up to 24 weeks. After 24 weeks, two doctors must approve the procedure.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 In WA, abortions can be performed at up to 20 weeks with the approval of two doctors. Termination after 20 weeks has to be approved by a panel of six doctors.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220627ei6r000uq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
