\par\par \uc2 Law\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18A mockery of democracy\u8217\'20\'19: \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  in question after  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ruling\b0\par\par\uc2 David Smith in Washington \par \uc2 1,952 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2022\par 22:31 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 In abruptly scrapping the constitutional right to terminate a pregnancy the court went against the popular will \u8211\'20\'13 only 25% of Americans now have confidence in the institution\par \par \uc2 Striding from the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  to the nearby US Capitol, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/TaylorTreacy/status/1540412775142494211?s=20&t=LYILARkJzVBRWDDu9M6YzQ"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 holding aloft a sign}}}\uc2  that said \u8220\'20\'1CMy body my choice\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1CWomen\u8217\'20\'19s right to choose\u8221\'20\'1D, Taylor Treacy was struggling to fathom how she had fewer constitutional rights now than when she awoke that morning.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s heartbreaking,\u8221\'20\'1D said the 28-year-old, who works in sports marketing. \u8220\'20\'1CThe people who have legally gotten abortions in the United States are mostly Black and brown women, yet the five justices able to have the final word were four powerful men and one white woman. We\u8217\'20\'19re allowing more access to guns yet we\u8217\'20\'19re taking away the rights of women. It just seems like we\u8217\'20\'19re going backwards.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Millions of women had just lost access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday after America\u8217\'20\'19s highest court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturned a near-50-year-old}}}\uc2  ruling and other precedents enshrining that right. The conservative justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  wrote in the court\u8217\'20\'19s majority {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://link.axios.com/click/28171488.87110/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuYXhpb3MuY29tLzIwMjIvMDUvMDMvcm9lLXYtd2FkZS1zdXByZW1lLWNvdXJ0LWxlYWstc3R1bnMtbmF0aW9uP3V0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zcG0mc3RyZWFtPXRvcA/58c6e533a79328702e8b4bbfBab02ee74"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 opinion}}}\uc2  that Roe v Wade was \u8220\'20\'1Cegregiously wrong and deeply damaging\u8221\'20\'1D, and that states should decide whether to limit or criminalise the procedure.\par \par \uc2 The court\u8217\'20\'19s liberal minority responded: \u8220\'20\'1CWith sorrow \u8211\'20\'13 for this Court, but more, for the many millions of American women who have today lost a fundamental constitutional protection \u8211\'20\'13 we dissent.\u8221\'20\'1D The ruling is expected to lead to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in roughly half the states, although the timing of those laws taking effect varies.\par \par \uc2 The decision, though widely expected a draft opinion {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/02/roe-v-wade-abortion-supreme-court-draft-opinion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 leaked last month}}}\uc2 , was nevertheless a stunning aftershock of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s presidency and sure to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/10/divided-america-roe-v-wade-supreme-court-cold-civil-war"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 enflame America\u8217\'20\'19s divisions}}}\uc2 . It also cemented the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s emergence an alternative centre of power that threatens to rupture the delicate governing balance of executive, legislature and judiciary.\par \par \uc2 Just 24 hours earlier, the justices had {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/23/us-supreme-court-new-york-law-gun-control"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 struck down}}}\uc2  New York state\u8217\'20\'19s limits on carrying concealed handguns in public, potentially opening the way to fresh legal challenges to other state-level gun laws despite recent mass shootings in California, New York and Texas. It was a triumph for the gun lobby and a blow to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s efforts to curb violence.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/simon_schama"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Simon Schama}}}\uc2 , a leading historian, tweeted on Friday: \u8220\'20\'1CAmerican democracy is in deep trouble. It can\u8217\'20\'19t survive in its present form if the constitution is manipulated to impose minority rule.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The back-to-back decisions were the fruit of a long campaign by conservatives to shift the judiciary to the right, powered by influential groups such as the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2  and \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 . The Republican presidents \uc2 George HW Bush\uc2  and \uc2 George W Bush\uc2  appointed \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2021/oct/11/us-supreme-court-john-roberts-politics"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 John Roberts}}}\uc2  (now chief justice) and Alito to the supreme court.\par \par \uc2 A democratic deficit opened when Senate Republicans blocked {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2016/mar/16/obama-nominates-merrick-garland-supreme-court-dc-appeals-court-judge"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Barack Obama\u8217\'20\'19s last nominee}}}\uc2  for the court, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , on the spurious grounds that it was an election year. Then Trump, a one-term president who had lost the national popular vote by 3m, appointed three justices: \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and Amy Coney Barrett. It has proven his most significant legacy.\par \par \uc2 The court struck down Roe v Wade against the wishes of a Democratic president, Democratic-controlled Congress and the citizenry. The majority of Americans (61%) believed that Roe should remain the law of the land, and only 36% supported overturning it, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.prri.org/spotlight/overturning-roe-would-fly-in-the-face-of-american-public-opinion/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Public Religion Research Institute thinktank}}}\uc2 . Even most religious Americans wanted to see Roe upheld.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://law.marquette.edu/faculty-and-staff-directory/detail/1996511"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Edward Fallone}}}\uc2 , an associate professor at Marquette University Law School in Milwaukee, Wisconsin, said: \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m afraid it\u8217\'20\'19s extremely undemocratic. You now have the least democratic branch of the federal government on an ideological agenda to roll back liberties that are extremely popular with the general public in America.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is a recipe for potential unrest, certainly demonstrations and political turmoil, as they seem intent on a course of action that will run counter to the will of the public.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The surge of judicial activism has knocked both the White House and Congress back on their heels. In Washington \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights protesters crowded outside the fenced-off supreme court on Friday, opposite the gleaming dome of the US Capitol, where their elected representatives vented frustration at the demise of Roe but were powerless to intervene.\par \par \uc2 This decision is the culmination of a deliberate effort over decades to upset the balance of our law\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\par \par \uc2 Two miles away at the White House, even the president seemed politically impotent. A solemn group of female staff, including domestic policy adviser Susan Rice and press secretary {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/16/karine-jean-pierre-biden-administration?ref=upstract.com&curator=upstract.com&utm_source=upstract.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre}}}\uc2 , gathered beneath a staircase in the cross-hall to watch Biden deliver a response. Portraits of \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  and \uc2 George W Bush\uc2 , presidents in an era when Roe seemed sacrosanct, looked on from opposing walls.\par \par \uc2 Calling it \u8220\'20\'1Ca sad day for the court and the country\u8221\'20\'1D, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.gov/briefing-room/speeches-remarks/2022/06/24/remarks-by-president-biden-on-the-supreme-court-decision-to-overturn-roe-v-wade/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Biden said}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CIt was three justices named by one president \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 who were the core of today\u8217\'20\'19s decision to upend the scales of justice and eliminate a fundamental right for women in this country.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMake no mistake: this decision is the culmination of a deliberate effort over decades to upset the balance of our law. It\u8217\'20\'19s a realisation of an extreme ideology and a tragic error by the supreme court, in my view.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He added: \u8220\'20\'1CWith this decision, the conservative majority of the supreme court shows how extreme it is, how far removed they are from the majority of this country. They have made the United States an outlier among developed nations in the world.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The president admitted that he cannot take executive action to secure a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to choose. The only hope is for Congress to restore the protections of Roe v Wade as federal law, which in turns depends on Democrats winning the midterm elections. \u8220\'20\'1CThis fall, Roe is on the ballot,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CPersonal freedoms are on the ballot.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Others argue that there is another solution to offset minority rule: expanding the supreme court beyond its current total of nine justices. The pressure group Demand Justice pointed to this week\u8217\'20\'19s rulings on guns and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as proof that reform is needed.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://demandjustice.org/christopher-kang/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Christopher Kang}}}\uc2 , its co-founder and chief counsel, said: \u8220\'20\'1CThis is part of the decades-long Republican agenda to accomplish through the supreme court what they cannot through the democratically elected branches of Congress. We\u8217\'20\'19ve seen in the last couple of days decisions making it harder for lawmakers to combat gun violence in the wake of some of the worst mass shootings in our country\u8217\'20\'19s history. We\u8217\'20\'19ve seen, now, overturning the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese are things that are supported by 70 to 80% of the American people and I think we\u8217\'20\'19ll see it again next week in a big case concerning whether or not the \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2  has the authority to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2022/06/22/1106863267/the-supreme-court-may-issue-a-ruling-that-could-hurt-bidens-climate-change-plans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 take action to fight climate change}}}\uc2 , another thing supported by 70 to 80% of the American people. This is a further example of what Republicans are doing through our unaccountable courts that they couldn\u8217\'20\'19t do through Congress or the White House.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Americans\u8217\'20\'19 faith in the supreme court has dropped to a historic low, with only 25% saying saying have \u8220\'20\'1Ca great deal\u8221\'20\'1D or \u8220\'20\'1Cquite a lot\u8221\'20\'1D of confidence in it, down from 36% a year ago, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/394103/confidence-supreme-court-sinks-historic-low.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to a Gallup poll}}}\uc2 . Kang believes that rebuilding trust is crucial to the health of America\u8217\'20\'19s increasingly fragile democracy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToday\u8217\'20\'19s ruling shows that the supreme court is the problem and so any solution has to address the supreme court,\u8221\'20\'1D he added. \u8220\'20\'1CThere are other things that the president can do or Congress, with greater majorities, could do but fundamentally we have to fix the court if we have any hope of addressing these problems.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This isn\u8217\'20\'19t the end, this is the opening salvo, and they made that clear in their decision\par \par \uc2 Cristina Tzintz\u250\'00\'FAn Ramirez\par \par \uc2 The calls take on even greater urgency because of what might be to come. Thomas wrote in a concurring opinion on Friday that the supreme court should reconsider other legal precedents protecting same-sex relationships, marriage equality and access to contraception. Biden warned: \u8220\'20\'1CThis is an extreme and dangerous path this court is taking us on.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Cristina Tzintz\u250\'00\'FAn Ramirez, president of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nextgenamerica.us8.list-manage.com/track/click?u=7dc0ad2e9cda1b13900c777f8&id=7d8ba9e27d&e=d7a7b5d23b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 NextGen America}}}\uc2 , an organisation that works to engage young voters, said: \u8220\'20\'1CIt is the takeover of an extreme rightwing minority that seeks to roll back the gains for the LGBTQ community, for women, for people of colour.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis isn\u8217\'20\'19t the end, this is the opening salvo, and they made that clear in their decision. You had \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  state they are going to take a look at how they can change the fundamental rights that the LGBTQ community has recently won in this country. \u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Ramirez added: \u8220\'20\'1CWe didn\u8217\'20\'19t defeat fascism in 2020; we beat it back. But to kill fascism in this country is going to require a lot more than one election cycle .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Friday\u8217\'20\'19s decision is set to create a patchwork of laws from state to state. Twenty-six are certain or likely to immediately ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://link.axios.com/click/28171488."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Guttmacher Institute}}}\uc2  thinktank. In Alabama, the state\u8217\'20\'19s three \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics stopped performing the procedure for fear providers would now be prosecuted under a law dating to 1951; women in the waiting room on Friday morning were suddenly turned away. Democratic state governors, however, promised to strengthen protections.\par \par \uc2 Back at the supreme court, the sun was shining but the mood was one of sombre defiance as hundreds of people waved placards, chanted slogans such as \u8220\'20\'1Cthe supreme court is illegitimate\u8221\'20\'1D and contemplated a leap into the unknown after half a century.\par \par \uc2 At 43, Tracy Tolk, a climate change and energy policy advocate, had known nothing but Roe her entire life. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m absolutely devastated,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI thought it would hurt less because we had a preview but it hurt more than I expected. It\u8217\'20\'19s gut-wrenching. People marched on the Capitol for less than this.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Millions of women will die. It\u8217\'20\'19s the beginning of the end of many things, as \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  said\par \par \uc2 Virginia Shadron\par \par \uc2 Virginia Shadron, 71, a retired academic administrator from Stone Mountain, Georgia, was wearing a badge with the face of late liberal justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 , whose death in 2020 allowed Trump to rush through the appointment of Barrett even with a presidential election already under way.\par \par \uc2 She said: \u8220\'20\'1CMillions of women will die. It sets back women and it\u8217\'20\'19s only the beginning. It\u8217\'20\'19s the beginning of the end of many things, as \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  said. Next, they\u8217\'20\'19ll take on contraception. Reasonable people can feel strongly and differently about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . I\u8217\'20\'19m glad for myself, I never had to make the choice, but if I had needed to, I would have wanted a safe, legal procedure.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 There was sadness in the eyes of Maureen John, 67, who warned that the decision to overturn Roe would lead to an increase in illegal and unsafe abortions. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m a nurse and I\u8217\'20\'19ve seen many unnecessary deaths because of the abortions done illegally,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 John was born in Guyana, moved to the US in 1976 and lives in Atlanta, Georgia. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m from the Caribbean and I came here and I became an American citizen because of democracy which wasn\u8217\'20\'19t available in my country at that time. I loved it. I love being American and now I\u8217\'20\'19m being disappointed at what\u8217\'20\'19s happening.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re making a mockery of democracy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220629ei6s002sj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
