\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 How Americans lost their right to abortions: a victory for conservatives, 50 years in the making\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza \par \uc2 1,351 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2022\par 14:57 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Why, and how, a decision opposed by a majority of Americans came about has everything to do with political power, experts say\par \par \uc2 The short version of how Americans lost their right to terminate a pregnancy might be summed up in one name: Trump.\par \par \uc2 The real estate tycoon and reality-TV star first shocked the world by winning the US presidency, then rewarded his base by confirming three supreme court justices to a nine-member bench, thus rebalancing the court to lean conservative for a generation to come.\par \par \uc2 That short road led to Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization, an opinion released this week in which supreme court justices voted to overturn the landmark case Roe v Wade, which in 1973 granted a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The end of federal protection for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/05/abortion-deserts-clinics-access-closed-map"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lead to 26 states}}}\uc2  banning the procedure immediately or as soon as practicable, affecting tens of millions of US women and people who can become pregnant.\par \par \uc2 The decision comes even though about 85% of Americans favor {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/1576/abortion.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 legal \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  in at least some circumstances. Why, and how, a decision opposed by a majority of Americans came about has everything to do with political power, experts said.\par \par \uc2 The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement is \u8220\'20\'1Cthe best organized faction in American politics\u8221\'20\'1D, said Frederick Clarkson, an expert on the Christian right and a senior research analyst at Political Research Associates, a progressive thinktank in Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey understand they\u8217\'20\'19re a minority of the population, of the electorate, and certainly a minority set of views on reproductive rights issues,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CBut because they know that, they\u8217\'20\'19ve found effective ways of maximizing their political clout by being better organized than numerically greater factions who are less well organized.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Put another way, he said, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement \u8220\'20\'1Cmastered the tools of democracy to achieve undemocratic outcomes\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The currents that led to the Dobbs decision are among the most powerful in American politics today. Over decades, a religious movement prevailed by harnessing the forces of polarization, the erosion of constitutional norms and the manipulation of US democracy, scholars said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not like we\u8217\'20\'19ve had this slow erosion of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights,\u8221\'20\'1D said Neil Siegel, an expert in constitutional law and professor at \uc2 Duke University\uc2  who clerked for former liberal Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 . Instead, justices issued an opinion that \u8220\'20\'1Cis utterly dismissive of what has been constitutional law for literally five decades\u8221\'20\'1D, and was \u8220\'20\'1Crepeatedly affirmed by justices appointed by both parties\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The conservative-leaning court will shatter one more constitutional norm, issuing a once-in-a-lifetime reversal, after another event without modern precedent: the leak of a supreme court draft opinion. Even before Dobbs was released, the leak spelled out the doom of Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe court is not the institution I served,\u8221\'20\'1D said Siegel.\par \par \uc2 Today, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is among the most partisan issues in the US, with Republicans and the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement so closely aligned there is little daylight between them. In the 1970s, however, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen as a \u8220\'20\'1CCatholic issue\u8221\'20\'1D, with both pro-choice Republicans and anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Democrats in Congress. The supreme court voted in favor of Roe v Wade by a 7-2 margin.\par \par \uc2 Some of this transformation reflects \u8220\'20\'1Cdeliberate changes by the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement, some of it is structural changes to US democracy, and some of it is just luck,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mary Ziegler, visiting professor at Harvard and a professor of constitutional law at the \uc2 University of California Davis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Contrary to popular belief, there was no immediate political backlash to Roe v Wade. In the years that followed, important bills banned the federal government from paying for abortions, but a constitutional amendment to outright ban the procedure failed.\par \par \uc2 It wasn\u8217\'20\'19t until the late 1970s that Republican strategists, such as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/30/revealed-council-national-policy-republicans-extremists"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Paul Weyrich}}}\uc2 , saw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as an issue that might unlock the votes of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/dec/05/abortion-opposition-focus-white-evangelical-anger"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 millions of white evangelical Christians}}}\uc2 , alongside opposition to women\u8217\'20\'19s rights and to desegregation court rulings. The plan worked: Catholics and white evangelical Protestants were brought into uneasy alliance with Republicans.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBack in the 70s and 80s, when the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement was maturing, I remember events where you would see one Catholic bishop sitting on stage uncomfortably with evangelicals,\u8221\'20\'1D said Clarkson.\par \par \uc2 It would {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2009/11/20/us/politics/20alliance.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 be decades}}}\uc2  before evangelical Christians and Catholics entirely fused their modern agenda, with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , gay marriage and religious freedom as top issues. Nevertheless, the new alliance soon produced a \u8220\'20\'1Cmoral majority\u8221\'20\'1D that buoyed \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s campaign. Like Trump, Reagan initially supported {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/1976/02/08/archives/reagan-affirms-antiabortion-stand.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cliberalized\u8221\'20\'1D \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  law}}}\uc2 , before he later promised to oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as president.\par \par \uc2 This political realignment was helped along by the Voting Rights Act of 1965, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=3744902#"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 constitutional scholars argued}}}\uc2  forced segregationist southern Democrats into real competition with Republicans for the first time.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou don\u8217\'20\'19t understand reproductive politics in this country if you don\u8217\'20\'19t understand racial politics in this country,\u8221\'20\'1D said Loretta Ross, founder of the SisterSong Women of Color Reproductive Health Collective, a reproductive rights organizer in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI believe the current restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , birth control and sex education are all designed to compel white women to have more babies,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ross. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m not convinced they want more brown or Black babies,\u8221\'20\'1D even though brown and Black women would be disproportionately affected by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, she said.\par \par \uc2 This political realignment also brought Republicans distinct structural advantages based on the architecture of the US constitution \u8211\'20\'13 a force Siegel describes as \u8220\'20\'1Crural favoritism\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The two-chamber Congress is made up of the House of Representatives, whose seats are based on population, and the Senate, which grants each state two votes no matter the population. \u8220\'20\'1CThe constitution has always disproportionately favored rural voters, but it didn\u8217\'20\'19t always favor one party,\u8221\'20\'1D said Siegel.\par \par \uc2 As Republican senators began to represent more white, Christian and rural voters, however, they also gained advantage of a feature baked into the US constitution. Today, Republicans collectively represent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vox.com/2020/11/6/21550979/senate-malapportionment-20-million-democrats-republicans-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 41.5 million fewer Americans}}}\uc2  than Democrats, even though the Senate is evenly split. As a result, the new conservative-leaning court was confirmed by a body which represents a minority of voters.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat is reflective of minority rule,\u8221\'20\'1D said Siegel.\par \par \uc2 Republican strategists\u8217\'20\'19 appeal to socially conservative voters also began to substantially redefine what it meant to be Republican.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe party professionals and establishment Republicans thought they could control them,\u8221\'20\'1D said Clarkson. \u8220\'20\'1CThey were wrong: they became the party.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The new anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  arrivals pushed for more power, working \u8220\'20\'1Cto exercise more influence over the composition of the GOP to ensure the nominees would be ideologically pure enough\u8221\'20\'1D, Clarkson said.\par \par \uc2 Demography added urgency to the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cause, Siegel said. The Republican party is overwhelmingly white and Christian, but the size of its base is threatened by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/aug/12/us-census-population-changes-white-americans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rapidly changing US demographics}}}\uc2  as America grows more racially diverse and less religious. White Americans are predicted to be a minority {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.brookings.edu/blog/the-avenue/2018/03/14/the-us-will-become-minority-white-in-2045-census-projects/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 by 2045}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That has pushed Republicans to practice \u8220\'20\'1Cexistential politics\u8221\'20\'1D, made each election cycle feel more critical than the last and forced the parties further apart, said Siegel. At the same time, partisan redistricting, known as gerrymandering, has allowed more extreme candidates to win uncompetitive districts, exacerbating polarization.\par \par \uc2 In the case of Dobbs, power and luck collided when the base elected Trump, a man who once professed to be pro-choice, won the election even as he lost the popular vote and was then offered the rare opportunity to confirm three new justices to the court.\par \par \uc2 The forces behind Dobbs also show how especially American values \u8211\'20\'13 autonomy, liberty and self-determination \u8211\'20\'13 will be redefined in a new supreme court era.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s mutual animosity between members of the two parties, but there is more of an asymmetry in terms of how far to the right the Republican party has moved, and willingness to break norms for short-term partisan advantage,\u8221\'20\'1D said Siegel. \u8220\'20\'1COr, in the case of the supreme court, for long-term partisan advantage.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 This article was amended on 24 June 2022 to clarify Frederick Clarkson\u8217\'20\'19s biography.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220624ei6o002be\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
