\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18I got in the car and he blindfolded me. I was willing to risk death\u8217\'20\'19: five women on abortions before Roe\b0\par\par\uc2 Candice Pires and Clare Considine \par \uc2 2,808 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2022\par 16:56 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 If the supreme court reverses the federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , some Americans will no longer have access to the procedure. Five women speak of their experience in pre-Roe v Wade era\par \par \uc2 Roe v Wade, the landmark \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  decision that has given Americans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights since 22 January 1973, was set to turn 50 next year. This June, as the supreme court approaches summer recess, it looks likely to release a decision that means the critical precedent will never reach its landmark birthday.\par \par \uc2 With the regulation of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  returned to individual states, a large swath of the midwest and south \u8211\'20\'13 about 20 states housing half of the country\u8217\'20\'19s population \u8211\'20\'13 will no longer have access to legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This is set to see a return to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  experiences that have many similarities to pre-Roe v Wade America. Pre-1973, those with the necessary means travelled across state lines to get the procedures they needed. Today, like back then, campaigners fear that poor, Black, Latina, teenage women and undocumented immigrants will be disproportionately affected. What is different today is that some women will be able to to access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills over the internet and self-manage the procedure.\par \par \uc2 One advocacy group, Grandmothers for Reproductive Rights, is made up of older women who fight to protect the reproductive rights they campaigned to secure pre-Roe v Wade. \u8220\'20\'1COften abortions are talked about as endings,\u8221\'20\'1D says executive director Kelli Wescott McCannell. \u8220\'20\'1CThe women in our program have decades of life since their abortions that show what was made possible for them because of that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Here we speak to five women from across the US about their experiences of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the pre-Roe v Wade era. Some were nervous, others defiant. But all shared their story in the hope that their past could shape America\u8217\'20\'19s future.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18I crawled up on the kitchen table and she had this can of Lysol\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Trudy Hale, 73, writer, Norwood, V\par \par \uc2 irginia\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 : 1967, aged 18, Memphis, Tennessee\par \par \uc2 I heard through the grapevine that a guy we knew, who was a dealer \u8211\'20\'13 it was all just grass and acid then \u8211\'20\'13 knew a doctor who did abortions. I asked the dealer, Larry, and he said, \u8220\'20\'1COh yeah, sure. It\u8217\'20\'19s $300.\u8221\'20\'1D I had no money. I lived with my dad and two brothers in a two-bedroom duplex and I was in my freshman year of college. So I stole clothes and sold them.\par \par \uc2 I gave Larry the $300 and he told me he\u8217\'20\'19d come pick me up at 6am. He drove this big blue Oldsmobile convertible. I got in the car and he blindfolded me. I was scared but had to be alert to try and sense where we were going. I was willing to risk death rather than have my father find out I was pregnant.\par \par \uc2 Larry led me into this house and up some stairs. He tightened the blindfold. The house sounded vacant. He took me into a room and said, \u8220\'20\'1CTake down your pants and lie down,\u8221\'20\'1D and then left. I remember lying there and a radio playing an interview with a farmer who was talking about how to plant crowder peas. Then I heard footsteps and at least two people came into the room. No one spoke. There was the clink and clank of instruments, and then Larry said, \u8220\'20\'1CSpread your legs.\u8221\'20\'1D Someone inserted a rubber tube into me to induce a miscarriage. There was no pain. They left the tube in there and it was cut long and left coiled in my underpants.\par \par \uc2 Larry drove me back and I only took the blindfold off when we got home. I was a good student and went straight to class. I remember sitting in the lunchroom, feeling this tube in my pants, and nothing happening. I went home, went to sleep, still nothing. The next day, I called Larry and told him. He reluctantly said he\u8217\'20\'19d set up a time to do it again. And so I went through the whole thing again. But the same thing happened, it didn\u8217\'20\'19t work.\par \par \uc2 I became desperate and terrified. I was getting bigger and couldn\u8217\'20\'19t button my pants. I must have been almost three months pregnant.\par \par \uc2 I told my friend, Mary-Ann, who was five years older and this rough, wild kind of character; she\u8217\'20\'19d been in the navy and had had a dishonourable discharge. \u8220\'20\'1CWell, I can do it,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen I was in the Navy I stole all the stuff.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 So we went to her father\u8217\'20\'19s house while he was out. She put on greens and was very proud of her surgical prowess. I crawled up on the kitchen table and she had this can of Lysol that she was spraying around the room to disinfect it. She used the same technique with the rubber tube except she inserted it much further.\par \par \uc2 I went home and the contractions started. By now it\u8217\'20\'19s night-time. My brothers were roaming around the house and I went into the bathroom and someone\u8217\'20\'19s banging on the door and I just freaked out because the pain was so bad. I had to get out.\par \par \uc2 I called Mary-Ann and she had a friend come pick me up. We went looking for a motel. It was cold, maybe late December. In a parking lot, I threw myself on the hood of a car so the snow would numb me.\par \par \uc2 We scraped together $8 for a single room and my friend snuck me in. It was eight hours of pain. I was beating my head against the wall while he brought me towels to soak up the blood. Finally, the fetus came out. I was shocked when the placenta came out after; I thought it was twins.\par \par \uc2 At 18, I hated the idea of having a child. There\u8217\'20\'19s no telling what kind of a mother I would have been then. I never regretted it. It\u8217\'20\'19s not until I was 30 that I chose to have children.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18When we got home we told people we\u8217\'20\'19d been in Memphis, shopping\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Sarah B Thompson, 68, retired school librarian, Fayetteville, Arkansas\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 : 1971, aged 17, New York\par \par \uc2 It was the summer before I was due to go to college. My parents let me fly to Oklahoma City from our home in Little Rock, Arkansas, to visit my best friend. There were lots of wild nights of partying, skinny dipping and beer drinking.\par \par \uc2 When my period was a day late, I suspected I might be pregnant but was in denial. Every afternoon, my mother and I would watch soap operas together while we ate lunch, and I started not being able to stay awake. My period never came.\par \par \uc2 I talked to a couple of friends. One told me about a local gynaecologist who could advise me what to do. When I called his office, I guess they realised they were dealing with a child because I got in quickly. I went by myself and the doctor was so patient and kind. He said I was about 12 weeks pregnant. \u8220\'20\'1CSarah,\u8221\'20\'1D he told me, \u8220\'20\'1Cyou\u8217\'20\'19re going to have to go home and tell your parents.\u8221\'20\'1D Because \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was illegal in Arkansas, he said I was going to have to travel to New York if I wanted one.\par \par \uc2 I had old southern parents, very traditional. When I told them, I could tell they were disappointed and worried. They contacted the gynaecologist and he connected them to a doctor in New York. It was arranged quickly. Mother and I were to fly to New York City and Daddy would stay home. We were not rich, but Daddy had savings.\par \par \uc2 The day of the flight, Mother and I got dressed up to travel, as you did in those days. Early the next morning, we went to New York City hospital. We had to find the bursar\u8217\'20\'19s office as she had to pay cash upfront. There were big signs saying, \u8220\'20\'1CNO CHECKS\u8221\'20\'1D. Then we went upstairs and I put on a gown.\par \par \uc2 After that, there\u8217\'20\'19s kind of a blank. I remember waiting on a gurney and then I remember waking up. I stayed in a ward overnight with three other women who\u8217\'20\'19d just had abortions. They were all older than me. We talked. One was putting on her false eyelashes and getting ready to go back to Florida. Another was married and her husband would come in to see her. I think we all felt fortunate to be there; to have safe, legal healthcare.\par \par \uc2 When we got home we told people we\u8217\'20\'19d been in Memphis, shopping for college clothes. It was a good story for the older relatives. My mother said, \u8220\'20\'1CSarah, we will never speak of this again.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Three weeks after the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , my parents dropped me off at the \uc2 University of Arkansas\uc2 , helped me unpack and drove away. I was able to show up with my classmates from high school and begin my new life.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18My rapist gave me a phone number and $100\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Fran Moreland Johns, 88, writer, San Francisco, C\par \par \uc2 alifornia\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 : 1956, aged 22, Atlanta, Georgia\par \par \uc2 When I realized I was pregnant, I went back to my rapist because I didn\u8217\'20\'19t have anywhere else to go. He gave me a phone number and $100, which was more than my monthly income. The man who raped me was basically my boss. I was right out of college and worked in public relations for petroleum companies. He was wealthy and prominent and had a family. I\u8217\'20\'19d babysat his kids. I lived with my sister. We were one year apart in school and joined at the hip but it was so shameful I didn\u8217\'20\'19t tell her.\par \par \uc2 I called the number and just said, \u8220\'20\'1CI need help.\u8221\'20\'1D A man on the other end replied, \u8220\'20\'1CDo you have the cash?\u8221\'20\'1D He gave me instructions to stand in front of a theatre downtown on Sunday at 9am, and that if anyone was with me, the deal would be off. I had no idea what was going to happen. I just figured he would get me \u8216\'20\'18unpregnant\u8217\'20\'19. I was completely numb.\par \par \uc2 It was a cold rainy February morning and the worst day of my life. A car pulled up, I got into the back seat and the man in the front handed me a blue bandanna to cover my eyes. We pulled up to a small house and he told me I could take my blindfold off. Inside was a woman, I presume his wife or girlfriend, and she showed me to a little room with a table. She told me to lie on the table and then the man came in, inserted something into my vagina, I think it was a straw, and that was it. I don\u8217\'20\'19t think he washed his hands. It took minutes. We got back in the car and he dropped me off where he\u8217\'20\'19d picked me up. He said, \u8220\'20\'1CYou\u8217\'20\'19ll start bleeding in a little while,\u8221\'20\'1D and I did and it did not stop and I was terrified.\par \par \uc2 Monday morning, I was still bleeding. I still didn\u8217\'20\'19t tell my sister. Getting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was more shameful than having been raped. I called into work sick and went to my obstetrician. \u8220\'20\'1CWho did this to you?\u8221\'20\'1D he said. I would not have told him, or anyone else, in a million years. My allegiance was to the abortionist \u8211\'20\'13 he had given me my life back. The obstetrician did a D&C and gave me a lot of medicine to combat the infection. I\u8217\'20\'19m just the luckiest person in the world to have not died of sepsis.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18I was so worried someone would find out\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman Barbara Lee, 75, California\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Aged 16, Mexico\par \par \uc2 There were two things I was afraid of. First of all, that I was going to die because unsafe septic abortions were the main cause of death for black women in the 1960s. And that I was going to be put in jail because it was illegal. On top of that, the stigma was awful. I was so worried someone would find out. I went to a Catholic school, was the first black cheerleader, and I was pregnant. As a teenager, those were very hard times before Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 We lived in California, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was illegal. When I told my mother, she let me make my own decision and then she sent me to a friend of hers in Texas, who traveled with me to have the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Mexico, where it was also illegal.\par \par \uc2 I grew up pretty quickly after because I recognized that I didn\u8217\'20\'19t have a right to make decisions about my own body. And that forced me to learn more about how this country provides for women\u8217\'20\'19s bodily autonomy. I got involved in campaigning for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/news/2005/jan/04/guardianobituaries.haroldjackson"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Shirley Chisholm }}}\uc2  and she was one of the first black women I knew who was out there speaking for reproductive justice, and how racial equity and economic equity had to be part of that.\par \par \uc2 My mother was so loving and caring in helping me make the decision. She insisted that I did not need to talk about it because it was so personal and that\u8217\'20\'19s how it should be. I didn\u8217\'20\'19t talk about it publicly but when Mississippi and Texas changed their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws last year, there was no way that I couldn\u8217\'20\'19t {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/sep/30/congresswomen-personal-abortion-stories-cori-bush-barbara-lee-pramila-jayapal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tell my story}}}\uc2 . I didn\u8217\'20\'19t want to, but I was compelled.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18I don\u8217\'20\'19t remember much about the procedure except the doctor saying, \u8216\'20\'18Don\u8217\'20\'19t make any noise\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Carol Deanow, 79, retired professor of social work, Brookline, Massachusetts\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 : 1965, aged 22, Washington\par \par \uc2 DC\par \par \uc2 I was dating somebody but we weren\u8217\'20\'19t at all serious. He refused to use a condom and I was too naive to say, \u8220\'20\'1CNo condom, no sex.\u8221\'20\'1D I missed my period and quickly knew what was happening. I never considered for a minute carrying the pregnancy to term. It was just not going to be part of my life at that point. I had a plan: I was going to finish my PhD and teach.\par \par \uc2 I told the guy I\u8217\'20\'19d been dating \u8211\'20\'13 we may not have even been dating by the time I realized I was pregnant. He said, \u8220\'20\'1CWell, I guess I should ask you to marry me.\u8221\'20\'1D I told him not to be silly. \u8220\'20\'1CWe both know we aren\u8217\'20\'19t going to do this,\u8221\'20\'1D I said. And I told a friend who I knew could get me the name of someone to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Her friend knew a friend who knew a friend who had accompanied somebody to one; it was that kind of underground network.\par \par \uc2 The unwritten rule among girls at my college was that you never let your checking account go under $300. We all knew what that meant. So I had the money. I don\u8217\'20\'19t think the guy contributed financially. And I don\u8217\'20\'19t know that I would have allowed him to; it was always considered the woman\u8217\'20\'19s problem. He did drive me the hour-long journey from Baltimore to Washington for the procedure.\par \par \uc2 We went to a small office and the person who carried it out was an MD. I was fortunate but it still felt very illegal. The room wasn\u8217\'20\'19t sterile, like an operating room, but it was clean and professional. I remember almost nothing about the procedure except the doctor saying, \u8220\'20\'1CDon\u8217\'20\'19t make any noise.\u8221\'20\'1D And that it was painful, there was no anaesthesia.\par \par \uc2 My ride drove me back to my dorm. That was the last time I saw him. I threw up in the middle of the lobby and told everyone I had the flu.\par \par \uc2 I was relieved. I felt like I\u8217\'20\'19d done something very wrong but I had to do it. We were taught to feel shame and guilt. It was the illegality of it. When I went home after that summer, I told my father I needed to see a psychiatrist. I felt like I wasn\u8217\'20\'19t making good decisions and was out of control.\par \par \uc2 A few years later, I went to social work school. When I was given an assignment to write about someone I knew who had done something deviant, I wrote about myself and my \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . My male instructor commented on the paper: \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s been a long time since I considered an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a deviant act.\u8221\'20\'1D It was the first time I\u8217\'20\'19d heard anyone talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without negativity. It was wonderful. Life-changing.\par \par \uc2 Interviews have been condensed for clarity.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220628ei6s002mh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
