\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Need an  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2 ? Better hope you have a generous boss\b0\par\par\uc2 Arwa Mahdawi \par \uc2 817 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2022\par 16:51 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s great that US companies are offering to reimburse employees who have to travel for a termination. But access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be a civil right, not a corporate perk\par \par \uc2 Not so long ago, shortly before everything went to hell, employers wooed workers with perks such as ping-pong tables and free snacks. Now, in a sign of increasingly dystopian times, they are offering all-expenses-paid \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  trips. In the wake of the US {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision}}}\uc2  on Friday to overturn {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Roe v Wade}}}\uc2 , a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/26/us-companies-pay-travel-costs-for-employees-seeking-abortions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 number of large corporations}}}\uc2  have announced they will pay for employees located in states that have banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to travel to places where it is still allowed. \uc2 Disney\uc2 , \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  and \uc2 Meta\uc2  are just some of the companies that have said their benefits packages will cover this.\par \par \uc2 I applaud any company that is genuinely trying to do the right thing. But before we all take a moment of silence to appreciate the benevolence of our corporate overlords, it is worth asking what some of the companies who profess to care so much about reproductive rights are up to behind the scenes. The answer, in many cases, is writing fat cheques to the politicians and political groups who are responsible for dismantling \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Take \uc2 T-Mobile\uc2  for example: the company has told its employees it will cover costs for abortions if needed, which is great. Not so great? In 2021, \uc2 T-Mobile\uc2  donated $100,000 to a group called the Republican Attorneys General Association (Raga), which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://popular.info/p/the-hypocrisy-of-abortion-as-a-corporate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 played a central role in getting Roe}}}\uc2  overturned. Awkward! And \uc2 T-Mobile\uc2  isn\u8217\'20\'19t the only company that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t seem to be putting its money where its mouth is. The journalist {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JuddLegum/status/1541386417821081605"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Judd Legum}}}\uc2  has dug into campaign finance data and published a handy list of all the companies that are pro-choice for PR purposes, but who aren\u8217\'20\'19t so public about their donations to Raga. They include \uc2 Match Group\uc2  (which operates dating apps such as Tinder), \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2 , \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Mastercard\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Why are companies in the US allowed to donate money to politicians, you might ask? It seems just a teeny bit corrupt. In poor countries you might describe this sort of thing as \u8220\'20\'1Cbribery\u8221\'20\'1D. In the US, however, it\u8217\'20\'19s not \u8220\'20\'1Cbribery\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 nothing like it \u8211\'20\'13 it\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aclu.org/other/aclu-and-citizens-united"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 constitutionally protected free speech.}}}\uc2  Corporations can pump money into politics thanks to a 2010 precedent set by the supreme court, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2010/jan/21/citizens-united-supreme-court-ruling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 known as Citizens United}}}\uc2 . Ah, the good old USA. Where corporations have more constitutional rights than women!\par \par \uc2 That someone who has made the decision to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have to share that with their employer is horrifying\par \par \uc2 Even if a company hasn\u8217\'20\'19t donated a cent to Republican politicians; even if it is really trying to do the right thing, the fact that employers get any say over whether you are able to access an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or not is horrifying. The fact that someone who has made the deeply personal decision to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have to share that with their employer is horrifying. Healthcare (and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is obviously healthcare) should never be tied to employment. And yet, in the US, it is. The quality of care you get \u8211\'20\'13 your ability to access care without draining your bank account \u8211\'20\'13 often depends on who you work for and the sort of coverage they provide.\par \par \uc2 Being able to control healthcare gives corporations a lot of power \u8211\'20\'13 something they are well aware of that. Last year, a number of \uc2 Kellogg\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s workers went on strike to protest against unfair working conditions. One of the first things management did was cut off their healthcare; striking workers had to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.rollingstone.com/politics/politics-features/kelloggs-strike-labor-wages-overtime-1261994/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pay up to $2,980 in Consolidated Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act (Cobra) payments}}}\uc2 . (\uc2 Cobra\uc2  gives you the right to continue with your employer\u8217\'20\'19s health plan after you are fired; you just need to pay a ridiculous amount of money for the privilege.) Now it looks suspiciously as if companies might start using access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a way to get their employees to step in line. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , for example, has said that it will reimburse \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  travel expenses under its healthcare plan but noted that it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.seattletimes.com/business/starbucks/starbucks-says-it-will-cover-abortion-travel-and-gender-affirming-care/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cannot \u8220\'20\'1Cmake promises of guarantees about any benefits}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D for unionised stores. Better not unionise if you don\u8217\'20\'19t want to be forced to give birth!\par \par \uc2 So that\u8217\'20\'19s where we are in the richest country on Earth: human rights being doled out by HR departments. Civil rights downgraded to corporate perks.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Arwa Mahdawi is a Guardian columnist\par \par \uc2 *\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://membership.theguardian.com/event/what-would-the-end-of-abortion-rights-in-america-mean-for-the-world-358372139717"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 What will the end of \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights in America mean for the world?}}}\par \par \uc2 Join Arwa Mahdawi, Michele Bratcher Goodwin and congresswoman Barbara Lee, who will discuss the landmark Roe v Wade ruling in this livestreamed event. On Wednesday 6 July 2022, 8pm-9pm BST/3pm EDT. Book tickets {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://membership.theguardian.com/event/what-will-the-end-of-abortion-rights-in-america-mean-for-the-world-358372139717"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220628ei6s001p7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
