\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Louisiana judge blocks  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban amid uproar after Roe v Wade ruling\b0\par\par\uc2 Martin Pengelly and Samira Asma-Sadeque in New York and agencies \par \uc2 1,094 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2022\par 14:06 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 State temporarily blocked from enforcing ban as other US states pass \u8216\'20\'18trigger laws\u8217\'20\'19 designed to severely curtail access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 A Louisiana judge on Monday temporarily stopped the state from enforcing Republican-backed laws banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , set to take effect after the \uc2 US supreme court{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/27/alexandria-ocasio-cortez-supreme-court-justices-impeach-kavanaugh-gorsuch-thomas"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ended}}}\uc2  the constitutional right to the procedure last week.\par \par \uc2 Louisiana is one of 13 states which passed \u8220\'20\'1Ctrigger laws\u8221\'20\'1D, to ban or severely restrict abortions once the supreme court overturned the 1973 Roe v Wade ruling that recognized a right to the procedure. It did so on Friday, stoking uproar among progressives and protests and counter-protests on the streets of major cities.\par \par \uc2 In New Orleans on Monday, an Orleans Parish civil district court judge, Robin Giarrusso, issued a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3OEBEbG"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 temporary restraining order}}}\uc2 , blocking the state ban.\par \par \uc2 The case before Judge Giarruso, a Democrat, was brought by Hope Medical Group for Women in Shreveport, one of three \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re going to do what we can,\u8221\'20\'1D Kathaleen Pittman, administrator of Hope Medical Group, told the Associated Press. \u8220\'20\'1CIt could all come to a screeching halt.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://reproductiverights.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/06/LA-Trigger-Ban-Claim.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Louisiana lawsuit}}}\uc2  is one of several challenging Republican-backed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws under state constitutions.\par \par \uc2 A Utah judge blocked that state\u8217\'20\'19s near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban from going into effect for 14 days, to allow time for the court to hear challenges to the state\u8217\'20\'19s trigger law. Planned Parenthood had challenged the law, which contains narrow exceptions for rape, incest or the mother\u8217\'20\'19s health, saying the law violates the equal protection and privacy provisions in the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 A Florida ban on abortions after 15 weeks is also the subject of a request for a temporary block. A ruling on that is expected Thursday \u8211\'20\'13 a day before the law is scheduled to take effect.\par \par \uc2 In Arizona, the \uc2 American Civil Liberties Union (ACLU) \uc2 and an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights group filed an emergency motion on Saturday, seeking to block a 2021 law they worry can be used to halt all abortions.\par \par \uc2 On the national stage on Monday, a group of 22 attorneys general issued a statement promising to \u8220\'20\'1Cleverage our collective resources\u8221\'20\'1D to help women in states where abortions are banned.\par \par \uc2 A statement said: \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care is healthcare. Period.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The statement was signed by the attorneys general of California, Colorado, Connecticut, Delaware, the District of Columbia, Hawaii, Illinois, Maine, Maryland, Massachusetts, Michigan, Minnesota, Nevada, New Jersey, New Mexico, New York, North Carolina, Oregon, Pennsylvania, Rhode Island, Vermont and Washington.\par \par \uc2 They said: \u8220\'20\'1CWe stand together, as our states\u8217\'20\'19 chief law officers, to proudly say that we will not back down in the fight to protect the rights of pregnant people in our states and across the country.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s decision reverses nearly half a century of legal precedent and undermines the rights of people across the United States, we\u8217\'20\'19re joining together to reaffirm our commitment to supporting and expanding access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care nationwide.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The statement added: \u8220\'20\'1CUltimately, what harms people in some states harms us all. The future and wellbeing of our nation is intrinsically tied to the ability of our residents to exercise their fundamental rights.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\u8230\'20\'26 If you seek access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and reproductive healthcare, we\u8217\'20\'19re committed to using the full force of the law to \u8230\'20\'26 fight for your rights and stand up for our laws.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe will support our partners and service providers. We will take on those who seek to control your bodies and leverage our collective resources \u8211\'20\'13 thousands of lawyers and dedicated public servants across our states. Together, we will persist.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As of Saturday, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services had stopped in at least 11 states. Speaking to the Associated Press, Brigitte Amiri, deputy director of the \uc2 ACLU\uc2  Reproductive Freedom Project, said the group was looking at \u8220\'20\'1Call options\u8221\'20\'1D to protect access.\par \par \uc2 But lawsuits may only buy time. Even if courts block restrictions, lawmakers could address any cited flaws.\par \par \uc2 That is likely to be the case in Louisiana. The plaintiffs in the suit there do not deny that the state can ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Instead, they contend Louisiana has multiple and conflicting trigger mechanisms in law.\par \par \uc2 The suit says the trigger laws, the first of which was passed in 2006, make it impossible to tell when they are in effect, if one or all are in force and what conduct is prohibited. The lawsuit contends that such vagueness has resulted in state and local officials issuing conflicting statements about whether the trigger bans are in effect.\par \par \uc2 Giarruso wrote: \u8220\'20\'1CEach of the three trigger bans excepts different conduct, making it impossible to know what \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care is illegal and what is allowed, including what care can be provided to save a woman\u8217\'20\'19s life or end a medically futile pregnancy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Giarruso scheduled an 8 July hearing to decide whether to further block enforcement of the ban. The Center for Reproductive Rights, which filed the suit on behalf of the clinic, said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care was resuming in Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 The Republican state attorney general, \uc2 Jeff Landry\uc2 , did not immediately comment. On Friday, he said those who challenged state bans would be \u8220\'20\'1Cin for a rough fight\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors in some Democratic-led cities in Republican-led states have indicated they will not enforce \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 The New Orleans district attorney, Jason Williams, said he would not criminalize abortions and that the overturning of Roe v Wade \u8220\'20\'1Cis a cruel and irresponsible stripping of a woman\u8217\'20\'19s agency\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Condemning leaders for not focusing on issues such as lifting children out of poverty, he {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/orleansparishda/status/1540416074272874498/photo/1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 added}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CIt would not be wise or prudent to shift our priority from tackling senseless violence happening in our city to investigating the choices women make with regard to their own body.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 On Monday, in light of moves by Cincinnati city leaders to support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, Joseph Deters, the Republican county prosecutor, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wvxu.org/local-news/2022-06-27/cincinnati-reimburse-city-workers-cost-traveling-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CI have repeatedly stated it is dangerous when prosecutors pick and choose what laws they want to enforce. When prosecutors do not follow their oath, it will promote lawlessness and challenge the basic structures of separation of powers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Regarding the Louisiana case, \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2 , chief executive of the \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://reproductiverights.org/abortion-providers-challenge-trigger-laws-in-louisiana-after-supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CA public health emergency is about to engulf the nation. We will be fighting to restore access in Louisiana and other states for as long as we can.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEvery day that a clinic is open and providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services can make a difference in a person\u8217\'20\'19s life.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220628ei6s001uu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
