\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Why US women are deleting their period tracking apps\b0\par\par\uc2 Flora Garamvolgyi in Los Angeles \par \uc2 1,740 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2022\par 14:10 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Even before the supreme court decision to overturn Roe v Wade, the trend to ditch the apps began amid fears of prosecution\par \par \uc2 Many American women in recent days have deleted period tracking apps from their cellphones, amid fears the data collected by the apps could be used against them in future criminal cases in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has become illegal.\par \par \uc2 The trend already started last month when a draft supreme court opinion that suggested the court was set to overturn Roe v Wade was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/02/roe-v-wade-abortion-supreme-court-draft-opinion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 leaked}}}\uc2 , and has only intensified since the court on Friday {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/24/scotus-roe-wade-decision-what-happens-next"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 revoked}}}\uc2  the federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 These concerns are not baseless. As with various other apps, cycle trackers collect, retain and at times share some of their users\u8217\'20\'19 data. In a state where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a crime, prosecutors could request information collected by these apps when building a case against someone. \u8220\'20\'1CIf they are trying to prosecute a woman for getting an illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they can subpoena any app on their device, including period trackers,\u8221\'20\'1D said Sara Spector, a Texas-based criminal defense attorney, and ex-prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut every company has their individual storage and privacy policy about how they use and how long they store data,\u8221\'20\'1D Spector added.\par \par \uc2 Cycle trackers are popular for a reason. Nearly a third of American women have been using them, according to a 2019 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kff.org/other/poll-finding/kff-health-apps-and-information-survey/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 survey}}}\uc2  published by the \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2 . They have helped make women\u8217\'20\'19s lives easier in many ways, from family planning and detecting early signs of health issues to choosing the perfect time for a holiday.\par \par \uc2 A 2019 study {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bmj.com/content/364/bmj.l920"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  published in the British Medical Journal (BMJ}}}\uc2  ) found that 79% of health apps available through the \uc2 Google\uc2  Play store that were related to medicine, including apps that help manage drugs, adherence, medicines, or prescribing information, regularly shared user data and were \u8220\'20\'1Cfar from transparent\u8221\'20\'1D. But many of the big players have made progress over the past years.\par \par \uc2 Two of the most popular period trackers in the US, Flo and Clue, have more than 55 million users combined. The Berlin-based app Clue said it was \u8220\'20\'1Ccommitted to protecting\u8221\'20\'1D users\u8217\'20\'19 private health data and that it was operating under strict European GDPR laws. The company\u8217\'20\'19s website says the app collects device data, event and usage data, in addition to a user\u8217\'20\'19s IP address, health and sensitive data it may use for the purpose of improving the app, the services, and preventing abusive use of its service. But Clue does not track users\u8217\'20\'19 precise location, and says it does not store sensitive personal data without a user\u8217\'20\'19s explicit permission. The company also tweeted that it would have a \u8220\'20\'1Cprimary legal duty under European law\u8221\'20\'1D not to disclose any private health data and it would \u8220\'20\'1Cnot respond to any disclosure request or attempted subpoena of their users\u8217\'20\'19 health data by US authorities\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 But just because data is being processed by a European company, doesn\u8217\'20\'19t mean that it is entirely immune from US prosecution, said Lucie Audibert, a lawyer at \uc2 Privacy International\uc2 , a global NGO that researches, litigates and advocates against abuses of technology and data by governments and corporations.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe fact that GDPR applies is not that relevant in this case. When it comes to a legitimate legal request from US authorities European companies usually comply. Also, a European company may be hosting data outside the EU, making it subject to different legal frameworks and cross-border agreements,\u8221\'20\'1D Audibert added. She also stressed that using a Europe-based app won\u8217\'20\'19t protect women from the courts requesting data from them directly. But it can be a slightly better option than using a US-based one because US companies are more easily compelled to comply with American authorities and courts\u8217\'20\'19 requests. Enforcement is more difficult against European ones.\par \par \uc2 Flo has come under fire for sharing its users\u8217\'20\'19 data before. The company says on its website it only uses data \u8220\'20\'1Cfor research activities\u8221\'20\'1D and that it only uses \u8220\'20\'1Cde-identified or aggregated data, which cannot be associated\u8221\'20\'1D with specific users. But an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/you-give-apps-sensitive-personal-information-then-they-tell-facebook-11550851636"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 investigative piece}}}\uc2  by the Wall Street Journal has found that the app informed \uc2 Facebook\uc2  when a user was on their period or if they intended to get pregnant. In 2021, the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  (FTC) reached a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2021/01/developer-popular-womens-fertility-tracking-app-settles-ftc-allegations-it-misled-consumers-about"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 settlement with Flo}}}\uc2 . Under the settlement, Flo must undergo an independent review of its privacy policy and obtain user permissions before sharing personal health information. Flo did not admit any wrongdoing.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, Flo {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/flotracker/status/1540404337842995200"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced}}}\uc2  that it will soon be launching an \u8220\'20\'1CAnonymous mode\u8221\'20\'1D that can help keep users\u8217\'20\'19 data safe in any circumstances.\par \par \uc2 The company did not respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Any app where a company [that could receive a subpoena] has access to their users\u8217\'20\'19 data could make it vulnerable for a legal request\par \par \uc2 Evan Greer\par \par \uc2 A relatively new, astrology-focused period tracker, Stardust, became the most downloaded free app on iOS in the days after the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision. Stardust\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Twitter\uc2  bio says it is a \u8220\'20\'1Cprivacy first period tracking app\u8221\'20\'1D. But as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vice.com/en/article/y3pgvg/the-1-period-tracker-on-the-app-store-will-hand-over-data-without-a-warrant"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Vice News}}}\uc2  reported, the company stated in its privacy policy that if authorities ask for user data, it will comply, whether legally required to or not. It said that the data was \u8220\'20\'1Canonymized\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cencrypted\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe may disclose your anonymized, encrypted information to third parties in order to protect the legal rights, safety and security of the company and the users of our services; enforce our terms of service; prevent fraud; and comply with or respond to law enforcement or a legal process or a request for cooperation by a government or other entity, whether or not legally required,\u8221\'20\'1D their privacy policy stated as of Monday.\par \par \uc2 Following Vice\u8217\'20\'19s request for comment, Stardust changed its privacy policy to omit the phrase about cooperating with law enforcement \u8220\'20\'1Cwhether or not legally required\u8221\'20\'1D to \u8220\'20\'1Cwhen legally required\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Stardust did not immediately respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood encourages people to use their app Spot On. \u8220\'20\'1CPeople who want to track their periods and birth control always have the option to remain anonymous by using the Spot On app without creating an account,\u8221\'20\'1D the organization said in a statement. \u8220\'20\'1CThis way, period or birth control data is only saved locally to a person\u8217\'20\'19s phone and can be deleted at any time by deleting the app.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Third-party apps are not the only option when it comes to period trackers. \uc2 Apple\uc2  has a built-in cycle tracker in its Health app that offers more privacy than most external apps. With just a few {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://appleinsider.com/articles/22/06/24/how-to-ensure-apple-health-cycle-tracking-data-stays-private"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 steps}}}\uc2 , one can turn off the storing of their health data in iCloud, and it also has the option to store the encrypted data on their computer or phone.\par \par \uc2 Evan Greer, deputy director of the non-profit advocacy group \uc2 Fight for the Future\uc2 , said the best way to protect sensitive health data was to only use apps that store data locally rather than in the cloud. \u8220\'20\'1CBecause any app where a company [that could receive a subpoena] has access to their users\u8217\'20\'19 data could make it vulnerable for a legal request.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Eva Blum-Dumontet, a tech policy consultant, said, \u8220\'20\'1CIt is normal that in times of concern, people are looking differently at technology and apps that we trusted.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think when there is a discourse around whether women should delete these apps, we have to think about why they use them in the first place,\u8221\'20\'1D Blum-Dumontet said. \u8220\'20\'1CThese trackers help them manage menstrual cycle when they are experiencing pain.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Blum-Dumontet stressed that instead of asking users to change their behaviors, \u8220\'20\'1Cit is period trackers that should change their practices\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey should never have owned so much data in the first place. If they adopted practices like storing data locally and minimizing the data to what\u8217\'20\'19s strictly necessary we wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be having this debate now. It\u8217\'20\'19s not too late for them to do the right thing,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe companies that have been making a profit out of women\u8217\'20\'19s bodies need to think very carefully about how they will protect their users,\u8221\'20\'1D she continued. \u8220\'20\'1C They haven\u8217\'20\'19t all been the best in the past when it comes to data sharing. The only way they can survive in this market, the only way they can make themselves trustworthy is by improving their privacy policy and giving users more control over their data,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CIf any of these apps will be used in court against their users, it will not be good PR for them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Melissa, a 27-year-old mother from Texas who is goingby only her first name to not jeopardize her employment, said she deleted the app because she fears that when she travels, her state could use her missed period data against her.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI will miss using the app so much. I have used it for so many things, like tracking my ovulation or predicting my mood changes. Sometimes I wake up feeling irritable, and I don\u8217\'20\'19t know why until my app tells me that this could be normal at this point of my cycle,\u8221\'20\'1D she added. Melissa also says she would have loved to use it for future conceptions, but now she can\u8217\'20\'19t.\par \par \uc2 Although much of the warnings on Friday were focused on just period trackers these are not the only apps that can be used against users when it comes to criminal prosecution, experts warned.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Google\uc2  Maps or a random game on your phone could just as easily be weaponized against someone as a menstrual tracking app,\u8221\'20\'1D Greer said. \u8220\'20\'1CWhile we need to educate each other and take precautions, it\u8217\'20\'19s not OK to put the responsibility solely on individuals. Companies and lawmakers need to act immediately to protect people.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The concerns over period tracking data are part of a broader conversation about the amount of personal information smartphones collect. Women\u8217\'20\'19s rights organizations all over the world are warning users to be more mindful of their digital presence, not just when it comes to period trackers.\par \par \uc2 Cycle tracking apps can be hugely useful for many women, said Jonathan Lord, UK medical director for MSI Reproductive Choices. \u8220\'20\'1CBut all data can be used against you.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 According to Lord, this danger will remain until \u8220\'20\'1Cwe treat \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  like all other healthcare \u8211\'20\'13 regulated like all other medical procedures, but not criminalized\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220628ei6s001e7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
