\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18They set a torch to it\u8217\'20\'19: Warren says court lost legitimacy with Roe reversal\b0\par\par\uc2 Edward Helmore in New York and Ramon Antonio Vargas \par \uc2 751 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2022\par 12:04 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 20\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Top Democrats again call for appointing additional justices to blunt conservative super-majority which made ruling possible\par \par \uc2 Leading Democrats on Sunday continued calling the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s legitimacy into question after it took away the nationwide right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week, and some again lobbied for appointing additional justices to the panel so as to blunt the conservative super-majority that made the controversial ruling possible.\par \par \uc2 The Massachusetts senator {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/elizabeth-warren"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Elizabeth Warren}}}\uc2  suggested to ABC\u8217\'20\'19s This Week that expanding the court was an urgent matter because supreme court justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  indicated in Friday\u8217\'20\'19s decision to overturn the landmark Roe v Wade ruling that he is open to reconsidering precedents guaranteeing the right to contraception, same-sex marriage and consensual gay sex.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey have burned whatever legitimacy they may still have had,\u8221\'20\'1D Warren said of the supreme court. \u8220\'20\'1CThey just took the last of it and set a torch to it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Warren joined Georgia gubernatorial candidate and Democratic organizer \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  in again lobbying to expand the supreme court in a way that balances the current makeup of six conservatives and three liberals.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has rejected the strategy. But Abrams \u8211\'20\'13 who previously served in Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s house of representatives \u8211\'20\'13 said the president doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have the final word on the matter, with legislators also having a say.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s nothing sacrosanct about nine members of the United States supreme court,\u8221\'20\'1D Abrams said on CNN\u8217\'20\'19s State of the Union.\par \par \uc2 Warren again discussed abolishing the filibuster, a tactic that both parties use to prevent legislative decisions, a move Biden and centrist Democrats have also rejected.\par \par \uc2 She also urged Biden to issue orders shielding medication abortions and authorizing the terminations of pregnancies on federal land.\par \par \uc2 Appearing on NBC\u8217\'20\'19s Meet the Press, the New York congresswoman {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/alexandria-ocasio-cortez"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez}}}\uc2  argued that drastic measures were justified.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI believe that the president and the Democratic party needs to come to terms with is that this is not just a crisis of Roe \u8211\'20\'13 this is a crisis of our democracy,\u8221\'20\'1D Ocasio-Cortez said.\par \par \uc2 The congresswoman also said the supreme court was undergoing \u8220\'20\'1Ca crisis of legitimacy\u8221\'20\'1D, alluding to how Thomas\u8217\'20\'19s wife, Ginni, emailed 29 Republican lawmakers in Arizona as she tried to help overturn Biden\u8217\'20\'19s victory over \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the 2020 presidential election.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe supreme court has dramatically overreached its authority,\u8221\'20\'1D Ocasio-Cortez said. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a crisis of legitimacy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Speaking from a Republican point of view on another program, South Dakota governor \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  said it was \u8220\'20\'1Cincredible\u8221\'20\'1D that reproductive laws had been returned to the states. South Dakota is one of 13 states where trigger laws banning most abortions came into effect after Friday\u8217\'20\'19s decision.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe supreme court did its job: it fixed a wrong decision it made many years ago and returned this power back to the states, which is how the constitution and our founders intended it,\u8221\'20\'1D Noem told \uc2 CBS\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 Face the Nation with Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 South Dakota, she said, would ensure that \u8220\'20\'1Cbabies are recognized and that every single life is precious\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The governor said the state would move to block Democratic efforts to allow access to out-of-state telemedicine and the ability of health practitioners in legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  states to provide pills in the mail that would allow them to end a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Noem said that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills were \u8220\'20\'1Cvery dangerous medical procedures\u8221\'20\'1D, though Face the Nation host Margaret Brennan correctly pointed out that the pills were approved by the \uc2 federal Food and Drug Administration\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, Noem claimed that \u8220\'20\'1Ca woman is five times more likely to end up in an emergency room if they\u8217\'20\'19re utilizing this kind of method for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s something that should be under the supervision of a medical doctor and it is something in South Dakota that we\u8217\'20\'19ve made sure happens that way.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The governor, a rising star in Republican circles, said that mothers would not be prosecuted for receiving abortions, rather the state planned to target illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe will make sure that mothers have the resources, protection and medical care that they need and we\u8217\'20\'19re being aggressive on that. And we\u8217\'20\'19ll also make sure that the federal government only does its job,\u8221\'20\'1D Noem added.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 This article was amended on 28 June 2022 to correct misspellings of Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\u8217\'20\'19s surname as \u8220\'20\'1COcascio-Cortez\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220626ei6q001xh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
