\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 The supreme court just overturned Roe v Wade \u8211\'20\'13 what happens next?\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza \par \uc2 788 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par 15:21 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Court\u8217\'20\'19s move will allow more than half of states to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with an immediate impact on tens of millions of Americans\par \par \uc2 The supreme court just overturned the landmark Roe v Wade case, which granted women in the US the right to terminate a pregnancy. A reversal of this magnitude is almost unprecedented, particularly on a case decided nearly 50 years ago.\par \par \uc2 The extraordinarily rare move will allow more than half of states to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with an immediate and enduring impact on tens of millions of Americans.\par \par \uc2 What happened?\par \par \uc2 The court decided there is no constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a case called Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization. In reaching that decision, the conservative-majority court overturned Roe v Wade, from 1973.\par \par \uc2 Historically, the court has overturned cases to grant more rights. The court has done the opposite here, and its decision will restrict a constitutional right generations of Americans have grown up taking for granted.\par \par \uc2 As a result of the reversal, states will again be permitted to ban or severely restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , changes that will indelibly alter the national understanding of liberty, self-determination and personal autonomy.\par \par \uc2 Where will this happen?\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/article/2021/10/26-states-are-certain-or-likely-ban-abortion-without-roe-heres-which-ones-and-why"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Twenty-six states}}}\uc2  are expected to do so immediately, or as soon as practicable. This will make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  illegal across most of the south and midwest.\par \par \uc2 In these states, women and other people who can become pregnant will need to either travel hundreds of miles to reach an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider or {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/07/abortion-pill-at-home-activists-future-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 self-manage abortions}}}\uc2  at home through {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/feb/24/medication-abortion-majority-2020-analysis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 medication}}}\uc2  or other means.\par \par \uc2 However, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws are not national. The US will have a patchwork of laws, including restrictions and protections, because some Democratic-led states such as California and New York expanded reproductive rights in the run-up to the decision.\par \par \uc2 Even so, new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans will make the US one of just four nations to roll back \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights since 1994, and by far the wealthiest and most influential nation to do so. The other three nations to curtail \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are Poland, El Salvador and Nicaragua, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/2022/05/05/only-3-countries-have-rolled-back-abortion-rights-since-1994"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights}}}\uc2 . More than half (58%) of all US women of reproductive age \u8211\'20\'13 or 40 million people \u8211\'20\'13 live in states {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/article/2019/08/state-abortion-policy-landscape-hostile-supportive"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hostile to \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 When will this happen?\par \par \uc2 Across most states, this will happen quickly. Thirteen states have \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans \u8220\'20\'1Ctriggered\u8221\'20\'1D by a reversal of Roe v Wade, though the laws vary in their enforcement dates. Louisiana, for example, has a trigger law that is supposed to take effect {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thecentersquare.com/louisiana/democrat-passed-louisiana-trigger-law-could-ban-abortion-statewide-if-roe-v-wade-is-overturned/article_2f849f74-caf2-11ec-857a-cbf0ccd04254.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 immediately}}}\uc2 . Idaho has a trigger ban that goes into effect in 30 days.\par \par \uc2 Other states have \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that pre-date the Roe decision, but have been unenforceable in the last five decades. Michigan has a pre-Roe ban that is currently the subject of a court challenge.\par \par \uc2 A final group of states intends to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  very early in pregnancy, often {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5269518/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 before women know they are pregnant}}}\uc2 . One such state is Georgia, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be banned at six weeks. Several states, such as Texas, have multiple bans in place.\par \par \uc2 In many cases, court challenges under state constitutions are likely, and experts believe there will be chaos for days or weeks as states implement bans.\par \par \uc2 Can the federal government stop this?\par \par \uc2 The most effective protection against state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans is a federal law, which would precede the states. Public opinion favors such statute \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/1576/abortion.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 85% of Americans}}}\uc2  believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in at least some circumstances.\par \par \uc2 Such a law would need the majority support of the House of Representatives, a 60-vote majority in the Senate, and a signature from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to pass. A majority of members of the House of Representatives support an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights statute, as does the White House.\par \par \uc2 However, Republicans are almost certain to block \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights laws in the Senate, which is evenly split with Democrats. One Democratic senator, Joe Manchin of West Virginia, has repeatedly crossed party lines to vote against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. That leaves just 49 Democrats, far short of the support needed to pass such a measure.\par \par \uc2 To overcome the evenly split Senate, Democrats would need to win landslide victories in the upcoming midterm elections. However, despite the fact that popular opinion favors \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, it is unclear how the midterms could be swayed by the issue.\par \par \uc2 And, regardless of the outcome of the next election, Dobbs will forever change life in the US. The lives of individuals will be irrevocably altered as people are denied reproductive healthcare, face long journeys or are forced to give birth.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 This article was amended on 27 June 2022 to clarify a statistic on Americans and their beliefs about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220624ei6o0028n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
