\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Roe v Wade: legal experts see limited opportunities to challenge court ruling\b0\par\par\uc2 Edward Helmore \par \uc2 1,002 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par 14:49 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 19\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Legal scholar Lawrence Tribe: \u8216\'20\'18We\u8217\'20\'19re in for a long, tangled, chaotic and, in terms of human suffering, horribly costly struggle\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  on Saturday renewed his criticism of the supreme court, a day after justices handed down a historic ruling that overturned a ruling that had guaranteed a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for almost half a century.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe supreme court has made some terrible decisions,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said at an event where he signed last week\u8217\'20\'19s bipartisan gun control bill into law. The president said he and the first lady, Jill Biden, knew \u8220\'20\'1Chow painful and devastating the decision is for so many Americans\u8221\'20\'1D and vowed that his administration would focus on how states implement the decision.\par \par \uc2 But the White House has limited options, leaving constitutional and legal experts to warn on Saturday that there was no short-term way besides statute law to restore federal guarantees to woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overruled Roe v Wade on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The opportunities to challenge the justices\u8217\'20\'19 ruling or re-present constitutional law arguments based on equal rights are for now limited.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re in for a long, tangled, chaotic and, in terms of human suffering, horribly costly struggle,\u8221\'20\'1D said the Harvard constitutional law professor Lawrence Tribe, who has described Friday\u8217\'20\'19s decision as \u8220\'20\'1Cunprincipled\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Tribe told the Guardian that it may take generations to completely restore \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights but there may be opportunities to minimize the effects of the ruling.\par \par \uc2 One of those could be for Congress to give powers to the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  or the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  to override state laws. That issue came up on Friday when the attorney general, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , said in a statement that states {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/attorney-general-merrick-b-garland-statement-supreme-court-ruling-dobbs-v-jackson-women-s"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cannot ban mifepristone}}}\uc2 , a medication that is used to bring about an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , based on disagreement with the federal government on its safety and efficacy.\par \par \uc2 Mifepristone and misoprostol, another drug used to induce \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , have been approved by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  for use in the first 10 weeks of pregnancy. But some states, including Louisiana, have adopted legislation to make it illegal for doctors to mail the medications.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Garland said the justice department \u8220\'20\'1Cstrongly supports efforts by Congress to codify Americans\u8217\'20\'19 reproductive rights, which it retains the authority to do\u8221\'20\'1D. But federal law is unclear as to whether states can ban the medication, and the question would probably return to the same bench that issued Friday\u8217\'20\'19s ruling.\par \par \uc2 Some scholars have gone back to Roe v Wade to find where that ruling gave the current majority on the bench space to vacate the decision.\par \par \uc2 One idea that has come up is that the original ruling discussed liberty but not equality.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s foolish really to attempt to explain why the ruling wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have got the highest grade in a constitutional law class,\u8221\'20\'1D says Tribe, who is cited four times in Friday\u8217\'20\'19s ruling. \u8220\'20\'1CThe court should have talked about equality, no question, but it also talked about liberty and liberty embodies the idea of equality.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 One avenue that could be more fruitful in repairing the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is in state constitutions that have their own protections around liberty and equality.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI would expect creative litigants to challenge state laws banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the basis of the state\u8217\'20\'19s own constitution. If the state courts, which are in many cases elected, look at this through the same lens as the supreme court did, they won\u8217\'20\'19t get anywhere,\u8221\'20\'1D Tribe said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut it is possible that in some states there are more liberal, progressive or pro-gender equality state courts that would interpret the state constitution as giving more rights to women than they have been given under the federal constitution,\u8221\'20\'1D Tribe added.\par \par \uc2 Kevin O\u8217\'20\'19Brien, a partner at Ford O\u8217\'20\'19Brien Landy, a boutique law firm, pointed out that Roe v Wade was written 50 years ago \u8211\'20\'13 a long time in terms of constitutional law.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was written in a benignly liberal era where there weren\u8217\'20\'19t ideological tensions. Under the Warren court the writing was a little more casual and the justices wrote a broad sweep. They tucked Roe under this rather vague notion that there was a privacy interest implicit in the constitution.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 O\u8217\'20\'19Brien cited the late justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 , who noted that Roe v Wade was argued on a right to privacy, rather than women\u8217\'20\'19s rights. \u8220\'20\'1CRoe isn\u8217\'20\'19t really about the woman\u8217\'20\'19s choice, is it?\u8221\'20\'1D Ginsburg once said. That held, of course, until it didn\u8217\'20\'19t. \u8220\'20\'1CIt didn\u8217\'20\'19t seem to require a lot of arguing among civilized people that people had autonomy in their own bedrooms and women had a right to control their own body, but then we got this revolution in thinking among conservative judges.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As the federal and some state governments work to navigate new legal barriers, private companies have signaled they will try to bridge the gap. Some of the most recognized US companies, including \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2  (parent of \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2 ), \uc2 Walt Disney\uc2 , \uc2 Tesla\uc2  and \uc2 Apple\uc2 , have said they will extend coverage to workers who need access to safe, legal abortions.\par \par \uc2 Conservative lawmakers have warned that companies could face legal action if they support employee \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  travel. \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  was warned by a Texas lawmaker that under that state\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law the bank could face criminal charges. In Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s concurring opinion in Friday\u8217\'20\'19s court ruling, the Trump-appointed justice said states cannot bar their residents from traveling to seek abortions.\par \par \uc2 But, as Tribe pointed out, beyond legislative action guaranteeing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights that few believe is possible, options are limited. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you mean recourse to completely restoring to before the ruling, that may take generations,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you mean minimizing the ruling\u8217\'20\'19s damage, taking steps along the margins to reduce the devastation of the ruling then, yes, that certainly can be done.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220626ei6q000uo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
