\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18A matter of life and death\u8217\'20\'19: maternal mortality rate will rise without Roe, experts warn\b0\par\par\uc2 Melody Schreiber \par \uc2 1,302 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par 14:24 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Pregnancy in the US is already dangerous, disproportionately so for people of color \u8211\'20\'13 and without \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access for those who need it, there will likely be more deaths\par \par \uc2 After the revocation of the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the United States, pregnancy-related deaths will almost certainly increase \u8211\'20\'13 especially among people of color, experts say. They called for urgent action to protect reproductive rights and the health of patients around the country.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are going to be more people who are forced to carry a pregnancy to term, which means that there\u8217\'20\'19s going to be a greater number of people who are at risk,\u8221\'20\'1D said Rachel Hardeman, a reproductive health equity professor and researcher at the University of Minnesota School of Public Health. \u8220\'20\'1CMore pregnancy means more likelihood of deaths.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Existing state bans could lead to an additional 75,000 births a year for those who can\u8217\'20\'19t access abortions, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Caitlin_K_Myers/status/1522540905881903104"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according}}}\uc2  to one estimate. The bans will disproportionately {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 affect}}}\uc2  younger, poorer people of color and those who already have children.\par \par \uc2 But America is an incredibly difficult place to be pregnant, with the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.commonwealthfund.org/publications/issue-briefs/2020/nov/maternal-mortality-maternity-care-us-compared-10-countries"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 highest maternal mortality rate}}}\uc2  by far of any developed country \u8211\'20\'13 and it\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/data/hestat/maternal-mortality/2020/maternal-mortality-rates-2020.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rising sharply}}}\uc2 . For every 100,000 births, 23.8 people died from pregnancy or childbirth-related causes in 2020 \u8211\'20\'13 a total of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/data/hestat/maternal-mortality/2020/maternal-mortality-rates-2020.htm#:~:text=In%202020%2C%20861%20women%20were,20.1%20in%202019%20(Table)."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 861 women}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 according to the \uc2 US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC)\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As Roe fell, states immediately moved to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/abortion-laws-by-state-map-clinics"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than half}}}\uc2  of US states expected to ultimately do so. But some, like former Vice-President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , want lawmakers to go even further, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2022/06/24/abortion-supreme-court-trump-pence-republicans-roe/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 calling}}}\uc2  for a nationwide ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 More pregnancy means more likelihood of deaths\par \par \uc2 Rachel Hardeman\par \par \uc2 A nationwide ban would result in a 21% increase in pregnancy-related mortality across the country, but it would be even worse for people of color, with a 33% rise in deaths, according to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://read.dukeupress.edu/demography/article/58/6/2019/265968/The-Pregnancy-Related-Mortality-Impact-of-a-Total"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 study}}}\uc2  by Amanda Jean Stevenson, an assistant professor of sociology at the \uc2 University of Colorado Boulder\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPregnancy is really quite dangerous,\u8221\'20\'1D Stevenson said.\par \par \uc2 And it\u8217\'20\'19s disproportionately more dangerous for people of color, including Black, Indigenous and Latino people.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe truth of the matter is, it\u8217\'20\'19s already hitting people [of color] harder than others \u8211\'20\'13 that\u8217\'20\'19s been the reality,\u8221\'20\'1D said Monica McLemore, an associate professor of family healthcare nursing at \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Black people in the US were already {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ajph.aphapublications.org/doi/full/10.2105/AJPH.2021.306375"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 3.5 times}}}\uc2  more likely than white peers to die because of pregnancy and childbirth , according to one study looking at data from 2016-2017, and 2.9 times more likely according to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/data/hestat/maternal-mortality/2020/maternal-mortality-rates-2020.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 CDC analysis}}}\uc2  in 2020. They are also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kff.org/womens-health-policy/state-indicator/abortions-by-race/?currentTimeframe=0&selectedDistributions=white--black--other--hispanic--total&sortModel=%7B%22colId%22:%22Location%22,%22sort%22:%22asc%22%7D"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more likely}}}\uc2  to need \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBecause Black, Indigenous, and Latinx communities are going to be disproportionately impacted by lack of access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, it\u8217\'20\'19s going to exacerbate the maternal mortality racial gap that we\u8217\'20\'19ve already seen in the United States,\u8221\'20\'1D Hardeman said.\par \par \uc2 Pregnant people of color have long been marginalized and neglected in the medical system, frequently experiencing racism and discrimination at all points of care.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s translating into not getting the care they need, which can be a matter of life and death,\u8221\'20\'1D Hardeman said. And racism also takes an immense {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1467758/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 physical toll}}}\uc2 , so \u8220\'20\'1Cby the time that person becomes pregnant, they are at less optimal health than their white counterparts who haven\u8217\'20\'19t experienced racism across the life course\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The cumulative and chronic effects of living in America as a person of color increases stress, which can also affect reproductive health. \u8220\'20\'1CWe know that the stress pathway is what leads to infant mortality, preterm birth, and other outcomes,\u8221\'20\'1D Hardeman said.\par \par \uc2 We have to be thinking about the \uc2 Scotus\uc2  decision and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans generally as a racist policy\par \par \uc2 Hardeman\par \par \uc2 Even living in a community or neighborhood with disproportionate levels of police surveillance and police contact, for instance, is associated with a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jamanetworkopen/fullarticle/2786940"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 greater risk}}}\uc2  of preterm birth \u8211\'20\'13 which can be dangerous for both the birthing person and the infant.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have to be thinking about the \uc2 Scotus\uc2  decision and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans generally as a racist policy, because the burden will fall the hardest on Black pregnant people, it\u8217\'20\'19s going to fall hard on Indigenous people and other people of color, people living in rural areas as well and people of lower socioeconomic status,\u8221\'20\'1D Hardeman said.\par \par \uc2 The supreme court decision on Friday and bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  instituted at the state level \u8220\'20\'1Cdisproportionately harm people of color and reinforce a system of inequity and, frankly, of white supremacy\u8221\'20\'1D, Hardeman said.\par \par \uc2 The states that have now banned or restricted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  also have some of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sph.tulane.edu/news/study-finds-higher-maternal-mortality-rates-states-more-abortion-restrictions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 highest}}}\uc2  mortality rates around pregnancy and childbirth, as well as the highest {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7312072/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 child mortality rates}}}\uc2 . Mississippi, for instance, where the supreme case that overturned Roe originated, has one of the highest maternal mortality rates \u8211\'20\'13 almost {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://rewirenewsgroup.com/article/2021/12/01/heres-what-mississippi-wont-tell-the-supreme-court-about-womens-well-being-in-the-state/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 twice as high}}}\uc2  as the rest of the country \u8211\'20\'13 and the highest infant mortality rate in the country.\par \par \uc2 Some people seek abortions because they are at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ansirh.org/research/ongoing/turnaway-study"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 high risk of dying}}}\uc2  from a pregnancy \u8211\'20\'13 because of a health condition, an unsafe home environment, harassment because of their identity, or another reason.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you think about why people get abortions, it\u8217\'20\'19s often because it\u8217\'20\'19s not safe for them to stay pregnant,\u8221\'20\'1D Stevenson said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe people who are currently having abortions are very likely to actually have higher rates of pregnancy-related deaths and maternal mortality than the people who are currently giving birth.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is \u8220\'20\'1Cmuch, much, much safer than staying pregnant\u8221\'20\'1D, Stevenson said. Researchers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22270271/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 estimate}}}\uc2  that childbirth is 14 times more deadly than having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But childbirth is just one risk of pregnancy. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s way, way more than 14 times more deadly to stay pregnant \u8211\'20\'13 that\u8217\'20\'19s a massive underestimate,\u8221\'20\'1D Stevenson said.\par \par \uc2 While roughly half of the country is poised to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , other states and cities have worked to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/10/delaware-access-abortion-care"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 expand access}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 including to out-of-state patients.But {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/29/washington-dc-abortion-sanctuary-city-congress"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 significant limitations}}}\uc2  on getting to those sanctuaries remain.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe question is, who is going to be able to access it?\u8221\'20\'1D Hardeman asked. Many people of color who face systemic barriers to healthcare may not have the tools, resources, money, time off work and childcare to travel to a sanctuary state or city to receive care, she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have to be thinking about the fact that because we live in a society where access to resources is based on racism and race, there are people who are not going to be able to access the services that are available.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 For many reproductive rights researchers, the court\u8217\'20\'19s decision came as no surprise. \u8220\'20\'1CThis has been coming for a long time,\u8221\'20\'1D McLemore said. \u8220\'20\'1CI get very grumpy when people just want me to regurgitate statistics about how Black people are going to be dying \u8211\'20\'13 we know that. What are we doing?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 First, she said, \u8220\'20\'1CCongress could act right now and render \uc2 Scotus\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s decision irrelevant\u8221\'20\'1D by enshrining reproductive rights into {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/mar/01/republicans-joe-manchin-us-senate-block-bill-abortion-rights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 national law}}}\uc2 . If this Congress doesn\u8217\'20\'19t, she said, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/fact-tank/2022/06/13/about-six-in-ten-americans-say-abortion-should-be-legal-in-all-or-most-cases-2/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 six in 10 Americans}}}\uc2  who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights should vote for a new Congress that will.\par \par \uc2 Members of the Black Maternal Health Caucus in Congress have been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/apr/19/black-mothers-birth-maternal-mortality"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 advocating}}}\uc2  for laws that would protect the well-being of birthing people, including the Momnibus Act of 2021.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers could also expand the social safety net, including paid family leave and health insurance for lower-income and postpartum patients, for the swelling number of people giving birth.\par \par \uc2 All of these strategies wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t just ensure that reproductive health continues to be offered to those who need it \u8211\'20\'13 they will also keep patients from dying, McLemore said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe need an all-hands-on-deck approach here \u8211\'20\'13 with brilliance, not fear.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220627ei6r001k0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
