\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 California  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  clinics braced for out-of-state surge as bans kick in\b0\par\par\uc2 Dani Anguiano in Los Angeles \par \uc2 972 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par 14:04 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The state has pledged to be a \u8216\'20\'18sanctuary\u8217\'20\'19, with people seeking reproductive care potentially rising from 46,000 to 1.4m\par \par \uc2 How many people to expect?\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s the question anguished reproductive care providers in California are grappling with after the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  voted to overturn the federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 With {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/03/what-states-abortion-clinics-access-trigger-laws"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than half of US states}}}\uc2  set to outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , California has vowed to become a \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/dec/08/california-sanctuary-abortion-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sanctuary}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D for people seeking reproductive care. The state has putforward a package of legislation to expand access for residents and support people seeking abortions from out of state. The state\u8217\'20\'19s governor on Friday signed a bill protecting patients and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in California from civil liability.\par \par \uc2 Researchers estimate that California could see a nearly 3,000% increase in the number of people whose nearest legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider is in the state. California\u8217\'20\'19s annual patient load could rise from from 46,000 people to 1.4 million, according to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://states.guttmacher.org/#california"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Guttmacher Institute report}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Providers in the state have been preparing for the moment for months.\par \par \uc2 Jessica Pinckney, the executive director of Access Reproductive Equality, said calls to the California-based \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund\u8217\'20\'19s healthline, through which people can get logistical and financial support to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , have doubled. The group is also seeing an influx of callers from Texas, which adopted strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions last year, and has offered support to nearly 30 people, 10 times as many as in previous years.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have known for quite some time that we would have to rise to this occasion,\u8221\'20\'1D Pinckney said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are trying to fill a need where the federal government and state governments have failed their people.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Clinics have already seen rising numbers of patients from states with restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, such as Texas, and they are preparing for more. Planned Parenthood Affiliates of California says its clinics have seen an increase in patients from Texas and Arizona and is treating roughly 1,500 out-of-state patients each month, a large proportion of whom are seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think it\u8217\'20\'19s going to be hard to tell what the surge is going to look like. Folks will be looking to California to get the care that they need,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lisa Matsubara, the general counsel and vice-president of policy of Planned Parenthood Affiliates of California. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re gonna do everything we can here \u8230\'20\'26 but that does not replace being able to get care in their home state. Some people are just not going to be able to travel and that\u8217\'20\'19s the devastating part of this decision today.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 FPA Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health, which has 25 offices across California, is also reporting an uptick in out-of-state patients. \u8220\'20\'1CWe have the capacity to see them. So far it is not outstripping supply. We\u8217\'20\'19ve been able to manage the demand,\u8221\'20\'1D said a spokesperson.\par \par \uc2 Still, FPA Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health is expanding capacity. The clinics plan to treat more California patients via medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by mail, in order to preserve further access to surgical abortions for those coming from out of state.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have streamlined access in our 11 surgical clinics to accommodate patients who choose to fly into California for care and fly home immediately,\u8221\'20\'1D the practice said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Women\u8217\'20\'19s Options Center, the hospital-based clinic at the \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 , is preparing to expand its \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, but doing so will require hiring more staff, which cannot happen until it is clear how many patients will need care. The pandemic has strained the larger healthcare system and cause increased burnout among healthcare workers that has led to early retirement, said Dr Eleanor Drey, the center\u8217\'20\'19s director.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI can\u8217\'20\'19t say we\u8217\'20\'19re able to turn on a dime and suddenly care for women around the country,\u8221\'20\'1D Drey said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s hard to imagine being able to adequately expand services if half the country will face bans and highly restrictive laws. We know there are people who cannot travel \u8230\'20\'26 Our patients don\u8217\'20\'19t have resources. That\u8217\'20\'19s why many of them are seeking to end their pregnancies.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws across the US will have a significant impact on patients, disproportionately on people of color, who \u8220\'20\'1Calready face the harsh impact of racism in healthcare and other aspects of their lives\u8221\'20\'1D, Drey said.\par \par \uc2 Choix, a telehealth clinic that provides medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, reported a 600% increase in visitors to its website on Friday, the day the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision was announced. The clinic offers services in California, Illinois and Colorado, and aims to expand to every state where it can legally provide care by next year.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are able to scale our operations fairly quickly in response to the need but we also want to ensure we do so in a way that is sustainable,\u8221\'20\'1D said Cindy Adam, a nurse practitioner and the Choix CEO. \u8220\'20\'1CCertainly since the leak and even more so today we are motivated to expand our work as quickly as possible \u8230\'20\'26 I think navigating the new legal landscape as states enact laws that will be overreaching, to say the least, will be the greatest challenge.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care providers said they felt galvanized and were committed to expanding services in order to better support patients in a post-Roe America, but remain grief-stricken for the patients who won\u8217\'20\'19t be able to obtain treatment.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople know their lives. They know their circumstances. They know their resources \u8211\'20\'13 and who are politicians to interfere with their families?\u8221\'20\'1D Drey said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s cruel and inhumane. If those justices were in the room with the people I see every day I can\u8217\'20\'19t imagine how they would make such a heartless decision.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220627ei6r001jz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
