\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 We Americans are dancing on the Titanic. Our iceberg is not far away\b0\par\par\uc2 Francine Prose \par \uc2 965 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par 10:16 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The greatest shock of all would be to wake up and find that while we were driving the kids to soccer practice and enjoying cocktails, autocracy took hold\par \par \uc2 By now the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s overturning of Roe v Wade hardly comes as a surprise. We\u8217\'20\'19ve known this was imminent since the leak, a month or so ago, of Justice Alito\u8217\'20\'19s memo. And yet it still delivers a profound shock \u8211\'20\'13 in fact, a series of shocks. Stunned, we ask, how could this happen? as if we hadn\u8217\'20\'19t known, for weeks, that it was a more or less done deal.\par \par \uc2 What\u8217\'20\'19s shocking is the actualization of the scary Handmaid\u8217\'20\'19s Tale scenario: our growing suspicion that Margaret Atwood\u8217\'20\'19s fictional dystopia \u8211\'20\'13 a society in which women are forced to bear children and brutally punished for disobedience \u8211\'20\'13 is nearer to becoming a reality than we might have imagined. What\u8217\'20\'19s shocking is this proof of the court\u8217\'20\'19s desire and ability to control and punish women, to deprive us of our constitutional rights. What\u8217\'20\'19s shocking is the justices\u8217\'20\'19 reckless disregard for the additional suffering that this ruling will cause poor women, women of color and those living in rural areas. What\u8217\'20\'19s shocking is the memory of three of the current justices swearing, under oath, to preserve the precedent established by Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 What\u8217\'20\'19s shocking is the realization that we are living in a country that now boasts some of the world\u8217\'20\'19s most misogynist and repressive laws. What\u8217\'20\'19s shocking is the knowledge that the institution I grew up seeing as committed to the most precious guarantees of the constitution and to the highest and most sensibly bipartisan ideals of justice is now in the hands of a powerful faction of extremists.\par \par \uc2 But what shocks me most is the fact that, according to surveys that keep surfacing and being reported, a substantial majority of Americans support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and oppose the outright ban. According to the latest Gallup poll, 85% of the population believes that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal under some circumstances. What\u8217\'20\'19s noteworthy is not that high number so much as the discrepancy between that figure and the substance of supreme court ruling. What\u8217\'20\'19s shocking is yet another fact that we have known or suspected for some time: that we are living under minority rule, that, in some of the most essential ways, the wishes of the majority no longer determine government policy, and that it has become a kind of joke to suggest that our government, at the highest level, is responding to \u8220\'20\'1Cthe will of the people\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile these shocks are intensified and amplified by how little we seem willing or able to do about the slow-motion stealth with which the seeds of autocracy are being planted. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re living under minority rule,\u8221\'20\'1D we say, and then go on to plan the kids\u8217\'20\'19 birthday parties, to try to find a job and pay the bills, to complain at the gas pump, see our friends, celebrate the good weather and the new freedom occasioned by the latest downturn in the pandemic. Social media is abuzz with valuable \u8211\'20\'13 and necessary \u8211\'20\'13 suggestions for circumventing the new measures: how to obtain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills from abroad, how to help women travel to states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still permitted. But I have yet to see a truly viable and broad-based plan for influencing the legislators of the so-called \u8220\'20\'1Ctrigger states\u8221\'20\'1D that have outlawed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the immediate wake of the supreme court ruling.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s never been more important to insist on our rights \u8211\'20\'13 not only as women, not only as Americans, but as human beings\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s hard not to notice that our passivity is being encouraged by the mainstream media\u8217\'20\'19s commitment to \u8220\'20\'1Cfair and balanced\u8221\'20\'1D reporting. In the coverage I watched on the night of the ruling \u8211\'20\'13 not only on the primetime channels but on PBS \u8211\'20\'13 equal time was given to the exultation of the \u8220\'20\'1Cpro-life\u8221\'20\'1D (that regrettable term suggesting that its opponents are anti-life ) faction and to the anger and disappointment of women who wish only to maintain control over our own bodies. How can it not add to our sense that the country is equally divided, deeply and hopeless factionalized, and therefore that nothing can be done? In fact the two sides are not equal, but one side is grievously underrepresented in the places where it matters most.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s never been more important to insist on our rights \u8211\'20\'13 not only as women, not only as Americans, but as human beings. We need to talk to our friends, make plans, apply unceasing pressure on our state and local governments, hold every political candidate accountable. We may need to forget our pressing worries over inflation and gasoline prices just long enough to take to the streets, with unceasing frequency and in greater numbers, in order to make our beliefs and our voices heard.\par \par \uc2 Because the greatest shock of all would be to wake up one morning and find that while we were driving the kids to soccer practice and enjoying that welcome after-work cocktail, more and more of our rights had been stripped away, as has happened in so many countries in which democracy vanished, overnight and in darkness \u8211\'20\'13when, as it were, no one was looking. The overturning of Roe v Wade should shock us even more than it already does \u8211\'20\'13 shock us into looking beyond the dance floor of the Titanic and spotting that iceberg, looming in our path, not so very far away.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Francine Prose is the author, most recently, of The Vixen. She was also the president of Pen America\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220627ei6r001k4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
