\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  rights were hard-won in Australia \u8211\'20\'13 the Roe v Wade ruling shows how easily they could be taken away\b0\par\par\uc2 Van Badham \par \uc2 960 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par 07:40 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The challenge now \u8211\'20\'13 for women everywhere \u8211\'20\'13 is to fight through our fear, and make our courage count\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  has overturned Roe v Wade and, with it, nearly 50 years of women\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in America.\par \par \uc2 The activism of the six ultraconservative justices \u8211\'20\'13 three of whom were appointed by former US president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 shreds precedent in more ways than one. It is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://mobile.twitter.com/tribelaw/status/1541016133448368129"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the first time in their history}}}\uc2  that Americans have woken up in the \u8220\'20\'1Cland of the free\u8221\'20\'1D with fewer rights than they had the night before.\par \par \uc2 America is a society with political structures and dynamics that are not replicated elsewhere, yet shock waves from their supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s radicalism are being felt around the world. Within the other liberal democratic states of the west, people aren\u8217\'20\'19t waking to a loss of rights \u8230\'20\'26 but even in our faraway corner of the Pacific, we are reverberating with fresh and terrible fear.\par \par \uc2 That fear is not sparing our most powerful. \uc2 Jacinda Ardern\uc2 , the prime minister of New Zealand, responded to the news with a statement released to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.facebook.com/search/top?q=jacinda%20ardern"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  Facebook}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CPeople are absolutely entitled to have deeply-held convictions on this issue,\u8221\'20\'1D she said, \u8220\'20\'1CBut those beliefs should never rob another from making their own decisions. To see that principle now lost in the United States feels like a loss for women everywhere.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 New Zealand completed full {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/article/us-newzealand-abortion-idUSKBN2153YN"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 decriminalisation}}}\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2020. In Australia, where individual states legislate the issue, a decade of decriminalising reform has even been enhanced by laws creating \u8220\'20\'1Csafe access zones\u8221\'20\'1D around reproductive healthcare providers. These not only protect the legal, medical, and moral right of women to access reproductive healthcare, but their physical right to walk into clinics without abuse and bullying.\par \par \uc2 This has happened in both countries not because of activist lawmakers, but through relentless grassroots campaigning to represent overwhelming public demand.\par \par \uc2 Decriminalisation was supported by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2019/03/revealed-large-majority-of-kiwis-want-abortion-law-change-newshub-reid-research-poll.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 69.9% of New Zealanders}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 a higher proportion than even the final parliamentary vote. In Australia, support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights is even higher, with public backing at a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ipsos.com/en-au/majority-australians-report-unwavering-support-abortion-access"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 staggeringly bipartisan 76%}}}\uc2 . The new prime minister, Anthony Albanese, was speaking for an Australian majority when he described what has happened in the US as \u8220\'20\'1Ca setback for women and their right to control their own bodies and their lives\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 And yet for all this popular support, Katy Gallagher, Albanese\u8217\'20\'19s minister for women, retained the same fearful tone as Ardern when she described the US events as \u8220\'20\'1Cdevastating\u8221\'20\'1D. She went as far as to instruct Australians \u8220\'20\'1Cto remain vigilant because hard fought-for wins before our parliament can be taken away easily\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Why the fear? Because what politicians understand better than anyone is the power of institutional capture. In America, also, most people support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights; 60%, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2022/06/25/roe-dobbs-scotus-opinon-abortion-restrictions-rights-polarization/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to polls this week}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 including majorities in states with the most punitive anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws. The exercise of power, even in a democracy, isn\u8217\'20\'19t through a simple force of numbers \u8211\'20\'13 it\u8217\'20\'19s about the channelling of the right force of numbers in the right place.\par \par \uc2 No one who believes in the fundamental human rights of women in our country or others have any laurels to rest on now\par \par \uc2 The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cause in the US worked out long ago they didn\u8217\'20\'19t need a popular majority to deliver their agenda. They just needed majorities in the places where the decision would be made.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s this revelation that terrifies \u8211\'20\'13 because the long, slow pace of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  reform in New Zealand and Australia has had to navigate the disproportional presence of anti-abortionists in institutions of real power, causing obstruction and delay and deprioritisation of reform even when the weight of public opinion was with the other side. Even in progressive Australia, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  still remains in Western Australia\u8217\'20\'19s criminal code.\par \par \uc2 One hopes the American tragedy will spur the Western Australian government to realise the importance of immediate, unambiguous reform \u8211\'20\'13 as an act of solidarity, as well as institutional example. Leftwing intellectuals once used to advise comrades to make the \u8220\'20\'1Clong march through the institutions\u8221\'20\'1D to bring about social change, to shape the norms and forms that imprint the values of society. Turns out, it was the right who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2018/oct/01/christine-blasey-ford-spoke-but-kavanaugh-just-gets-closer-so-what-now-for-women?CMP=gu_com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 took their advice}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Could it happen here? America shows it can happen anywhere, when vigilance is lost, when numbers are channelled not towards power but towards shaming the powerful who feel no shame. No one who believes in the fundamental human rights of women in our country or others have any laurels to rest on now. Radical anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nuff-nuff MP Bernie Finn may have finally been ejected from the Victorian Liberal party for unhinged {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/may/24/victorian-mp-booted-from-parliamentary-liberal-party-after-praying-for-abortion-to-be-banned"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 social media posts}}}\uc2 , but conservative LNP senator \uc2 Matt Canavan\uc2  remains. Canavan was posting anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  tweets celebrating when the supreme court ruling was made, and he\u8217\'20\'19s not {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.news.com.au/lifestyle/real-life/news-life/queensland-abortion-laws-passed-list-of-mps-shows-it-was-mostly-men-who-voted-to-keep-abortion-illegal/news-story/b5460470e064ec4168995c9026243afe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 alone in his opinions}}}\uc2  in his party.\par \par \uc2 One of the saddest realisations of this terrible moment is that Martin Luther King\u8217\'20\'19s promise that \u8220\'20\'1Cthe arc of the universe is long, but it bends towards justice\u8221\'20\'1D has been denied. It is instead \uc2 Julia Gillard\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2013/jun/11/julia-gillard-abortion-coalition-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2013 speech warning}}}\uc2  of \u8220\'20\'1Can Australia where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  again becomes the political plaything of men who think they know better\u8221\'20\'1D that sticks in the mind today.\par \par \uc2 It is also Gillard who advised that there\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Ca moment in every fight where you\u8217\'20\'19ve got to choose whether you are daunted by it or energised\u8221\'20\'1D. The end of Roe in the US has shattered the illusion that the fight was ever over. The challenge now \u8211\'20\'13 for women everywhere \u8211\'20\'13 is to fight through our fear, and make our courage count.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220627ei6r0012x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
