\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Protests continue across US to voice anger over supreme court ruling\b0\par\par\uc2 Victoria Bekiempis and Edward Helmore in New York \par \uc2 952 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par 16:49 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 In New York, thousands gathered downtown to celebrate Pride and give voice to anger after decision that overturned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights\par \par \uc2 Protests over a \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  decision that overturned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights continued across the country this weekend. In New York, thousands marched to voice their anger at the ruling that came at the end of a dizzying week around not just reproductive rights but also gun carry laws and the US Capitol attack.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNot your uterus, not your choice,\u8221\'20\'1D many shouted as the demonstrations progressed in Washington DC., New York City, Chicago, Los Angeles, Seattle, Atlanta and Austin.\par \par \uc2 In Providence, Rhode Island, tempers flared so much that an off-duty police officer was accused of punching a woman at an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protest. Jennifer Rourke, a state senate candidate, told the Providence Journal she was punched in the face by Jeann Lugo, who had been running for the GOP nomination for a Rhode Island state senate seat but dropped out the race.\par \par \uc2 Lugo said he was \u8220\'20\'1Cnot going to deny\u8221\'20\'1D the punching allegation but added that \u8220\'20\'1Ceverything happened very fast\u8221\'20\'1D. For the most part, protests across the US have been peaceful.\par \par \uc2 In New York, they fell across Pride weekend honoring the achievements of the LGBTQ community, with thousands gathering downtown to simultaneously celebrate and give voice to anger. Marchers said in some cases they were both shell-shocked by the supreme court decision and happy to be celebrating, gender identities and sexual orientations that some like the court\u8217\'20\'19s conservative justices might find contrary.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a similar feeling to when Trump got elected,\u8221\'20\'1D said film editor Oriana Soddu. Soddu said she knew the stripping of nationwide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights was coming after the 2 May leak of a draft ruling saying so, but \u8220\'20\'1Cfor it to actually happen is still a shock\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The anger, Soddu said, was toward the political system itself. \u8220\'20\'1CThe Republicans clearly have a very strong agenda and we\u8217\'20\'19ve let this happen,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CMy fear is they\u8217\'20\'19re going to go after gay marriage\u8221\'20\'1D next.\par \par \uc2 The crowds that gathered in New York\u8217\'20\'19s Washington Square on Saturday were, for the most part, there to celebrate the 30th anniversary of the New York City Dyke March. Organizers billed the march as a \u8220\'20\'1Ccelebration of our beautiful and diverse dyke lives\u8221\'20\'1D that also doubled as a protest of discrimination, harassment, and violence against lesbians, but it also energized a pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights demonstration.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s been different to realize that in the eyes of the constitution and the court you\u8217\'20\'19re not really a person and you don\u8217\'20\'19t have autonomy over yourself,\u8221\'20\'1D said longtime American activist and socialist organizer Leslie Cagan. \u8220\'20\'1CA lot of good things have come for virtually every community that has struggled for a modicum of rights, and now it\u8217\'20\'19s all hanging by a thread.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI hope that those people and those communities are beginning to get it that if we don\u8217\'20\'19t work together and get beyond the rhetoric of solidarity in which everybody does their own thing, none of our people are going to win,\u8221\'20\'1D Cagan added. \u8220\'20\'1CWe haven\u8217\'20\'19t been collectively tuned in to how big and dangerous the power against us is.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Cindy Greenberg, also marching Saturday, said she thought those forces were really not committed to the notion of a democracy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt feels like when Trump was elected,\u8221\'20\'1D Greenberg said. \u8220\'20\'1CThis whole period of time has shown that they\u8217\'20\'19re not. This week has been extraordinary \u8211\'20\'13 it shown us that they\u8217\'20\'19re willing to sell all of us down the river.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Lisa Ann Markuson said she came with her typewriter to write poems for protesters gathered in the park in part because having a normal Pride party day felt strange. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not, \u8216\'20\'18Yay, we\u8217\'20\'19re cool, we\u8217\'20\'19re queer!\u8217\'20\'19 It felt farcical to come out here and party like it\u8217\'20\'19s 2008 because it isn\u8217\'20\'19t. People want to set something on fire, but there\u8217\'20\'19s also a sense of apathy and alienation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAmerica is supposed to be about freedom but [what] is this? Corporations have freedom and people are supposed to think they have freedom because they have a lot of consumer choices.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Mel Melendes said that being proud and protesting were one and the same. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m proud to be here because the louder we express ourselves the more you shine light on what\u8217\'20\'19s wrong.\u8221\'20\'1D Added Elisa Buttafuoco: \u8220\'20\'1CIf we weren\u8217\'20\'19t fighting we wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be ourselves, we wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be the queer community. Queer rights is \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights is trans rights. It\u8217\'20\'19s all interwoven.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Some on the march wondered if the protest would go the same way if the decision to lift \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections primarily affected the queer community.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs a minority community it feels like we\u8217\'20\'19re protesting for everything,\u8221\'20\'1D said Afrah Boateng. \u8220\'20\'1CIt feels like there is something to protest every year around Pride. Today it\u8217\'20\'19s for straight families and straight women. But I guess Pride started as protest, so it\u8217\'20\'19s built in.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 According to a \uc2 CBS\uc2  poll published Sunday, most disapprove of overturning the nationwide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights established by the landmark Roe v Wade case, including two-thirds of women. By more than a 20-point margin, Americans call it a step backward for the US.\par \par \uc2 Younger people are especially likely to disapprove; most moderates disapprove along with nine in 10 liberals; two-thirds of Hispanic Americans disapprove, three-fourths of Black Americans and just over half of White Americans disapprove.\par \par \uc2 The three-fourths of conservatives who do support the ruling said they felt hopeful and happy.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220626ei6q001jp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
