\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Patients in Texas clinics were waiting to get their abortions. Then Roe fell\b0\par\par\uc2 Chabeli Carrazana for the 19th \par \uc2 914 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par 15:37 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Providers reported \u8216\'20\'18complete despair\u8217\'20\'19 as patients with appointments only minutes away were turned away\par \par \uc2 Originally published by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://19thnews.org/2022/06/patients-abortion-clinics-texas-turned-away-roe-overturned/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The 19th}}}\par \par \uc2 Patients were in the lobby, waiting, the moment it became a post-Roe America.\par \par \uc2 The staff at Alamo Women\u8217\'20\'19s Reproductive Services Clinic in San Antonio had just received a call from their attorney: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedures in Texas would have to stop immediately. The dozen or so patients in the lobby on Friday morning would have to be turned away. The clinic staff would have to be the ones to tell them.\par \par \uc2 Andrea Gallegos, the clinic\u8217\'20\'19s administrator, and the rest of the staff walked out and addressed the room: \u8220\'20\'1CThe supreme court made this decision today and, unfortunately, your geographical location affects your bodily autonomy,\u8221\'20\'1D she said they told waiting patients.\par \par \uc2 Gallegos watched each word land like a blow. People cried. They screamed. They begged for help, she said. It was \u8220\'20\'1Ccomplete despair\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Hours later, the clinic had emptied of all but those who had received their abortions hours or minutes before Roe v Wade, the 50-year-old court case that enshrined \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a right, was overturned by the supreme court on Friday, leaving the question of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access up to individual states. Only those with follow-up appointments could be seen.\par \par \uc2 Gallegos and her staff called about 20 people who were scheduled to come in later that day. Some were caught off guard, Gallegos said. \u8220\'20\'1CWhy today? Why the day of their appointment did this happen?\u8221\'20\'1D patients asked her.\par \par \uc2 Those turned away were patients who were now outside an already small window: in September, Texas banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  past six weeks of pregnancy. That law was the first in a series of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions passed in states across the country in the last year that served as a preview of life after Roe.\par \par \uc2 Texas also has a \u8220\'20\'1Ctrigger\u8221\'20\'1D law that would ban abortions from the moment of conception and would go into effect as soon as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2022/06/24/texas-clinics-abortions-whole-womans-health/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 about two months from now}}}\uc2 . But in the chaos of Friday\u8217\'20\'19s ruling, clinics across the state chose to cease all \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services in case the ban would come into effect even sooner. The Texas attorney general, \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , said on Friday that, under a separate pre-Roe ban in the state, \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers could be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2022/06/24/texas-clinics-abortions-whole-womans-health/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 criminally liable}}}\uc2  for providing abortions starting today\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Across Texas and other states where trigger bans are in place and where, hour-by-hour, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is being completely outlawed, the same scene was playing out simultaneously: waiting rooms were emptied. Waitlists were pulled up. Phone calls were made to people who had their abortions scheduled.\par \par \uc2 At Whole Woman\u8217\'20\'19s Health clinics across Texas, staff received notice in a conference call on Friday morning. Marva Sadler, the senior director for clinical services for Whole Woman\u8217\'20\'19s, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider with locations in five states, said clinic managers brought patients in from the lobby one by one to deliver the news.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEach patient was given the opportunity to have their reactions and their emotions privately,\u8221\'20\'1D Sadler said, conversations clinic staff had become well versed in having over the 10 months {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://19thnews.org/2021/09/abortion-texas-whole-womans-clinic/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 since Texas\u8217\'20\'19s ban had passed}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It was difficult to be an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider in the only state at the time to ban the procedure so early in gestation, Sadler said.\par \par \uc2 Now that that reality is rippling through America, \u8220\'20\'1Cyou realize it\u8217\'20\'19s so many more people and so many more families that are going to be devastated and affected,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s really hard to wrap your mind around.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 When the news hit as she was driving, she pulled over to the side of the road and wept before she addressed her staff. Then she got to work calling her clinics in other states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still legal. Could they take patients? Could they help coordinate travel?\par \par \uc2 Gallegos is also working to refer patients out to clinics in nearby states \u8211\'20\'13 Colorado, Kansas and New Mexico \u8211\'20\'13 where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still protected. But many of their patients are low-income. They were choosing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in part because they feared being unable to afford a child. They don\u8217\'20\'19t have the funds to travel.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese are patients that oftentimes are already mothers, they are already taking care of children, some are living paycheck-to-paycheck,\u8221\'20\'1D Gallegos said. \u8220\'20\'1CThese are the folks that are going to be forced into having another child if they can\u8217\'20\'19t make it out of state, and those effects, all the way around, are just devastating. Several say, \u8216\'20\'18How am I supposed to do this? I took off work to be here today and now you want me to travel?\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Clinic staff is gripped by uncertainty. Many have been providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services for decades. A physician at the San Antonio clinic was providing abortions pre-Roe, Gallegos said \u8211\'20\'13 and now he must consider how he will do that \u8220\'20\'1Cpost-Roe\u8221\'20\'1D, she notes. Sadler has been working to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access for about two decades.\par \par \uc2 She said the fall of Roe only strengthens her determination, and that of her colleagues, to continue providing reproductive care, in whatever form, as long as they\u8217\'20\'19re able.\par \par \uc2 Her staff is standing by, she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf I told them we could see patients at midnight tonight,\u8221\'20\'1D Sadler said, \u8220\'20\'1CI have no doubt that every last one of them would show up without question.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220625ei6p001up\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
