\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Republican-run US states move to immediately ban  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  after court overturns Roe v Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 Victoria Bekiempis and agencies \par \uc2 1,065 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par 14:56 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Moves were swift in many of the thirteen US states which have previously passed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -banning \u8216\'20\'18trigger laws\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 On the day that the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  ruled to overturn Roe v Wade, stripping constitutional protections for reproductive health, a slew of US states immediately moved to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  across Republican-run swaths of America.\par \par \uc2 The moves were swift in many of the 13 US states which have previously passed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -banning \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/24/scotus-roe-wade-decision-what-happens-next"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 trigger laws}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D designed to come into effect when the federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was removed.\par \par \uc2 These states include Arkansas, Idaho, Kentucky, Louisiana, North Dakota, Oklahoma, South Dakota, Tennessee, Texas, Utah and Wyoming, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2022/06/24/abortion-state-laws-criminalization-roe/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Washington Post.}}}\uc2  Those statutes either banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  once this decision came down, required some sort of action from a state official certifying the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling, or stipulated that the ban would go into effect days or weeks afterward.\par \par \uc2 Missouri\u8217\'20\'19s legislation immediately went into effect. Six minutes after the supreme court decision came down, Missouri\u8217\'20\'19s attorney general, \uc2 Eric Schmitt\uc2 , certified this decision, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AGEricSchmitt/status/1540338042413944832?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw%7Ctwcamp%5Etweetembed%7Ctwterm%5E1540338042413944832%7Ctwgr%5E%7Ctwcon%5Es1_&ref_url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.kcur.org%2Fnews%2F2022-06-24%2Fmissouri-abortion-laws-roe-v-wade-birth-control-iud-ectopic-pregnancy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tweeted}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CFollowing the \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2  ruling overturning Roe v. Wade, Missouri has just become the first in the country to effectively end \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with our AG opinion signed moments ago. This is a monumental day for the sanctity of life.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Missouri law, which provides no exception for rape or incest, classifies the act of inducing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a class B felony, meaning it could result in a five- to 15-year prison sentence. It applies to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, though it\u8217\'20\'19s unclear whether someone could be prosecuted for using \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -inducing medications, per the NPR station.\par \par \uc2 In South Dakota, as of today, all abortions are against the law \u8220\'20\'1Cunless there is appropriate and reasonable medical judgment that performance of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is necessary to preserve the life of the pregnant female\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.sd.gov/newsitem.aspx?id=30323"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced South Dakota\u8217\'20\'19s governor}}}\uc2 , \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2 , with top state lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 They added that they were planning a special legislative session \u8220\'20\'1Cto save lives and help mothers impacted by the decision\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Kentucky\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  prohibition \u8211\'20\'13 which permits the procedure solely to save a pregnant person\u8217\'20\'19s life or prevent debilitating injury \u8211\'20\'13 has also gone into effect, the Louisville Courier-Journal {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.sd.gov/newsitem.aspx?id=30323"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2 . The state\u8217\'20\'19s trigger law similarly stated that the state\u8217\'20\'19s anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation \u8220\'20\'1Cshall become effective immediately\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The American Civil Liberties Union of Kentucky reportedly said that the state\u8217\'20\'19s only full-time \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic has stopped providing these procedures \u8220\'20\'1Cas a precaution\u8221\'20\'1D. People seeking abortions will have few options within close proximity, as Illinois and Virginia are the only two of the seven states neighboring Kentucky that have mostly unrestricted reproductive health services.\par \par \uc2 Louisiana\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  went into effect after Roe was overturned. It bars most abortions and makes providing them illegal. The law does not provide any exceptions for rape or incest, no matter the age of the pregnant person, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nola.com/news/healthcare_hospitals/article_ba416134-e8e7-11ec-b6e2-8f8cd0d27442.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Nola.com}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The law allows exceptions when there is an extreme risk of death or disability. At the same time, the statute requires that a doctor makes \u8220\'20\'1Creasonable\u8221\'20\'1D efforts to save the fetus and mother, the site said. Legislators in the state have also ruled to criminalize mail or internet-based purchase of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills.\par \par \uc2 Arkansas\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban will go into effect as soon as the state\u8217\'20\'19s attorney certifies that Roe has been overturned. Almost all abortions are prohibited, except when a pregnant person faces a life-threatening medical emergency, the Arkansas Democrat Gazette {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.arkansasonline.com/news/2022/jun/24/what-overturning-roe-v-wade-means-for-arkansas/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mississippi\u8217\'20\'19s ban will go into effect \u8220\'20\'1Cwithin days\u8221\'20\'1D and also requires the state attorney general\u8217\'20\'19s sign-off. In North Dakota, the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  prohibition is enacted in 30 days after its legislative council greenlights the state attorney general\u8217\'20\'19s certification, the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Wyoming and Utah will see bans within 30 days once state authorities officially recognize the supreme court decision. Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s ban will go into effect in 30 days, as will Tennessee\u8217\'20\'19s, per the newspaper.\par \par \uc2 Some states, including Arkansas and Missouri, had \u8220\'20\'1Ctrigger law\u8221\'20\'1D bans on the books that went into effect as soon as Roe fell.\par \par \uc2 In Alabama, the state\u8217\'20\'19s three \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics stopped performing the procedure for fear providers would be prosecuted under a law dating to 1951.\par \par \uc2 At the Alabama Women\u8217\'20\'19s Center for Reproductive Alternatives in Huntsville, the staff had to tell women in the waiting room Friday morning that they could not perform any more abortions that day. Some had come from as far away as Texas for an appointment.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA lot of them just started breaking down crying. Can you imagine if you had driven 12 hours to receive this care in this state and you are not able to?\u8221\'20\'1D clinic owner Dalton Johnson said. Patients were given a list of out-of-state places still doing abortions.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers across Arizona likewise stopped doing procedures while they try to determine if a law dating to pre-statehood days before 1912 means doctors and nurses will face prison time now.\par \par \uc2 In Texas, providers wondered which law they had to follow: a 1925 ban, a 2021 law that limits abortions to the first six weeks of pregnancy, or a trigger law that bans the procedure outright, but wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t take effect for a month or more. The confusion led them to suspend abortions while they seek legal advice.\par \par \uc2 In Texas the Republican attorney general, \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , warned they could face immediate prosecution for performing abortions under the Prohibition-era ban, which carries a sentence of two to five years in prison.\par \par \uc2 It was the risk of prosecution under a 19th-century \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban punishable by prison that led the Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Center of West Virginia to stop performing the procedure.\par \par \uc2 The West Virginia governor Jim Justice, a Republican, said he will not hesitate to call the legislature into special session if the ban needs to be clarified.\par \par \uc2 In Ohio, a federal judge dissolved an injunction, allowing a 2019 state law to take effect banning most abortions at the first detectable fetal heartbeat.\par \par \uc2 Oklahoma, which has the most severe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in the US, also has a trigger law. Under this statute, providing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a felony, carrying a prison sentence of twoto five years, the Oklahoman reported.\par \par \uc2 The Associated Press contributed to this story\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220624ei6o002mh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
