\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Tech firms under pressure to safeguard user data as  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  prosecutions loom\b0\par\par\uc2 Kari Paul \par \uc2 649 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par 12:35 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Private information collected and retained by companies could be weaponized to prosecute \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  seekers and providers\par \par \uc2 After the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Roe v Wade}}}\uc2  on Friday, calls increased for tech companies to take a stand about the use of online data to incriminate individuals seeking or providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and civil rights advocates have warned that there are few federal regulations on what information is collected and retained by tech firms, making it easy for law enforcement officials to access incriminating data on location, internet searches and communication history.\par \par \uc2 Such data has already been used to prosecute people for miscarriages and pregnancy termination in states with strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2022/apr/04/us-law-enforcement-agencies-access-your-data-apple-meta"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 including one case}}}\uc2  in which a woman\u8217\'20\'19s online search for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills was brought against her in court. This kind of legal response may now become more widespread, said Imran Ahmed, chief executive officer of advocacy group the Center for Countering Digital Hate.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese companies need to think very long and hard about the ways in which their platforms will be weaponized to criminalize people looking to access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  healthcare, and they need to ensure that it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t happen,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Despite these growing calls, no major tech companies as of Friday afternoon had made public statements on how they will handle such data and respond to related law enforcement requests moving forward.\par \par \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and WhatsApp parent company \uc2 Meta\uc2  did not respond to request for comment. Ride share firms \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  did not respond to request for comment. \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Apple\uc2  did not respond to request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Smaller companies are also being targeted with questions over their data practices, as frantic calls {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/f1schbach/status/1540350994793598976?s=21&t=vgFsqGXfYpsa9jfkNA7kWw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to delete period tracking apps}}}\uc2  went viral following the supreme court decision. Some of those companies, unlike the tech giants, have taken public stands.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAt this fraught moment, we hear the anger and the anxiety coming from our US community,\u8221\'20\'1D period tracking app Clue said in a statement. \u8220\'20\'1CWe remain committed to protecting your reproductive health data.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Digital rights advocacy group the \uc2 Electronic Frontier Foundation (EFF) \uc2 has advised companies in the tech world to pre-emptively prepare for a future in which they are served with subpoenas and warrants seeking user data to prosecute \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  seekers and providers.\par \par \uc2 It recommends companies allow pseudonymous or anonymous access, stop behavioral tracking, and retain as little data as possible. It also advocated for end-to-end encryption by default and refrain from collecting any location information.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe best thing to do for tech companies is to not have this data when people come knocking through subpoenas or other legal actions,\u8221\'20\'1D said Shirin Mori, a data privacy expert at EFF.\par \par \uc2 Experts are also encouraging individuals seeking abortions to use heightened data security practices, including encrypted communications and disabling location tracking. The Roe decision has highlighted a longstanding privacy crisis affecting users of the most commonly used tech services, said Ahmed.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is very clarifying the extent to which the cost for these free services is held in the data that we willingly give them, which can now be weaponized against us,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 The threat of the fall of Roe, from when the decision was first leaked earlier this year, has intensified calls for federal data privacy legislation. Last week legislators including Senator Elizabeth Warren introduced a bill that would would bar \u8220\'20\'1Cdata brokers from selling or transferring location data and health data\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CData brokers profit from the location data of millions of people, posing serious risks to Americans everywhere by selling their most private information,\u8221\'20\'1D Warren said in a statement at the time. \u8220\'20\'1CWith this extremist supreme court poised to overturn Roe v Wade and states seeking to criminalize essential health care, it is more crucial than ever for Congress to protect consumers\u8217\'20\'19 sensitive data.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Johana Bhuiyan contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220625ei6p0018i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
