\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Trying to avoid burn-out: a Colorado  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  clinic braces for even more patients\b0\par\par\uc2 Carey Dunne \par \uc2 1,037 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par 11:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The overturning of Roe v Wade will bring an influx of patients from neighboring states where abortions are severely restricted\par \par \uc2 Dr Angela Marchin stares at a US map on the wall of the Denver Planned Parenthood where she works. The map is overwhelmed by pushpins, each representing the location from which a patient has traveled to the clinic for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CColorado is in the middle of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  desert. It feels like a stampede closing in all around us,\u8221\'20\'1D says Marchin, pointing out locations as far away as North Dakota and Florida.\par \par \uc2 The clinic had already been experiencing an influx of patients in recent months \u8211\'20\'13 many traveled from Texas, which banned abortions after six weeks last September, and Oklahoma, which banned nearly all abortions at fertilization in June. Now, in the wake of Roe\u8217\'20\'19s overturning, Dr Marchin and her colleagues \u8211\'20\'13 who provide abortions for between 300 and 400 patients a month \u8211\'20\'13 are bracing for an even greater onslaught.\par \par \uc2 In April, reacting to the leaked draft opinion, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/colorado-abortion-law-2022-governor-jared-polis-signs-bill/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Colorado state lawmakers enacted a statutory protection for \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  as a fundamental right. Its \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws are some of the least restrictive in the nation. States with severely restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws \u8211\'20\'13 Wyoming, Texas, Nebraska, Oklahoma, Arizona, Utah \u8211\'20\'13 form a ring around it. This means Colorado\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics (there are estimated to be fewer than 18) will be the closest providers for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://states.guttmacher.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 1.2 million women from surrounding states}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The flood of out-of-state patients has pushed many Colorado providers to their limits. Interviewed last week, coming off a night shift at the hospital where she works, Marchin was heading into another long shift at Planned Parenthood. Along with many of her fellow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, she has recently increased her hours at the clinic.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe more patients we have on the schedule who are getting procedural abortions, the longer our day is going to be,\u8221\'20\'1D Marchin says of the process, which can be done only in a doctor\u8217\'20\'19s office and is the only method suitable for later-term pregnancies. \u8220\'20\'1CWe feel grateful to be in a state where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is protected and so we\u8217\'20\'19re galvanized to continue this work. But at the same time, we\u8217\'20\'19re trying to be cautious about not becoming burnt out.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Clinics in Colorado were already overbooked, leaving patients facing weeks-long wait times for appointments. Now, such clinics are expecting an 80% increase in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care patient volume, according to stats from Planned Parenthood of the Rocky Mountains. All of them have seen an increase in out-of-state patients around the 12-week stage of pregnancy, the point at which providers can no longer offer medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is lower-risk and less invasive than a procedural \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and can be done at home by taking two pills.\par \par \uc2 To prepare for overwhelming demand, the staff at Denver Planned Parenthood are planning to expand telemedicine and mail order services for medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They\u8217\'20\'19re also thinking about how to stay resilient in an era of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  prohibition, developing support programs for staff and sharing recommendations for therapists for its staff.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 Having to travel for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 scrambling to get an appointment at an overbooked clinic, securing money for transportation, lodging, childcare and the procedure itself, getting time off work and flying or driving for hours \u8211\'20\'13 has been proven to take a significant toll on patients\u8217\'20\'19 mental health. For many women, crossing state lines to access care amplifies the stigma surrounding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In a 2017 qualitative study, women variously {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5953191/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 described}}}\uc2  the experience of forced \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  travel as \u8220\'20\'1Cnerve-racking\u8221\'20\'1D, \u8220\'20\'1Cso much extra stress\u8221\'20\'1D, \u8220\'20\'1Ca nightmare\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cmaking you feel like you\u8217\'20\'19re doing something bad\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 More than half of respondents reported mental health consequences. Some had considered or attempted self-induction. In a 2020 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0242463"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 study}}}\uc2  of patients seeking abortions, women who encountered logistical difficulties, including having to travel, were more likely to report symptoms of stress, anxiety and depression.\par \par \uc2 Every week, the clinic where Marchin works sees patients enduring unimaginable traumas. In early June, a mother arrived from Texas with her 11-year-old daughter who was more than 20 weeks pregnant as a result of rape. (The Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban makes no exceptions for rape or incest.)\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis was a child who maybe just barely got her first period and probably has no idea what\u8217\'20\'19s going on in her body,\u8221\'20\'1D Marchin says. On top of helping her daughter cope with the trauma of rape and carrying a pregnancy as a child, the mother, who spoke no English, had to find financial help through an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund, then take time off work and pull her daughter out of school to travel hundreds of miles.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey had never been on a plane before,\u8221\'20\'1D Marchin says. \u8220\'20\'1CThey didn\u8217\'20\'19t know how to book a flight. If she could\u8217\'20\'19ve stayed in Texas for her care, she could\u8217\'20\'19ve at least accessed it a little bit earlier.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Many of Dr Marchin\u8217\'20\'19s out-of-state patients are facing desperate situations. Some are trapped in abusive relationships. Some have drained their bank accounts to pay for the trip to Colorado. Some have chronic health conditions that would have become life-threatening had they been forced to continue their pregnancies. In most cases, after discharging them from the clinic with aftercare instructions and wishing them the best on their journeys home, Dr Marchin will never see these patients again. While she doesn\u8217\'20\'19t worry about the physical or psychological effects of the procedure itself \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.today.com/health/mind-body/mental-health-abortion-rcna27261"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 research}}}\uc2  shows that getting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  overwhelmingly leads to feelings of relief, not regret \u8211\'20\'13 she\u8217\'20\'19s often left with lingering concerns about how her patients will fare in states that deny their rights to bodily autonomy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI struggle with not being able to know what happens to these people after they leave. We do our best \u8211\'20\'13 we take very good care of these patients \u8211\'20\'13 but we don\u8217\'20\'19t get to follow up with them,\u8221\'20\'1D Marchin says. \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is extremely safe, and I\u8217\'20\'19m not worried about medical complications, but I don\u8217\'20\'19t know the answer to what psychological toll [forced travel] is having.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220625ei6p001b9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
