\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 How the Christian right took over the judiciary and changed America\b0\par\par\uc2 Katherine Stewart \par \uc2 1,379 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par 09:33 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Leaders of the movement understood very well that if you can capture the courts, you can change society\par \par \uc2 The supreme court decision in Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization, which reverses the constitutional \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights that American women have enjoyed over the past 50 years, has come as a surprise to many voters. A majority, after all, support reproductive rights and regard their abolition as regressive and barbaric.\par \par \uc2 Understood in the context of the movement that created the supreme court in its current incarnation, however, there is nothing surprising about it. In fact, it marks the beginning rather than the endpoint of the agenda this movement has in mind.\par \par \uc2 At the core of the Dobbs decision lies the conviction that the power of government can and should be used to impose a certain moral and religious vision \u8211\'20\'13 a supposedly biblical and regressive understanding of the Christian religion \u8211\'20\'13 on the population at large.\par \par \uc2 How did this conviction come to have such influence in the courts, given America\u8217\'20\'19s longstanding principle of church-state separation? To understand why this is happening now, it\u8217\'20\'19s important to know something about the Christian nationalist movement\u8217\'20\'19s history, how its leaders chose the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a means of creating single-issue voters, and how they united conservatives across denominational barriers by, in effect, inventing a new form of intensely political religion.\par \par \uc2 Christian nationalists often claim their movement got its start as a grassroots reaction to Roe v Wade in 1973. But the movement actually gelled several years later with a crucial assist from a group calling itself the \u8220\'20\'1CNew Right\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Paul Weyrich\uc2 , Howard Phillips, \uc2 Phyllis Schlafly\uc2  and other leaders of this movement were dissatisfied with the direction of the Republican party and the culture at large. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are radicals who want to change the existing power structure. We are not conservatives in the sense that conservative means accepting the status quo,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Paul Weyrich\uc2  said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe want change \u8211\'20\'13 we are the forces of change.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 They were angry at liberals, who they believed threatened to undermine national security with their softness on communism. They were angry at establishment conservatives \u8211\'20\'13 the \u8220\'20\'1CRockefeller Republicans\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 for siding with the liberals; they were angry about the rising tide of feminism, which they saw as a menace to the social order, and about the civil rights movement and the danger it posed to segregation. One thing that they were not particularly angry about, at least initially, was the matter of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 New Right leaders formed common cause with a handful of conservative Catholics, including George Weigel and Richard John Neuhaus, who shared their concerns, and drew in powerful conservative preachers such as \uc2 Jerry Falwell\uc2  and Bob Jones Sr. They were determined to ignite a hyper-conservative counter-revolution. All they needed now was an issue that could be used to unify its disparate elements and draw in the rank and file.\par \par \uc2 Among their core concerns was the fear that the supreme court might end tax exemptions for segregated Christian schools. \uc2 Jerry Falwell\uc2  and many of his fellow southern, white, conservative pastors were closely involved with segregated schools and universities \u8211\'20\'13 Jones went so far as to call segregation \u8220\'20\'1CGod\u8217\'20\'19s established order\u8221\'20\'1D and referred to desegregationists as \u8220\'20\'1CSatanic propagandists\u8221\'20\'1D who were \u8220\'20\'1Cleading colored Christians astray\u8221\'20\'1D. As far as these pastors were concerned, they had the right not just to separate people on the basis of race but to also receive federal money for the purpose.\par \par \uc2 They knew, however, that \u8220\'20\'1CStop the tax on segregation!\u8221\'20\'1D wasn\u8217\'20\'19t going to be an effective rallying cry for their new movement. As the historian and author Randall Balmer {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/magazine/story/2014/05/religious-right-real-origins-107133/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote}}}\uc2 , \u8220\'20\'1CIt wasn\u8217\'20\'19t until 1979 \u8211\'20\'13 a full six years after Roe \u8211\'20\'13 that evangelical leaders, at the behest of conservative activist \uc2 Paul Weyrich\uc2 , seized on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  not for moral reasons, but as a rallying-cry to deny President Jimmy Carter a second term. Why? Because the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  crusade was more palatable than the religious right\u8217\'20\'19s real motive: protecting segregated schools.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In many respects \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was an unlikely choice, because when the Roe v Wade decision was issued, most Protestant Republicans supported it. The \uc2 Southern Baptist Convention\uc2  passed resolutions in 1971 and 1974 expressing support for the liberalization of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, and an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://media.sbhla.org.s3.amazonaws.com/3521,31-Jan-1973.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 editorial}}}\uc2  in their wire service hailed the passage of Roe v Wade, declaring that \u8220\'20\'1Creligious liberty, human equality and justice are advanced by the Supreme Court \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision.\u8221\'20\'1D As governor of California, \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  passed the most liberal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law in the country in 1967. Conservative icon Barry Goldwater supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law liberalization too, at least early in his career, and his wife Peggy was a cofounder of Planned Parenthood in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 Yet \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  turned out to be the critical unifying issue for two fundamentally political reasons. First, it brought together conservative Catholics who supplied much of the intellectual leadership of the movement with conservative Protestants and evangelicals. Second, by tying \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the perceived social ills of the age \u8211\'20\'13 the sexual revolution, the civil rights movement, and women\u8217\'20\'19s liberation \u8211\'20\'13 the issue became a focal point for the anxieties about social change welling up from the base.\par \par \uc2 Over time, pro-choice voices were purged from the Republican party. In her 2016 book, How the Republican Party Became Pro-Life, \uc2 Phyllis Schlafly\uc2  details the considerable effort it took, over several decades, to force the Republican party to change its views on the issue. What her book and the history shows is that the \u8220\'20\'1Cpro-life religion\u8221\'20\'1D that we see today, which cuts across denominational boundaries on the political right, is a modern creation.\par \par \uc2 In recent decades, the religious right has invested many hundreds of millions of dollars developing a complex and coordinated infrastructure, whose features include rightwing policy groups, networking organizations, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/mar/03/bill-dallas-christian-nationalist-right-donald-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 data initiatives}}}\uc2  and media. A critical component of this infrastructure is its sophisticated legal sphere.\par \par \uc2 Movement leaders understood very well that if you can capture the courts, you can change society. Leading organizations include the \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2 , which is involved in many of the recent cases intended to degrade the principle of church-state separation; First Liberty; Becket, formerly known as the Becket Fund for Religious Liberty; and the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2 , a networking and support organization for rightwing jurists and their allies whose leader, \uc2 Leonard Leo\uc2 , has directed hundreds of millions of dollars to a network of affiliated organizations. This infrastructure has created a pipeline to funnel ideologues to important judicial positions at the national and federal level. Nearly 90% of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s appellate court nominees were or are \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2  members, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.senate.gov/news/speeches/the-third-federalist-society"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according}}}\uc2  to Senator Sheldon Whitehouse, and all six conservative justices on the supreme court are current or former members.\par \par \uc2 The rightwing legal movement has spent several decades establishing a new regime in which \u8220\'20\'1Creligious liberty\u8221\'20\'1D is reframed as an exemption from the law, one enjoyed by a certain preferred category of religion. LGBT advocacy groups are concerned that the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s willingness, in the next session, to hear the case of a Colorado website designer who wishes to refuse services to same-sex couples is a critical step to overturning a broad range of anti-discrimination laws that protect LGBT Americans along with women, members of religious minority groups and others.\par \par \uc2 The legal powerhouses of the Christian right have also recognized that their efforts can be turned into a gravy train of public money. That is one of the reasons a recent supreme court decision, which ruled Maine must fund religious schools as part of a state tuition program, was predicted by observers of this movement. This decision forces the state to fund religious schools no matter how discriminatory their practices and sectarian their teachings. \u8220\'20\'1CThis court continues to dismantle the wall of separation between church and state,\u8221\'20\'1D Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  wrote in her dissent.\par \par \uc2 This supreme court has already made clear how swiftly our Christian nationalist judiciary will change the law to suit this vision of a society ruled by a reactionary elite, a society with a preferred religion and a prescribed code of sexual behavior, all backed by the coercive power of the state. The idea that they will stop with overturning Roe v Wade is a delusion.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220625ei6p000ul\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
